<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract.</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">With a basic knowledge of cohomology theory, the background necessary to
understand Hodge theory
and polarization, Deligne’s Mixed Hodge
Structure on cohomology of complex algebraic varieties is
described.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Key words and phrases: </h6>Hodge theory, algebraic geometry, Mixed Hodge structure
</div>
<div class="ltx_classification"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_classification">1991 Mathematics Subject Classification</h6>Primary 14C30, 32S35; Secondary 14F40, 14F05, 32J25
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">Summer School on Hodge Theory and Related
Topics</span></p>
<p id="p1.2" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="p1.2.1" class="ltx_text">International center for theoretical Physics</span></p>
<p id="p1.3" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="p1.3.1" class="ltx_text">14 June 2010 - 2 July 2010</span></p>
<p id="p1.4" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="p1.4.1" class="ltx_text">Trieste -
ITALY</span></p>
</div>
<section id="Sx1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Introduction</h2>

<div id="Sx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We assume that the reader is familiar with the basic theory of
manifolds, basic algebraic geometry as well as cohomology theory. For instance, the reader
should be familiar with the contents of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite> or <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>,
the beginning of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>, the notes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> in this book.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">According to Deligne <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>, the cohomology space <math id="Sx1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{n}(X,\mathbb{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{n}(X,\mathbb{C})</annotation></semantics></math>
of a complex algebraic variety <math id="Sx1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> carries two finite filtrations by
complex sub-vector spaces, the weight filtration <math id="Sx1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> and the Hodge filtration <math id="Sx1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>. In
addition, the subspaces <math id="Sx1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> are rationally defined, i.e., <math id="Sx1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> is
generated by rational elements in <math id="Sx1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{n}(X,\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{n}(X,\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For a
smooth compact variety, the filtration <math id="Sx1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> is trivial; however the filtration <math id="Sx1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> and
its conjugate <math id="Sx1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{F}</annotation></semantics></math>, with respect to the rational
cohomology, define the Hodge decomposition.
The structure in linear algebra
defined on a complex vector space by such decomposition is called a Hodge
Structure (HS).</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">For any complex algebraic variety <math id="Sx1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, the filtration <math id="Sx1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> is defined on <math id="Sx1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{n}(X,\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{n}(X,\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math>.
We can define the homogeneous part <math id="Sx1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr^{W}_{j}H^{n}(X,\mathbb{Q})=W_{j}/W_{j-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr^{W}_{j}H^{n}(X,\mathbb{Q})=W_{j}/W_{j-1}</annotation></semantics></math>
of degree <math id="Sx1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> in the graded vector space
<math id="Sx1.p4.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="Gr^{W}_{*}H^{n}(X,\mathbb{Q}):=\oplus_{j\in\mathbb{Z}}W_{j}/W_{j-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo><mi>W</mi></msubsup><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">:=</mo><msub><mo lspace="0em">⊕</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr^{W}_{*}H^{n}(X,\mathbb{Q}):=\oplus_{j\in\mathbb{Z}}W_{j}/W_{j-1}</annotation></semantics></math>
associated to the filtration <math id="Sx1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="Sx1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{n}(X,\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{n}(X,\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math>.
For each integer <math id="Sx1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists
an isomorphism:</p>
<table id="Sx1.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Sx1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr^{W}_{j}H^{n}(X,\mathbb{Q})\otimes_{\mathbb{Q}}\mathbb{C}\simeq Gr^{W}_{j}H^{n}(X,\mathbb{C})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></msub><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr^{W}_{j}H^{n}(X,\mathbb{Q})\otimes_{\mathbb{Q}}\mathbb{C}\simeq Gr^{W}_{j}H^{n}(X,\mathbb{C}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Sx1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">such that conjugation on <math id="Sx1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr^{W}_{j}H^{n}(X,\mathbb{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr^{W}_{j}H^{n}(X,\mathbb{C})</annotation></semantics></math> with
respect to <math id="Sx1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr^{W}_{j}H^{n}(X,\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr^{W}_{j}H^{n}(X,\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> is well-defined. Deligne showed
that the
filtration <math id="Sx1.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> with its conjugate induce a Hodge
decomposition on <math id="Sx1.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr^{W}_{j}H^{n}(X,\mathbb{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr^{W}_{j}H^{n}(X,\mathbb{C})</annotation></semantics></math> of weight <math id="Sx1.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>.
These data define a Mixed Hodge Structure
on the cohomology of the complex algebraic variety <math id="Sx1.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">On a smooth variety <math id="Sx1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>,
the weight filtration <math id="Sx1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> and the Hodge filtration <math id="Sx1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> reflect properties
of the Normal Crossing
Divisor (NCD) at infinity of a completion of the variety, but are
independent of the choice of the Normal Crossing
Divisor.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Inspired by the properties of étale cohomology
of varieties over
fields with positive characteristic, constructed by A. Grothendieck
and M. Artin, Deligne established the existence of a Mixed Hodge Structure on the
cohomology of complex algebraic varieties, depending naturally
on algebraic morphisms but not on
continuous maps. The theory has been fundamental in the study of
topological
properties of complex algebraic varieties.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">After this introduction, we recall the analytic background of
Hodge decomposition on the cohomology of Kähler manifolds.
We mention <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite>, which are
references more adapted to algebraic
geometers, and where one can also find
other sources as classical books by A.
Weil <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite> and R.O. Wells <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite> for which we refer for full proofs in the first two sections. In the second section, we recall the Hard
Lefschetz Theorem and Riemann bilinear relations in order to define a
polarization on the cohomology of projective smooth varieties.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">We give an abstract definition of Mixed Hodge Structures in the
third section as an object of
interest in linear algebra, following closely Deligne <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>. The
algebraic properties of Mixed Hodge Structures are
developed on objects with three opposite
filtrations in any abelian category. In section four, we need to
develop algebraic homology techniques on filtered complexes up to
filtered quasi-isomorphisms of complexes. The main contribution by
Deligne was to fix the axioms of a Mixed Hodge Complex (MHC)
giving rise to a Mixed Hodge Structure on its cohomology.
A delicate lemma of Deligne on two filtrations needed to prove that the
weight spectral sequence of a Mixed Hodge Complex is in the category
of Hodge Structure is explained.
This central result is
stated and applied in section five, where in particular, the
extension of Hodge Structures to all smooth compact complex algebraic
varieties is described by
Hodge filtrations on the analytic de Rham complex and not harmonic
forms, although the proof is by
reduction to Kähler manifolds.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">For a non-compact smooth algebraic variety <math id="Sx1.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, we
need to take into account the properties at infinity, that is the properties
of the Normal Crossing Divisor, the complement of the variety
in a proper compactification, and introduce
Deligne’s logarithmic de Rham
complex in section <math id="Sx1.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p10.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="Sx1.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is singular, we introduce a smooth simplicial
covering to construct the Mixed Hodge Complex in section <math id="Sx1.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>7</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7</annotation></semantics></math>. We mention also an
alternative construction.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p11.1" class="ltx_p">Each section starts with the statement of the results. The proofs
are given in later subsections because they can be technical.
Since a Mixed Hodge Structure involves rational cohomology constructed via
techniques different than de Rham cohomology, when we define a
morphism of Mixed Hodge Structures, we ask for compatibility with the filtrations
as well as the identification of cohomology via the various
constructions. For this reason it is convenient to work
constantly at the level of complexes in the language of filtered
and bi-filtered derived categories to ensure that the Mixed Hodge Structures
constructed on cohomology are canonical and do not depend on the
particular definition of cohomology used. Hence, we add a
lengthy introduction on hypercohomology in section <math id="Sx1.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p12.1" class="ltx_p">As applications let us mention Deformations of smooth proper
analytic families which define a linear invariant called Variation
of Hodge Structure (VHS) introduced by P. Griffiths, and limit Mixed Hodge
Structures adapted to study the degeneration of Variation of
Hodge Structures. Variation of Mixed Hodge
Structure (VMHS) are the topics of other lectures.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p13.1" class="ltx_p">Finally we stress that we only introduce the necessary language
to express the statements and their implications in Hodge theory,
but we encourage mathematicians
to look into the foundational work of Deligne in references
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> to discover his dense and unique style, since here intricate
proofs in Hodge theory and spectral sequences are only surveyed.
We remark that further developments in the theory occurred
for cohomology with coefficients in Variations of Hodge
Structure and the theory of differential Hodge modules (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>,<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite>).</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p14.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="Sx1.p14.1.1" class="ltx_text">Contents</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p15.1" class="ltx_p">Introduction  1 
<br class="ltx_break">1 Hodge decomposition  3 
<br class="ltx_break">1.1 Harmonic forms on a differentiable manifold  4 
<br class="ltx_break">1.2 Complex manifolds and decomposition into types  6 
<br class="ltx_break">1.3 Hermitian metric, its associated Riemannian metric and <math id="Sx1.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,1)</annotation></semantics></math> form  10 
<br class="ltx_break">1.4 Harmonic forms on compact complex manifolds  12 
<br class="ltx_break">1.5 Kähler manifolds  13
<br class="ltx_break">1.6 Cohomology class of a subvariety and Hodge conjecture  15 
<br class="ltx_break">2 Lefschetz decomposition and Polarized Hodge structure  20 
<br class="ltx_break">2.1 Lefschetz decomposition and primitive cohomology  20 
<br class="ltx_break">2.2 The category of Hodge Structures  23 
<br class="ltx_break">2.3 Examples  27 
<br class="ltx_break">3 Mixed Hodge Structures (MHS)  29 
<br class="ltx_break">3.1 Filtrations  31 
<br class="ltx_break">3.2 Opposite filtrations  33 
<br class="ltx_break">3.3 Proof of the canonical decomposition of the weight filtration  36
<br class="ltx_break">3.4 Complex Mixed Hodge Structures  38 
<br class="ltx_break">4 Hypercohomology and spectral sequence of a filtered complex  39 
<br class="ltx_break">4.1 Derived filtered category  42 
<br class="ltx_break">4.2 Derived functor on a filtered complex  45 
<br class="ltx_break">4.3 The spectral sequence defined by a filtered complex  47 
<br class="ltx_break">5 Mixed Hodge Complex (MHC)  50 
<br class="ltx_break">5.1 HS on the cohomology of a smooth compact algebraic variety  54 
<br class="ltx_break">5.2 MHS on the cohomology of a mixed Hodge Complex  56 
<br class="ltx_break">6 Logarithmic complex, normal crossing divisor and the mixed cone  59 
<br class="ltx_break">6.1 MHS on the cohomology of smooth varieties  60 
<br class="ltx_break">6.2 MHS of a normal crossing divisor (NCD)  66 
<br class="ltx_break">6.3 Relative cohomology and the mixed cone  68 
<br class="ltx_break">7 MHS on the cohomology of a complex algebraic variety  69 
<br class="ltx_break">7.1 MHS on cohomology of simplicial varieties  70 
<br class="ltx_break">7.2 MHS on the cohomology of a complete embedded algebraic variety  77 
<br class="ltx_break">References  79</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1. </span>Hodge decomposition</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section we explain the importance of the Hodge
decomposition and its relation to various concepts in geometry.
Let <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}^{*}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}^{*}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the de Rham complex of differential forms
with complex values on a complex manifold <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}^{p,q}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}^{p,q}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>
the differentiable forms with complex coefficients of type <math id="S1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p,q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p,q)</annotation></semantics></math> (see <a href="#S1.SS2.SSS3" title="1.2.3. Decomposition into types on the complexified tangent bundle ‣ 1.2. Complex manifolds and decomposition into types ‣ 1. Hodge decomposition ‣ Mixed Hodge Structures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2.3</span></a> below).
The cohomology subspaces of type <math id="S1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p,q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p,q)</annotation></semantics></math>
are defined as:</p>
<table id="S1.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{p,q}(X)=\frac{Z_{d}^{p,q}(X)}{d\mathcal{E}^{*}(X)\cap Z_{d}^{p,q}(X)}\,\,\mbox{ where }\,\,Z_{d}^{p,q}(X)=Ker\,d\cap\mathcal{E}^{p,q}(X)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.340em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> where </mtext><mo lspace="0.340em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{p,q}(X)=\frac{Z_{d}^{p,q}(X)}{d\mathcal{E}^{*}(X)\cap Z_{d}^{p,q}(X)}\,\,\mbox{ where }\,\,Z_{d}^{p,q}(X)=Ker\,d\cap\mathcal{E}^{p,q}(X)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.1</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Hodge decomposition)<span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> be a compact Kähler
manifold (see <a href="#S1.SS5" title="1.5. Kähler manifolds ‣ 1. Hodge decomposition ‣ Mixed Hodge Structures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.5</span></a>). There exists a decomposition, called the Hodge decomposition,
of the complex cohomology
spaces into a direct sum of complex subspaces:</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="H^{i}(X,\mathbb{C})=\oplus_{p+q=i}H^{p,q}(X),\quad{\rm satisfying}\quad H^{p,q}(X)=\overline{H^{q,p}(X)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><msub><mo lspace="0em">⊕</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mi>satisfying</mi><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{i}(X,\mathbb{C})=\oplus_{p+q=i}H^{p,q}(X),\quad{\rm satisfying}\quad H^{p,q}(X)=\overline{H^{q,p}(X)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Since a smooth complex projective variety is Kähler (see <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem24" title="Example 1.24. ‣ 1.5. Kähler manifolds ‣ 1. Hodge decomposition ‣ Mixed Hodge Structures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.24</span></a>), we
deduce:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 1.2</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">There exists a Hodge decomposition on the
cohomology of a smooth complex projective variety.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The above Hodge decomposition theorem uses successive
fundamental concepts from Riemannian geometry
such as harmonic forms, complex analytic manifolds such as
Dolbeault cohomology and from Hermitian geometry. It will be extended
later to algebraic geometry.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The manifold <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> will denote successively in this section a
differentiable, complex analytic, and finally a compact
Kähler manifold. We give in this section a summary of the theory for the reader who is not
familiar with the above theorem, which includes the definition of the <span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Laplacian</span> in Riemannian geometry and the space of harmonic forms isomorphic to the cohomology of <math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.
On complex manifolds, we start with a <span id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Hermitian structure</span>, its underlying <span id="S1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Riemannian structure</span> and its associated <span id="S1.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">fundamental <math id="S1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,1)</annotation></semantics></math> form </span> used to construct the Lefschetz decomposition.
It is only on <span id="S1.p4.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Kähler manifolds</span> when the form is closed that the components of type <math id="S1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p,q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p,q)</annotation></semantics></math> of an <span id="S1.p4.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">harmonic form</span> are harmonics. For an extended exposition of the theory with
full proofs, including the subtle linear algebra of Hermitian metrics needed here, we
refer to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite>, see also <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite> ( for original articles see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite> and even <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite>).</p>
</div>
<section id="S1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.1. </span>Harmonic forms on a differentiable manifold</h3>

<div id="S1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A Riemannian manifold <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is endowed with a <span id="S1.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">scalar product on
its tangent bundle </span> defining a metric. Another basic result in
analysis states that
the <span id="S1.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">cohomology of a compact
smooth Riemannian manifold is
represented by real harmonic global differential forms</span>.
To define harmonic forms, we need to introduce Hodge <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{S}}tar" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{S}}tar</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-operator and the Laplacian</span>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S1.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">1.1.1. </span>Riemannian metric</h4>

<div id="S1.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A bilinear form <math id="S1.SS1.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S1.SS1.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>
is defined at each point <math id="S1.SS1.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> as a product on the
tangent space <math id="S1.SS1.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S1.SS1.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="S1.SS1.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math></p>
<table id="S1.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="g_{x}(,):T_{x}\otimes_{{\mathbb{R}}}T_{x}\to{\mathbb{R}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo>,</mo><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{x}(,):T_{x}\otimes_{{\mathbb{R}}}T_{x}\to{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS1.SSS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S1.SS1.SSS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> vary smoothly with <math id="S1.SS1.SSS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, that is <math id="S1.SS1.SSS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{ij}(x):=g_{x}(\frac{\partial}{\partial x_{i}},\frac{\partial}{\partial x_{j}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mfrac><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{ij}(x):=g_{x}(\frac{\partial}{\partial x_{i}},\frac{\partial}{\partial x_{j}})</annotation></semantics></math> is
differentiable in <math id="S1.SS1.SSS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, then the product is given as <math id="S1.SS1.SSS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{x}=\sum_{i,j}h_{ij}(x)dx_{i}\otimes dx_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{x}=\sum_{i,j}h_{ij}(x)dx_{i}\otimes dx_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>. It is the local
expression of a global product on fiber bundles <math id="S1.SS1.SSS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g:T_{X}\otimes T_{X}\to{\mathbb{R}}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g:T_{X}\otimes T_{X}\to{\mathbb{R}}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>. It is called a metric if moreover the
matrix of the the product defined by <math id="S1.SS1.SSS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{ij}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{ij}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is positive
definite, that is: <math id="S1.SS1.SSS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{x}(u,u)&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{x}(u,u)&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S1.SS1.SSS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\neq 0\in T_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\neq 0\in T_{x}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A globally induced metric on the cotangent bundle, is defined
locally on the fiber <math id="S1.SS1.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}^{1}_{x}:=T_{x}^{*}:=Hom(T_{x},{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>:=</mo><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}^{1}_{x}:=T_{x}^{*}:=Hom(T_{x},{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math> and more
generally on the differential forms <math id="S1.SS1.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}^{p}_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>x</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}^{p}_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> as follows.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S1.SS1.SSS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{1},\ldots,e_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{1},\ldots,e_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> be an orthonormal basis of <math id="S1.SS1.SSS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{x}</annotation></semantics></math>,
and <math id="S1.SS1.SSS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(e_{j}^{*})_{j\in[1,n]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>e</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(e_{j}^{*})_{j\in[1,n]}</annotation></semantics></math> its dual basis of <math id="S1.SS1.SSS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{x}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{x}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>. The vectors
<math id="S1.SS1.SSS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{i_{1}}^{*}\wedge\ldots\wedge e_{i_{p}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>e</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>∧</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>∧</mo><msubsup><mi>e</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{i_{1}}^{*}\wedge\ldots\wedge e_{i_{p}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, for all <math id="S1.SS1.SSS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{1}&lt;\ldots&lt;i_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{1}&lt;\ldots&lt;i_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>, is a
basis of <math id="S1.SS1.SSS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}^{p}_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>x</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}^{p}_{x}</annotation></semantics></math>, for all <math id="S1.SS1.SSS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in X</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemmadefinition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma and Definition 1.3</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There exists
a unique metric globally defined on the bundle <math id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}^{p}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}^{p}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> such
that, for all <math id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in X</annotation></semantics></math>, the vectors
<math id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{i_{1}}^{*}\wedge\ldots\wedge e_{i_{p}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>e</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>∧</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>∧</mo><msubsup><mi>e</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{i_{1}}^{*}\wedge\ldots\wedge e_{i_{p}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{1}&lt;\ldots&lt;i_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{1}&lt;\ldots&lt;i_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>, is an orthonormal
basis of <math id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}^{p}_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>x</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}^{p}_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> whenever <math id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{1},\ldots,e_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{1},\ldots,e_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is an orthonormal basis of <math id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{x}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.SSS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Indeed, <math id="S1.SS1.SSS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{i_{1}}^{*}\wedge\ldots,\wedge e_{i_{p}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>e</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>∧</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">∧</mo><msubsup><mi>e</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{i_{1}}^{*}\wedge\ldots,\wedge e_{i_{p}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> defines a local differentiable section of <math id="S1.SS1.SSS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}^{p}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}^{p}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> due to
the fact that the Gram-Schmidt process of normalization construct
an orthonormal basis varying differentiably in
terms of the point <math id="S1.SS1.SSS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in X</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 1.4</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We suppose that the manifold <math id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is oriented.
For each point <math id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in X</annotation></semantics></math> let <math id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{j},j\in[1,n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{j},j\in[1,n]</annotation></semantics></math> be an orthonormal
positively oriented basis of <math id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{X,x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{X,x}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The volume form, denoted <math id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="vol" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">vol</annotation></semantics></math>, is defined by the metric as the unique
nowhere vanishing section of <math id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega^{n}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega^{n}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>,
satisfying <math id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="vol_{x}:=e_{1}^{*}\wedge\ldots\wedge e_{n}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>∧</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>∧</mo><msubsup><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">vol_{x}:=e_{1}^{*}\wedge\ldots\wedge e_{n}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> for each point <math id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in X</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_xca">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Exercise 1.5</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The volume form may be defined directly without
orthonormalization process as follows.
Let <math id="S1.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1},\ldots,x_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1},\ldots,x_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> denote a local ordered set of
coordinates on an open subset <math id="S1.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> of a covering of <math id="S1.Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>,
compatible with the orientation. Prove that</p>
<table id="S1.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{det(h_{ij})}dx_{1}\wedge\cdots\wedge dx_{n}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{det(h_{ij})}dx_{1}\wedge\cdots\wedge dx_{n}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem5.p1.2" class="ltx_p">defines the global volume form.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">1.1.2. </span><math id="S1.SS1.SSS2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> metric</h4>

<div id="S1.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S1.SS1.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> compact, we deduce from the volume form a global pairing
called the <math id="S1.SS1.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> metric</p>
<table id="S1.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\forall\psi,\,\eta\in\mathcal{E}^{i}(X),(\psi,\,\eta)_{L^{2}}=\int_{X}g_{x}(\psi(x),\,\eta(x))vol(x)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>ψ</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><mi>X</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\forall\psi,\,\eta\in\mathcal{E}^{i}(X),(\psi,\,\eta)_{L^{2}}=\int_{X}g_{x}(\psi(x),\,\eta(x))vol(x)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS1.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">1.1.3. </span>Laplacian</h4>

<div id="S1.SS1.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We prove the existence of an operator <math id="S1.SS1.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{*}:\mathcal{E}^{i+1}_{X}\to\mathcal{E}^{i}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{*}:\mathcal{E}^{i+1}_{X}\to\mathcal{E}^{i}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> called formal adjoint to the differential
<math id="S1.SS1.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d:\mathcal{E}^{i}_{X}\to\mathcal{E}^{i+1}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d:\mathcal{E}^{i}_{X}\to\mathcal{E}^{i+1}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, satisfying:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S1.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(d\psi,\eta)_{L^{2}}=(\psi,d^{*}\eta)_{L^{2}},\quad\forall\psi\in\mathcal{E}^{i}(X),\forall\eta\in\mathcal{E}^{i+1}(X)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ψ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></msub></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>ψ</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(d\psi,\eta)_{L^{2}}=(\psi,d^{*}\eta)_{L^{2}},\quad\forall\psi\in\mathcal{E}^{i}(X),\forall\eta\in\mathcal{E}^{i+1}(X).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.SSS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.SSS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The adjoint operator is defined by constructing first an operator <math id="S1.SS1.SSS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="*" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∗</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">*</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.SSS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S1.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}^{i}_{X}\xrightarrow{*}\mathcal{E}^{n-i}_{X}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">∗</mo></mover><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}^{i}_{X}\xrightarrow{*}\mathcal{E}^{n-i}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.SSS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.SSS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">by requiring at each point <math id="S1.SS1.SSS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in X</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.SSS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S1.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="g_{x}(\psi(x),\,\eta(x))vol_{x}=\psi_{x}\wedge*\eta_{x},\quad\forall\psi_{x},\,\eta_{x}\in\mathcal{E}^{i}_{X,x}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mi>η</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>v</mi><mi>o</mi><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo rspace="0em">∧</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{x}(\psi(x),\,\eta(x))vol_{x}=\psi_{x}\wedge*\eta_{x},\quad\forall\psi_{x},\,\eta_{x}\in\mathcal{E}^{i}_{X,x}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.SSS3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.SSS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">The section <math id="S1.SS1.SSS3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="vol" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">vol</annotation></semantics></math> defines an isomorphism of bundles
<math id="S1.SS1.SSS3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}_{X}\simeq\mathcal{E}^{n}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>≃</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}_{X}\simeq\mathcal{E}^{n}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, inducing the isomorphisms:
<math id="S1.SS1.SSS3.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Hom(\mathcal{E}^{i}_{X},{\mathbb{R}}_{X})\simeq Hom(\mathcal{E}^{i}_{X},\mathcal{E}^{n}_{X})\simeq\mathcal{E}^{n-i}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Hom(\mathcal{E}^{i}_{X},{\mathbb{R}}_{X})\simeq Hom(\mathcal{E}^{i}_{X},\mathcal{E}^{n}_{X})\simeq\mathcal{E}^{n-i}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Finally, we deduce <math id="S1.SS1.SSS3.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="*" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∗</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">*</annotation></semantics></math> by composition with
the isomorphism <math id="S1.SS1.SSS3.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}^{i}_{X}\rightarrow Hom(\mathcal{E}^{i}_{X},{\mathbb{R}}_{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}^{i}_{X}\rightarrow Hom(\mathcal{E}^{i}_{X},{\mathbb{R}}_{X})</annotation></semantics></math> defined by the scalar product
on <math id="S1.SS1.SSS3.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}^{i}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}^{i}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Locally, in terms of an orthonormal basis, the formula apply for <math id="S1.SS1.SSS3.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi(x)=e_{i_{1}}^{*}\wedge\ldots\wedge e_{i_{p}}^{*}=\eta(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>e</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>∧</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>∧</mo><msubsup><mi>e</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi(x)=e_{i_{1}}^{*}\wedge\ldots\wedge e_{i_{p}}^{*}=\eta(x)</annotation></semantics></math>; it shows</p>
<table id="S1.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="*(\psi(x))=\epsilon e_{j_{1}}^{*}\wedge\ldots\wedge e_{j_{n-p}}^{*}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>e</mi><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>∧</mo><msubsup><mi>e</mi><msub><mi>j</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">*(\psi(x))=\epsilon e_{j_{1}}^{*}\wedge\ldots\wedge e_{j_{n-p}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS1.SSS3.p7.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S1.SS1.SSS3.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{j_{1},\ldots,j_{n-p}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{j_{1},\ldots,j_{n-p}\}</annotation></semantics></math> is the complement of <math id="S1.SS1.SSS3.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{i_{1},\ldots,i_{p}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{i_{1},\ldots,i_{p}\}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S1.SS1.SSS3.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[1,n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[1,n]</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S1.SS1.SSS3.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> is the sign of the permutation of <math id="S1.SS1.SSS3.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[1,n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[1,n]</annotation></semantics></math> defined by <math id="S1.SS1.SSS3.p7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{i_{1},\ldots,i_{p},j_{1},\ldots,j_{n-p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{i_{1},\ldots,i_{p},j_{1},\ldots,j_{n-p}}</annotation></semantics></math>. We have on <math id="S1.SS1.SSS3.p7.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}^{i}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}^{i}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.SSS3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S1.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="*^{2}=(-1)^{i(n-i)}Id,\quad d^{*}=(-1)^{n+in+1}*\circ d\circ*=(-1)^{i}*^{-1}\circ d\circ*" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mo>∗</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em">=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mi>I</mi><mi>d</mi><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">∘</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo lspace="0em">=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup><msup><mo rspace="0em">∗</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">∘</mo><mo lspace="0em">∗</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">*^{2}=(-1)^{i(n-i)}Id,\quad d^{*}=(-1)^{n+in+1}*\circ d\circ*=(-1)^{i}*^{-1}\circ d\circ*</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 1.6</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We have for all <math id="S1.Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha,\beta\in\mathcal{E}^{i}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha,\beta\in\mathcal{E}^{i}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(\alpha,\,\beta)_{L^{2}}=\int_{X}\alpha\wedge*\beta." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><msub><mo>∫</mo><mi>X</mi></msub><mi>α</mi><mo rspace="0em">∧</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\alpha,\,\beta)_{L^{2}}=\int_{X}\alpha\wedge*\beta.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 1.7</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The formal adjoint <math id="S1.Thmtheorem7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S1.Thmtheorem7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is a differential operator
and the operator <math id="S1.Thmtheorem7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> defined as:</p>
<table id="S1.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta=d^{*}\circ d+d\circ d^{*}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta=d^{*}\circ d+d\circ d^{*}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem7.p1.2" class="ltx_p">is called the Laplacian. Harmonic forms are defined as the solutions of the
Laplacian and denoted by:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem7.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S1.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{i}(X)=\{\psi\in\mathcal{E}^{i}(X):\Delta(\psi)=0\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{i}(X)=\{\psi\in\mathcal{E}^{i}(X):\Delta(\psi)=0\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.SSS3.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.SSS3.p9.1" class="ltx_p">The Harmonic forms depend on the choice of the metric. A fundamental result
due to Hodge and stated here without proof is that harmonic
forms represents the cohomology spaces:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.8</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">On a compact
smooth oriented Riemannian manifold each cohomology class is
represented by a unique real harmonic global differential form:</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{i}(X)\simeq H^{i}(X,{\mathbb{R}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{i}(X)\simeq H^{i}(X,{\mathbb{R}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.2. </span>Complex manifolds and decomposition into types</h3>

<div id="S1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">An underlying real differentiable structure is attached to a complex manifold <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, then
a real and a complex tangent bundles are defined and denoted by <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{X,{\mathbb{R}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{X,{\mathbb{R}}}</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>. The comparison is possible by embedding <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{X,{\mathbb{R}}}\otimes\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{X,{\mathbb{R}}}\otimes\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, then
a delicate point is to describe holomorphic functions among differentiable functions, and more generally the complex tangent
space and holomorphic differential forms in terms of the real structure
of the manifold. Such description
is a prelude to Hodge theory and is fundamental in Geometry,
whether we mention Cauchy-Riemann equations satisfied by the real
components of a holomorphic function, or the position of the
holomorphic tangent bundle inside the complexified real tangent
bundle. A weaker feature of a complex manifold is the existence of
an almost complex structure <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> on the real tangent bundle
which is enough
to define the type of differential forms.</p>
</div>
<section id="S1.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">1.2.1. </span>Almost-complex structure</h4>

<div id="S1.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S1.SS2.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> be a real vector space.
An almost-complex structure on <math id="S1.SS2.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> is defined by
a linear map <math id="S1.SS2.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J:V\to V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J:V\to V</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying <math id="S1.SS2.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J^{2}=-Id" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>J</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J^{2}=-Id</annotation></semantics></math>; then <math id="S1.SS2.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim_{{\mathbb{R}}}V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>dim</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim_{{\mathbb{R}}}V</annotation></semantics></math> is even
and the eigenvalues are <math id="S1.SS2.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS2.SSS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-i</annotation></semantics></math> with associated eigenspaces
<math id="S1.SS2.SSS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS2.SSS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{-}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S1.SS2.SSS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\mathbb{C}}=V\otimes_{{\mathbb{R}}}\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\mathbb{C}}=V\otimes_{{\mathbb{R}}}\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S1.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{+}=\{x-iJx:x\in V\}\subset V_{\mathbb{C}},\quad V^{-}=\{x+iJx:x\in V\}\subset V_{\mathbb{C}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>J</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>J</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{+}=\{x-iJx:x\in V\}\subset V_{\mathbb{C}},\quad V^{-}=\{x+iJx:x\in V\}\subset V_{\mathbb{C}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS2.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">where we write <math id="S1.SS2.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S1.SS2.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\otimes 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\otimes 1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS2.SSS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="iv" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">iv</annotation></semantics></math> for the element <math id="S1.SS2.SSS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\otimes i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\otimes i</annotation></semantics></math>. The
conjugation <math id="S1.SS2.SSS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S1.SS2.SSS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℂ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math> extends to a real linear map <math id="S1.SS2.SSS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>
on <math id="S1.SS2.SSS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S1.SS2.SSS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(a+ib)=a-ib" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(a+ib)=a-ib</annotation></semantics></math>;
we write <math id="S1.SS2.SSS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(\alpha)={\overline{\alpha}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(\alpha)={\overline{\alpha}}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S1.SS2.SSS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in V_{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in V_{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>. We note that the subspaces <math id="S1.SS2.SSS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS2.SSS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{-}</annotation></semantics></math> are
conjugate: <math id="S1.SS2.SSS1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{V^{+}}=V^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>−</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{V^{+}}=V^{-}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S1.SS2.SSS1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> induces an anti-linear
isomorphism <math id="S1.SS2.SSS1.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma:V^{+}\simeq V^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>≃</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>−</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma:V^{+}\simeq V^{-}</annotation></semantics></math> .</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 1.9</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S1.Thmtheorem9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> be a complex vector space, then
the identity bijection with the underlying real space <math id="S1.Thmtheorem9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{{\mathbb{R}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{{\mathbb{R}}}</annotation></semantics></math> of
<math id="S1.Thmtheorem9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.Thmtheorem9.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi:V\to V_{{\mathbb{R}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ℝ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi:V\to V_{{\mathbb{R}}}</annotation></semantics></math> transports the action of <math id="S1.Thmtheorem9.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> to a
real linear action <math id="S1.Thmtheorem9.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying <math id="S1.Thmtheorem9.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J^{2}=-Id" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>J</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J^{2}=-Id</annotation></semantics></math>, defining an
almost complex structure on <math id="S1.Thmtheorem9.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{{\mathbb{R}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{{\mathbb{R}}}</annotation></semantics></math>. A complex basis
<math id="S1.Thmtheorem9.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(e_{j})_{j\in[1,n]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(e_{j})_{j\in[1,n]}</annotation></semantics></math> defines a
real basis <math id="S1.Thmtheorem9.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\varphi(e_{j}),i(\varphi(e_{j}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\varphi(e_{j}),i(\varphi(e_{j}))</annotation></semantics></math>
for <math id="S1.Thmtheorem9.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\in[1,n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\in[1,n]</annotation></semantics></math>, such that: <math id="S1.Thmtheorem9.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J(\varphi(e_{j}):=i\varphi(e_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J(\varphi(e_{j}):=i\varphi(e_{j})</annotation></semantics></math>.
For <math id="S1.Thmtheorem9.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V=\mathbb{C}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V=\mathbb{C}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.Thmtheorem9.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(z_{1},\ldots,z_{n})=(x_{1},y_{1},\ldots,x_{n},y_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(z_{1},\ldots,z_{n})=(x_{1},y_{1},\ldots,x_{n},y_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> and</p>
<table id="S1.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J(x_{1},y_{1},\ldots,x_{n},y_{n})=(-y_{1},x_{1},\ldots,-y_{n},x_{n})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J(x_{1},y_{1},\ldots,x_{n},y_{n})=(-y_{1},x_{1},\ldots,-y_{n},x_{n}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem9.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Reciprocally,
an almost-complex structure <math id="S1.Thmtheorem9.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> on a real vector space <math id="S1.Thmtheorem9.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to a structure of
complex vector space on <math id="S1.Thmtheorem9.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> as follows. There exists a family of
vectors (<math id="S1.Thmtheorem9.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{j})_{j\in[1,n]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{j})_{j\in[1,n]}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S1.Thmtheorem9.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S1.Thmtheorem9.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{e_{j},J(e_{j})\}_{j\in[1,n]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{e_{j},J(e_{j})\}_{j\in[1,n]}</annotation></semantics></math> form a basis of <math id="S1.Thmtheorem9.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math>. First, one chooses a non-zero
vector <math id="S1.Thmtheorem9.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S1.Thmtheorem9.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> and proves that the space <math id="S1.Thmtheorem9.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> generated by <math id="S1.Thmtheorem9.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{1},J(e_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{1},J(e_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>
is of dimension two, then we continue with <math id="S1.Thmtheorem9.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{2}\notin W_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∉</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{2}\notin W_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>
and so on; then, there is a complex structure on <math id="S1.Thmtheorem9.p1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> for which
<math id="S1.Thmtheorem9.p1.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{j},j\in[1,n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{j},j\in[1,n]</annotation></semantics></math> is a complex basis with the action of <math id="S1.Thmtheorem9.p1.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>
defined by <math id="S1.Thmtheorem9.p1.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i.e_{j}:=J(e_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i.e_{j}:=J(e_{j})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 1.10</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem10.p1.1" class="ltx_p">An almost-complex structure on a real space <math id="S1.Thmtheorem10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> is equivalent to a
complex structure. In what follows we work essentially with
<math id="S1.Thmtheorem10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\mathbb{C}}:=V\otimes_{{\mathbb{R}}}\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\mathbb{C}}:=V\otimes_{{\mathbb{R}}}\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, then the product with a complex number <math id="S1.Thmtheorem10.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda.(v\otimes 1)=v\otimes\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda.(v\otimes 1)=v\otimes\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> will be written <math id="S1.Thmtheorem10.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda v</annotation></semantics></math>. This product is not to
confuse with the product <math id="S1.Thmtheorem10.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda*v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda*v</annotation></semantics></math> for the complex structure
on <math id="S1.Thmtheorem10.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>; in particular, <math id="S1.Thmtheorem10.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\otimes i:=iv" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\otimes i:=iv</annotation></semantics></math> while <math id="S1.Thmtheorem10.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i*v:=Jv" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i*v:=Jv</annotation></semantics></math>.
We note that the map <math id="S1.Thmtheorem10.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi:V\to V^{+}\subset V_{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi:V\to V^{+}\subset V_{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>
defined by <math id="S1.Thmtheorem10.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\mapsto x-iJx" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>J</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\mapsto x-iJx</annotation></semantics></math> is an isomorphism of complex
vector spaces.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">1.2.2. </span>Decomposition into types</h4>

<div id="S1.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S1.SS2.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> be a complex vector space and <math id="S1.SS2.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(V_{{\mathbb{R}}},J)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(V_{{\mathbb{R}}},J)</annotation></semantics></math> the underlying
real vector space with its involution <math id="S1.SS2.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> defined by
multiplication by <math id="S1.SS2.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>. The following decomposition of <math id="S1.SS2.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>
is called a decomposition into types</p>
<table id="S1.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\mathbb{C}}\simeq V^{1,0}\oplus V^{0,1}\,\hbox{ where }V^{1,0}=V^{+},\,V^{0,1}=V^{-},\,{\overline{V}^{1,0}}=V^{0,1}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> where </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>−</mo></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\mathbb{C}}\simeq V^{1,0}\oplus V^{0,1}\,\hbox{ where }V^{1,0}=V^{+},\,V^{0,1}=V^{-},\,{\overline{V}^{1,0}}=V^{0,1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS2.SSS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">the conjugation is with respect to the real structure of <math id="S1.SS2.SSS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>,
that is inherited from the conjugation on <math id="S1.SS2.SSS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℂ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S1.SS2.SSS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W:=Hom_{{\mathbb{R}}}(V_{{\mathbb{R}}},{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W:=Hom_{{\mathbb{R}}}(V_{{\mathbb{R}}},{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS2.SSS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{\mathbb{C}}:=W\otimes_{{\mathbb{R}}}\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{\mathbb{C}}:=W\otimes_{{\mathbb{R}}}\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>;
a morphism in <math id="S1.SS2.SSS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Hom_{\mathbb{C}}(V,\mathbb{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Hom_{\mathbb{C}}(V,\mathbb{C})</annotation></semantics></math> embeds, by extension to <math id="S1.SS2.SSS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>, into
<math id="S1.SS2.SSS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math> and its image is denoted by <math id="S1.SS2.SSS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{1,0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{1,0}</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S1.SS2.SSS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{1,0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{1,0}</annotation></semantics></math> vanishes on
<math id="S1.SS2.SSS2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{0,1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{0,1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S1.SS2.SSS2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{0,1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{0,1}</annotation></semantics></math> be the subspace vanishing on
<math id="S1.SS2.SSS2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{1,0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{1,0}</annotation></semantics></math>, thus
we deduce from the almost complex structure a decomposition
<math id="S1.SS2.SSS2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{\mathbb{C}}\simeq W^{1,0}\oplus W^{0,1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><mo>⊕</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{\mathbb{C}}\simeq W^{1,0}\oplus W^{0,1}</annotation></semantics></math> orthogonal to the
decomposition of <math id="S1.SS2.SSS2.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Such decomposition extends to the exterior power:</p>
<table id="S1.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\wedge^{n}W_{\mathbb{C}}=\oplus_{p+q=n}W^{pq},\quad{\rm where}\,\,W^{pq}=\wedge^{p}W^{1,0}\otimes\wedge^{q}W^{0,1},{\overline{W}^{p,q}}=W^{q,p}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∧</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><mi>W</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">⊕</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mi>W</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mi>where</mi><mi>W</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">∧</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></msup><mi>W</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">⊗</mo><mo lspace="0em">∧</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>q</mi></msup><mi>W</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>W</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\wedge^{n}W_{\mathbb{C}}=\oplus_{p+q=n}W^{pq},\quad{\rm where}\,\,W^{pq}=\wedge^{p}W^{1,0}\otimes\wedge^{q}W^{0,1},{\overline{W}^{p,q}}=W^{q,p}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem11" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem11.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 1.11</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem11.p1.1" class="ltx_p">On a fixed vector space, the notion of almost-complex
structure is equivalent to complex structure. The
interest in this notion will become clear for a
differentiably varying complex structure on the tangent bundle
of a manifold, since not all such structures determine an
analytic complex structure on <math id="S1.Thmtheorem11.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>. In order to lift an almost-complex
structure on <math id="S1.Thmtheorem11.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{M}</annotation></semantics></math> to a complex structure
on <math id="S1.Thmtheorem11.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, necessary and sufficient
conditions on
are stated in Newlander-Nirenberg
theorem (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite> Theorem 2.24).</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS2.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">1.2.3. </span>Decomposition into types on the complexified tangent bundle</h4>

<div id="S1.SS2.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The existence of a complex
structure on the manifold gives an almost-complex structure on
the differentiable tangent bundle <math id="S1.SS2.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{X,{\mathbb{R}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{X,{\mathbb{R}}}</annotation></semantics></math> and a decomposition
into types of the complexified tangent bundle <math id="S1.SS2.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{X,{\mathbb{R}}}\otimes_{{\mathbb{R}}}\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{X,{\mathbb{R}}}\otimes_{{\mathbb{R}}}\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math> which splits as a direct sum <math id="S1.SS2.SSS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{X}^{1,0}\oplus T_{X}^{0,1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mi>X</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>⊕</mo><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mi>X</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{X}^{1,0}\oplus T_{X}^{0,1}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S1.SS2.SSS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{X,z}^{1,0}=\{u-iJu:u\in T_{X,{\mathbb{R}},z}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>J</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{X,z}^{1,0}=\{u-iJu:u\in T_{X,{\mathbb{R}},z}\}</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S1.SS2.SSS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{T_{X}^{1,0}}=T_{X}^{0,1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mi>X</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mi>X</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{T_{X}^{1,0}}=T_{X}^{0,1}</annotation></semantics></math>. 
<br class="ltx_break">For a complex manifold <math id="S1.SS2.SSS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> with local complex coordinates <math id="S1.SS2.SSS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{j}=x_{j}+iy_{j},j\in[1,n]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{j}=x_{j}+iy_{j},j\in[1,n],</annotation></semantics></math> we define <math id="S1.SS2.SSS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J(\frac{\partial}{\partial x_{j}})=\frac{\partial}{\partial y_{j}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mfrac><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J(\frac{\partial}{\partial x_{j}})=\frac{\partial}{\partial y_{j}}</annotation></semantics></math> on the real tangent space
<math id="S1.SS2.SSS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{X,{\mathbb{R}},z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{X,{\mathbb{R}},z}</annotation></semantics></math> of the underlying differentiable manifold. Since the change of coordinates is
analytic, the definition of <math id="S1.SS2.SSS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> is compatible with the
corresponding change of differentiable coordinates, hence <math id="S1.SS2.SSS3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> is
globally defined on the tangent bundle <math id="S1.SS2.SSS3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{X,{\mathbb{R}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{X,{\mathbb{R}}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The
holomorphic tangent space <math id="S1.SS2.SSS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{X,z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{X,z}</annotation></semantics></math> embeds isomorphically onto
<math id="S1.SS2.SSS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{X}^{1,0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mi>X</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{X}^{1,0}</annotation></semantics></math> generated by <math id="S1.SS2.SSS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\partial}{\partial z_{j}}:=\frac{1}{2}(\frac{\partial}{\partial x_{j}}-i\frac{\partial}{\partial y_{j}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\partial}{\partial z_{j}}:=\frac{1}{2}(\frac{\partial}{\partial x_{j}}-i\frac{\partial}{\partial y_{j}})</annotation></semantics></math> of the form <math id="S1.SS2.SSS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{j}-iJ(u_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>J</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{j}-iJ(u_{j})</annotation></semantics></math> such that
the basis (<math id="S1.SS2.SSS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\partial}{\partial z_{j}},j\in[1,n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\partial}{\partial z_{j}},j\in[1,n]</annotation></semantics></math>) in <math id="S1.SS2.SSS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{X,{\mathbb{R}},z}\otimes\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{X,{\mathbb{R}},z}\otimes\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>
is complex dual to (<math id="S1.SS2.SSS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="dz_{j}=dx_{j}+idy_{j},j\in[1,n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">dz_{j}=dx_{j}+idy_{j},j\in[1,n]</annotation></semantics></math>). From now on we
identify the two complex bundles <math id="S1.SS2.SSS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS2.SSS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{X}^{1,0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mi>X</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{X}^{1,0}</annotation></semantics></math> via the isomorphism:</p>
<table id="S1.Ex19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{X,{\mathbb{R}},z}\xrightarrow{\sim}T_{X,z}^{1,0}:\frac{\partial}{\partial x_{j}}\mapsto\frac{\partial}{\partial z_{j}}=\frac{1}{2}(\frac{\partial}{\partial x_{j}}-i\frac{\partial}{\partial y_{j}}),\frac{\partial}{\partial y_{j}}\mapsto i\frac{\partial}{\partial z_{j}}=\frac{1}{2}(\frac{\partial}{\partial y_{j}}+i\frac{\partial}{\partial x_{j}})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></msub><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo></mover><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mfrac><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mfrac><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mfrac><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{X,{\mathbb{R}},z}\xrightarrow{\sim}T_{X,z}^{1,0}:\frac{\partial}{\partial x_{j}}\mapsto\frac{\partial}{\partial z_{j}}=\frac{1}{2}(\frac{\partial}{\partial x_{j}}-i\frac{\partial}{\partial y_{j}}),\frac{\partial}{\partial y_{j}}\mapsto i\frac{\partial}{\partial z_{j}}=\frac{1}{2}(\frac{\partial}{\partial y_{j}}+i\frac{\partial}{\partial x_{j}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS2.SSS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">which transforms the action of <math id="S1.SS2.SSS3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> into the product with <math id="S1.SS2.SSS3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> since
<math id="S1.SS2.SSS3.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J(\frac{\partial}{\partial x_{j}})=\frac{\partial}{\partial y_{j}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mfrac><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J(\frac{\partial}{\partial x_{j}})=\frac{\partial}{\partial y_{j}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The inverse is defined by twice the real part:
<math id="S1.SS2.SSS3.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u+iv\to 2u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u+iv\to 2u</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.SSS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.SSS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The dual of the above decomposition of the tangent space is
written as:</p>
<table id="S1.Ex20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}^{1}_{X}\otimes_{{\mathbb{R}}}\mathbb{C}\simeq\mathcal{E}^{1,0}_{X}\oplus\mathcal{E}^{0,1}_{X}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>X</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>⊕</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>X</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}^{1}_{X}\otimes_{{\mathbb{R}}}\mathbb{C}\simeq\mathcal{E}^{1,0}_{X}\oplus\mathcal{E}^{0,1}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS2.SSS3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">which induces a decomposition of the sheaves of
differential forms into types:</p>
<table id="S1.Ex21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{E}^{k}_{X}\otimes_{{\mathbb{R}}}\mathbb{C}\simeq\oplus_{p+q=k}\,\mathcal{E}^{p,q}_{X}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mi>ℂ</mi><mo rspace="0em">≃</mo><mo lspace="0em">⊕</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}^{k}_{X}\otimes_{{\mathbb{R}}}\mathbb{C}\simeq\oplus_{p+q=k}\,\mathcal{E}^{p,q}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS2.SSS3.p3.3" class="ltx_p">In terms of complex local coordinates on an open set <math id="S1.SS2.SSS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.SS2.SSS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi\in\mathcal{E}^{p,q}(U)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi\in\mathcal{E}^{p,q}(U)</annotation></semantics></math> is written as a linear combination with differentiable
functions as coefficients of:</p>
<table id="S1.Ex22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="dz_{I}\wedge d\overline{z}_{J}:=dz_{i_{1}}\wedge\cdots\wedge dz_{i_{p}}\wedge d\overline{z}_{j_{1}}\wedge\cdots\wedge d\overline{z}_{j_{q}}\quad{\rm where}\,\,I=\{i_{1},\cdots,i_{p}\},J=\{j_{1},\cdots,j_{q}\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>I</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>J</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mi>where</mi><mo lspace="0.340em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">dz_{I}\wedge d\overline{z}_{J}:=dz_{i_{1}}\wedge\cdots\wedge dz_{i_{p}}\wedge d\overline{z}_{j_{1}}\wedge\cdots\wedge d\overline{z}_{j_{q}}\quad{\rm where}\,\,I=\{i_{1},\cdots,i_{p}\},J=\{j_{1},\cdots,j_{q}\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS2.SSS4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">1.2.4. </span>The double complex</h4>

<div id="S1.SS2.SSS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.SSS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The differential <math id="S1.SS2.SSS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> decomposes
as well into <math id="S1.SS2.SSS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="d=\partial+{\overline{\partial}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mo>∂</mo><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mover accent="true"><mo>∂</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=\partial+{\overline{\partial}}</annotation></semantics></math>, where:</p>
<table id="S1.Ex23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\partial:\mathcal{E}^{p,q}_{X}\to\mathcal{E}^{p+1,q}_{X}:&amp;fdz_{I}\wedge d\overline{z}_{J}\mapsto\sum_{i}\frac{\partial}{\partial z_{i}}(f)dz_{i}\wedge dz_{I}\wedge d\overline{z}_{J},\\
&amp;\overline{\partial}:\mathcal{E}^{p,q}_{X}\to\mathcal{E}^{p,q+1}_{X}:fdz_{I}\wedge d\overline{z}_{J}\mapsto\sum_{i}(\frac{\partial}{\partial\overline{z}_{i}})(f)d{\overline{z}_{i}}\wedge dz_{I}\wedge d\overline{z}_{J}\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>X</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>I</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>J</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em" stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>i</mi></munder><mrow><mfrac><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>I</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>J</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>∂</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>I</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>J</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em" stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mi>i</mi></munder><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mfrac><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>I</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>J</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\partial:\mathcal{E}^{p,q}_{X}\to\mathcal{E}^{p+1,q}_{X}:&amp;fdz_{I}\wedge d\overline{z}_{J}\mapsto\sum_{i}\frac{\partial}{\partial z_{i}}(f)dz_{i}\wedge dz_{I}\wedge d\overline{z}_{J},\\
&amp;\overline{\partial}:\mathcal{E}^{p,q}_{X}\to\mathcal{E}^{p,q+1}_{X}:fdz_{I}\wedge d\overline{z}_{J}\mapsto\sum_{i}(\frac{\partial}{\partial\overline{z}_{i}})(f)d{\overline{z}_{i}}\wedge dz_{I}\wedge d\overline{z}_{J}\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS2.SSS4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">are compatible with
the decomposition up to a shift on the bidegree, we deduce from
<math id="S1.SS2.SSS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="d^{2}=\partial^{2}+{\overline{\partial}}^{2}+\partial\circ{\overline{\partial}}+{\overline{\partial}}\circ\partial=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mo>∂</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mover accent="true"><mo>∂</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mo rspace="0.055em">∂</mo><mo rspace="0.055em">∘</mo><mover accent="true"><mo>∂</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mover accent="true"><mo rspace="0.055em">∂</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo rspace="0.055em">∘</mo><mo>∂</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{2}=\partial^{2}+{\overline{\partial}}^{2}+\partial\circ{\overline{\partial}}+{\overline{\partial}}\circ\partial=0</annotation></semantics></math> that <math id="S1.SS2.SSS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\partial^{2}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial^{2}=0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.SS2.SSS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\overline{\partial}}^{2}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mo>∂</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\overline{\partial}}^{2}=0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS2.SSS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\partial\circ{\overline{\partial}}+{\overline{\partial}}\circ\partial=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">∂</mo><mo rspace="0.055em">∘</mo><mover accent="true"><mo>∂</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mover accent="true"><mo rspace="0.055em">∂</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo rspace="0.055em">∘</mo><mo>∂</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial\circ{\overline{\partial}}+{\overline{\partial}}\circ\partial=0</annotation></semantics></math>. It follows that <math id="S1.SS2.SSS4.p1.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(\mathcal{E}_{X}^{*,*},\partial,\overline{\partial})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">∗</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo>∂</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mover accent="true"><mo>∂</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{E}_{X}^{*,*},\partial,\overline{\partial})</annotation></semantics></math> is a double complex (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite> p 442).
Its
associated simple
complex, denoted <math id="S1.SS2.SSS4.p1.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="s(\mathcal{E}_{X}^{*,*},\partial,\overline{\partial})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">∗</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo>∂</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mover accent="true"><mo>∂</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s(\mathcal{E}_{X}^{*,*},\partial,\overline{\partial})</annotation></semantics></math>, is isomorphic to
the de Rham complex:</p>
<table id="S1.Ex24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex24.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(\mathcal{E}^{*}_{X}\otimes_{{\mathbb{R}}}\mathbb{C},d)\simeq s(\mathcal{E}_{X}^{*,*},\partial,\overline{\partial})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">∗</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo>∂</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mover accent="true"><mo>∂</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{E}^{*}_{X}\otimes_{{\mathbb{R}}}\mathbb{C},d)\simeq s(\mathcal{E}_{X}^{*,*},\partial,\overline{\partial}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS2.SSS4.p1.3" class="ltx_p">In particular we recover the notion of the subspace of cohomology
of type <math id="S1.SS2.SSS4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p,q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p,q)</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem12" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem12.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 1.12</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem12.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem12.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The subspace of de Rham cohomology represented
by a global closed form of type (<math id="S1.Thmtheorem12.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p,q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p,q</annotation></semantics></math>) on <math id="S1.Thmtheorem12.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is denoted as:</p>
<table id="S1.Ex25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{p,q}(X):=\{{\rm cohomology\,classes\,representable\,by\,a\,global\,closed\,form\,of\,type}\,\,(p,q)\,\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>cohomology</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>classes</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>representable</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>by</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>global</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>closed</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>form</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>of</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>type</mi><mo lspace="0.340em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{p,q}(X):=\{{\rm cohomology\,classes\,representable\,by\,a\,global\,closed\,form\,of\,type}\,\,(p,q)\,\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.SSS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS2.SSS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We have: <math id="S1.SS2.SSS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{H^{p,q}(X)}=H^{q,p}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{H^{p,q}(X)}=H^{q,p}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem13" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem13.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 1.13</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem13.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem13.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem13.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The definition
shows that <math id="S1.Thmtheorem13.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{p+q=i}H^{p,q}(X)\subset H^{i}_{DR}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{p+q=i}H^{p,q}(X)\subset H^{i}_{DR}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Although the differential forms decompose into types, it does not
follow that the cohomology also decomposes into a direct sum of
subspaces <math id="S1.Thmtheorem13.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{p,q}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{p,q}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> , since the differentials
are not compatible with types. It is striking that there is a
condition, easy to state, on Kähler manifolds, that imply
the full decomposition. Hodge theory on such
manifolds shows that:</p>
<table id="S1.Ex26" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex26.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="H^{i}_{DR}(X)=\oplus_{p+q=i}H^{p,q}(X)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><msub><mo lspace="0em">⊕</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{i}_{DR}(X)=\oplus_{p+q=i}H^{p,q}(X).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem14" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem14.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 1.14</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem14.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda\subset\mathbb{C}^{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>r</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda\subset\mathbb{C}^{r}</annotation></semantics></math> be a rank-<math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2r</annotation></semantics></math> lattice</p>
<table id="S1.Ex27" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex27.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Lambda=\{m_{1}\omega_{1}+\ldots+m_{2r}\omega_{2r};\quad m_{1},\ldots,m_{2r}\in\mathbb{Z}\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="1.167em">;</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda=\{m_{1}\omega_{1}+\ldots+m_{2r}\omega_{2r};\quad m_{1},\ldots,m_{2r}\in\mathbb{Z}\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are complex vectors in <math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}^{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}^{r}</annotation></semantics></math>, linearly
independent over <math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℝ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The quotient group <math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\Lambda}=\mathbb{C}^{r}/\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><mo>/</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\Lambda}=\mathbb{C}^{r}/\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math>
inherits a complex structure locally
isomorphic to <math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}^{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}^{r}</annotation></semantics></math>, with change of coordinates induced by
translations by elements of <math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover <math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\Lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\Lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>
is compact. The quotient <math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\Lambda}=\mathbb{C}^{r}/\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><mo>/</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\Lambda}=\mathbb{C}^{r}/\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math> is a compact
complex analytic manifold called a complex torus. The projection
<math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi:\mathbb{C}^{r}\to T_{\Lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:\mathbb{C}^{r}\to T_{\Lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>
which is holomorphic and locally isomorphic, is in fact a
universal covering.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Family of tori.</span> The above torus depends on the choice of the lattice.
Given two lattices <math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\Lambda_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\Lambda_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\Lambda_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\Lambda_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists
an <math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>−</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}-</annotation></semantics></math>linear isomorphism <math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\in GL(2r,{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\in GL(2r,{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math> such that
<math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g({\Lambda_{1}})={\Lambda_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g({\Lambda_{1}})={\Lambda_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>, hence it induces a diffeomorphism
of the underlying differentiable structure of the tori
<math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g:T_{\Lambda_{1}}\simeq T_{\Lambda_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>≃</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g:T_{\Lambda_{1}}\simeq T_{\Lambda_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>. However, it is not true
that all tori are isomorphic as complex manifolds.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem15" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem15.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 1.15</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem15.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem15.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem15.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem15.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Two complex tori <math id="S1.Thmtheorem15.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\Lambda_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\Lambda_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.Thmtheorem15.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\Lambda_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\Lambda_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>
are isomorphic if and only if there exists a complex linear map
<math id="S1.Thmtheorem15.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\in GL(r,\mathbb{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\in GL(r,\mathbb{C})</annotation></semantics></math> inducing an isomorphism of lattices <math id="S1.Thmtheorem15.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g:{\Lambda_{1}}\simeq{\Lambda_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≃</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g:{\Lambda_{1}}\simeq{\Lambda_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem14.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Given two complex torus <math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\Lambda_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\Lambda_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\Lambda_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\Lambda_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>,
an analytic morphism <math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:T_{\Lambda_{1}}\to T_{\Lambda_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:T_{\Lambda_{1}}\to T_{\Lambda_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> will
induce a morphism on the fundamental groups and there exists an holomorphic
lifting to the
covering <math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F:\mathbb{C}^{r}\to\mathbb{C}^{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>r</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F:\mathbb{C}^{r}\to\mathbb{C}^{r}</annotation></semantics></math> compatible with
<math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> via the projections:</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex28" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{2}\circ F=f\circ\pi_{1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{2}\circ F=f\circ\pi_{1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text">Moreover, given a point <math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{1}\in\mathbb{C}^{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>r</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{1}\in\mathbb{C}^{r}</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{1}\in\Lambda_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{1}\in\Lambda_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists <math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{2}\in\Lambda_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{2}\in\Lambda_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>
such that <math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(z_{1}+\lambda_{1})=F(z_{1})+\lambda_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(z_{1}+\lambda_{1})=F(z_{1})+\lambda_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">By continuity, this relation
will remain true by varying <math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z\in\mathbb{C}^{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>r</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z\in\mathbb{C}^{r}</annotation></semantics></math>; then <math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> and its
derivative are periodic. This derivative depends holomorphically
on parameters of <math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\Lambda_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\Lambda_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math>, hence it is necessarily constant; then, <math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> is
complex affine on <math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}^{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}^{r}</annotation></semantics></math>, since its derivative is constant.
We may suppose that <math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> is complex linear after composition with
a translation.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Moduli space</span>. We may parameterize all lattices as follows:
<br class="ltx_break">- the group <math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="GL(2r,{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">GL(2r,{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math> acts transitively on the set of all lattices of <math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}^{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}^{r}</annotation></semantics></math>.
<br class="ltx_break">We choose a basis <math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=(\tau_{1},\ldots,\tau_{2i-1},\tau_{2i}\ldots\tau_{2r})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=(\tau_{1},\ldots,\tau_{2i-1},\tau_{2i}\ldots\tau_{2r}),</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[1,r]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[1,r]</annotation></semantics></math>, of
a lattice <math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>, then it defines a basis of <math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}^{2r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}^{2r}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℝ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math>. An element <math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> of
<math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="GL(2r,{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">GL(2r,{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math> is given by the linear transformation which sends <math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> into
the basis <math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(\tau)=\tau^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(\tau)=\tau^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}^{2r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}^{2r}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℝ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The
element <math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="GL(2r,{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">GL(2r,{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math> is carrying the lattice <math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p5.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> onto
the lattice <math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p5.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> defined by the <math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p5.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>τ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.
<br class="ltx_break">- The isotopy group of this action is <math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p5.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="GL(2r,\mathbb{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">GL(2r,\mathbb{Z})</annotation></semantics></math>, since
<math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p5.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p5.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>τ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> define the same lattice if and only if
<math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p5.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi\in GL(2r,\mathbb{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi\in GL(2r,\mathbb{Z})</annotation></semantics></math>.
<br class="ltx_break">Hence the space of lattices is the quotient group <math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p5.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="GL(2r,{\mathbb{R}})/GL(2r,\mathbb{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">GL(2r,{\mathbb{R}})/GL(2r,\mathbb{Z})</annotation></semantics></math>.
<br class="ltx_break">- Two tori defined by the lattice <math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p5.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p5.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> are analytically isomorphic
if and only if there is an element of <math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p5.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="GL(r,\mathbb{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">GL(r,\mathbb{C})</annotation></semantics></math> which transform
the lattice <math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p5.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> into the lattice <math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p5.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, as the preceding lemma states.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p6.1" class="ltx_p">It follows
that the parameter space of complex tori is the quotient:</p>
<table id="S1.Ex29" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="GL(2r,\mathbb{Z})\backslash GL(2r,{\mathbb{R}})/GL(r,\mathbb{C})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo rspace="0.222em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">GL(2r,\mathbb{Z})\backslash GL(2r,{\mathbb{R}})/GL(r,\mathbb{C})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p6.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="GL(r,\mathbb{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">GL(r,\mathbb{C})</annotation></semantics></math> is
embedded naturally in <math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="GL(2r,{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">GL(2r,{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math> as a complex linear map is
<math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>−</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}-</annotation></semantics></math>linear.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p7.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=1</annotation></semantics></math>, the quotient <math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="GL(2,{\mathbb{R}})/GL(1,\mathbb{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">GL(2,{\mathbb{R}})/GL(1,\mathbb{C})</annotation></semantics></math> is
isomorphic to <math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}-{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}-{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math>, since, up to complex isomorphisms, a
lattice is generated by <math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1,\tau\in\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1,\tau\in\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math> independent over <math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℝ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math>,
hence
completely determined by <math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\in\mathbb{C}-{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\in\mathbb{C}-{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The moduli space is the orbit space of the action
of <math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="GL(2,\mathbb{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">GL(2,\mathbb{Z})</annotation></semantics></math> on the space <math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="GL(2,{\mathbb{R}})/GL(1,\mathbb{C})=\mathbb{C}-{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">GL(2,{\mathbb{R}})/GL(1,\mathbb{C})=\mathbb{C}-{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since <math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="GL(2,\mathbb{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">GL(2,\mathbb{Z})</annotation></semantics></math> is the disjoint union of
<math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="SL(2,\mathbb{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">SL(2,\mathbb{Z})</annotation></semantics></math> and the integral <math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\times 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\times 2</annotation></semantics></math>-matrices of determinant
equal to <math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1</annotation></semantics></math>, that orbit space is the one of the action of
<math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="SL(2,\mathbb{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">SL(2,\mathbb{Z})</annotation></semantics></math> on the upper half plane:</p>
<table id="S1.Ex30" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\left(\begin{array}[]{cc}a&amp;b\\
c&amp;d\\
\end{array}\right),z)\mapsto\frac{az+b}{cz+d}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mi>a</mi></mtd><mtd><mi>b</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>c</mi></mtd><mtd><mi>d</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\left(\begin{array}[]{cc}a&amp;b\\
c&amp;d\\
\end{array}\right),z)\mapsto\frac{az+b}{cz+d}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Hodge decomposition on complex tori</span>. The cohomology of a
complex torus <math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\Lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\Lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> is easy to compute by Künneth
formula, since it is diffeomorphic to a product of circles:
<math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\Lambda}\simeq(S^{1})^{2r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><mo>≃</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\Lambda}\simeq(S^{1})^{2r}</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence <math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{1}(T_{\Lambda},\mathbb{Z})\simeq\mathbb{Z}^{2r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{1}(T_{\Lambda},\mathbb{Z})\simeq\mathbb{Z}^{2r}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p8.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="H^{j}(T_{\Lambda},\mathbb{Z})\simeq\wedge^{j}H^{1}(T_{\Lambda},\mathbb{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">≃</mo><msup><mo lspace="0em">∧</mo><mi>j</mi></msup><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{j}(T_{\Lambda},\mathbb{Z})\simeq\wedge^{j}H^{1}(T_{\Lambda},\mathbb{Z})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p9.1" class="ltx_p">The cohomology with complex coefficients
may be computed by de Rham cohomology. In this case, since the
complex tangent space is trivial, we have natural cohomology
elements given by the translation-invariant forms, hence with
constant coefficients.
The finite complex vector space <math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{0}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{0}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> of constant holomorphic <math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-</annotation></semantics></math>forms
is isomorphic to <math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}^{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}^{r}</annotation></semantics></math> and generated by
<math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="dz_{j},j\in[1,r]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">dz_{j},j\in[1,r]</annotation></semantics></math> and the Hodge decomposition reduces to prove
<math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p9.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="H^{j}(X,\mathbb{C})\simeq\oplus_{p+q=j}\wedge^{p}T_{0}^{*}\otimes\wedge^{q}\overline{T_{0}^{*}},p\geq 0,q\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">≃</mo><msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⊕</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mo lspace="0em">∧</mo><mi>p</mi></msup><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">⊗</mo><msup><mo lspace="0em">∧</mo><mi>q</mi></msup><mover accent="true"><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{j}(X,\mathbb{C})\simeq\oplus_{p+q=j}\wedge^{p}T_{0}^{*}\otimes\wedge^{q}\overline{T_{0}^{*}},p\geq 0,q\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> which is here a consequence
of the above computation of the cohomology.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS2.SSS5" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">1.2.5. </span>Dolbeault cohomology</h4>

<div id="S1.SS2.SSS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.SSS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS2.SSS5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text"></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.SSS5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS2.SSS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S1.SS2.SSS5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, the complex <math id="S1.SS2.SSS5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{E}_{X}^{r,*},\overline{\partial})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">∗</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mover accent="true"><mo>∂</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{E}_{X}^{r,*},\overline{\partial})</annotation></semantics></math>, is
called the <math id="S1.SS2.SSS5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>-Dolbeault complex.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem16" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem16.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 1.16</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem16.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem16.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem16.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The <math id="S1.Thmtheorem16.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th cohomology of the global
sections of the <math id="S1.Thmtheorem16.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>-Dolbeault complex is called the
<math id="S1.Thmtheorem16.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\partial}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mo>∂</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\partial}</annotation></semantics></math> cohomology of <math id="S1.Thmtheorem16.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> of type <math id="S1.Thmtheorem16.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r,i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r,i)</annotation></semantics></math></p>
<table id="S1.Ex31" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex31.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="H_{\overline{\partial}}^{r,i}(X):=H^{i}(\mathcal{E}_{X}^{r,*}(X),\overline{\partial})=\frac{Ker(\overline{\partial}^{i}:\mathcal{E}^{r,i}(X)\to\mathcal{E}^{r,i+1}(X))}{Im(\overline{\partial}^{i-1}:\mathcal{E}^{r,i-1}(X)\to\mathcal{E}^{r,i}(X))}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mover accent="true"><mo>∂</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">∗</mo></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mover accent="true"><mo>∂</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>K</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.278em">∂</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.278em">∂</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\overline{\partial}}^{r,i}(X):=H^{i}(\mathcal{E}_{X}^{r,*}(X),\overline{\partial})=\frac{Ker(\overline{\partial}^{i}:\mathcal{E}^{r,i}(X)\to\mathcal{E}^{r,i+1}(X))}{Im(\overline{\partial}^{i-1}:\mathcal{E}^{r,i-1}(X)\to\mathcal{E}^{r,i}(X))}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.SSS5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.SSS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">On complex manifolds, we consider the sheaves of holomorphic forms <math id="S1.SS2.SSS5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega^{r}_{X}:=\wedge\Omega 1_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo rspace="0em">:=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">∧</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mn>1</mn><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega^{r}_{X}:=\wedge\Omega 1_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem17" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem17.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 1.17</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem17.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem17.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem17.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem17.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The Dolbeault complex <math id="S1.Thmtheorem17.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{E}_{X}^{r,*},\overline{\partial})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">∗</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mover accent="true"><mo>∂</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{E}_{X}^{r,*},\overline{\partial})</annotation></semantics></math> for
<math id="S1.Thmtheorem17.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, is a fine resolution of <math id="S1.Thmtheorem17.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega^{r}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega^{r}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, hence</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex32" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{i}(X,\Omega^{r}_{X})\simeq H^{i}(\mathcal{E}_{X}^{r,*}(X),\overline{\partial}):=H_{\overline{\partial}}^{r,i}(X)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">∗</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mover accent="true"><mo>∂</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mover accent="true"><mo>∂</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{i}(X,\Omega^{r}_{X})\simeq H^{i}(\mathcal{E}_{X}^{r,*}(X),\overline{\partial}):=H_{\overline{\partial}}^{r,i}(X)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.SSS5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.SSS5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">For the proof, see the <math id="S1.SS2.SSS5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\partial}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mo>∂</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\partial}</annotation></semantics></math>-Poincaré lemma in
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite> p. 25 (continued p. 45). 
<br class="ltx_break">A cohomology class of <math id="S1.SS2.SSS5.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> of type <math id="S1.SS2.SSS5.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r,i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r,i)</annotation></semantics></math> defines a
<math id="S1.SS2.SSS5.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\partial}-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>∂</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>−</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\partial}-</annotation></semantics></math>cohomology class of the same type, since (<math id="S1.SS2.SSS5.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\varphi=0)\Rightarrow(\overline{\partial}\varphi=0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>φ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⇒</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>φ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\varphi=0)\Rightarrow(\overline{\partial}\varphi=0)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.SSS5.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.SSS5.p5.1" class="ltx_p">On a
Kähler manifold (see below in <a href="#S1.SS5" title="1.5. Kähler manifolds ‣ 1. Hodge decomposition ‣ Mixed Hodge Structures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.5</span></a>)
we can find a particular representative
<math id="S1.SS2.SSS5.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> in each class of <math id="S1.SS2.SSS5.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\partial}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mo>∂</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\partial}</annotation></semantics></math>-cohomology, called
harmonic form for which:</p>
<table id="S1.Ex33" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\overline{\partial}\omega=0)\Rightarrow(d\omega=0)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⇒</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\overline{\partial}\omega=0)\Rightarrow(d\omega=0).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS2.SSS6" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">1.2.6. </span>Holomorphic Poincaré lemma</h4>

<div id="S1.SS2.SSS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.SSS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The holomorphic version of Poincaré lemma
shows that the de Rham complex of
holomorphic forms <math id="S1.SS2.SSS6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega^{*}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega^{*}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> is a resolution of the constant
sheaf <math id="S1.SS2.SSS6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\mathbb{C}}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\mathbb{C}}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>. However, since the resolution is not
acyclic, cohomology spaces are computed only as
hypercohomology of the global sections functor, that is
after acyclic resolution.</p>
<table id="S1.Ex34" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{i}(X,\mathbb{C})\simeq\mathbb{H}^{i}(X,\Omega^{*}_{X}):=R^{i}\Gamma(X,\Omega^{*}_{X})\simeq H^{i}(\mathcal{E}^{*}(X)\otimes_{{\mathbb{R}}}\mathbb{C})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℍ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{i}(X,\mathbb{C})\simeq\mathbb{H}^{i}(X,\Omega^{*}_{X}):=R^{i}\Gamma(X,\Omega^{*}_{X})\simeq H^{i}(\mathcal{E}^{*}(X)\otimes_{{\mathbb{R}}}\mathbb{C})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.3. </span>Hermitian metric, its associated Riemannian metric and <math id="S1.SS3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,1)</annotation></semantics></math> form</h3>

<div id="S1.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The Hermitian product <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> on the complex manifold <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>
is defined at each point <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math> as a product on the holomorphic
tangent space <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{z}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math></p>
<table id="S1.Ex35" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex35.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{z}:T_{z}\times T_{z}\to\mathbb{C}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>z</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{z}:T_{z}\times T_{z}\to\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">satisfying <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(u,v)=\overline{h(v,u)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(u,v)=\overline{h(v,u)}</annotation></semantics></math>, linear in <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> and anti-linear in <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>, moreover
<math id="S1.SS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{z}</annotation></semantics></math> vary smoothly with <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math>. In terms of the basis
<math id="S1.SS3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\frac{\partial}{\partial z_{j}},j\in[1,n])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mfrac><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\frac{\partial}{\partial z_{j}},j\in[1,n])</annotation></semantics></math> dual to
the basis <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(dz_{j},j\in[1,n])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(dz_{j},j\in[1,n])</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{z}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mi>z</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{z}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>,
the matrix <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{ij}(z):=h_{z}(\frac{\partial}{\partial z_{i}},\frac{\partial}{\partial z_{j}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mfrac><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{ij}(z):=h_{z}(\frac{\partial}{\partial z_{i}},\frac{\partial}{\partial z_{j}})</annotation></semantics></math>
is differentiable in <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{ij}(z)=\overline{h_{ji}(z)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{ij}(z)=\overline{h_{ji}(z)}</annotation></semantics></math>.
In terms of <span id="S1.SS3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">the above isomorphism <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{z}\xrightarrow{\sim}T^{1,0}_{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo></mover><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mi>z</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{z}\xrightarrow{\sim}T^{1,0}_{z}</annotation></semantics></math></span> defined by <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\partial}{\partial z_{j}}=\frac{1}{2}(\frac{\partial}{\partial x_{j}}-i\frac{\partial}{\partial y_{j}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\partial}{\partial z_{j}}=\frac{1}{2}(\frac{\partial}{\partial x_{j}}-i\frac{\partial}{\partial y_{j}})</annotation></semantics></math>,
it is
equivalent to define the Hermitian product as</p>
<table id="S1.Ex36" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex36.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{z}:T^{1,0}_{z}\times T^{1,0}_{z}\to\mathbb{C},\,\,h_{z}=\sum_{i,j}h_{ij}(z)dz_{i}\otimes d\overline{z_{j}}\in\mathcal{E}^{1,0}_{z}\otimes_{\mathbb{C}}\mathcal{E}^{0,1}_{z}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mi>z</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mi>z</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.507em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>z</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>z</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{z}:T^{1,0}_{z}\times T^{1,0}_{z}\to\mathbb{C},\,\,h_{z}=\sum_{i,j}h_{ij}(z)dz_{i}\otimes d\overline{z_{j}}\in\mathcal{E}^{1,0}_{z}\otimes_{\mathbb{C}}\mathcal{E}^{0,1}_{z}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where the Hermitian product on <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{1,0}_{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mi>z</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{1,0}_{z}</annotation></semantics></math> is viewed as a linear form on
<math id="S1.SS3.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{1,0}_{z}\otimes\overline{T^{1,0}_{z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mi>z</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mover accent="true"><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mi>z</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{1,0}_{z}\otimes\overline{T^{1,0}_{z}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The product is called a Hermitian metric if moreover the real
number <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{z}(u,\overline{u})&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{z}(u,\overline{u})&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> is positive definite for all <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\neq 0\in T_{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>z</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\neq 0\in T_{z}</annotation></semantics></math>; equivalently the matrix <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> of the product defined by
<math id="S1.SS3.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{ij}(z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{ij}(z)</annotation></semantics></math> (equal to its conjugate transpose: <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h={{}^{t}{\overline{h}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mmultiscripts><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></mmultiscripts></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h={{}^{t}{\overline{h}}}</annotation></semantics></math> is positive
definite: <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{}^{t}{\overline{u}}}hu&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mmultiscripts><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{}^{t}{\overline{u}}}hu&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S1.SS3.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">1.3.1. </span>Associated Riemannian structure</h4>

<div id="S1.SS3.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The Hermitian metric may be defined on
<math id="S1.SS3.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{X,{\mathbb{R}},z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{X,{\mathbb{R}},z}</annotation></semantics></math> identified with <math id="S1.SS3.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{1,0}_{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mi>z</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{1,0}_{z}</annotation></semantics></math> via <math id="S1.SS3.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\partial}{\partial x_{i}}\to\frac{\partial}{\partial z_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mfrac><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\partial}{\partial x_{i}}\to\frac{\partial}{\partial z_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> and extended via the complex structure s.t.
<math id="S1.SS3.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\partial}{\partial y_{i}}=J(\frac{\partial}{\partial x_{i}}):=i\frac{\partial}{\partial x_{i}}\to i\frac{\partial}{\partial z_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mfrac><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\partial}{\partial y_{i}}=J(\frac{\partial}{\partial x_{i}}):=i\frac{\partial}{\partial x_{i}}\to i\frac{\partial}{\partial z_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>, then using the decomposition
as the sum of its
real and imaginary parts</p>
<table id="S1.Ex37" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex37.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{z|T_{X,{\mathbb{R}},z}}=Re\,(h_{z})+iIm\,(h_{z})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{z|T_{X,{\mathbb{R}},z}}=Re\,(h_{z})+iIm\,(h_{z})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS3.SSS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">we associate to the metric a Riemannian structure on <math id="S1.SS3.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>
defined by <math id="S1.SS3.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{z}:=Re\,(h_{z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{z}:=Re\,(h_{z})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS3.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In this way, the complex manifold <math id="S1.SS3.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> of dimension <math id="S1.SS3.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is viewed as a Riemann manifold
of dimension <math id="S1.SS3.SSS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2n</annotation></semantics></math> with metric <math id="S1.SS3.SSS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{z}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Note that <math id="S1.SS3.SSS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> is defined over the real numbers, since it is represented by a
real matrix over a basis of the real tangent space.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS3.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Since <math id="S1.SS3.SSS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{z}</annotation></semantics></math> is hermitian, the metric satisfy:
<math id="S1.SS3.SSS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{z}(Ju,Jv)=g_{z}(u,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{z}(Ju,Jv)=g_{z}(u,v)</annotation></semantics></math>. In fact we can recover the Hermitian product from
a Riemannian structure satisfying this property.
<br class="ltx_break">From now on, the Hodge operator star will be defined on <math id="S1.SS3.SSS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{X,{\mathbb{R}},z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{X,{\mathbb{R}},z}</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to <math id="S1.SS3.SSS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>
and extended complex linearly to <math id="S1.SS3.SSS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{X,{\mathbb{R}},z}\otimes\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{X,{\mathbb{R}},z}\otimes\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem18" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemmadefinition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem18.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma and Definition 1.18</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem18.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>( The (1,1)-form <math id="S1.Thmtheorem18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>)<span id="S1.Thmtheorem18.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem18.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem18.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem18.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The hermitian metric defines a
real exterior <math id="S1.Thmtheorem18.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2-</annotation></semantics></math>form
<math id="S1.Thmtheorem18.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega:=-\frac{1}{2}Im\,(h)\," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo rspace="0em">:=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega:=-\frac{1}{2}Im\,(h)\,</annotation></semantics></math> of type (<math id="S1.Thmtheorem18.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1,1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1,1</annotation></semantics></math>) on <math id="S1.Thmtheorem18.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\,T_{X,{\mathbb{R}},z}\," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\,T_{X,{\mathbb{R}},z}\,</annotation></semantics></math>,
satisfying:
<br class="ltx_break"><math id="S1.Thmtheorem18.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega(Ju,v)=-\omega(u,Jv)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega(Ju,v)=-\omega(u,Jv)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.Thmtheorem18.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega(Ju,Jv)=\omega(u,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega(Ju,Jv)=\omega(u,v)</annotation></semantics></math>; moreover: <math id="S1.Thmtheorem18.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\omega(u,Jv)=g(u,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\omega(u,Jv)=g(u,v)</annotation></semantics></math> and</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex38" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex38.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega=\frac{i}{2}\sum_{i,j}h_{ij}(z)dz_{i}\wedge d\overline{z_{j}}\quad\hbox{if}\quad h_{z}=\sum_{i,j}h_{ij}(z)dz_{i}\otimes d\overline{z_{j}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">if</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega=\frac{i}{2}\sum_{i,j}h_{ij}(z)dz_{i}\wedge d\overline{z_{j}}\quad\hbox{if}\quad h_{z}=\sum_{i,j}h_{ij}(z)dz_{i}\otimes d\overline{z_{j}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem18.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem18.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS3.SSS1.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S1.SS3.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.SSS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS3.SSS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">We have an exterior <math id="S1.SS3.SSS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-form since <math id="S1.SS3.SSS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\omega(v,u)=-Im\,h(v,u)=Im\,h(u,v)=-2\omega(u,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\omega(v,u)=-Im\,h(v,u)=Im\,h(u,v)=-2\omega(u,v)</annotation></semantics></math>. We check:
<math id="S1.SS3.SSS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\omega(u,Jv)=-Im\,h(u,iv)=-Im\,(-ih(u,v))=Re\,(h(u,v)=g(u,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\omega(u,Jv)=-Im\,h(u,iv)=-Im\,(-ih(u,v))=Re\,(h(u,v)=g(u,v)</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S1.SS3.SSS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\omega(Ju,v)=-Im\,h(iu,v)=-Im\,(ih(u,v))=-Re\,h(u,v)=-g(u,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\omega(Ju,v)=-Im\,h(iu,v)=-Im\,(ih(u,v))=-Re\,h(u,v)=-g(u,v)</annotation></semantics></math>,
while <math id="S1.SS3.SSS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="Im\,h=\frac{1}{2i}\sum_{i,j}h_{ij}(z)(dz_{i}\otimes d\overline{z_{j}}-d\overline{z_{j}}\otimes dz_{i})=-i\sum_{i,j}h_{ij}(z)dz_{i}\wedge d\overline{z_{j}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mfrac><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>d</mi><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>i</mi><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∧</mo><mi>d</mi><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Im\,h=\frac{1}{2i}\sum_{i,j}h_{ij}(z)(dz_{i}\otimes d\overline{z_{j}}-d\overline{z_{j}}\otimes dz_{i})=-i\sum_{i,j}h_{ij}(z)dz_{i}\wedge d\overline{z_{j}}</annotation></semantics></math>
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem19" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem19.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 1.19</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem19.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem19.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem19.p1.1" class="ltx_p">1) The Hermitian metric on <math id="S1.Thmtheorem19.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined by
<math id="S1.Thmtheorem19.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h:=\sum_{i=1}^{n}dz_{i}\otimes d\overline{z_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h:=\sum_{i=1}^{n}dz_{i}\otimes d\overline{z_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S1.Ex39" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex39.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(z,w)=\sum_{i=1}^{n}z_{i}{\overline{w}}_{i}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(z,w)=\sum_{i=1}^{n}z_{i}{\overline{w}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem19.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S1.Thmtheorem19.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z=(z_{1},\ldots,z_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z=(z_{1},\ldots,z_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.Thmtheorem19.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w=(w_{1},\ldots,w_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w=(w_{1},\ldots,w_{n})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Considering the underlying real structure on <math id="S1.Thmtheorem19.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}^{n}\simeq{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>≃</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}^{n}\simeq{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math> defined by <math id="S1.Thmtheorem19.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(z_{k}=x_{k}+iy_{k})\mapsto(x_{k},y_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(z_{k}=x_{k}+iy_{k})\mapsto(x_{k},y_{k})</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S1.Thmtheorem19.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Re\,h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Re\,h</annotation></semantics></math> is the Euclidean inner product on <math id="S1.Thmtheorem19.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.Thmtheorem19.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Im\,h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Im\,h</annotation></semantics></math> is a non-degenerate
alternating bilinear form, i.e., a symplectic form: <math id="S1.Thmtheorem19.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega=\frac{i}{2}\sum_{i=1}^{n}dz_{i}\wedge d\overline{z_{i}}=\sum_{i=1}^{n}dx_{i}\wedge dy_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega=\frac{i}{2}\sum_{i=1}^{n}dz_{i}\wedge d\overline{z_{i}}=\sum_{i=1}^{n}dx_{i}\wedge dy_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem19.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem19.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Explicitly, on <math id="S1.Thmtheorem19.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, the standard Hermitian form is expressed on <math id="S1.Thmtheorem19.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math> as:</p>
<table id="S1.Ex40" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex40.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}h((z_{1,1},z_{1,2}),(z_{2,1},z_{2,2}))=&amp;x_{1,1}x_{2,1}+x_{1,2}x_{2,2}+y_{1,1}y_{2,1}+y_{1,2}y_{2,2}\\
&amp;+i(x_{2,1}y_{1,1}-x_{1,1}y_{2,1}+x_{2,2}y_{1,2}-x_{1,2}y_{2,2}).\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}h((z_{1,1},z_{1,2}),(z_{2,1},z_{2,2}))=&amp;x_{1,1}x_{2,1}+x_{1,2}x_{2,2}+y_{1,1}y_{2,1}+y_{1,2}y_{2,2}\\
&amp;+i(x_{2,1}y_{1,1}-x_{1,1}y_{2,1}+x_{2,2}y_{1,2}-x_{1,2}y_{2,2}).\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem19.p2.2" class="ltx_p">2) If <math id="S1.Thmtheorem19.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda\subset\mathbb{C}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda\subset\mathbb{C}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is a full lattice, then the
above metric induces a Hermitian metric on the torus
<math id="S1.Thmtheorem19.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}^{n}/\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>/</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}^{n}/\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem19.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem19.p3.1" class="ltx_p">3) The <span id="S1.Thmtheorem19.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fubini -Study metric on the projective space <math id="S1.Thmtheorem19.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math></span>.
Let <math id="S1.Thmtheorem19.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi:\mathbb{C}^{n+1}-\{0\}\to\mathbb{P}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:\mathbb{C}^{n+1}-\{0\}\to\mathbb{P}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> be the projection and consider a
section of <math id="S1.Thmtheorem19.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> on an open subset <math id="S1.Thmtheorem19.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z:U\to\mathbb{C}^{n+1}-\{0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z:U\to\mathbb{C}^{n+1}-\{0\}</annotation></semantics></math>,
then the form <math id="S1.Thmtheorem19.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega=\frac{i}{2\pi}\partial{\overline{\partial}}log||Z||^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>i</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega=\frac{i}{2\pi}\partial{\overline{\partial}}log||Z||^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>
is a fundamental <math id="S1.Thmtheorem19.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,1)</annotation></semantics></math> form, globally defined on <math id="S1.Thmtheorem19.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, since the forms
glue together on the intersection of two open subsets (this formula is related to
the construction of the first Chern class of the canonical line bundle on the projective space,
in particular the coefficient <math id="S1.Thmtheorem19.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{i}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{i}{2}</annotation></semantics></math> leads later to a positive definite form, while <math id="S1.Thmtheorem19.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>
leads to an integral cohomology class).
For the
section <math id="S1.Thmtheorem19.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z=(1,w_{1},\ldots,w_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z=(1,w_{1},\ldots,w_{n})</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S1.Ex41" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex41.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega=\frac{i}{2\pi}\frac{(1+|w|^{2})\sum_{i=1}^{n}dw_{i}\wedge d\overline{w_{j}}-\sum_{i,j=1}^{n}\overline{w_{j}}w_{i}dw_{i}\wedge d\overline{w_{j}}}{(1+|w|^{2})^{2}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>i</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mrow><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega=\frac{i}{2\pi}\frac{(1+|w|^{2})\sum_{i=1}^{n}dw_{i}\wedge d\overline{w_{j}}-\sum_{i,j=1}^{n}\overline{w_{j}}w_{i}dw_{i}\wedge d\overline{w_{j}}}{(1+|w|^{2})^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem19.p3.2" class="ltx_p">which reduces at the point <math id="S1.Thmtheorem19.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O=(1,0,\ldots,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O=(1,0,\ldots,0)</annotation></semantics></math>, to <math id="S1.Thmtheorem19.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega=\frac{i}{2\pi}\sum_{i=1}^{n}dw_{i}\wedge d\overline{w_{i}}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mi>i</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega=\frac{i}{2\pi}\sum_{i=1}^{n}dw_{i}\wedge d\overline{w_{i}}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, hence a positive real form , i.e., it takes real values on real tangent vectors, and
is associated to a positive Hermitian form.
Since <math id="S1.Thmtheorem19.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> is invariant under the transitive holomorphic action of <math id="S1.Thmtheorem19.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="SU(n+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">SU(n+1)</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S1.Thmtheorem19.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>,
we deduce that the hermitian form</p>
<table id="S1.Ex42" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex42.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h=(\sum_{i,j=1}^{n}h_{i,j}(w)dw_{i}\otimes d\overline{w_{i}})(1+|w|^{2})^{-2},\,\pi h_{i,j}(w)=(1+|w|^{2})\delta_{ij}-\overline{w_{j}}w_{i}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h=(\sum_{i,j=1}^{n}h_{i,j}(w)dw_{i}\otimes d\overline{w_{i}})(1+|w|^{2})^{-2},\,\pi h_{i,j}(w)=(1+|w|^{2})\delta_{ij}-\overline{w_{j}}w_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem19.p3.3" class="ltx_p">is a positive definite Hermitian form. Hence
the projective space has a Hermitian metric.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.4. </span>Harmonic forms on compact complex manifolds</h3>

<div id="S1.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this
section, a study of the <math id="S1.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\partial}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mo>∂</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\partial}</annotation></semantics></math> operator, similar
to the previous study of <math id="S1.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>, leads to the representation of
Dolbeault cohomology by <math id="S1.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\partial}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mo>∂</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\partial}</annotation></semantics></math>-harmonic forms
(Hodge theorem).</p>
</div>
<section id="S1.SS4.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">1.4.1. </span><math id="S1.SS4.SSS1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-inner product </h4>

<div id="S1.SS4.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS4.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We choose a Hermitian metric <math id="S1.SS4.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> with associated <math id="S1.SS4.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,1)</annotation></semantics></math>-form
<math id="S1.SS4.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> and volume form <math id="S1.SS4.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="vol=\frac{1}{n!}\omega^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">vol=\frac{1}{n!}\omega^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We extend the <math id="S1.SS4.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-inner product <math id="S1.SS4.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\psi,\eta)_{L^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\psi,\eta)_{L^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> ( resp. the Hodge star * operator) defined on the real forms by the underlying Riemannian metric,
first linearly into a complex bilinear form <math id="S1.SS4.SSS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;\psi,\eta&gt;_{L^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo></mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;\psi,\eta&gt;_{L^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S1.SS4.SSS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}^{*}_{X}:=\mathcal{E}^{*}_{X,{\mathbb{R}}}\otimes\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}^{*}_{X}:=\mathcal{E}^{*}_{X,{\mathbb{R}}}\otimes\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>
( resp. <math id="S1.SS4.SSS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{*}:\mathcal{E}^{p}_{X,{\mathbb{R}}}\otimes\mathbb{C}\to\mathcal{E}^{2n-p}_{X,{\mathbb{R}}}\otimes\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>∗</mo><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{*}:\mathcal{E}^{p}_{X,{\mathbb{R}}}\otimes\mathbb{C}\to\mathcal{E}^{2n-p}_{X,{\mathbb{R}}}\otimes\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>).
However, it is more appropriate to introduce the conjugate operator:</p>
<table id="S1.Ex43" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex43.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="*:\mathcal{E}^{p,q}_{X}\to\mathcal{E}^{n-p,n-q}_{X},\quad*(\varphi):={\tilde{*}}(\overline{\varphi})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>X</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo rspace="0.833em">,</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">:=</mo><mover accent="true"><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">*:\mathcal{E}^{p,q}_{X}\to\mathcal{E}^{n-p,n-q}_{X},\quad*(\varphi):={\tilde{*}}(\overline{\varphi})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS4.SSS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">and then we consider the Hermitian product :
<math id="S1.SS4.SSS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\psi,\eta)_{L^{2}}:=&lt;\psi,\overline{\eta}&gt;_{L^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></msub><mo rspace="0em">:=</mo><msub><mrow><mo lspace="0em">&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo></mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\psi,\eta)_{L^{2}}:=&lt;\psi,\overline{\eta}&gt;_{L^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>, hence:</p>
<table id="S1.Ex44" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex44.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(\psi,\eta)_{L^{2}}=\int_{X}\psi\wedge*\eta,\,\psi,\eta\in\mathcal{E}^{p}(X),\,\hbox{and}\,(\psi,\eta)_{L^{2}}=0\,\hbox{if}\,\psi\in\mathcal{E}^{p}(X),\eta\in\mathcal{E}^{q}(X),p\not=q." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><msub><mo>∫</mo><mi>X</mi></msub><mi>ψ</mi><mo rspace="0em">∧</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mi>η</mi><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mtext>and</mtext><msub><mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mtext>if</mtext><mi>ψ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\psi,\eta)_{L^{2}}=\int_{X}\psi\wedge*\eta,\,\psi,\eta\in\mathcal{E}^{p}(X),\,\hbox{and}\,(\psi,\eta)_{L^{2}}=0\,\hbox{if}\,\psi\in\mathcal{E}^{p}(X),\eta\in\mathcal{E}^{q}(X),p\not=q.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS4.SSS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">This definition enables us to obtain a Hermitian metric on the complex vector space of global forms
<math id="S1.SS4.SSS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}^{*}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}^{*}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S1.SS4.SSS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, out of the real metric since we have <math id="S1.SS4.SSS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="v\wedge*v=\sum_{|I|=p}|v_{I}|^{2}*(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo rspace="0em">∧</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mi>v</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><msub><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><msup><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\wedge*v=\sum_{|I|=p}|v_{I}|^{2}*(1)</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S1.SS4.SSS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="*(1)=vol,v=\sum_{|I|=p}v_{I}e_{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>v</mi><mi>o</mi><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>I</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">*(1)=vol,v=\sum_{|I|=p}v_{I}e_{I}</annotation></semantics></math> for
ordered multi-indices <math id="S1.SS4.SSS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I=i_{1}&lt;\ldots&lt;i_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I=i_{1}&lt;\ldots&lt;i_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> of an orthonormal basis <math id="S1.SS4.SSS1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> , hence the integral vanish iff all <math id="S1.SS4.SSS1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{I}</annotation></semantics></math> vanish everywhere (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite> p.<math id="S1.SS4.SSS1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="174" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>174</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">174</annotation></semantics></math>).
With respect to such Hermitian metric,
the adjoint operator to <math id="S1.SS4.SSS1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\partial}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mo>∂</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\partial}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined:</p>
<table id="S1.Ex45" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex45.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\overline{\partial}^{*}:\mathcal{E}^{p,q}_{X}\to\mathcal{E}^{p,q-1}_{X},\,\,\overline{\partial}^{*}=-*\circ\overline{\partial}\circ*\,:\,(\overline{\partial}^{*}\psi,\eta)_{L^{2}}=(\psi,\overline{\partial}\eta)_{L^{2}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mo>∂</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo rspace="0.340em">,</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mo>∂</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">−</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.055em">∘</mo><mover accent="true"><mo rspace="0.055em">∂</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo rspace="0em">∘</mo><mo lspace="0em">∗</mo><mo rspace="0.448em">:</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>∗</mo></msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mover accent="true"><mo>∂</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\partial}^{*}:\mathcal{E}^{p,q}_{X}\to\mathcal{E}^{p,q-1}_{X},\,\,\overline{\partial}^{*}=-*\circ\overline{\partial}\circ*\,:\,(\overline{\partial}^{*}\psi,\eta)_{L^{2}}=(\psi,\overline{\partial}\eta)_{L^{2}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS4.SSS1.p1.4" class="ltx_p">Then, we introduce the <math id="S1.SS4.SSS1.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\partial}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mo>∂</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\partial}</annotation></semantics></math>-Laplacian:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem20" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem20.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 1.20</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem20.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem20.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem20.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The <math id="S1.Thmtheorem20.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\partial}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mo>∂</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\partial}</annotation></semantics></math>-Laplacian is defined as:</p>
<table id="S1.Ex46" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex46.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{\overline{\partial}}=\overline{\partial}\circ\overline{\partial}^{*}+\overline{\partial}^{*}\circ\overline{\partial}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mover accent="true"><mo>∂</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mo rspace="0.055em">∂</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo rspace="0.055em">∘</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mo>∂</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mo rspace="0.055em">∂</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.055em">∘</mo><mover accent="true"><mo>∂</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{\overline{\partial}}=\overline{\partial}\circ\overline{\partial}^{*}+\overline{\partial}^{*}\circ\overline{\partial}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem20.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Harmonic forms of type <math id="S1.Thmtheorem20.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p,q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p,q)</annotation></semantics></math> are defined as the solutions of the
<math id="S1.Thmtheorem20.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\partial}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mo>∂</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\partial}</annotation></semantics></math>-Laplacian</p>
<table id="S1.Ex47" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex47.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{\overline{\partial}}^{p,q}(X)=\{\psi\in\mathcal{E}^{p,q}(X)\otimes\mathbb{C}:\Delta_{\overline{\partial}}(\psi)=0\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mover accent="true"><mo>∂</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mover accent="true"><mo>∂</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{\overline{\partial}}^{p,q}(X)=\{\psi\in\mathcal{E}^{p,q}(X)\otimes\mathbb{C}:\Delta_{\overline{\partial}}(\psi)=0\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS4.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS4.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A basic result, which proof involves deep analysis in Elliptic operator theory and it is well documented
in various books, for instance (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite> chap. IV, sec. 4) or <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>, p. 82 - 84, is stated here without proof:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem21" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem21.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.21</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem21.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Hodge theorem)<span id="S1.Thmtheorem21.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem21.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem21.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem21.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">On a compact
complex Hermitian manifold each <math id="S1.Thmtheorem21.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\partial}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mo>∂</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\partial}</annotation></semantics></math>-cohomology
class of type <math id="S1.Thmtheorem21.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p,q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p,q)</annotation></semantics></math> is represented by a unique
<math id="S1.Thmtheorem21.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\partial}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mo>∂</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\partial}</annotation></semantics></math>-harmonic global
complex differential form of type
<math id="S1.Thmtheorem21.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p,q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p,q)</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex48" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex48.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\overline{\partial}}^{p,q}(X)\simeq\mathcal{H}_{\overline{\partial}}^{p,q}(X)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mover accent="true"><mo>∂</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mover accent="true"><mo>∂</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\overline{\partial}}^{p,q}(X)\simeq\mathcal{H}_{\overline{\partial}}^{p,q}(X)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem21.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem21.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">moreover the space of <math id="S1.Thmtheorem21.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\overline{\partial}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mo>∂</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\overline{\partial}}</annotation></semantics></math>-harmonic forms is
finite dimensional, hence the space of
<math id="S1.Thmtheorem21.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\overline{\partial}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mo>∂</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\overline{\partial}}</annotation></semantics></math>-cohomology also.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem22" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem22.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 1.22</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem22.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem22.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem22.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For a compact
complex Hermitian manifold:</p>
<table id="S1.Ex49" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex49.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{q}(X,\Omega^{p}_{X})\simeq\mathcal{H}_{\overline{\partial}}^{p,q}(X)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mover accent="true"><mo>∂</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{q}(X,\Omega^{p}_{X})\simeq\mathcal{H}_{\overline{\partial}}^{p,q}(X)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS4.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS4.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In general there is a spectral sequence relating Dolbeault and de Rham cohomology groups <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite> . It will follow from Hodge theory that this spectral sequence degenerates at rank <math id="S1.SS4.SSS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.5. </span>Kähler manifolds</h3>

<div id="S1.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The exterior product of harmonic forms is not in general harmonic
neither the restriction of harmonic forms to a submanifold is
harmonic for the induced metric. In general, the
<math id="S1.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\partial}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mo>∂</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\partial}</annotation></semantics></math>-Laplacian <math id="S1.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{\overline{\partial}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mover accent="true"><mo>∂</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{\overline{\partial}}</annotation></semantics></math> and
the Laplacian <math id="S1.SS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> are not related. The theory becomes
natural when we add the Kähler condition on the metric, when the fundamental form
is closed with respect to the differential <math id="S1.SS5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>; we refer then to (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite>, ch. V, section 4) to
establish the relation between
<math id="S1.SS5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{\overline{\partial}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mover accent="true"><mo>∂</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{\overline{\partial}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> and for full proofs in general,
most importantly the type components of harmonic forms become
harmonic. The proofs in Hodge theory involve the theory of
elliptic differential operators on a manifold and will not be
given here as we aim just to give the statements.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem23" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem23.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 1.23</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem23.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem23.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem23.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The Hermitian metric is Kähler
if its associated <math id="S1.Thmtheorem23.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,1)</annotation></semantics></math>-form <math id="S1.Thmtheorem23.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>
is closed: <math id="S1.Thmtheorem23.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\omega=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\omega=0</annotation></semantics></math>. In this case, the manifold <math id="S1.Thmtheorem23.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>
is called a Kähler manifold.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem24" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem24.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 1.24</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem24.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem24.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem24.p1.1" class="ltx_p">1) A Hermitian metric on a compact Riemann surface (of complex dimension <math id="S1.Thmtheorem24.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>)
is Kähler
since <math id="S1.Thmtheorem24.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\omega</annotation></semantics></math> of degree <math id="S1.Thmtheorem24.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> must vanish. 
<br class="ltx_break">2) The Hermitian metric on a compact complex torus is Kähler. However, in
general a complex tori is not a projective variety. Indeed, by the projective embedding theorem of
Kodaira (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite> Theorem 7.11 p.164), the cohomology must be polarized, or the Kähler form is integral in order to get an algebraic torus,
called also an abelian variety since it is a commutative group. This will be the case for <math id="S1.Thmtheorem24.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=1</annotation></semantics></math>, indeed a
complex tori of dimension <math id="S1.Thmtheorem24.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> can be always embedded as a cubic curve in the
projective plane via Weierstrass function and its derivative.
<br class="ltx_break">3) The projective space with the Fubini-Study metric is Kähler.
<br class="ltx_break">4) The restriction of a Kähler metric on a submanifold is
Kähler with associated <math id="S1.Thmtheorem24.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,1)</annotation></semantics></math>-form
induced by the associated <math id="S1.Thmtheorem24.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,1)</annotation></semantics></math>-form
on the ambient manifold.
<br class="ltx_break">5) The product of two Kähler manifolds is Kähler.
<br class="ltx_break">6) On the opposite, a Hopf surface is an example of a compact complex smooth surface which is not Kähler. Indeed such surface is the orbit space of the action of a group isomorphic to <math id="S1.Thmtheorem24.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S1.Thmtheorem24.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}^{2}-\{0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}^{2}-\{0\}</annotation></semantics></math> and generated by the automorphism:
<math id="S1.Thmtheorem24.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(z_{1},z_{2})\to(\lambda_{1}z_{1},\lambda_{2}z_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(z_{1},z_{2})\to(\lambda_{1}z_{1},\lambda_{2}z_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S1.Thmtheorem24.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{1},\lambda_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{1},\lambda_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are complex of module <math id="S1.Thmtheorem24.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math> (or <math id="S1.Thmtheorem24.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem24.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem24.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For example, consider
<math id="S1.Thmtheorem24.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{3}=\{z=(z_{1},z_{2})\in\mathbb{C}^{2}:|z_{1}|^{2}+|z_{2}|^{2}=1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{3}=\{z=(z_{1},z_{2})\in\mathbb{C}^{2}:|z_{1}|^{2}+|z_{2}|^{2}=1\}</annotation></semantics></math> and consider</p>
<table id="S1.Ex50" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex50.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:S^{3}\times{\mathbb{R}}\simeq\mathbb{C}^{2}-\{0\}:f(z_{1},z_{2},t)=(e^{t}z_{1},e^{t}z_{2})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:S^{3}\times{\mathbb{R}}\simeq\mathbb{C}^{2}-\{0\}:f(z_{1},z_{2},t)=(e^{t}z_{1},e^{t}z_{2})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem24.p2.2" class="ltx_p">then the action of <math id="S1.Thmtheorem24.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S1.Thmtheorem24.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℝ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math> defined for <math id="S1.Thmtheorem24.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S1.Thmtheorem24.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\to t+m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\to t+m</annotation></semantics></math> is transformed into the action by <math id="S1.Thmtheorem24.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\lambda_{1}^{m},\lambda_{2}^{m})=(e^{m},e^{m})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\lambda_{1}^{m},\lambda_{2}^{m})=(e^{m},e^{m})</annotation></semantics></math>. We deduce that the action has no fixed point and the quotient surface is homeomorhic to <math id="S1.Thmtheorem24.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{3}\times S^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{3}\times S^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, hence compact. Its fundamental group is isomorphic to <math id="S1.Thmtheorem24.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> as the surface is a quotient of a simply connected surface and its cohomology in degree <math id="S1.Thmtheorem24.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> is also isomorphic to <math id="S1.Thmtheorem24.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>,
while the cohomology of a Kähler variety in odd degree must have an even dimension as a consequence of the conjugation property in the Hodge decomposition.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">On
Kähler manifolds, the following key relation between Laplacians is just stated here (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite> for a proof, or <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite> p. 115):</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem25" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem25.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 1.25</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem25.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem25.p1" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S1.Ex51" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex51.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{d}=2\Delta_{\overline{\partial}}=2\Delta_{\partial}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mover accent="true"><mo>∂</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>∂</mo></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{d}=2\Delta_{\overline{\partial}}=2\Delta_{\partial}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We denote by <math id="S1.SS5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{p,q}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{p,q}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> the space of harmonic forms of type
(<math id="S1.SS5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p,q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p,q</annotation></semantics></math>), that is the global sections <math id="S1.SS5.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi\in\mathcal{E}^{p,q}_{X}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi\in\mathcal{E}^{p,q}_{X}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S1.SS5.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{d}(\varphi)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{d}(\varphi)=0</annotation></semantics></math>, which is equivalent to
<math id="S1.SS5.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{\overline{\partial}}\varphi=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mover accent="true"><mo>∂</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>φ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{\overline{\partial}}\varphi=0</annotation></semantics></math>
as a consequence of the lemma.
<br class="ltx_break">Let <math id="S1.SS5.p3.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\varphi:=\sum_{p+q=i}\varphi^{p,q}\in\oplus_{p+q=i}\mathcal{E}^{p,q}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mi>φ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0em">∈</mo><msub><mo lspace="0em">⊕</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi:=\sum_{p+q=i}\varphi^{p,q}\in\oplus_{p+q=i}\mathcal{E}^{p,q}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> be harmonic,</p>
<table id="S1.Ex52" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex52.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Delta_{d}\varphi=2\sum_{p+q=r}(\Delta_{\overline{\partial}}\varphi^{p,q})=0\in\oplus_{p+q=r}\mathcal{E}^{p,q}(X)\Rightarrow\forall p,q,\,2\Delta_{\overline{\partial}}\varphi^{p,q}=\Delta_{d}\varphi^{p,q}=0" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mi>φ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mover accent="true"><mo>∂</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></msub><msup><mi>φ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0em">∈</mo><msub><mo lspace="0em">⊕</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⇒</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mn> 2</mn><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mover accent="true"><mo>∂</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></msub><msup><mi>φ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><msup><mi>φ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{d}\varphi=2\sum_{p+q=r}(\Delta_{\overline{\partial}}\varphi^{p,q})=0\in\oplus_{p+q=r}\mathcal{E}^{p,q}(X)\Rightarrow\forall p,q,\,2\Delta_{\overline{\partial}}\varphi^{p,q}=\Delta_{d}\varphi^{p,q}=0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS5.p3.2" class="ltx_p">since <math id="S1.SS5.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{\overline{\partial}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mover accent="true"><mo>∂</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{\overline{\partial}}</annotation></semantics></math> is compatible with type.
Hence, we deduce:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem26" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem26.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 1.26</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem26.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem26.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem26.p1.1" class="ltx_p">i) The <math id="S1.Thmtheorem26.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\partial}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mo>∂</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\partial}</annotation></semantics></math>-harmonic forms of type
(<math id="S1.Thmtheorem26.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p,q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p,q</annotation></semantics></math>) coincide with the harmonic forms of same type:
<math id="S1.Thmtheorem26.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{p,q}_{\overline{\partial}}(X)=\mathcal{H}^{p,q}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mover accent="true"><mo>∂</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{p,q}_{\overline{\partial}}(X)=\mathcal{H}^{p,q}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>.
<br class="ltx_break">ii) The projection on the <math id="S1.Thmtheorem26.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p,q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p,q)</annotation></semantics></math> component of
an harmonic form is harmonic and we have a natural decomposition:</p>
<table id="S1.Ex53" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex53.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{r}(X)\otimes\mathbb{C}=\oplus_{p+q=r}\mathcal{H}^{p,q}(X),\,\mathcal{H}^{p,q}(X)=\overline{\mathcal{H}^{q,p}(X)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><msub><mo lspace="0em">⊕</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{r}(X)\otimes\mathbb{C}=\oplus_{p+q=r}\mathcal{H}^{p,q}(X),\,\mathcal{H}^{p,q}(X)=\overline{\mathcal{H}^{q,p}(X)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS5.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Since <math id="S1.SS5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> is real, we deduce the conjugation
property.</p>
</div>
<section id="S1.SS5.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">1.5.1. </span>Hodge decomposition</h4>

<div id="S1.SS5.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS5.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S1.SS5.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{p,q}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{p,q}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the Dolbeault groups
represented by <math id="S1.SS5.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\overline{\partial}}-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>∂</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>−</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\overline{\partial}}-</annotation></semantics></math>closed <math id="S1.SS5.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p,q)-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p,q)-</annotation></semantics></math> forms. In general, such forms need not
to be <math id="S1.SS5.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d-</annotation></semantics></math>closed, and reciprocally the <math id="S1.SS5.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p,q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p,q)</annotation></semantics></math> components of a <math id="S1.SS5.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d-</annotation></semantics></math> closed form are not necessarily <math id="S1.SS5.SSS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\overline{\partial}}-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>∂</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>−</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\overline{\partial}}-</annotation></semantics></math>closed nor <math id="S1.SS5.SSS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d-</annotation></semantics></math> closed, however this cannot happens on compact Kähler manifolds.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem27" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem27.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.27</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem27.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem27.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem27.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem27.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S1.Thmtheorem27.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> be a compact Kähler
manifold. There is an isomorphism of cohomology classes of type
<math id="S1.Thmtheorem27.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p,q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p,q)</annotation></semantics></math> with harmonic forms of the same type</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex54" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex54.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{p,q}(X)\simeq\mathcal{H}^{p,q}(X)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{p,q}(X)\simeq\mathcal{H}^{p,q}(X).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS5.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS5.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Indeed, let <math id="S1.SS5.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi=\varphi^{r,0}+\cdots+\varphi^{0,r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>φ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>φ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi=\varphi^{r,0}+\cdots+\varphi^{0,r}</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S1.SS5.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{\overline{\partial}}\varphi=\Delta_{\overline{\partial}}\varphi^{r,0}+\cdots+\Delta_{\overline{\partial}}\varphi^{0,r}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mover accent="true"><mo>∂</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>φ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mover accent="true"><mo>∂</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>φ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mover accent="true"><mo>∂</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>φ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{\overline{\partial}}\varphi=\Delta_{\overline{\partial}}\varphi^{r,0}+\cdots+\Delta_{\overline{\partial}}\varphi^{0,r}=0</annotation></semantics></math> is equivalent to <math id="S1.SS5.SSS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{d}\varphi^{p,q}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>φ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{d}\varphi^{p,q}=0</annotation></semantics></math> for each <math id="S1.SS5.SSS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p+q=r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p+q=r</annotation></semantics></math>, since
<math id="S1.SS5.SSS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{d}=2\Delta_{\overline{\partial}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mover accent="true"><mo>∂</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{d}=2\Delta_{\overline{\partial}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS5.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">1.5.2. </span>Applications to Hodge theory</h4>

<div id="S1.SS5.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS5.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We remark first the
isomorphisms:</p>
<table id="S1.Ex55" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex55.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{p,q}(X)\simeq H_{\overline{\partial}}^{p,q}(X)\simeq H^{q}(X,\Omega_{X}^{p})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mover accent="true"><mo>∂</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{p,q}(X)\simeq H_{\overline{\partial}}^{p,q}(X)\simeq H^{q}(X,\Omega_{X}^{p})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS5.SSS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">which shows in particular for <math id="S1.SS5.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=0</annotation></semantics></math> that the space of global holomorphic
<math id="S1.SS5.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>-forms <math id="S1.SS5.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{0}(X,\Omega_{X}^{p})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{0}(X,\Omega_{X}^{p})</annotation></semantics></math> injects into <math id="S1.SS5.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{p}_{DR}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{p}_{DR}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>
with image <math id="S1.SS5.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{p,0}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{p,0}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>.
<br class="ltx_break">- Global holomorphic <math id="S1.SS5.SSS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>-forms are closed and harmonic for any Kähler
metric. They vanish if and only if they are exact.
<br class="ltx_break">- There are no non-zero global holomorphic forms on <math id="S1.SS5.SSS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>,
besides locally constant functions, and
more generally:</p>
<table id="S1.Ex56" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex56.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\overline{\partial}}^{p,q}(\mathbb{P}^{n})\simeq H^{q}(\mathbb{P}^{n},\Omega_{\mathbb{P}^{n}}^{p})=\begin{cases}0,&amp;\mbox{if }p\neq q\\
\mathbb{C}&amp;\mbox{if }p=q\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mover accent="true"><mo>∂</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mi>ℂ</mi></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\overline{\partial}}^{p,q}(\mathbb{P}^{n})\simeq H^{q}(\mathbb{P}^{n},\Omega_{\mathbb{P}^{n}}^{p})=\begin{cases}0,&amp;\mbox{if }p\neq q\\
\mathbb{C}&amp;\mbox{if }p=q\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem28" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem28.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 1.28</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem28.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem28.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem28.p1.1" class="ltx_p">i) The holomorphic form <math id="S1.Thmtheorem28.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z\,dw" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z\,dw</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S1.Thmtheorem28.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is not closed.
<br class="ltx_break">ii) The spaces <math id="S1.Thmtheorem28.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{p,q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{p,q}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S1.Thmtheorem28.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{DR}^{p+q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{DR}^{p+q}</annotation></semantics></math> are isomorphic to the
holomorphic invariant <math id="S1.Thmtheorem28.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{q}(X,\Omega^{p})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{q}(X,\Omega^{p})</annotation></semantics></math>, but the behavior
relative to the fiber of a proper holomorphic family <math id="S1.Thmtheorem28.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\to T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\to T</annotation></semantics></math>
for <math id="S1.Thmtheorem28.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in T</annotation></semantics></math> is different for each space: <math id="S1.Thmtheorem28.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{q}(X_{t},\Omega^{p})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{q}(X_{t},\Omega^{p})</annotation></semantics></math>
is holomorphic in <math id="S1.Thmtheorem28.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, de Rham cohomology <math id="S1.Thmtheorem28.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{DR}^{p+q}(X_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{DR}^{p+q}(X_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> is
locally constant but the embedding of <math id="S1.Thmtheorem28.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{p,q}(X_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{p,q}(X_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> into de Rham
cohomology <math id="S1.Thmtheorem28.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{DR}^{p+q}(X_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{DR}^{p+q}(X_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> is not holomorphic.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem29" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_xca">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem29.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Exercise 1.29</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem29.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem29.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem29.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S1.Thmtheorem29.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> be a compact Kähler
manifold and <math id="S1.Thmtheorem29.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega:=-Im\,h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo rspace="0em">:=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega:=-Im\,h</annotation></semantics></math> its real <math id="S1.Thmtheorem29.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-form of type <math id="S1.Thmtheorem29.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,1)</annotation></semantics></math>
(<math id="S1.Thmtheorem29.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega\in\mathcal{E}_{X}^{1,1}\cap\mathcal{E}^{2}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>X</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>∩</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega\in\mathcal{E}_{X}^{1,1}\cap\mathcal{E}^{2}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>).
The volume form <math id="S1.Thmtheorem29.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="vol\in\mathcal{E}^{2n}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">vol\in\mathcal{E}^{2n}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> defined by <math id="S1.Thmtheorem29.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S1.Thmtheorem29.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>,
can be defined by <math id="S1.Thmtheorem29.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> and
is equal to <math id="S1.Thmtheorem29.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{n!}\omega^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{n!}\omega^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.
<br class="ltx_break">Indeed, if we consider an orthonormal complex basis <math id="S1.Thmtheorem29.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{1},\ldots,e_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{1},\ldots,e_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> of the tangent space
<math id="S1.Thmtheorem29.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\,T_{X,{\mathbb{R}},x}\," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\,T_{X,{\mathbb{R}},x}\,</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S1.Thmtheorem29.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{1},Ie_{1},\ldots,e_{n},Ie_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{1},Ie_{1},\ldots,e_{n},Ie_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is a real
oriented orthonormal basis for <math id="S1.Thmtheorem29.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>
and we need to prove <math id="S1.Thmtheorem29.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{n!}\omega^{n}(e_{1}\wedge Ie_{1}\wedge\cdots\wedge Ie_{n})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{n!}\omega^{n}(e_{1}\wedge Ie_{1}\wedge\cdots\wedge Ie_{n})=1</annotation></semantics></math>.
<br class="ltx_break">Let <math id="S1.Thmtheorem29.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{i},i\in[1,n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{i},i\in[1,n]</annotation></semantics></math> denote a local basis of the complex cotangent bundle <math id="S1.Thmtheorem29.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{*(1,0)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{*(1,0)}</annotation></semantics></math>,
unitary for the Hermitian metric, then it is orthogonal and <math id="S1.Thmtheorem29.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\varphi_{j}\|^{2}=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\varphi_{j}\|^{2}=2</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to the Riemannian metric (for example on <math id="S1.Thmtheorem29.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, the complex unitary basis <math id="S1.Thmtheorem29.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="dz_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">dz_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> is written as <math id="S1.Thmtheorem29.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="dx_{j}+idy_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">dx_{j}+idy_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S1.Thmtheorem29.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="dx_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">dx_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.Thmtheorem29.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="dy_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">dy_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> elements of an orthonormal basis for the Riemannian structure). We deduce from the decomposition
<math id="S1.Thmtheorem29.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{j}=\psi^{\prime}_{j}+i\psi^{\prime\prime}j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>ψ</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{j}=\psi^{\prime}_{j}+i\psi^{\prime\prime}j</annotation></semantics></math> into real components, the following formula of the volume:</p>
<table id="S1.Ex57" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex57.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega=\frac{i}{2}\sum_{j}\varphi_{j}\wedge{\overline{\varphi}}_{j},\quad vol=\frac{\omega^{n}}{n!}=\psi^{\prime}_{1}\wedge\psi^{\prime\prime}_{1}\wedge\cdots\wedge\psi^{\prime}_{n}\wedge\psi^{\prime\prime}_{n}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>j</mi></munder><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>∧</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>ω</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>∧</mo><msubsup><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><mo>∧</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>∧</mo><msubsup><mi>ψ</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>∧</mo><msubsup><mi>ψ</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>′′</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega=\frac{i}{2}\sum_{j}\varphi_{j}\wedge{\overline{\varphi}}_{j},\quad vol=\frac{\omega^{n}}{n!}=\psi^{\prime}_{1}\wedge\psi^{\prime\prime}_{1}\wedge\cdots\wedge\psi^{\prime}_{n}\wedge\psi^{\prime\prime}_{n}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem29.p1.2" class="ltx_p">since <math id="S1.Thmtheorem29.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{j}\wedge{\overline{\varphi}}_{j}=(\frac{2}{i})\psi^{\prime}_{j}\wedge\psi^{\prime\prime}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∧</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ψ</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>∧</mo><msubsup><mi>ψ</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′′</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{j}\wedge{\overline{\varphi}}_{j}=(\frac{2}{i})\psi^{\prime}_{j}\wedge\psi^{\prime\prime}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, <math id="S1.Thmtheorem29.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ω</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> never vanish.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem30" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem30.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 1.30</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem30.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem30.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem30.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem30.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For a compact Kähler manifold <math id="S1.Thmtheorem30.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex58" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex58.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{p,p}(X,\mathbb{C})\not=0\quad\hbox{ for }0\leq p\leq\dim X." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> for </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>p</mi><mo rspace="0.1389em">≤</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{p,p}(X,\mathbb{C})\not=0\quad\hbox{ for }0\leq p\leq\dim X.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS5.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS5.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In fact, the integral of the volume form <math id="S1.SS5.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int_{X}\omega^{n}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><mi>X</mi></msub><msup><mi>ω</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int_{X}\omega^{n}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.
It follows that the cohomology class <math id="S1.SS5.SSS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega^{n}\not=0\in H^{2n}(X,\mathbb{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ω</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega^{n}\not=0\in H^{2n}(X,\mathbb{C})</annotation></semantics></math>, hence the cohomology class <math id="S1.SS5.SSS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega^{p}\not=0\in H^{p,p}(X,\mathbb{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ω</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega^{p}\not=0\in H^{p,p}(X,\mathbb{C})</annotation></semantics></math> since its cup product with <math id="S1.SS5.SSS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega^{n-p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega^{n-p}</annotation></semantics></math> is not
zero.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.6. </span>Cohomology class of a subvariety and Hodge conjecture</h3>

<div id="S1.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To state the Hodge conjecture,
we construct the class in de Rham cohomology
of a closed complex algebraic
subvariety (resp. complex analytic subspace) of codimension <math id="S1.SS6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> in
a smooth complex projective variety (resp. compact Kähler
manifold); it is of Hodge type (<math id="S1.SS6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p,p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p,p</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem31" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem31.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 1.31</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem31.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem31.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem31.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem31.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S1.Thmtheorem31.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> be a complex manifold and <math id="S1.Thmtheorem31.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> a compact complex
subanalytic space of dim <math id="S1.Thmtheorem31.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S1.Thmtheorem31.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. The integral of the
restriction of
a form <math id="S1.Thmtheorem31.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S1.Thmtheorem31.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> to the smooth
open subset of <math id="S1.Thmtheorem31.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> is convergent and defines a linear function
on forms of degree <math id="S1.Thmtheorem31.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2m</annotation></semantics></math>. It induces a linear map on cohomology</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex59" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex59.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="cl(Z):H^{2m}(X,\mathbb{C})\to\mathbb{C},\quad[\omega]\mapsto\int_{Z_{smooth}}\omega_{|Z}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mi>l</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em" stretchy="false">↦</mo><msub><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub></msub><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">cl(Z):H^{2m}(X,\mathbb{C})\to\mathbb{C},\quad[\omega]\mapsto\int_{Z_{smooth}}\omega_{|Z}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem31.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem31.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Moreover, if <math id="S1.Thmtheorem31.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is compact Kähler, <math id="S1.Thmtheorem31.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="cl(Z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">cl(Z)</annotation></semantics></math> vanish on all
components of the Hodge decomposition which are distinct from <math id="S1.Thmtheorem31.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{m,m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{m,m}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S1.SS6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> is compact and smooth, the integral is well defined, because, by
Stokes theorem, if <math id="S1.SS6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega=d\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega=d\eta</annotation></semantics></math>, the integral vanish since
it is equal to the integral
of <math id="S1.SS6.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> on the boundary <math id="S1.SS6.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial Z=\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial Z=\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S1.SS6.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S1.SS6.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> is not smooth, the easiest proof is to use a
desingularisation (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>) <math id="S1.SS6.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi:Z^{\prime}\to Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Z</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:Z^{\prime}\to Z</annotation></semantics></math> inducing an
isomorphism <math id="S1.SS6.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{\prime}_{smooth}\simeq Z_{smooth}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>≃</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{\prime}_{smooth}\simeq Z_{smooth}</annotation></semantics></math> which implies an equality
of the integrals of <math id="S1.SS6.p3.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi^{*}(\omega_{|Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{*}(\omega_{|Z})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS6.p3.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\omega_{|Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{|Z}</annotation></semantics></math>, which
is independent of the choice of <math id="S1.SS6.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Z</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. The restriction of <math id="S1.SS6.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>
of degree <math id="S1.SS6.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2m</annotation></semantics></math> vanish unless it is of type <math id="S1.SS6.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m,m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m,m</annotation></semantics></math> since
<math id="S1.SS6.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{smooth}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{smooth}</annotation></semantics></math> is an analytic manifold of dimension <math id="S1.SS6.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S1.SS6.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">1.6.1. </span>Poincaré duality</h4>

<div id="S1.SS6.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS6.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">On compact oriented
differentiable manifolds, we use the
wedge product of differential forms to define the cup-product on
de Rham cohomology and integration to define the trace, so that
we can state Poincaré duality <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS6.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS6.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS6.SSS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Cup-product.</span>
For a manifold <math id="S1.SS6.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, the cup product is a natural bilinear
operation on de Rham cohomology</p>
<table id="S1.Ex60" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex60.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{i}(X,\mathbb{C})\otimes H^{j}(X,\mathbb{C})\xrightarrow{\smile}H^{i+j}(X,\mathbb{C})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>j</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">⌣</mo></mover><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{i}(X,\mathbb{C})\otimes H^{j}(X,\mathbb{C})\xrightarrow{\smile}H^{i+j}(X,\mathbb{C})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS6.SSS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">defined by the
wedge product on the level of differential forms.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS6.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS6.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">It is a topological product defined on cohomology with coefficients in <math id="S1.SS6.SSS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>,
but its
definition on cohomology groups with integer coefficients is less
straightforward.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS6.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS6.SSS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS6.SSS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The Trace map</span>. On a compact oriented
manifold <math id="S1.SS6.SSS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> of dimension <math id="S1.SS6.SSS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, the integral over <math id="S1.SS6.SSS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> of a
differential <math id="S1.SS6.SSS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> of highest degree <math id="S1.SS6.SSS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> depends only on its
class modulo boundary by Stokes theorem, hence it defines a map
called the trace:</p>
<table id="S1.Ex61" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex61.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Tr:H^{n}(X,\mathbb{C})\to\mathbb{C}\quad[\omega]\mapsto\int_{X}\omega." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em" stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><mi>X</mi></msub><mi>ω</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Tr:H^{n}(X,\mathbb{C})\to\mathbb{C}\quad[\omega]\mapsto\int_{X}\omega.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS6.SSS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">The following theorem is stated without proof:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem32" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem32.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.32</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem32.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Poincaré duality )<span id="S1.Thmtheorem32.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem32.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem32.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem32.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S1.Thmtheorem32.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> be a compact
oriented manifold of dimension <math id="S1.Thmtheorem32.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>. The cup-product
and the trace map:</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex62" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex62.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{j}(X,\mathbb{C})\otimes H^{n-j}(X,\mathbb{C})\xrightarrow{\smile}H^{n}(X,\mathbb{C})\xrightarrow{Tr}\mathbb{C}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">⌣</mo></mover><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">r</mi></mrow></mover><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{j}(X,\mathbb{C})\otimes H^{n-j}(X,\mathbb{C})\xrightarrow{\smile}H^{n}(X,\mathbb{C})\xrightarrow{Tr}\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem32.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem32.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">define an isomorphism:</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex63" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex63.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{j}(X,\mathbb{C})\xrightarrow{\sim}Hom(H^{n-j}(X,\mathbb{C}),\mathbb{C})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo></mover><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{j}(X,\mathbb{C})\xrightarrow{\sim}Hom(H^{n-j}(X,\mathbb{C}),\mathbb{C}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS6.SSS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS6.SSS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">If the compact complex analytic space <math id="S1.SS6.SSS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> is of codimension <math id="S1.SS6.SSS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> in
the smooth compact complex manifold <math id="S1.SS6.SSS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> its class <math id="S1.SS6.SSS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="cl(Z)\in H^{2n-2p}(X,\mathbb{C})^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">cl(Z)\in H^{2n-2p}(X,\mathbb{C})^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds, by Poincaré duality on <math id="S1.SS6.SSS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, to
a fundamental cohomology class <math id="S1.SS6.SSS1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\eta_{Z}]\in H^{2p}(X,\mathbb{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\eta_{Z}]\in H^{2p}(X,\mathbb{C})</annotation></semantics></math>. Then
we have by definition:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem33" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemmadefinition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem33.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma and Definition 1.33</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem33.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem33.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem33.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem33.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For a complex compact manifold <math id="S1.Thmtheorem33.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>,
the fundamental cohomology class <math id="S1.Thmtheorem33.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\eta_{Z}]\in H^{p,p}(X,\mathbb{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\eta_{Z}]\in H^{p,p}(X,\mathbb{C})</annotation></semantics></math> of
a closed complex submanifold <math id="S1.Thmtheorem33.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> of codimension <math id="S1.Thmtheorem33.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies the
following relation:</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex64" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex64.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\int_{X}\varphi\wedge\eta_{Z}=\int_{Z}\varphi_{|Z},\quad\forall\varphi\in\mathcal{E}^{n-p,n-p}(X)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><mi>X</mi></msub><mi>φ</mi><mo>∧</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><msub><mo>∫</mo><mi>Z</mi></msub><msub><mi>φ</mi><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int_{X}\varphi\wedge\eta_{Z}=\int_{Z}\varphi_{|Z},\quad\forall\varphi\in\mathcal{E}^{n-p,n-p}(X).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem34" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem34.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 1.34</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem34.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem34.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem34.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem34.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For a compact Kähler manifold <math id="S1.Thmtheorem34.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>,
the cohomology class of a compact complex analytic closed
submanifold <math id="S1.Thmtheorem34.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> of codim <math id="S1.Thmtheorem34.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> is a non-zero element <math id="S1.Thmtheorem34.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\eta_{Z}]\not=0\in H^{p,p}(X,\mathbb{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\eta_{Z}]\not=0\in H^{p,p}(X,\mathbb{C})</annotation></semantics></math>, for <math id="S1.Thmtheorem34.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq p\leq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>≤</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq p\leq</annotation></semantics></math> dim <math id="S1.Thmtheorem34.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS6.SSS1.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S1.SS6.SSS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS6.SSS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS6.SSS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">For a compact Kähler manifold <math id="S1.SS6.SSS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>,
let <math id="S1.SS6.SSS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> be a Kähler form, then the integral on <math id="S1.SS6.SSS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> of
the restriction <math id="S1.SS6.SSS1.p6.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\omega_{|Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{|Z}</annotation></semantics></math> is positive since it is a
Kähler form on <math id="S1.SS6.SSS1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>, hence <math id="S1.SS6.SSS1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\eta_{Z}]\not=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\eta_{Z}]\not=0</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex65" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex65.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\int_{X}(\wedge^{n-p}\omega)\wedge\eta_{Z}=\int_{Z}\wedge^{n-p}(\omega_{|Z})&gt;0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><mi>X</mi></msub><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mo lspace="0em">∧</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∧</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><msub><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><mi>Z</mi></msub><msup><mo lspace="0em">∧</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int_{X}(\wedge^{n-p}\omega)\wedge\eta_{Z}=\int_{Z}\wedge^{n-p}(\omega_{|Z})&gt;0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS6.SSS1.p6.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS6.SSS1.p6.2.1" class="ltx_text">∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS6.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">1.6.2. </span>Topological construction</h4>

<div id="S1.SS6.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS6.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The dual vector space
<math id="S1.SS6.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Hom_{\mathbb{C}}(H^{2m}(X,\mathbb{C}),\mathbb{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Hom_{\mathbb{C}}(H^{2m}(X,\mathbb{C}),\mathbb{C})</annotation></semantics></math> is naturally isomorphic
to the homology vector space which suggests that the
fundamental class is defined naturally
in homology. Indeed, Hodge conjecture has gained so much attention that
it is of fundamental importance to discover what conditions can be made
on classes of algebraic subvarieties, including the definition of the classes
in cohomology with coefficients in <math id="S1.SS6.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS6.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS6.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The theory of homology and
cohomology traces its origin to the work of Poincaré in the late
nineteenth century. There are actually many different theories,
for example, simplicial and singular. In 1931, Georges de Rham
proved a conjecture of Poincaré on a relationship between cycles
and differential forms that establishes for a compact orientable manifold
an isomorphism between singular cohomology with real
coefficients and what is known now as de Rham cohomology. In fact
the direct homological construction of the class of <math id="S1.SS6.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> is
natural and well known.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS6.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS6.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS6.SSS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Homological class</span>. The idea that homology should
represent the classes of topological subspaces has been probably
at the origin of homology theory, although the effective
construction of homology groups is different and more elaborate. The simplest way to construct Homology
groups is to use singular simplices. But the definition of
homology groups <math id="S1.SS6.SSS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{j}(X,\mathbb{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{j}(X,\mathbb{Z})</annotation></semantics></math> of a triangulated space is quite
natural. Indeed, the sum of oriented triangles of highest
dimensions of an oriented triangulated topological space <math id="S1.SS6.SSS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> of
real dimension <math id="S1.SS6.SSS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, defines an homology class
<math id="S1.SS6.SSS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[X]\in H_{n}(X,\mathbb{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[X]\in H_{n}(X,\mathbb{Z})</annotation></semantics></math> (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>, Ch 0, paragraph 4).</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS6.SSS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS6.SSS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We take as granted here that a closed subvariety <math id="S1.SS6.SSS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> of dimension <math id="S1.SS6.SSS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> in
a compact complex algebraic variety <math id="S1.SS6.SSS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> can be triangulated such
that the sum of its oriented triangles of highest dimensions
defines its class in the homology group <math id="S1.SS6.SSS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{2m}(X,\mathbb{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{2m}(X,\mathbb{Z})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS6.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">1.6.3. </span>Cap product</h4>

<div id="S1.SS6.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS6.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Cohomology groups of a topological space <math id="S1.SS6.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{i}(X,\mathbb{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{i}(X,\mathbb{Z})</annotation></semantics></math> are dually defined
, and there exists a topological operation on homology and
cohomology, called the cap product:</p>
<table id="S1.Ex66" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex66.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frown:H^{q}(X,\mathbb{Z})\otimes H_{p}(X,\mathbb{Z})\rightarrow H_{p-q}(X,\mathbb{Z})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">⌢</mo><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frown:H^{q}(X,\mathbb{Z})\otimes H_{p}(X,\mathbb{Z})\rightarrow H_{p-q}(X,\mathbb{Z})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS6.SSS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">We can state now a duality theorem of Poincaré, frequently used
in geometry.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem35" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem35.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.35</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem35.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Poincaré duality isomorphism)<span id="S1.Thmtheorem35.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem35.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem35.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem35.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S1.Thmtheorem35.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> be a compact
oriented topological manifold of dimension <math id="S1.Thmtheorem35.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>. The cap product
with the fundamental class <math id="S1.Thmtheorem35.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[X]\in H_{n}(X,\mathbb{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[X]\in H_{n}(X,\mathbb{Z})</annotation></semantics></math> defines an
isomorphism, for all <math id="S1.Thmtheorem35.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.Thmtheorem35.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq j\leq n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq j\leq n</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex67" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex67.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{X}:H^{j}(X,\mathbb{Z})\xrightarrow{\frown[X]}H_{n-j}(X,\mathbb{Z})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo mathsize="0.700em">⌢</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">[</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">X</mi><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mover><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{X}:H^{j}(X,\mathbb{Z})\xrightarrow{\frown[X]}H_{n-j}(X,\mathbb{Z})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS6.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS6.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The homology class <math id="S1.SS6.SSS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{*}[Z]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{*}[Z]</annotation></semantics></math> of a compact complex analytic subspace <math id="S1.SS6.SSS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i:Z\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i:Z\to X</annotation></semantics></math> of codimension <math id="S1.SS6.SSS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> in
the smooth compact complex manifold <math id="S1.SS6.SSS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> of dimension <math id="S1.SS6.SSS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds by the inverse of Poincaré duality
isomorphism <math id="S1.SS6.SSS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, to a fundamental cohomology class:</p>
<table id="S1.Ex68" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex68.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\eta_{Z}]^{top}\in H^{2p}(X,\mathbb{Z})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\eta_{Z}]^{top}\in H^{2p}(X,\mathbb{Z}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem36" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem36.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 1.36</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem36.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem36.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem36.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem36.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The canonical morphism <math id="S1.Thmtheorem36.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{2n-2p}(X,\mathbb{Z})\to H^{2n-2p}(X,\mathbb{C})^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{2n-2p}(X,\mathbb{Z})\to H^{2n-2p}(X,\mathbb{C})^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>
carry the topological class <math id="S1.Thmtheorem36.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[Z]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[Z]</annotation></semantics></math> of an analytic subspace
<math id="S1.Thmtheorem36.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> of codimension <math id="S1.Thmtheorem36.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S1.Thmtheorem36.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> into the
fundamental class <math id="S1.Thmtheorem36.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="cl(Z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">cl(Z)</annotation></semantics></math>.
<br class="ltx_break">Respectively. the morphism <math id="S1.Thmtheorem36.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2p}(X,\mathbb{Z})\to H^{2p}(X,\mathbb{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2p}(X,\mathbb{Z})\to H^{2p}(X,\mathbb{C})</annotation></semantics></math> carry the topological class
<math id="S1.Thmtheorem36.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\eta_{Z}]^{top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\eta_{Z}]^{top}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S1.Thmtheorem36.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\eta_{Z}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\eta_{Z}]</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS6.SSS4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">1.6.4. </span>Intersection product in topology and geometry</h4>

<div id="S1.SS6.SSS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS6.SSS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">On
a triangulated space, cycles are defined as a sum of triangles
with boundary zero, and homology is defined by classes of cycles
modulo boundaries.
It is always possible to represent two homology classes of degree <math id="S1.SS6.SSS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS6.SSS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>
by two cycles of codim. <math id="S1.SS6.SSS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS6.SSS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> in
‘transversal position’ , so that
their intersection is defined as a cycle of
codim.<math id="S1.SS6.SSS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p+q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p+q</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, for two representations by
transversal cycles, the intersections cycles are homologous
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>, (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> 2.8). Then a theory
of intersection product on homology can be deduced:</p>
<table id="S1.Ex69" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex69.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{n-p}(X,\mathbb{Z})\otimes H_{n-q}(X,\mathbb{Z})\xrightarrow{\cap}H_{n-p-q}(X,\mathbb{Z})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">∩</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{n-p}(X,\mathbb{Z})\otimes H_{n-q}(X,\mathbb{Z})\xrightarrow{\cap}H_{n-p-q}(X,\mathbb{Z})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS6.SSS4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">In geometry, two closed submanifolds
<math id="S1.SS6.SSS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS6.SSS4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> of a compact oriented manifold <math id="S1.SS6.SSS4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> can be deformed into a
transversal position so that their intersection can be defined as
a submanifold <math id="S1.SS6.SSS4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> with a sign depending on the orientation (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>, ch 2), then <math id="S1.SS6.SSS4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> is defined up to a
deformation such that the homology classes satisfy the relation <math id="S1.SS6.SSS4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[V_{1}]\cap[V_{2}]=\pm[W]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">±</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[V_{1}]\cap[V_{2}]=\pm[W]</annotation></semantics></math>.
The deformation class of <math id="S1.SS6.SSS4.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> with sign is called
<math id="S1.SS6.SSS4.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{1}\cap V_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{1}\cap V_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS6.SSS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS6.SSS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS6.SSS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Poincaré duality in Homology</span> (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>, p 53) . 
<br class="ltx_break">The intersection pairing:</p>
<table id="S1.Ex70" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex70.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{j}(X,\mathbb{Z})\otimes H_{n-j}(X,\mathbb{Z})\xrightarrow{\cap}H_{0}(X,\mathbb{Z})\xrightarrow{degree}\mathbb{Z}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">∩</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">e</mi></mrow></mover><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{j}(X,\mathbb{Z})\otimes H_{n-j}(X,\mathbb{Z})\xrightarrow{\cap}H_{0}(X,\mathbb{Z})\xrightarrow{degree}\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS6.SSS4.p2.2" class="ltx_p">for <math id="S1.SS6.SSS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq j\leq n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq j\leq n</annotation></semantics></math> is unimodular: the induced morphism</p>
<table id="S1.Ex71" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex71.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{j}(X,\mathbb{Z})\rightarrow Hom(H_{n-j}(X,\mathbb{Z}),\mathbb{Z})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{j}(X,\mathbb{Z})\rightarrow Hom(H_{n-j}(X,\mathbb{Z}),\mathbb{Z})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS6.SSS4.p2.3" class="ltx_p">is surjective and its kernel is the torsion subgroup of
<math id="S1.SS6.SSS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{j}(X,\mathbb{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{j}(X,\mathbb{Z})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS6.SSS5" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">1.6.5. </span>Relation between Intersection and cup products</h4>

<div id="S1.SS6.SSS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS6.SSS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The cup product previously defined in de Rham cohomology
is a topological product defined with coefficients in <math id="S1.SS6.SSS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>, but
its definition on cohomology groups is less straightforward. The
trace map can also be defined with coefficients in <math id="S1.SS6.SSS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>, and we
also have the corresponding duality statement,</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS6.SSS5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS6.SSS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS6.SSS5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Poincaré duality in cohomology</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS6.SSS5.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS6.SSS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The cup-product and the trace map:</p>
<table id="S1.Ex72" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex72.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{j}(X,\mathbb{Z})\otimes H^{n-j}(X,\mathbb{Z})\xrightarrow{\smile}H^{n}(X,\mathbb{Z})\xrightarrow{Tr}\mathbb{Z}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">⌣</mo></mover><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">r</mi></mrow></mover><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{j}(X,\mathbb{Z})\otimes H^{n-j}(X,\mathbb{Z})\xrightarrow{\smile}H^{n}(X,\mathbb{Z})\xrightarrow{Tr}\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS6.SSS5.p3.2" class="ltx_p">define a unimodular pairing inducing an isomorphism:</p>
<table id="S1.Ex73" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex73.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{j}(X,\mathbb{Q})\xrightarrow{\sim}Hom(H^{n-j}(X,\mathbb{Q}),\mathbb{Q})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo></mover><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{j}(X,\mathbb{Q})\xrightarrow{\sim}Hom(H^{n-j}(X,\mathbb{Q}),\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS6.SSS5.p3.3" class="ltx_p">compatible with the pairing in de Rham cohomology.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS6.SSS5.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS6.SSS5.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS6.SSS5.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Poincaré duality isomorphism transforms the intersection
pairing into the cup product:
<br class="ltx_break"></span>The following result is proved in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite> (p. 59) in the case
<math id="S1.SS6.SSS5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{\prime}=n-k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{\prime}=n-k</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS6.SSS5.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS6.SSS5.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Let
<math id="S1.SS6.SSS5.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> be a <math id="S1.SS6.SSS5.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k-</annotation></semantics></math>cycle on an oriented manifold <math id="S1.SS6.SSS5.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> of real dimension
<math id="S1.SS6.SSS5.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS6.SSS5.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> an <math id="S1.SS6.SSS5.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{\prime}-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{\prime}-</annotation></semantics></math>cycle on <math id="S1.SS6.SSS5.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> with Poincaré duals
<math id="S1.SS6.SSS5.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{\sigma}\in H^{n-k}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{\sigma}\in H^{n-k}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS6.SSS5.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{\tau}\in H^{n-k^{\prime}}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{\tau}\in H^{n-k^{\prime}}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>,
then:</p>
<table id="S1.Ex74" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex74.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{\sigma}\smile\eta_{\tau}=\eta_{\sigma\frown\tau}\in H^{n-k-k^{\prime}}(X)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo>⌣</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⌢</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{\sigma}\smile\eta_{\tau}=\eta_{\sigma\frown\tau}\in H^{n-k-k^{\prime}}(X)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS6.SSS6" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">1.6.6. </span></h4>

<div id="S1.SS6.SSS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS6.SSS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The Hodge type <math id="S1.SS6.SSS6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p,p)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p,p)</annotation></semantics></math> of the fundamental
class of analytic compact submanifold of codimension <math id="S1.SS6.SSS6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> is an
analytic condition. The search for properties characterizing
classes of cycles has been motivated by a question of Hodge.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem37" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem37.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 1.37</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem37.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem37.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem37.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S1.Thmtheorem37.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S1.Thmtheorem37.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.Thmtheorem37.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math> and
let <math id="S1.Thmtheorem37.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi:H^{2p}(X,A)\to H^{2p}(X,\mathbb{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi:H^{2p}(X,A)\to H^{2p}(X,\mathbb{C})</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the canonical map.
The group of cycles</p>
<table id="S1.Ex75" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex75.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{p,p}(X,A):=\{x\in H^{2p}(X,A):\varphi(x)\in H^{p,p}(X,\mathbb{C})\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{p,p}(X,A):=\{x\in H^{2p}(X,A):\varphi(x)\in H^{p,p}(X,\mathbb{C})\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem37.p1.2" class="ltx_p">is called the group of Hodge classes of type <math id="S1.Thmtheorem37.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p,p)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p,p)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem38" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem38.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 1.38</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem38.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem38.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem38.p1.1" class="ltx_p">An <math id="S1.Thmtheorem38.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r-</annotation></semantics></math>cycle of an algebraic variety <math id="S1.Thmtheorem38.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is
a formal finite linear combination <math id="S1.Thmtheorem38.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i\in[1,h]}m_{i}Z_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i\in[1,h]}m_{i}Z_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>
of closed irreducible subvarieties <math id="S1.Thmtheorem38.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> of dimension <math id="S1.Thmtheorem38.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> with
integer coefficients <math id="S1.Thmtheorem38.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. The group of <math id="S1.Thmtheorem38.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r-</annotation></semantics></math>cycles is denoted by
<math id="S1.Thmtheorem38.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Z}_{r}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Z}_{r}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS6.SSS6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS6.SSS6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For a compact complex algebraic manifold, the class of
closed irreducible subvarieties of codimension <math id="S1.SS6.SSS6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>
extends into a linear morphism:</p>
<table id="S1.Ex76" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex76.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="cl_{A}:\mathcal{Z}_{p}(X)\otimes A\to H^{p,p}(X,A):\sum_{i\in[1,h]}m_{i}Z_{i}\mapsto\sum_{i\in[1,h]}m_{i}\eta_{Z_{i}},\forall m_{i}\in A" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em" stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">cl_{A}:\mathcal{Z}_{p}(X)\otimes A\to H^{p,p}(X,A):\sum_{i\in[1,h]}m_{i}Z_{i}\mapsto\sum_{i\in[1,h]}m_{i}\eta_{Z_{i}},\forall m_{i}\in A</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS6.SSS6.p2.2" class="ltx_p">The
elements of the image of <math id="S1.SS6.SSS6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="cl_{\mathbb{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>ℚ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">cl_{\mathbb{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math> are called rational algebraic
Hodge classes of type <math id="S1.SS6.SSS6.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p,p)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p,p)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS6.SSS7" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">1.6.7. </span>Hodge conjecture</h4>

<div id="S1.SS6.SSS7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS6.SSS7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Is the map <math id="S1.SS6.SSS7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="cl_{\mathbb{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>ℚ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">cl_{\mathbb{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math> surjective
when <math id="S1.SS6.SSS7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is a projective manifold? In other terms, is any rational
Hodge class algebraic?</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS6.SSS7.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS6.SSS7.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The question is to construct a cycle of codimension <math id="S1.SS6.SSS7.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> out of a
rational cohomology element of type (<math id="S1.SS6.SSS7.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p,p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p,p</annotation></semantics></math>) knowing that
cohomology is constructed via topological technique.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS6.SSS7.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS6.SSS7.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Originally, the Hodge conjecture was stated with <math id="S1.SS6.SSS7.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>-coefficients,
but there are torsion elements
which cannot be represented by algebraic cycles. There exists
compact Kähler complex manifolds not containing enough
analytic subspaces to represent all Hodge cycles <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem39" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem39.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 1.39</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem39.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> </span>(Absolute Hodge cycle)<span id="S1.Thmtheorem39.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem39.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem39.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In fact
Deligne added another property for algebraic cycles by introducing
the notion of absolute Hodge cycle (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>). An
algebraic cycle <math id="S1.Thmtheorem39.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> is necessarily defined over a field extension
<math id="S1.Thmtheorem39.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> of finite type over <math id="S1.Thmtheorem39.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then its cohomology class in the de Rham
cohomology of <math id="S1.Thmtheorem39.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> over the field <math id="S1.Thmtheorem39.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> defines, for each embedding <math id="S1.Thmtheorem39.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma:K\to\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma:K\to\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, a cohomology class <math id="S1.Thmtheorem39.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[Z]_{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>σ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[Z]_{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math> of
type <math id="S1.Thmtheorem39.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p,p)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p,p)</annotation></semantics></math> in the cohomology of the complex manifold
<math id="S1.Thmtheorem39.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{\sigma}^{an}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{\sigma}^{an}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem40" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem40.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 1.40</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem40.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> </span>(Grothendieck fundamental class)<span id="S1.Thmtheorem40.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem40.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem40.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For an algebraic
subvariety <math id="S1.Thmtheorem40.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> of codimension <math id="S1.Thmtheorem40.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> in a variety <math id="S1.Thmtheorem40.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> of dimension
<math id="S1.Thmtheorem40.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, the fundamental class can be defined as an element of the
group <math id="S1.Thmtheorem40.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Ext^{p}(\mathcal{O}_{Z},\Omega^{p}_{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Ext^{p}(\mathcal{O}_{Z},\Omega^{p}_{X})</annotation></semantics></math> (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>). Let
<math id="S1.Thmtheorem40.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> be an affine subset and suppose that <math id="S1.Thmtheorem40.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\cap U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\cap U</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as a complete
intersection by <math id="S1.Thmtheorem40.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> regular functions <math id="S1.Thmtheorem40.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, if we use the Koszul
resolution of <math id="S1.Thmtheorem40.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{Z\cap U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{Z\cap U}</annotation></semantics></math> defined by the elements <math id="S1.Thmtheorem40.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> to
compute the extension
groups, then the cohomology class is defined by a symbol: 
<br class="ltx_break"></p>
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem40.p1.2" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem40.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text"><math id="S1.Thmtheorem40.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left[\begin{array}[]{c}df_{1}\wedge\cdots\wedge df_{p}\\
f_{1}\cdots f_{p}\end{array}\right]\in Ext^{p}(\mathcal{O}_{Z\cap U},\Omega^{p}_{U})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>f</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>f</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>U</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left[\begin{array}[]{c}df_{1}\wedge\cdots\wedge df_{p}\\
f_{1}\cdots f_{p}\end{array}\right]\in Ext^{p}(\mathcal{O}_{Z\cap U},\Omega^{p}_{U}).</annotation></semantics></math></span></p>
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem40.p1.3" class="ltx_p">This symbol is
independent of the choice of generators of <math id="S1.Thmtheorem40.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{Z\cap U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{Z\cap U}</annotation></semantics></math>, and
it is the restriction of a unique class defined over <math id="S1.Thmtheorem40.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> which
defines the cohomology class of <math id="S1.Thmtheorem40.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> in the de Rham cohomology
group <math id="S1.Thmtheorem40.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{H}^{2p}_{Z}(X,\Omega^{*}_{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>ℍ</mi><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{H}^{2p}_{Z}(X,\Omega^{*}_{X})</annotation></semantics></math> with support in <math id="S1.Thmtheorem40.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>).
The extension groups and cohomology groups with support in closed
algebraic subvarieties form the basic idea to construct the
dualizing complex
<math id="S1.Thmtheorem40.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K^{*}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K^{*}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S1.Thmtheorem40.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> as part of Grothendieck duality theory (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>).</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>Lefschetz decomposition and Polarized Hodge structure</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this chapter, we give more specific results for smooth
complex <span id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">projective</span> varieties, namely the Lefschetz decomposition
and primitive cohomology subspaces, Riemann bilinear relations and
their abstract formulation into polarization of Hodge Structures. We start
with the statement of the results. Proofs and complements follow.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1. </span>Lefschetz decomposition and primitive cohomology</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> be a compact
Kähler manifold of class <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\omega]\in H^{2}(X,{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\omega]\in H^{2}(X,{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math> of Hodge
type <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,1)</annotation></semantics></math>. The cup-product with <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\omega]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\omega]</annotation></semantics></math> defines morphisms:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L:H^{q}(X,{\mathbb{R}})\to H^{q+2}(X,{\mathbb{R}}),\quad L:H^{q}(X,\mathbb{C})\to H^{q+2}(X,\mathbb{C})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L:H^{q}(X,{\mathbb{R}})\to H^{q+2}(X,{\mathbb{R}}),\quad L:H^{q}(X,\mathbb{C})\to H^{q+2}(X,\mathbb{C})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Referring to de Rham cohomology, the action of <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is represented
on the level of forms as <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi\mapsto\varphi\wedge\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>∧</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi\mapsto\varphi\wedge\omega</annotation></semantics></math>. Since
<math id="S2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> is closed, the image of a closed form (resp. a boundary) is
closed (resp. a boundary). Let
<math id="S2.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=\dim X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=\dim X</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.1</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The primitive cohomology subspaces are defined
as:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="H_{prim}^{n-i}({\mathbb{R}}):=Ker(L^{i+1}:H^{n-i}(X,{\mathbb{R}})\to H^{n+i+2}(X,{\mathbb{R}}))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mi>K</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{prim}^{n-i}({\mathbb{R}}):=Ker(L^{i+1}:H^{n-i}(X,{\mathbb{R}})\to H^{n+i+2}(X,{\mathbb{R}}))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">and similarly for complex coefficients
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\rm prim}^{n-i}(\mathbb{C})\simeq H_{\rm prim}^{n-i}({\mathbb{R}})\otimes_{{\mathbb{R}}}\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>prim</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>prim</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\rm prim}^{n-i}(\mathbb{C})\simeq H_{\rm prim}^{n-i}({\mathbb{R}})\otimes_{{\mathbb{R}}}\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The operator <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is compatible with Hodge decomposition since it sends the subspace
<math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{p,q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{p,q}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{p+1,q+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{p+1,q+1}</annotation></semantics></math>. We shall say that the morphism <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is of Hodge type <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,1)</annotation></semantics></math>; hence
the action <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{i+1}:H^{n-i}(X,\mathbb{C})\to H^{n+i+2}(X,\mathbb{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{i+1}:H^{n-i}(X,\mathbb{C})\to H^{n+i+2}(X,\mathbb{C})</annotation></semantics></math> is a morphism of Hodge type <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i+1,i+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i+1,i+1)</annotation></semantics></math>, and the kernel is endowed with an induced
Hodge decomposition. This is a strong
condition on the primitive subspace,
since if we
let:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{p,q}_{\rm prim}:=H^{p+q}_{\rm prim}\cap H^{p,q}(X,\mathbb{C})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>prim</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>prim</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{p,q}_{\rm prim}:=H^{p+q}_{\rm prim}\cap H^{p,q}(X,\mathbb{C}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">then:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="H_{\rm prim}^{i}(X,\mathbb{C})=\oplus_{p+q=i}H^{p,q}_{prim}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>prim</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><msub><mo lspace="0em">⊕</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\rm prim}^{i}(X,\mathbb{C})=\oplus_{p+q=i}H^{p,q}_{prim}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The following isomorphism, referred to as Hard Lefschetz Theorem, puts a
strong condition on the cohomology of projective, and more generally
compact Kähler, manifolds
and gives rise to a decomposition of the cohomology in terms of
primitive subspaces:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2.2</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> be a compact Kähler manifold.
<br class="ltx_break">i) </span>Hard Lefschetz Theorem<span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.
The iterated linear operator
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> induces isomorphisms for each <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{i}:H^{n-i}(X,{\mathbb{R}})\xrightarrow{\sim}H^{n+i}(X,{\mathbb{R}}),\,L^{i}:H^{n-i}(X,\mathbb{C})\xrightarrow{\sim}H^{n+i}(X,\mathbb{C})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo></mover><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo></mover><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{i}:H^{n-i}(X,{\mathbb{R}})\xrightarrow{\sim}H^{n+i}(X,{\mathbb{R}}),\,L^{i}:H^{n-i}(X,\mathbb{C})\xrightarrow{\sim}H^{n+i}(X,\mathbb{C})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">ii) </span>Lefschetz Decomposition<span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">. The cohomology decomposes into
a direct sum of image of primitive subspaces by <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{r},\,r\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{r},\,r\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="H^{q}(X,{\mathbb{R}})=\oplus_{r\geq 0}L^{r}H_{\rm prim}^{q-2r}({\mathbb{R}}),\quad H^{q}(X,\mathbb{C})=\oplus_{r\geq 0}L^{r}H_{\rm prim}^{q-2r}(\mathbb{C})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><msub><mo lspace="0em">⊕</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mi>L</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>prim</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><msub><mo lspace="0em">⊕</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mi>L</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>prim</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{q}(X,{\mathbb{R}})=\oplus_{r\geq 0}L^{r}H_{\rm prim}^{q-2r}({\mathbb{R}}),\quad H^{q}(X,\mathbb{C})=\oplus_{r\geq 0}L^{r}H_{\rm prim}^{q-2r}(\mathbb{C})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The Lefschetz decomposition is compatible with Hodge decomposition.
<br class="ltx_break">iii) If <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is moreover projective, then the action of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is
defined with rational coefficients and the decomposition applies to
rational cohomology.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.1.1. </span>Hermitian product on cohomology</h4>

<div id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">From the isomorphism in the Hard Lefschetz theorem and
Poincaré duality, we deduce a scalar product on cohomology of smooth complex projective varieties compatible
with Hodge Structures and satisfying relations known as Hodge
Riemann relations leading to a polarization of the primitive
cohomology which is an additional highly rich structure characteristic of such varieties.
<br class="ltx_break">Representing
cohomology classes by differential forms, we define a bilinear
form:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q(\alpha,\beta)=(-1)^{\frac{j(j-1)}{2}}\int_{X}\alpha\wedge\beta\wedge\omega^{n-j},\quad\forall[\alpha],[\beta]\in H^{j}(X,\mathbb{C})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><mi>X</mi></msub><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>∧</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mo>∀</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q(\alpha,\beta)=(-1)^{\frac{j(j-1)}{2}}\int_{X}\alpha\wedge\beta\wedge\omega^{n-j},\quad\forall[\alpha],[\beta]\in H^{j}(X,\mathbb{C})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> is the Kähler class, the product of <math id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>
with <math id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega^{n-j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega^{n-j}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the action of <math id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{n-j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{n-j}</annotation></semantics></math> and the integral
of the product with <math id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> represents Poincaré duality.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Properties of the product.</span>
The above product <math id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hbox{Q}(\alpha,\beta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Q</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hbox{Q}(\alpha,\beta)</annotation></semantics></math>
depends only on the class of <math id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>.
The following properties are satisfied:
<br class="ltx_break">i) the product <span id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">Q</span> is real ( it takes real values on real forms) since <math id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> is real, in other terms the matrix of <span id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">Q</span> is real,
skew-symmetric if <math id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> is odd and symmetric
if <math id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> is even;
<br class="ltx_break">ii) It is non degenerate, by Lefschetz isomorphism and Poincaré
duality;
<br class="ltx_break">iii) By consideration of type, the Hodge and Lefschetz
decompositions are orthogonal relative to <span id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">Q</span>:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hbox{Q}(H^{p,q},H^{p^{\prime},q^{\prime}})=0,\quad\mbox{unless}\,\,p=p^{\prime},q=q^{\prime}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Q</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>unless</mtext><mo lspace="0.340em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hbox{Q}(H^{p,q},H^{p^{\prime},q^{\prime}})=0,\quad\mbox{unless}\,\,p=p^{\prime},q=q^{\prime}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">On projective varieties the Kähler class is
in the integral lattice defined by cohomology with coefficients in <math id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>, hence the product
is defined on rational cohomology and preserves the integral lattice.
In this case we have more precise positivity relations in terms of
the primitive component <math id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{prim}^{p,q}(\mathbb{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{prim}^{p,q}(\mathbb{C})</annotation></semantics></math> of the cohomology
<math id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{p+q}(X,\mathbb{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{p+q}(X,\mathbb{C})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 2.3</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Hodge-Riemann bilinear relations)<span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The product <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i^{p-q}Q(\alpha,\overline{\alpha})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i^{p-q}Q(\alpha,\overline{\alpha})</annotation></semantics></math> is positive definite on the
primitive component <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{prim}^{p,q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{prim}^{p,q}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S2.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i^{p-q}Q(\alpha,\overline{\alpha})&gt;0,\quad\forall\alpha\in H_{prim}^{p,q},\,\alpha\neq 0" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i^{p-q}Q(\alpha,\overline{\alpha})&gt;0,\quad\forall\alpha\in H_{prim}^{p,q},\,\alpha\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.1.2. </span>Summary of the proof of Lefschetz decomposition </h4>

<div id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">1) First, we consider the action of <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> on sheaves,
<math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=\wedge\omega:\mathcal{E}^{r}_{X}\to\mathcal{E}^{r+2}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">∧</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=\wedge\omega:\mathcal{E}^{r}_{X}\to\mathcal{E}^{r+2}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, then
we introduce its formal adjoint operator with respect to the
Hermitian form: <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda=L^{*}:\mathcal{E}^{r}_{X}\to\mathcal{E}^{r-2}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda=L^{*}:\mathcal{E}^{r}_{X}\to\mathcal{E}^{r-2}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> which can
be defined, using the Hodge star operator, by <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Lambda=*^{-1}\circ L\circ*" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∗</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">∘</mo><mo lspace="0em">∗</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda=*^{-1}\circ L\circ*</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that the operator:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h=\sum_{p=0}^{2n}(n-p)\Pi^{p},\quad h(\sum_{p}\omega_{p})=\sum_{p}(n-p)\omega_{p},\quad{\rm for}\,\,\omega_{p}\in\mathcal{E}^{p}_{X}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></munderover><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>p</mi></munder><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mi>p</mi></munder><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>for</mi><mo lspace="0.340em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h=\sum_{p=0}^{2n}(n-p)\Pi^{p},\quad h(\sum_{p}\omega_{p})=\sum_{p}(n-p)\omega_{p},\quad{\rm for}\,\,\omega_{p}\in\mathcal{E}^{p}_{X},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi^{p}</annotation></semantics></math> is the projection in degree <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\oplus_{p\in[0,2n]}\mathcal{E}^{p}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\oplus_{p\in[0,2n]}\mathcal{E}^{p}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=\dim X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=\dim X</annotation></semantics></math>, satisfies the relation:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\Lambda,L]=h." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\Lambda,L]=h.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p2.3" class="ltx_p">from which we can deduce the injectivity of the morphism:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{i}:\mathcal{E}_{X}^{n-i}\rightarrow\mathcal{E}_{X}^{n+i}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{i}:\mathcal{E}_{X}^{n-i}\rightarrow\mathcal{E}_{X}^{n+i}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p2.4" class="ltx_p">For this we use the following formula, for <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in\mathcal{E}_{X}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in\mathcal{E}_{X}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[L^{r},\Lambda](\alpha)=(r(k-n)+r(r-1))L^{r-1}(\alpha)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[L^{r},\Lambda](\alpha)=(r(k-n)+r(r-1))L^{r-1}(\alpha),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p2.5" class="ltx_p">which is proved by induction on <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>. 
<br class="ltx_break">The morphism <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{j}</annotation></semantics></math>
<span id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p2.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">commutes with the Laplacian </span> and, since cohomology classes can be
represented by
global harmonic sections, <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{j}</annotation></semantics></math> induces an isomorphism on
cohomology vector spaces which are of finite equal dimension by Poincaré
duality:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{j}:H^{n-j}(X,{\mathbb{R}})\xrightarrow{\sim}H^{n+j}(X,{\mathbb{R}})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo></mover><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{j}:H^{n-j}(X,{\mathbb{R}})\xrightarrow{\sim}H^{n+j}(X,{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p2.6" class="ltx_p">Moreover the extension of the operator <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> to complex
coefficients is compatible with the bigrading <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p,q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p,q)</annotation></semantics></math> since
<math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> is of type (<math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1,1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1,1</annotation></semantics></math>).
The decomposition of the cohomology into direct sum
of image of primitive subspaces by <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{r},\,r\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{r},\,r\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> follows from
the previous isomorphisms.
<br class="ltx_break">2) Another proof is based on the representation theory of the Lie
algebra <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="sl_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">sl_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. We represent <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> by the action on global sections
of the operator:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=\wedge\omega:\mathcal{E}^{p,q}(X)\to\mathcal{E}^{p+1,q+1}(X)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">∧</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=\wedge\omega:\mathcal{E}^{p,q}(X)\to\mathcal{E}^{p+1,q+1}(X)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p2.7" class="ltx_p">and its adjoint <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda=L^{*}:\mathcal{E}^{p,q}(X)\to\mathcal{E}^{p-1,q-1}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda=L^{*}:\mathcal{E}^{p,q}(X)\to\mathcal{E}^{p-1,q-1}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> is defined by <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p2.m21" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Lambda=*^{-1}\circ L\circ*" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∗</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">∘</mo><mo lspace="0em">∗</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda=*^{-1}\circ L\circ*</annotation></semantics></math>.
We have the relations:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S2.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\Lambda,L]=h,\quad[h,L]=-2L,\quad[h,\Lambda]=2\Lambda" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\Lambda,L]=h,\quad[h,L]=-2L,\quad[h,\Lambda]=2\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Hence, the operators <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> generate a Lie algebra
isomorphic to <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="sl_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">sl_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. We deduce from the action of the operators <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L,\Lambda,h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L,\Lambda,h</annotation></semantics></math> on the
space of harmonic forms <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\oplus\mathcal{H}^{*}_{d}(X)\simeq\oplus H^{*}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0em">≃</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">⊕</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\oplus\mathcal{H}^{*}_{d}(X)\simeq\oplus H^{*}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>, a
representation of the Lie algebra <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="sl_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">sl_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> by identifying the
matrices:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(\begin{array}[]{cc}0&amp;1\\
0&amp;0\\
\end{array}\right),\,\left(\begin{array}[]{cc}0&amp;0\\
1&amp;0\\
\end{array}\right),\,\left(\begin{array}[]{cc}1&amp;0\\
0&amp;-1\\
\end{array}\right)\,\mbox{resp. with}\,\Lambda,\,L,\,h." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>resp. with</mtext><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(\begin{array}[]{cc}0&amp;1\\
0&amp;0\\
\end{array}\right),\,\left(\begin{array}[]{cc}0&amp;0\\
1&amp;0\\
\end{array}\right),\,\left(\begin{array}[]{cc}1&amp;0\\
0&amp;-1\\
\end{array}\right)\,\mbox{resp. with}\,\Lambda,\,L,\,h.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">Then the
theorem follows from the general structure of such
representation (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite> p. 122-124).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS1.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.1.3. </span>Proof of Hodge-Riemann bilinear relations</h4>

<div id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We recall the notion of a primitive form.
A differential form <math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in\Omega^{k}_{X,x,{\mathbb{R}}},k\leq n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in\Omega^{k}_{X,x,{\mathbb{R}}},k\leq n</annotation></semantics></math> is said to be primitive if
<math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{n-k+1}\alpha=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{n-k+1}\alpha=0</annotation></semantics></math>; then every form <math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\in\Omega^{k}_{X,x,{\mathbb{R}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\in\Omega^{k}_{X,x,{\mathbb{R}}}</annotation></semantics></math> decomposes in
a unique way into a direct sum <math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=\sum_{r}L^{r}\beta_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=\sum_{r}L^{r}\beta_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> where each <math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> is primitive of degree <math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k-2r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k-2r</annotation></semantics></math> with the condition <math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k-2r\leq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k-2r\leq</annotation></semantics></math>inf<math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(k,2n-k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(k,2n-k)</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, the decomposition
applies to the complexified forms and it is compatible with bidegrees <math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p,q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p,q)</annotation></semantics></math> of forms.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The following lemma is admitted and is useful in the proof
(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite> prop.6.29)</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.4</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma\in\Omega^{q,p}_{X,x}\subset\mathcal{E}^{j}_{X,x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>⊂</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mi>j</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma\in\Omega^{q,p}_{X,x}\subset\mathcal{E}^{j}_{X,x}</annotation></semantics></math> be a primitive element, then</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="*\gamma=(-1)^{\frac{j(j+1)}{2}}i^{q-p}\frac{L^{n-j}}{(n-j)!}\gamma" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">*\gamma=(-1)^{\frac{j(j+1)}{2}}i^{q-p}\frac{L^{n-j}}{(n-j)!}\gamma</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">It is applied to <math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> in the definition of the product <math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> as follows.
We represent a primitive cohomolgy class by a primitive
harmonic form <math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> of degree <math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j=p+q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j=p+q</annotation></semantics></math>, then we write
the product in terms of the <math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{2}-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{2}-</annotation></semantics></math>norm, as follows</p>
<table id="S2.Ex19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\hbox{F}(\alpha,\alpha)=i^{p-q}Q(\alpha,\overline{\alpha})=(n-j)!\int_{X}\tilde{\alpha}\wedge*\overline{\tilde{\alpha}}\\
=(n-j)!||\tilde{\alpha}||^{2}_{L^{2}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>F</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo><msub><mo>∫</mo><mi>X</mi></msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo rspace="0em">∧</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mover accent="true"><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msubsup><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hbox{F}(\alpha,\alpha)=i^{p-q}Q(\alpha,\overline{\alpha})=(n-j)!\int_{X}\tilde{\alpha}\wedge*\overline{\tilde{\alpha}}\\
=(n-j)!||\tilde{\alpha}||^{2}_{L^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Indeed, since <math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(-1)^{j}i^{q-p}=(-1)^{q-p}i^{q-p}=i^{p-q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(-1)^{j}i^{q-p}=(-1)^{q-p}i^{q-p}=i^{p-q}</annotation></semantics></math> we deduce from the lemma
<math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="L^{n-j}\overline{\tilde{\alpha}}=i^{q-p}(n-j)!(-1)^{\frac{j(j-1)}{2}}(*\overline{\tilde{\alpha}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup><mover accent="true"><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mover accent="true"><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{n-j}\overline{\tilde{\alpha}}=i^{q-p}(n-j)!(-1)^{\frac{j(j-1)}{2}}(*\overline{\tilde{\alpha}})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">This result suggest to introduce the Weil operator <math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> on
cohomology:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C(\alpha)=i^{p-q}\alpha,\quad\forall\alpha\in H^{p,q}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(\alpha)=i^{p-q}\alpha,\quad\forall\alpha\in H^{p,q}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p5.2" class="ltx_p">Notice that <math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is a real operator since for a real vector <math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v=\sum_{p+q=j}v^{p,q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v=\sum_{p+q=j}v^{p,q}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v^{q,p}=\overline{v^{p,q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><msup><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v^{q,p}=\overline{v^{p,q}}</annotation></semantics></math>, hence
<math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{Cv}=\sum\overline{i^{p-q}v^{p,q}}=\sum\overline{i}^{p-q}v^{q,p}=i^{q-p}v^{q,p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mo>∑</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><msup><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{Cv}=\sum\overline{i^{p-q}v^{p,q}}=\sum\overline{i}^{p-q}v^{q,p}=i^{q-p}v^{q,p}</annotation></semantics></math>, as <math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{i}^{p-q}=i^{q-p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{i}^{p-q}=i^{q-p}</annotation></semantics></math>. It depends on the decomposition, in
particular the action of <math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> in a variation of Hodge structure is
differentiable in the parameter space. We deduce from <math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> a new
non degenerate Hermitian product:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\hbox{F}(\alpha,\beta)=Q(C(\alpha),\overline{\beta}),\,\,\hbox{F}(\beta,\alpha)=\overline{\hbox{F}(\alpha,\beta)}\quad\forall[\alpha],[\beta]\in H^{j}(X,\mathbb{C})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>F</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.507em">,</mo><mtext>F</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><mtext>F</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>∀</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hbox{F}(\alpha,\beta)=Q(C(\alpha),\overline{\beta}),\,\,\hbox{F}(\beta,\alpha)=\overline{\hbox{F}(\alpha,\beta)}\quad\forall[\alpha],[\beta]\in H^{j}(X,\mathbb{C})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p5.3" class="ltx_p">We use <math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{Q(\alpha,\beta)}=Q(\overline{\alpha},\overline{\beta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{Q(\alpha,\beta)}=Q(\overline{\alpha},\overline{\beta})</annotation></semantics></math> since <math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> is real,
to check for <math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha,\beta\in H^{p,q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha,\beta\in H^{p,q}</annotation></semantics></math>:
<br class="ltx_break"><math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\hbox{F}(\alpha,\beta)}=\overline{Q(i^{p-q}\alpha,\overline{\beta})}=\overline{i}^{p-q}Q(\overline{\alpha},\beta)=(-1)^{j}i^{p-q}Q(\overline{\alpha},\beta)=(-1)^{2j}i^{p-q}Q(\beta,\overline{\alpha})=\hbox{F}(\beta,\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mrow><mtext>F</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>F</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\hbox{F}(\alpha,\beta)}=\overline{Q(i^{p-q}\alpha,\overline{\beta})}=\overline{i}^{p-q}Q(\overline{\alpha},\beta)=(-1)^{j}i^{p-q}Q(\overline{\alpha},\beta)=(-1)^{2j}i^{p-q}Q(\beta,\overline{\alpha})=\hbox{F}(\beta,\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 2.5</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">i) When the class <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\omega]\in H^{j}(X,\mathbb{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\omega]\in H^{j}(X,\mathbb{Z})</annotation></semantics></math> is integral, which is the case for algebraic varieties,
the product <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> is integral, i.e., with integral value on integral
classes.
<br class="ltx_break">ii) The integral bilinear form <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> extends by linearity to the
complex space <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}_{\mathbb{Z}}\otimes\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}_{\mathbb{Z}}\otimes\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>. Its associated form
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hbox{F}(\alpha,\beta):=Q(\alpha,\overline{\beta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>F</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hbox{F}(\alpha,\beta):=Q(\alpha,\overline{\beta})</annotation></semantics></math> is not Hermitian
if <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is odd. One way to overcome this sign problem is to define
<span id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">F</span> as <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hbox{F}(\alpha,\beta):=i^{k}Q(\alpha,\overline{\beta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>F</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hbox{F}(\alpha,\beta):=i^{k}Q(\alpha,\overline{\beta})</annotation></semantics></math>,
still this form will not be positive definite in general.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS1.SSS4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.1.4. </span>Projective case</h4>

<div id="S2.SS1.SSS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the special projective case
we can choose the fundamental class of an hyperplane section to
represent the Kähler class <math id="S2.SS1.SSS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\omega]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\omega]</annotation></semantics></math>, hence we have an
integral representative of the class <math id="S2.SS1.SSS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\omega]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\omega]</annotation></semantics></math> in the image of <math id="S2.SS1.SSS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}(X,\mathbb{Z})\to H^{2}(X,\mathbb{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}(X,\mathbb{Z})\to H^{2}(X,\mathbb{C})</annotation></semantics></math>,
which has an important consequence since the operator <math id="S2.SS1.SSS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L:H^{q}(X,\mathbb{Q})\to H^{q+2}(X,\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L:H^{q}(X,\mathbb{Q})\to H^{q+2}(X,\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> acts on rational cohomology.
This fact characterizes projective varieties among compact Kähler
manifolds since a theorem of Kodaira
( <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite> chap. VI)
states that a Kähler variety
with an integral class <math id="S2.SS1.SSS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\omega]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\omega]</annotation></semantics></math> is projective, i.e., it can be
embedded as a closed analytic subvariety in a projective space,
hence by Chow Lemma it is necessarily an algebraic subvariety.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 2.6</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> </span>(Topological interpretation)<span id="S2.Thmtheorem6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the projective case, the class <math id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\omega]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\omega]</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to the
homology class of an hyperplane section <math id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[H]\in H_{2n-2}(X,\mathbb{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[H]\in H_{2n-2}(X,\mathbb{Z})</annotation></semantics></math>, so that the operator <math id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to
the intersection with <math id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[H]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[H]</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and
the result is an isomorphism:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{n+k}(X)\xrightarrow{(\cap[H])^{k}}H_{n-k}(X)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="0.700em">∩</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">[</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">H</mi><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow><mi mathsize="0.710em">k</mi></msup></mover><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{n+k}(X)\xrightarrow{(\cap[H])^{k}}H_{n-k}(X)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The primitive cohomology <math id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{prim}^{n-k}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{prim}^{n-k}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to the
image of:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{n-k}(X-H,\mathbb{Q})\rightarrow H_{n-k}(X,\mathbb{Q})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{n-k}(X-H,\mathbb{Q})\rightarrow H_{n-k}(X,\mathbb{Q}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.3" class="ltx_p">In his original work, Lefschetz used the hyperplane section of a projective variety
in homology to investigate the decomposition theorem proved later in the above analysis
setting.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.7</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The cohomology of a projective complex smooth
variety with its Hodge decomposition and the above positive definite Hermitian product is called a polarized Hodge Structure.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2. </span>The category of Hodge Structures</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">It is rewarding to
introduce the Hodge decomposition as a formal structure in
linear algebra without any reference to its construction.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.8</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A Hodge structure (HS) of weight <math id="S2.Thmtheorem8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is defined
by the data: 
<br class="ltx_break">i) A finitely generated abelian group <math id="S2.Thmtheorem8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math>; 
<br class="ltx_break">ii) A decomposition by complex subspaces:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex24.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="H_{\mathbb{C}}:=H_{\mathbb{Z}}\otimes_{\mathbb{Z}}\mathbb{C}=\oplus_{p+q=n}H^{p,q}\,\,\,\,\mbox{satisfying }\,\,\,\,H^{p,q}=\overline{H^{q,p}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mi>H</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mi>ℂ</mi><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">⊕</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mi>H</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mtext>satisfying </mtext><mi>H</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\mathbb{C}}:=H_{\mathbb{Z}}\otimes_{\mathbb{Z}}\mathbb{C}=\oplus_{p+q=n}H^{p,q}\,\,\,\,\mbox{satisfying }\,\,\,\,H^{p,q}=\overline{H^{q,p}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The conjugation on <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math> makes sense with respect to
<math id="S2.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math>. A subspace <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\subset H_{\mathbb{C}}:=H_{\mathbb{Z}}\otimes_{\mathbb{Z}}\mathbb{C}\," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\subset H_{\mathbb{C}}:=H_{\mathbb{Z}}\otimes_{\mathbb{Z}}\mathbb{C}\,</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{V}=V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{V}=V</annotation></semantics></math> has a real structure, that is
<math id="S2.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V=(V\cap H_{{\mathbb{R}}})\otimes_{{\mathbb{R}}}\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℝ</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V=(V\cap H_{{\mathbb{R}}})\otimes_{{\mathbb{R}}}\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{p,p}=(H^{p,p}\cap H_{{\mathbb{R}}})\otimes_{{\mathbb{R}}}\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℝ</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{p,p}=(H^{p,p}\cap H_{{\mathbb{R}}})\otimes_{{\mathbb{R}}}\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We may suppose that <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a free abelian group (the lattice),
if we are interested only in its image in <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\mathbb{Q}}:=H_{\mathbb{Z}}\otimes_{\mathbb{Z}}\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℚ</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\mathbb{Q}}:=H_{\mathbb{Z}}\otimes_{\mathbb{Z}}\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">With such an abstract definition we can perform linear algebra
operations on a Hodge Structure and define morphisms. We remark that <span id="S2.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Poincaré duality</span>
is compatible with the Hodge Structure defined by Hodge decomposition, after an adequate shift in the weight (see below).
We deduce from the previous results the notion of polarization:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.9</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Polarization of HS)<span id="S2.Thmtheorem9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A Hodge structure
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(H_{\mathbb{Z}},(H_{\mathbb{C}}\simeq\oplus_{p+q=n}H^{p,q}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo rspace="0em">≃</mo><msub><mo lspace="0em">⊕</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(H_{\mathbb{Z}},(H_{\mathbb{C}}\simeq\oplus_{p+q=n}H^{p,q}))</annotation></semantics></math> of weight <math id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is
polarized if a non-degenerate scalar product <math id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> is defined on
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math>, alternating if <math id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is odd and symmetric if <math id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is even,
such that the terms <math id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{p,q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{p,q}</annotation></semantics></math> of the Hodge decomposition are
orthogonal to each other relative to the
Hermitian form <span id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">F</span> on <math id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math> defined as
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hbox{F}(\alpha,\beta):=i^{p-q}Q(\alpha,\overline{\beta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>F</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hbox{F}(\alpha,\beta):=i^{p-q}Q(\alpha,\overline{\beta})</annotation></semantics></math> for
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha,\beta\in H^{p,q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha,\beta\in H^{p,q}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hbox{F}(\alpha,{\alpha})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>F</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hbox{F}(\alpha,{\alpha})</annotation></semantics></math> is positive definite
on the component of type <math id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p,q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p,q)</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., it satisfies the Hodge-Riemann
bilinear relations.</p>
</div>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.2.1. </span>Equivalent definition of HS </h4>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">(see <a href="#S2.SS2.SSS5" title="2.2.5. Proof of the equivalence with the action of the group 
            
              S ‣ 2.2. The category of Hodge Structures ‣ 2. Lefschetz decomposition and Polarized Hodge structure ‣ Mixed Hodge Structures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2.5</span></a> below) Let <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{S}}({\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕊</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{S}}({\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the
subgroup:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex25.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\mathbb{S}}({\mathbb{R}})=\left\{M(u,v)=\begin{pmatrix}u&amp;-v\\
v&amp;u\end{pmatrix}\in GL(2,{\mathbb{R}}),\quad u,v\in{\mathbb{R}}\right\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕊</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>M</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mi>u</mi></mtd><mtd><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>v</mi></mtd><mtd><mi>u</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>G</mi><mi>L</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{S}}({\mathbb{R}})=\left\{M(u,v)=\begin{pmatrix}u&amp;-v\\
v&amp;u\end{pmatrix}\in GL(2,{\mathbb{R}}),\quad u,v\in{\mathbb{R}}\right\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">It is isomorphic to
<math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> via the group homomorphism <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(u,v)\mapsto z=u+iv" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(u,v)\mapsto z=u+iv</annotation></semantics></math>.
<br class="ltx_break">The interest in this isomorphism is to put the structure of a real
algebraic group on <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>; indeed the set of matrices <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(u,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(u,v)</annotation></semantics></math> is
a real algebraic subgroup of <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="GL(2,{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">GL(2,{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.10</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem10.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A rational Hodge Structure of weight <math id="S2.Thmtheorem10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>, is defined by
a <math id="S2.Thmtheorem10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>−</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}-</annotation></semantics></math>vector space <math id="S2.Thmtheorem10.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> and a representation of real algebraic
groups <math id="S2.Thmtheorem10.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi:{\mathbb{S}}({\mathbb{R}})\to GL(H_{{\mathbb{R}}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝕊</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi:{\mathbb{S}}({\mathbb{R}})\to GL(H_{{\mathbb{R}}})</annotation></semantics></math> such that
for <math id="S2.Thmtheorem10.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in{\mathbb{R}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in{\mathbb{R}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.Thmtheorem10.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(t)(v)=t^{m}v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(t)(v)=t^{m}v</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S2.Thmtheorem10.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in H_{{\mathbb{R}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℝ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in H_{{\mathbb{R}}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.2.2. </span>The Hodge filtration.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To study variations of Hodge Structure, Griffiths introduced an equivalent
structure defined by the Hodge filtration
which varies holomorphically with parameters.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem11" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem11.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.11</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem11.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given a Hodge Structure (<math id="S2.Thmtheorem11.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\mathbb{Z}},H^{p,q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\mathbb{Z}},H^{p,q}</annotation></semantics></math>)
of weight <math id="S2.Thmtheorem11.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, we define a decreasing filtration <math id="S2.Thmtheorem11.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> by subspaces of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem11.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem11.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S2.Ex26" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex26.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="F^{p}H_{\mathbb{C}}:=\oplus_{r\geq p}H^{r,n-r}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></msup><mi>H</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo rspace="0em">:=</mo><mo lspace="0em">⊕</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mi>H</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{p}H_{\mathbb{C}}:=\oplus_{r\geq p}H^{r,n-r}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Then, the following decomposition is satisfied:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S2.Ex27" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\mathbb{C}}=F^{p}H_{\mathbb{C}}\oplus\overline{F^{n-p+1}H_{\mathbb{C}}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\mathbb{C}}=F^{p}H_{\mathbb{C}}\oplus\overline{F^{n-p+1}H_{\mathbb{C}}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The Hodge decomposition may be recovered from the filtration by
the formula:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S2.Ex28" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{p,q}=F^{p}H_{\mathbb{C}}\cap\overline{F^{q}H_{\mathbb{C}}},\quad{\rm for}\,p+q=n." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>for</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{p,q}=F^{p}H_{\mathbb{C}}\cap\overline{F^{q}H_{\mathbb{C}}},\quad{\rm for}\,p+q=n.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Hence, we obtain an equivalent definition of Hodge Structures, which played an
important role in the development of Hodge theory, since we will
see in chapter <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math> that the Hodge filtration exists naturally on
the cohomology of any complex proper smooth algebraic variety <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>
and it is induced by the trivial filtration on the de Rham
complex which may be defined algebraically.
However, the Hodge decomposition itself is still deduced
from the existence of a birational projective variety over <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and
the decomposition on the cohomology of this projective variety
viewed as a Kähler manifold.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.2.3. </span>Linear algebra operations on Hodge structures</h4>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The interest of the above abstract definition of Hodge Structures is to apply
classical linear algebra operations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem12" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem12.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.12</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem12.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem12.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A morphism <math id="S2.Thmtheorem12.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:H=(H_{\mathbb{Z}},H^{p,q})\rightarrow H^{\prime}=(H^{\prime}_{\mathbb{Z}},H^{\prime p,q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">′</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:H=(H_{\mathbb{Z}},H^{p,q})\rightarrow H^{\prime}=(H^{\prime}_{\mathbb{Z}},H^{\prime p,q})</annotation></semantics></math>
of Hodge Structures, is an
homomorphism <math id="S2.Thmtheorem12.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon H_{\mathbb{Z}}\rightarrow{H^{\prime}}_{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>H</mi><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon H_{\mathbb{Z}}\rightarrow{H^{\prime}}_{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math> such that
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem12.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{{\mathbb{C}}}\colon H_{{\mathbb{C}}}\rightarrow{H^{\prime}}_{{\mathbb{C}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>H</mi><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{{\mathbb{C}}}\colon H_{{\mathbb{C}}}\rightarrow{H^{\prime}}_{{\mathbb{C}}}</annotation></semantics></math> is compatible
with the decompositions, i.e, for any <math id="S2.Thmtheorem12.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p,q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p,q</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.Thmtheorem12.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math> induces a <math id="S2.Thmtheorem12.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℂ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>-linear map
from <math id="S2.Thmtheorem12.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{p,q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{p,q}</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S2.Thmtheorem12.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\prime p,q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">′</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\prime p,q}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Therefore Hodge Structures form a category whose objects are Hodge Structures and morphisms
are morphisms of Hodge Structures that we have just defined. We have the following important
result:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem13" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem13.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 2.13</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem13.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem13.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem13.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem13.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The Hodge Structures of same weight <math id="S2.Thmtheorem13.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> form an abelian category.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In
particular, the decomposition on the kernel (resp. cokernel) of a
morphism <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi:H\to H^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi:H\to H^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is induced by the decomposition of
<math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> (resp. <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>). For a general sub-vector space <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>, the
induced subspaces <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{p,q}\cap V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo>∩</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{p,q}\cap V</annotation></semantics></math> do not define a decomposition
on <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> are of distinct weights, <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is
necessarily <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.2.4. </span>Tensor product and Hom </h4>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be
two HS of weight <math id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.
<br class="ltx_break">1) We define their Hodge Structure tensor product
<math id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H\otimes H^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H\otimes H^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> of weight <math id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n+n^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+n^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> as follows:
<br class="ltx_break">i) <math id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(H\otimes H^{\prime})_{\mathbb{Z}}=H_{\mathbb{Z}}\otimes H^{\prime}_{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(H\otimes H^{\prime})_{\mathbb{Z}}=H_{\mathbb{Z}}\otimes H^{\prime}_{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math> 
<br class="ltx_break">ii) the bigrading
of <math id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(H\otimes H^{\prime})_{{\mathbb{C}}}=H_{{\mathbb{C}}}\otimes H^{\prime}_{{\mathbb{C}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(H\otimes H^{\prime})_{{\mathbb{C}}}=H_{{\mathbb{C}}}\otimes H^{\prime}_{{\mathbb{C}}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the
tensor product of the bigradings
of <math id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{{\mathbb{C}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{{\mathbb{C}}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\prime}_{{\mathbb{C}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\prime}_{{\mathbb{C}}}</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p1.m11" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(H\otimes H^{\prime})^{a,b}:=\oplus_{p+p^{\prime}=a,q+q^{\prime}=b}H^{p,q}\otimes H^{\prime p^{\prime},q^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0em">:=</mo><msub><mo lspace="0em">⊕</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">′</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(H\otimes H^{\prime})^{a,b}:=\oplus_{p+p^{\prime}=a,q+q^{\prime}=b}H^{p,q}\otimes H^{\prime p^{\prime},q^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem14" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem14.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 2.14</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem14.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem14.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem14.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Tate Hodge structure <math id="S2.Thmtheorem14.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}(1)</annotation></semantics></math> is a Hodge Structure of weight <math id="S2.Thmtheorem14.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-2</annotation></semantics></math>
defined by:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex29" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\mathbb{Z}}=2i\pi\mathbb{Z}\subset\mathbb{C},\quad H_{\mathbb{C}}=H^{-1,-1}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\mathbb{Z}}=2i\pi\mathbb{Z}\subset\mathbb{C},\quad H_{\mathbb{C}}=H^{-1,-1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem14.p1.2" class="ltx_p">It is purely bigraded of type <math id="S2.Thmtheorem14.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(-1,-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(-1,-1)</annotation></semantics></math> of rank <math id="S2.Thmtheorem14.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>.
The <math id="S2.Thmtheorem14.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m-</annotation></semantics></math>tensor product <math id="S2.Thmtheorem14.p1.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{Z}(1)\otimes\cdots\otimes\mathbb{Z}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊗</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}(1)\otimes\cdots\otimes\mathbb{Z}(1)</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem14.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}(1)</annotation></semantics></math> is a Hodge Structure of weight <math id="S2.Thmtheorem14.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-2m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-2m</annotation></semantics></math> denoted by <math id="S2.Thmtheorem14.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}(m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}(m)</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex30" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\mathbb{Z}}=(2i\pi)^{m}\mathbb{Z}\subset\mathbb{C},\quad H_{\mathbb{C}}=H^{-m,-m}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>m</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\mathbb{Z}}=(2i\pi)^{m}\mathbb{Z}\subset\mathbb{C},\quad H_{\mathbb{C}}=H^{-m,-m}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem14.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem14.p1.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="H=(H_{\mathbb{Z}},\oplus_{p+q=n}H^{p,q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><msub><mo lspace="0em">⊕</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H=(H_{\mathbb{Z}},\oplus_{p+q=n}H^{p,q})</annotation></semantics></math> be a Hodge Structure of weight <math id="S2.Thmtheorem14.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>,
its <math id="S2.Thmtheorem14.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m-</annotation></semantics></math>twist is a Hodge Structure of weight <math id="S2.Thmtheorem14.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n-2m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n-2m</annotation></semantics></math> denoted <math id="S2.Thmtheorem14.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(m)</annotation></semantics></math> and
defined by</p>
<table id="S2.Ex31" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(m)_{\mathbb{Z}}:=H_{\mathbb{Z}}\otimes(2i\pi)^{m}\mathbb{Z},\quad H(m)^{p,q}:=H^{p+m,q+m}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>ℤ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>:=</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(m)_{\mathbb{Z}}:=H_{\mathbb{Z}}\otimes(2i\pi)^{m}\mathbb{Z},\quad H(m)^{p,q}:=H^{p+m,q+m}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">2) Similarly, <math id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\wedge^{p}H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∧</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></msup><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\wedge^{p}H</annotation></semantics></math> is a Hodge Structure of weight <math id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="pn" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">pn</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">3) We define a Hodge Structure on <math id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Hom(H,H^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Hom(H,H^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> of weight <math id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n^{\prime}-n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n^{\prime}-n</annotation></semantics></math> as follows:
<br class="ltx_break">i) <math id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Hom(H,H^{\prime})_{\mathbb{Z}}:=Hom_{\mathbb{Z}}(H_{\mathbb{Z}},H^{\prime}_{\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>ℤ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Hom(H,H^{\prime})_{\mathbb{Z}}:=Hom_{\mathbb{Z}}(H_{\mathbb{Z}},H^{\prime}_{\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math> ;
<br class="ltx_break">ii) the decomposition of <math id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Hom(H,H^{\prime})_{\mathbb{C}}:=Hom_{\mathbb{Z}}(H_{\mathbb{Z}},H^{\prime}_{\mathbb{Z}})\otimes\mathbb{C}\simeq Hom_{\mathbb{C}}(H_{\mathbb{C}},H^{\prime}_{\mathbb{C}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>ℂ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Hom(H,H^{\prime})_{\mathbb{C}}:=Hom_{\mathbb{Z}}(H_{\mathbb{Z}},H^{\prime}_{\mathbb{Z}})\otimes\mathbb{C}\simeq Hom_{\mathbb{C}}(H_{\mathbb{C}},H^{\prime}_{\mathbb{C}})</annotation></semantics></math> is given by
<math id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p3.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="Hom(H,H^{\prime})^{a,b}:=\oplus_{p^{\prime}-p=a,q^{\prime}-q=b}Hom_{\mathbb{C}}(H^{p,q},H^{p^{\prime},q^{\prime}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mi>o</mi><mi>m</mi><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0em">:=</mo><msub><mo lspace="0em">⊕</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mi>H</mi><mi>o</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Hom(H,H^{\prime})^{a,b}:=\oplus_{p^{\prime}-p=a,q^{\prime}-q=b}Hom_{\mathbb{C}}(H^{p,q},H^{p^{\prime},q^{\prime}})</annotation></semantics></math>.
<br class="ltx_break">In particular the dual <math id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>
is a Hodge Structure of weight <math id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-n</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem15" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem15.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 2.15</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem15.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The group of morphisms of Hodge Structures is called the internal morphism group
of the category of Hodge Structures and denoted by
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Hom_{HS}(H,H^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Hom_{HS}(H,H^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>; it is the sub-group of
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Hom_{{\mathbb{Z}}}(H_{{\mathbb{Z}}},H^{\prime}_{\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Hom_{{\mathbb{Z}}}(H_{{\mathbb{Z}}},H^{\prime}_{\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math>
of elements of type <math id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(0,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(0,0)</annotation></semantics></math> in the Hodge Structure on <math id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Hom(H,H^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Hom(H,H^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>.
A homomorphism of type
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r,r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r,r)</annotation></semantics></math> is a morphism of the Hodge Structures: <math id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H\to H^{\prime}(-r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H\to H^{\prime}(-r)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Poincaré duality.</span> If we consider the following trace
map:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex32" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2n}(X,\mathbb{C})\xrightarrow{\sim}\mathbb{C}(-n),\quad\omega\to\frac{1}{(2i\pi)^{n}}\int_{X}\omega." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo></mover><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><mi>X</mi></msub><mi>ω</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2n}(X,\mathbb{C})\xrightarrow{\sim}\mathbb{C}(-n),\quad\omega\to\frac{1}{(2i\pi)^{n}}\int_{X}\omega.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p4.2" class="ltx_p">then Poincaré duality will be compatible with Hodge Structures:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex33" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{n-i}(X,\mathbb{C})\simeq Hom(H^{n+i}(X,\mathbb{C}),\mathbb{C}(-n))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{n-i}(X,\mathbb{C})\simeq Hom(H^{n+i}(X,\mathbb{C}),\mathbb{C}(-n)).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p4.3" class="ltx_p">The Hodge Structure on homology is defined by duality:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex34" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(H_{i}(X,\mathbb{C}),F)\simeq Hom((H^{i}(X,\mathbb{C}),F),\mathbb{C})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(H_{i}(X,\mathbb{C}),F)\simeq Hom((H^{i}(X,\mathbb{C}),F),\mathbb{C})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p4.4" class="ltx_p">with <math id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℂ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math> pure of
weight <math id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>, hence <math id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{i}(X,\mathbb{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{i}(X,\mathbb{Z})</annotation></semantics></math> is pure of weight <math id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-i</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, Poincaré duality becomes an isomorphism of Hodge Structures:
<math id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{n+i}(X,\mathbb{C})\simeq H_{n-i}(X,\mathbb{C})(-n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{n+i}(X,\mathbb{C})\simeq H_{n-i}(X,\mathbb{C})(-n)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Gysin morphism.</span> Let <math id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:X\to Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:X\to Y</annotation></semantics></math> be an
algebraic morphism with <math id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim X=n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim X=n</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim Y=m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim Y=m</annotation></semantics></math>, since <math id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}:H^{i}(Y,\mathbb{Q})\to H^{i}(Y,\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}:H^{i}(Y,\mathbb{Q})\to H^{i}(Y,\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> is compatible with Hodge Structures, its Poincaré
dual <math id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gysin(f):H^{j}(Y,\mathbb{Q})\to H^{j+2(m-n)}(Y,\mathbb{Q})(m-n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gysin(f):H^{j}(Y,\mathbb{Q})\to H^{j+2(m-n)}(Y,\mathbb{Q})(m-n)</annotation></semantics></math> is
compatible with Hodge Structures after a shift by <math id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-2(m-n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-2(m-n)</annotation></semantics></math> on the right term.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS5" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.2.5. </span>Proof of the equivalence with the action of the group <math id="S2.SS2.SSS5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{S}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕊</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{S}}</annotation></semantics></math></h4>

<div id="S2.Thmtheorem16" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem16.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.16</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem16.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem16.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem16.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem16.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem16.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(H_{{\mathbb{R}}},F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(H_{{\mathbb{R}}},F)</annotation></semantics></math> be a real Hodge Structure of weight <math id="S2.Thmtheorem16.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>, defined by the
decomposition <math id="S2.Thmtheorem16.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="H_{\mathbb{C}}=\oplus_{p+q=m}H^{p,q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">⊕</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mi>H</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\mathbb{C}}=\oplus_{p+q=m}H^{p,q}</annotation></semantics></math>, then the action
of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem16.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{S}}({\mathbb{R}})=\mathbb{C}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝕊</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{S}}({\mathbb{R}})=\mathbb{C}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.Thmtheorem16.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>, defined by:</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex35" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex35.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(z,v)\mapsto\sum_{p+q=m}z^{p}{\overline{z}}^{q}v_{p,q}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em" stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>q</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(z,v)\mapsto\sum_{p+q=m}z^{p}{\overline{z}}^{q}v_{p,q},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem16.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem16.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">for <math id="S2.Thmtheorem16.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v=\sum_{p+q=m}v_{p,q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v=\sum_{p+q=m}v_{p,q}</annotation></semantics></math>, corresponds to a real representation <math id="S2.Thmtheorem16.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi:{\mathbb{S}}({\mathbb{R}})\to GL(H_{{\mathbb{R}}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝕊</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi:{\mathbb{S}}({\mathbb{R}})\to GL(H_{{\mathbb{R}}})</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying <math id="S2.Thmtheorem16.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(t)(v)=t^{m}v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(t)(v)=t^{m}v</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.Thmtheorem16.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in{\mathbb{R}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in{\mathbb{R}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We prove that <math id="S2.SS2.SSS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\varphi(z)}=\varphi(z),z\in\mathbb{C}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\varphi(z)}=\varphi(z),z\in\mathbb{C}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>,
hence it is defined by a real matrix. We deduce from <math id="S2.SS2.SSS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{v}=\sum_{p+q=m}\overline{v_{p,q}}\in\oplus H^{q,p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo rspace="0em">∈</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">⊕</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{v}=\sum_{p+q=m}\overline{v_{p,q}}\in\oplus H^{q,p}</annotation></semantics></math> that
<math id="S2.SS2.SSS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\overline{v})_{q,p}=\overline{v_{p,q}}\in H^{q,p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\overline{v})_{q,p}=\overline{v_{p,q}}\in H^{q,p}</annotation></semantics></math>, then: 
<br class="ltx_break"><math id="S2.SS2.SSS5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\varphi(z)}(v):=\overline{\varphi(z)(\overline{v})}=\overline{\sum_{p+q=m}z^{q}{\overline{z}}^{p}\overline{v_{p,q}}}=\sum_{p+q=m}z^{p}{\overline{z}}^{q}v_{p,q}=\varphi(z)(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>q</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\varphi(z)}(v):=\overline{\varphi(z)(\overline{v})}=\overline{\sum_{p+q=m}z^{q}{\overline{z}}^{p}\overline{v_{p,q}}}=\sum_{p+q=m}z^{p}{\overline{z}}^{q}v_{p,q}=\varphi(z)(v)</annotation></semantics></math>.
<br class="ltx_break">In particular, for <math id="S2.SS2.SSS5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS2.SSS5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(t)(v)=t^{m}v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(t)(v)=t^{m}v</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S2.SS2.SSS5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(i)(v)=\sum_{p+q=m}i^{p-q}v_{p,q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msup><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(i)(v)=\sum_{p+q=m}i^{p-q}v_{p,q}</annotation></semantics></math> is the Weil operator <math id="S2.SS2.SSS5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.SS2.SSS5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Reciprocally, a representation of the multiplicative commutative
torus:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex36" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex36.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{S}}(\mathbb{C})\to GL(H_{\mathbb{C}})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝕊</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{S}}(\mathbb{C})\to GL(H_{\mathbb{C}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS5.p2.2" class="ltx_p">splits into a direct sum of
representations <math id="S2.SS2.SSS5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{S}}(\mathbb{C})\to GL(H^{p,q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝕊</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{S}}(\mathbb{C})\to GL(H^{p,q})</annotation></semantics></math> acting via <math id="S2.SS2.SSS5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\to z^{p}{\overline{z}}^{q}v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>q</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\to z^{p}{\overline{z}}^{q}v</annotation></semantics></math>, moreover if the representation is real, then
<math id="S2.SS2.SSS5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{H^{p,q}}=H^{q,p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{H^{p,q}}=H^{q,p}</annotation></semantics></math>. The real group <math id="S2.SS2.SSS5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> acts on
<math id="S2.SS2.SSS5.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{p,q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{p,q}</annotation></semantics></math> by multiplication by <math id="S2.SS2.SSS5.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{p+q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{p+q}</annotation></semantics></math>, so that the sum
<math id="S2.SS2.SSS5.p2.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\oplus_{p+q=m}H^{p,q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mi>H</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\oplus_{p+q=m}H^{p,q}</annotation></semantics></math> defines a sub-Hodge Structure of weight <math id="S2.SS2.SSS5.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem17" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem17.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 2.17</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem17.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem17.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem17.p1.1" class="ltx_p">i) The
complex points of:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex37" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex37.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\mathbb{S}}(\mathbb{C})=\left\{M(u,v)=\begin{pmatrix}u&amp;-v\\
v&amp;u\end{pmatrix}\in GL(2,\mathbb{C}),\quad u,v\in\mathbb{C}\right\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕊</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>M</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mi>u</mi></mtd><mtd><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>v</mi></mtd><mtd><mi>u</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>G</mi><mi>L</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{S}}(\mathbb{C})=\left\{M(u,v)=\begin{pmatrix}u&amp;-v\\
v&amp;u\end{pmatrix}\in GL(2,\mathbb{C}),\quad u,v\in\mathbb{C}\right\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem17.p1.2" class="ltx_p">are the matrices with
complex coefficients <math id="S2.Thmtheorem17.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u,v\in\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u,v\in\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math> with determinant <math id="S2.Thmtheorem17.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{2}+v^{2}\not=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{2}+v^{2}\not=0</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem17.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z=u+iv,z^{\prime}=u-iv" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z=u+iv,z^{\prime}=u-iv</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S2.Thmtheorem17.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="zz^{\prime}=u^{2}+v^{2}\not=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">zz^{\prime}=u^{2}+v^{2}\not=0</annotation></semantics></math>
such that <math id="S2.Thmtheorem17.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S(\mathbb{C})\xrightarrow{\sim}\mathbb{C}^{*}\times\mathbb{C}^{*}:(u,v)\mapsto(z,z^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo></mover><mrow><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S(\mathbb{C})\xrightarrow{\sim}\mathbb{C}^{*}\times\mathbb{C}^{*}:(u,v)\mapsto(z,z^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> is an isomorphism satisfying <math id="S2.Thmtheorem17.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z^{\prime}=\overline{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z^{\prime}=\overline{z}</annotation></semantics></math>,
if <math id="S2.Thmtheorem17.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u,v\in{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u,v\in{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math>; in particular <math id="S2.Thmtheorem17.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}^{*}\hookrightarrow\mathbb{C}^{*}\times\mathbb{C}^{*}:t\mapsto(t,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}^{*}\hookrightarrow\mathbb{C}^{*}\times\mathbb{C}^{*}:t\mapsto(t,t)</annotation></semantics></math>.
<br class="ltx_break">ii) We can write <math id="S2.Thmtheorem17.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}^{*}\simeq S^{1}\times{\mathbb{R}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}^{*}\simeq S^{1}\times{\mathbb{R}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> as the product of
the real points of the unitary subgroup <math id="S2.Thmtheorem17.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U(1)</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem17.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{S}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕊</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{S}}</annotation></semantics></math> defined by
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem17.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{2}+v^{2}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{2}+v^{2}=1</annotation></semantics></math> and of the multiplicative subgroup <math id="S2.Thmtheorem17.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{G}_{m}({\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{G}_{m}({\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math>
defined by <math id="S2.Thmtheorem17.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v=0</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.Thmtheorem17.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{S}}({\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕊</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{S}}({\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.Thmtheorem17.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{S}}({\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕊</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{S}}({\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math> is the semi-product
of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem17.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmtheorem17.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the representation gives rise to a
representation of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem17.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> called the scaling since it fixes the
weight and of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem17.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U(1)</annotation></semantics></math> which fixes the decomposition into <math id="S2.Thmtheorem17.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{p,q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{p,q}</annotation></semantics></math>
and on which <math id="S2.Thmtheorem17.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(z)</annotation></semantics></math> acts as multiplication by <math id="S2.Thmtheorem17.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z^{p-q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z^{p-q}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3. </span>Examples</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Cohomology of projective spaces.</span> The polarization is
defined by the first Chern class of the
canonical line bundle <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H=c_{1}({\mathcal{O}}_{\mathbb{P}^{n}}(1))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H=c_{1}({\mathcal{O}}_{\mathbb{P}^{n}}(1))</annotation></semantics></math>, dual to the homology
class of a hyperplane.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem18" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem18.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 2.18</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem18.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem18.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem18.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.Thmtheorem18.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{i}(\mathbb{P}^{n},\mathbb{Z})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{i}(\mathbb{P}^{n},\mathbb{Z})=0</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.Thmtheorem18.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> odd and <math id="S2.Thmtheorem18.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{i}(\mathbb{P}^{n},\mathbb{Z})=\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{i}(\mathbb{P}^{n},\mathbb{Z})=\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.Thmtheorem18.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>
even with generator <math id="S2.Thmtheorem18.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[H]^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[H]^{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, the cup product to the power <math id="S2.Thmtheorem18.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> of
the cohomology class of an hyperplane, hence: <math id="S2.Thmtheorem18.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2r}(\mathbb{P}^{n},\mathbb{Z})=\mathbb{Z}(-r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2r}(\mathbb{P}^{n},\mathbb{Z})=\mathbb{Z}(-r)</annotation></semantics></math> as Hodge Structure.</p>
</div>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS3.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.3.1. </span>Hodge Structures of dimension 2 and weight 1</h4>

<div id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> be a real vector space of dimension <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> endowed with a
skew symmetric quadratic form, <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(e_{1},e_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(e_{1},e_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> a basis in which the matrix
of <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> is</p>
<table id="S2.Ex38" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex38.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="Q=\begin{pmatrix}0&amp;1\\
-1&amp;0\end{pmatrix},\quad Q(u,v)={{}^{t}v}Qu,\quad u,v\in\mathbb{Q}^{2}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>v</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></mmultiscripts><mi>Q</mi><mi>u</mi><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℚ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q=\begin{pmatrix}0&amp;1\\
-1&amp;0\end{pmatrix},\quad Q(u,v)={{}^{t}v}Qu,\quad u,v\in\mathbb{Q}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">then Hodge decomposition
<math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\mathbb{C}}=H^{1,0}\oplus H^{0,1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><mo>⊕</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\mathbb{C}}=H^{1,0}\oplus H^{0,1}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined by the one
dimensional subspace <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{1,0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{1,0}</annotation></semantics></math> with generator <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> of coordinates
<math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(v_{1},v_{2})\in\mathbb{C}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(v_{1},v_{2})\in\mathbb{C}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. While <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q(v,v)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q(v,v)=0</annotation></semantics></math>, the Hodge-Riemann
positivity condition is written as <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="iQ(v,\overline{v})=-i(v_{1}\overline{v}_{2}-\overline{v}_{1}v_{2})&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">iQ(v,\overline{v})=-i(v_{1}\overline{v}_{2}-\overline{v}_{1}v_{2})&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, hence <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{2}\not=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{2}\not=0</annotation></semantics></math>, so
we divide by <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> to get a unique representative of <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{1,0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{1,0}</annotation></semantics></math>
by a vector of the form <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v=(\tau,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v=(\tau,1)</annotation></semantics></math> with Im(<math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau)&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau)&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> . Hence the
Poincaré half plane { <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z\in\mathbb{C}:" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z\in\mathbb{C}:</annotation></semantics></math> Im<math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>} is a classifying
space for polarized Hodge Structures of dimension <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>. This will apply to the
cohomology <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H:=H^{1}(T,{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H:=H^{1}(T,{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math> of a complex torus of dim.<math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>. Note
that the torus is a projective variety. Indeed, a non degenerate
lattice in <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℂ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math> defines a Weirstrass function <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}(z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}(z)</annotation></semantics></math> that
defines with its derivative an embedding of the torus onto a
smooth elliptic curve <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{P}(z),\mathcal{P}^{\prime}(z)):T\rightarrow\mathbb{P}_{C}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{P}(z),\mathcal{P}^{\prime}(z)):T\rightarrow\mathbb{P}_{C}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.3.2. </span>Moduli space of Hodge structures of weight 1 (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>)</h4>

<div id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The first cohomology group <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math> of a smooth compact algebraic
curve <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> of genus <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> is of rank <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g</annotation></semantics></math>. The cohomology with
coefficients in <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℂ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math> decomposes into the direct sum:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex39" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex39.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\mathbb{C}}=H^{1,0}\oplus H^{0,1}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><mo>⊕</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\mathbb{C}}=H^{1,0}\oplus H^{0,1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">of two conjugate subspaces of dimension
<math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>. We view here the differentiable structure as fixed, on which
the algebraic structure vary, hence the cohomology is a fixed
vector space on which the decomposition vary. The problem here, is
the classification of the different Hodge Structures on the cohomology.
Recall that <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math> is endowed via the cup-product with a non
degenerate alternating form:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex40" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex40.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q:(\alpha,\beta)\to\int\alpha\wedge\beta." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q:(\alpha,\beta)\to\int\alpha\wedge\beta.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">The subspace <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{1,0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{1,0}</annotation></semantics></math> is an element of the
Grassmann variety <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(g,H_{\mathbb{C}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(g,H_{\mathbb{C}})</annotation></semantics></math> parametrizing complex vector
subspaces of dimension
<math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The first Riemann bilinear relation states that
<math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q(\alpha,\beta)=0,\,\forall\alpha,\beta\in H^{1,0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q(\alpha,\beta)=0,\,\forall\alpha,\beta\in H^{1,0}</annotation></semantics></math>,
which translates into a set of algebraic equations satisfied by
the point
representing <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{1,0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{1,0}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(g,H_{\mathbb{C}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(g,H_{\mathbb{C}})</annotation></semantics></math>, hence the family of such subspaces
is parameterized by an algebraic subvariety
denoted Ď <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\subset G(g,H_{\mathbb{C}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\subset G(g,H_{\mathbb{C}})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Sp(H_{\mathbb{C}},Q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Sp(H_{\mathbb{C}},Q)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the symplectic
subgroup of complex linear automorphisms of <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math> commuting with
<math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>; it acts transitively on Ď (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>), hence such variety is
smooth.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The second positivity relation is an open condition,
hence it defines an open subset <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> of elements representing
<math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{1,0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{1,0}</annotation></semantics></math> in Ď. The subspace <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is called the
classifying space of polarized Hodge Structures of type <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>
(the cohomology here is primitive, hence polarized).</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To describe <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> with coordinates, we choose
a basis <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{b_{1},\ldots,b_{g},b_{g+1},\ldots,b_{2g}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{b_{1},\ldots,b_{g},b_{g+1},\ldots,b_{2g}\}</annotation></semantics></math> of
<math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math> in which the matrix of <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> is written as a matrix <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math>
below, then <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{1,0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{1,0}</annotation></semantics></math> is represented by a choice of free <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>
generators which may be written as a matrix <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> columns
and <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g</annotation></semantics></math> rows satisfying the condition:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex41" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex41.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J=\begin{pmatrix}0&amp;I_{n}\\
-I_{n}&amp;0\end{pmatrix},M=\begin{pmatrix}M_{1}\\
M_{2}\end{pmatrix}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J=\begin{pmatrix}0&amp;I_{n}\\
-I_{n}&amp;0\end{pmatrix},M=\begin{pmatrix}M_{1}\\
M_{2}\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S2.Ex42" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex42.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{pmatrix}{{}^{t}M}_{1},\,{{}^{t}M}_{2}\end{pmatrix}\cdot\begin{pmatrix}0&amp;I_{n}\\
-I_{n}&amp;0\end{pmatrix}\cdot\begin{pmatrix}M_{1}\\
M_{2}\end{pmatrix}={{}^{t}M}_{1}M_{2}-{{}^{t}M}_{2}M_{1}=0" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></mmultiscripts><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn><mrow></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></mmultiscripts></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn><mrow></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{pmatrix}{{}^{t}M}_{1},\,{{}^{t}M}_{2}\end{pmatrix}\cdot\begin{pmatrix}0&amp;I_{n}\\
-I_{n}&amp;0\end{pmatrix}\cdot\begin{pmatrix}M_{1}\\
M_{2}\end{pmatrix}={{}^{t}M}_{1}M_{2}-{{}^{t}M}_{2}M_{1}=0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The positivity condition <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="iQ(\alpha,\overline{\alpha})&gt;0,\forall\alpha\not=0\in H^{1,0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">iQ(\alpha,\overline{\alpha})&gt;0,\forall\alpha\not=0\in H^{1,0}</annotation></semantics></math> translates into</p>
<table id="S2.Ex43" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex43.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\cdot\begin{pmatrix}{{}^{t}\overline{M}}_{1}\,{{}^{t}\overline{M}}_{2}\end{pmatrix}\cdot\begin{pmatrix}0&amp;I_{n}\\
-I_{n}&amp;0\end{pmatrix}\cdot\begin{pmatrix}M_{1}\\
M_{2}\end{pmatrix}=i({{}^{t}\overline{M}}_{1}M_{2}-{{}^{t}\overline{M}}_{2}M_{1})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mmultiscripts><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn><mrow></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mmultiscripts><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>2</mn><mrow></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></mmultiscripts></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mmultiscripts><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn><mrow></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mmultiscripts><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>2</mn><mrow></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\cdot\begin{pmatrix}{{}^{t}\overline{M}}_{1}\,{{}^{t}\overline{M}}_{2}\end{pmatrix}\cdot\begin{pmatrix}0&amp;I_{n}\\
-I_{n}&amp;0\end{pmatrix}\cdot\begin{pmatrix}M_{1}\\
M_{2}\end{pmatrix}=i({{}^{t}\overline{M}}_{1}M_{2}-{{}^{t}\overline{M}}_{2}M_{1})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p5.2" class="ltx_p">is positive definite. We deduce in particular that the determinant
<math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\det(M_{2})\not=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">det</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\det(M_{2})\not=0</annotation></semantics></math> and we may choose <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{2}=I_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{2}=I_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{1}=Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{1}=Z</annotation></semantics></math> such that</p>
<table id="S2.Ex44" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex44.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="D\simeq\{Z:{{}^{t}Z}=Z,\rm{Im}(Z)&gt;0\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>Z</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></mmultiscripts><mo>=</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Im</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\simeq\{Z:{{}^{t}Z}=Z,\rm{Im}(Z)&gt;0\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p5.3" class="ltx_p">that is the imaginary part of the matrix <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> defines a positive
definite form. Then <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is called the generalized Siegel upper
half-plane.
If <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=1</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is the upper half plane.
Let Sp<math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(H_{\mathbb{C}},Q)\simeq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(H_{\mathbb{C}},Q)\simeq</annotation></semantics></math> Sp<math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(g,\mathbb{C})=\{X\in{\rm GL}(n,\mathbb{C}):{{}^{t}X}\cdot J=J\cdot X\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>GL</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>X</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(g,\mathbb{C})=\{X\in{\rm GL}(n,\mathbb{C}):{{}^{t}X}\cdot J=J\cdot X\}</annotation></semantics></math>, then Sp<math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(V,Q):=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(V,Q):=</annotation></semantics></math> Sp<math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(H_{\mathbb{C}},Q)\cap" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(H_{\mathbb{C}},Q)\cap</annotation></semantics></math>GL<math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(H_{{\mathbb{R}}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(H_{{\mathbb{R}}})</annotation></semantics></math> acts transitively on <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> as follows:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex45" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex45.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{pmatrix}A&amp;B\\
C&amp;D\end{pmatrix}\cdot Z=(A\cdot Z+B)\cdot(C\cdot Z+D)^{-1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mi>A</mi></mtd><mtd><mi>B</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>C</mi></mtd><mtd><mi>D</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{pmatrix}A&amp;B\\
C&amp;D\end{pmatrix}\cdot Z=(A\cdot Z+B)\cdot(C\cdot Z+D)^{-1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">It is a classical but not a trivial result
that smooth compact curves are homeomorphic as real surfaces
to the “connected” sum of <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> tori
with a natural basis of paths <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{1},\ldots,\gamma_{g},\gamma_{g+1},\ldots,\gamma_{2g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{1},\ldots,\gamma_{g},\gamma_{g+1},\ldots,\gamma_{2g}</annotation></semantics></math>, whose classes generates the homology and such that
the matrix of the Intersection product is <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">The classes obtained by Poincaré
duality isomorphism <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{1},\ldots,\eta_{2g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{1},\ldots,\eta_{2g}</annotation></semantics></math> form a basis
of <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math> on which the matrix of the form <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>
defined by the product on cohomology is still <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\gamma^{*}_{i}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\gamma^{*}_{i}]</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the ordinary
dual basis of cohomology. It is not difficult to check on the
definitions that <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\gamma^{*}_{i}]=-\eta_{i+g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\gamma^{*}_{i}]=-\eta_{i+g}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[1,g]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[1,g]</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\gamma^{*}_{i+g}]=\eta_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\gamma^{*}_{i+g}]=\eta_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[g,2g]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[g,2g]</annotation></semantics></math>, from which we
deduce that the dual basis satisfy also the matrix <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math>. Let
<math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> be a set of differential forms defining a basis of
<math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{1,0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{1,0}</annotation></semantics></math>, then the period matrix of the basis <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> with
respect to the basis <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p7.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\gamma^{*}_{i}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\gamma^{*}_{i}]</annotation></semantics></math> is defined by the coordinates
on that basis given by the entries <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p7.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int_{\gamma_{i}}\omega_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int_{\gamma_{i}}\omega_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> for
<math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p7.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[1,2g],j\in[1,g]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[1,2g],j\in[1,g]</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence such matrices represent
elements in <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p7.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.3.3. </span>Hodge structures of weight 1 and abelian varieties</h4>

<div id="S2.SS3.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given a Hodge Structure:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex46" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex46.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(H_{\mathbb{Z}},H^{1,0}\oplus H^{0,1})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><mo>⊕</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(H_{\mathbb{Z}},H^{1,0}\oplus H^{0,1}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">the projection on <math id="S2.SS3.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{0,1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{0,1}</annotation></semantics></math>
induces an isomorphism of real vector spaces:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex47" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex47.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{{\mathbb{R}}}\to H_{\mathbb{C}}=H^{1,0}\oplus H^{0,1}\to H^{0,1}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><mo>⊕</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{{\mathbb{R}}}\to H_{\mathbb{C}}=H^{1,0}\oplus H^{0,1}\to H^{0,1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">since <math id="S2.SS3.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{H^{0,1}}=H^{1,0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{H^{0,1}}=H^{1,0}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then we deduce that
<math id="S2.SS3.SSS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a lattice in the complex space <math id="S2.SS3.SSS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{0,1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{0,1}</annotation></semantics></math>, and
the quotient <math id="S2.SS3.SSS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T:=H^{0,1}/H_{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T:=H^{0,1}/H_{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a complex torus .
When <math id="S2.SS3.SSS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math> is identified with the image
of cohomology spaces <math id="S2.SS3.SSS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Im(H^{1}(X,\mathbb{Z})\to H^{1}(X,{\mathbb{R}}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Im(H^{1}(X,\mathbb{Z})\to H^{1}(X,{\mathbb{R}}))</annotation></semantics></math> of a complex manifold
<math id="S2.SS3.SSS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, resp. <math id="S2.SS3.SSS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{0,1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{0,1}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.SS3.SSS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{1}(X,\mathcal{O})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{1}(X,\mathcal{O})</annotation></semantics></math>, the torus <math id="S2.SS3.SSS3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>
will be identified with the Picard variety
<math id="S2.SS3.SSS3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Pic^{0}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Pic^{0}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> parameterizing the holomorphic line bundles on <math id="S2.SS3.SSS3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>
with first Chern class equal to zero as follows. We consider the
exact sequence of sheaves defined by <math id="S2.SS3.SSS3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\mapsto e^{2i\pi f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\mapsto e^{2i\pi f}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex48" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex48.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\to\mathbb{Z}\to\mathcal{O}_{X}\to\mathcal{O}_{X}^{*}\to 1" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\to\mathbb{Z}\to\mathcal{O}_{X}\to\mathcal{O}_{X}^{*}\to 1</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS3.p1.4" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS3.SSS3.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> is the neutral element of the sheaf of multiplicative
groups <math id="S2.SS3.SSS3.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{X}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{X}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, and its associated long exact sequence of
derived functors of the global sections functor:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex49" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex49.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\to H^{1}(X,\mathbb{Z})\to H^{1}(X,\mathcal{O}_{X})\to H^{1}(X,\mathcal{O}_{X}^{*})\to H^{2}(X,\mathbb{Z})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to H^{1}(X,\mathbb{Z})\to H^{1}(X,\mathcal{O}_{X})\to H^{1}(X,\mathcal{O}_{X}^{*})\to H^{2}(X,\mathbb{Z})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS3.p1.5" class="ltx_p">where the morphisms can be interpreted geometrically; when the
space <math id="S2.SS3.SSS3.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{1}(X,\mathcal{O}_{X}^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{1}(X,\mathcal{O}_{X}^{*})</annotation></semantics></math> is identified with isomorphisms classes of
line bundles on <math id="S2.SS3.SSS3.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, the last morphism defines the Chern class of
the line bundle. Hence the torus <math id="S2.SS3.SSS3.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is identified with the
isomorphism classes <math id="S2.SS3.SSS3.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.SS3.SSS3.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{1}(\mathcal{L})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{1}(\mathcal{L})=0</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex50" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex50.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T:=\frac{H^{1}(X,\mathcal{O}_{X})}{ImH^{1}(X,\mathbb{Z})}\simeq Pic^{0}(X):=Ker(H^{1}(X,\mathcal{O}_{X}^{*})\xrightarrow{c_{1}}H^{2}(X,\mathbb{Z}))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>:=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">c</mi><mn mathsize="0.710em">1</mn></msub></mover><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T:=\frac{H^{1}(X,\mathcal{O}_{X})}{ImH^{1}(X,\mathbb{Z})}\simeq Pic^{0}(X):=Ker(H^{1}(X,\mathcal{O}_{X}^{*})\xrightarrow{c_{1}}H^{2}(X,\mathbb{Z}))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS3.p1.6" class="ltx_p">It is possible to show that the Picard variety of a smooth
projective variety is an abelian variety. (Define a Kähler
form with integral class on <math id="S2.SS3.SSS3.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Pic^{0}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Pic^{0}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3.SSS4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.3.4. </span>Hodge structures of weight 2</h4>

<div id="S2.SS3.SSS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S2.Ex51" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex51.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(H_{\mathbb{Z}},\,H^{2,0}\oplus H^{1,1}\oplus H^{0,2};\,H^{0,2}=\overline{H^{2,0}},\,H^{1,1}=\overline{H^{1,1}};\,Q)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><mo>⊕</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>⊕</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(H_{\mathbb{Z}},\,H^{2,0}\oplus H^{1,1}\oplus H^{0,2};\,H^{0,2}=\overline{H^{2,0}},\,H^{1,1}=\overline{H^{1,1}};\,Q)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">the intersection form <math id="S2.SS3.SSS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> is symmetric and
<math id="S2.SS3.SSS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hbox{F}(\alpha,\beta)=Q(\alpha,\overline{\beta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>F</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hbox{F}(\alpha,\beta)=Q(\alpha,\overline{\beta})</annotation></semantics></math> is Hermitian.
The decomposition is orthogonal for <span id="S2.SS3.SSS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">F</span> with
<span id="S2.SS3.SSS4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">F</span> positive definite on <math id="S2.SS3.SSS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{1,1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{1,1}</annotation></semantics></math>, negative definite on
<math id="S2.SS3.SSS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2,0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2,0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.SSS4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{0,2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{0,2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem19" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem19.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.19</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem19.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem19.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem19.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem19.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The HS is completely determined by the subspace
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem19.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2,0}\subset H_{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2,0}\subset H_{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S2.Thmtheorem19.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2,0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2,0}</annotation></semantics></math> is totally isotropic for <math id="S2.Thmtheorem19.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>
and the associated Hermitian product <math id="S2.Thmtheorem19.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is negative definite
on <math id="S2.Thmtheorem19.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2,0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2,0}</annotation></semantics></math>. The signature of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem19.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> is (<math id="S2.Thmtheorem19.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{1,1},2h^{2,0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{1,1},2h^{2,0}</annotation></semantics></math>).</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.SSS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In the lemma <math id="S2.SS3.SSS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{0,2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{0,2}</annotation></semantics></math> is determined by conjugation
and <math id="S2.SS3.SSS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{1,1}=(H^{2,0}\oplus H^{0,2})^{\bot}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><mo>⊕</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊥</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{1,1}=(H^{2,0}\oplus H^{0,2})^{\bot}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3. </span>Mixed Hodge Structures (MHS)</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The theory of Mixed Hodge Structures (MHS) introduced by
Deligne in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> is a generalization of Hodge Structures that
can be defined on the cohomology of all algebraic varieties.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Since we are essentially concerned by filtrations of vector
spaces, it is not more difficult to describe this notion in the
terminology of abelian categories. We start with a formal study of
Mixed Hodge Structures.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A={\mathbb{Z}},{\mathbb{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A={\mathbb{Z}},{\mathbb{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\mathbb{R}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℝ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\mathbb{R}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and define <math id="S3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\otimes\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\otimes\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> if
<math id="S3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℝ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math> if
<math id="S3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A={\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A={\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For an <math id="S3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>-module <math id="S3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{A}</annotation></semantics></math> of
finite type, we write <math id="S3.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math> for the <math id="S3.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(A\otimes\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(A\otimes\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math>-vector space
<math id="S3.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(H_{A})\otimes_{A}(A\otimes\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>A</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(H_{A})\otimes_{A}(A\otimes\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math>. It is a
rational space if <math id="S3.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S3.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> and a real space if <math id="S3.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A={\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A={\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Mixed Hodge structure (MHS))<span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">An <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>-Mixed Hodge
Structure <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> consists of:
<br class="ltx_break">1) an <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>-module of finite type
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{A}</annotation></semantics></math>; 
<br class="ltx_break">2) a finite increasing filtration <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> of the
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\otimes\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\otimes\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-vector space <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{A}\otimes\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{A}\otimes\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>
called the weight filtration; 
<br class="ltx_break">3) a finite decreasing filtration <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> of the <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℂ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>-vector space
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\mathbb{C}}=H_{A}\otimes_{A}\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>A</mi></msub><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\mathbb{C}}=H_{A}\otimes_{A}\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, called the Hodge filtration, such
that the systems:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr^{W}_{n}H=(Gr^{W}_{n}(H_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}}),(Gr^{W}_{n}H_{\mathbb{C}},F))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr^{W}_{n}H=(Gr^{W}_{n}(H_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}}),(Gr^{W}_{n}H_{\mathbb{C}},F))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">consist of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\otimes{\mathbb{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\otimes{\mathbb{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math>-Hodge Structure of weight <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The Mixed Hodge Structure is called real, if <math id="S3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A={\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A={\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math>, rational,
if <math id="S3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>, and integral, if <math id="S3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>. We need to define
clearly, how the filtrations are induced, for example: 
<br class="ltx_break"><math id="S3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{p}Gr^{W}_{n}H_{\mathbb{C}}:=((F^{p}\cap W_{n}\otimes\mathbb{C})+W_{n-1}\otimes\mathbb{C})/W_{n-1}\otimes\mathbb{C}\subset(W_{n}\otimes\mathbb{C})/W_{n-1}\otimes\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{p}Gr^{W}_{n}H_{\mathbb{C}}:=((F^{p}\cap W_{n}\otimes\mathbb{C})+W_{n-1}\otimes\mathbb{C})/W_{n-1}\otimes\mathbb{C}\subset(W_{n}\otimes\mathbb{C})/W_{n-1}\otimes\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Morphism of MHS. </span> A morphism <math id="S3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:H\rightarrow H^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:H\rightarrow H^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>
of Mixed Hodge Structures
is a morphism <math id="S3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:H_{A}\rightarrow H^{\prime}_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>A</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:H_{A}\rightarrow H^{\prime}_{A}</annotation></semantics></math> whose extension to
<math id="S3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\mathbb{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℚ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\mathbb{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math> (resp. <math id="S3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>) is compatible with the
filtration <math id="S3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., <math id="S3.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(W_{j}H_{A})\subset W_{j}H^{\prime}_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>A</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(W_{j}H_{A})\subset W_{j}H^{\prime}_{A}</annotation></semantics></math> (resp. <math id="S3.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e.,
<math id="S3.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(F^{j}H_{A})\subset F^{j}H^{\prime}_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>A</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(F^{j}H_{A})\subset F^{j}H^{\prime}_{A}</annotation></semantics></math>), which implies that it is also compatible with
<math id="S3.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{F}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">These definitions allow us to speak of the category of
Mixed Hodge Structures.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">The main result of this chapter is</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.2</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Deligne)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The category of Mixed Hodge
Structures is abelian.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p8.1" class="ltx_p">The proof relies on the following:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Canonical decomposition of the weight filtration.</span>
While there is an equivalence between the Hodge filtration and
the Hodge decomposition, there is no such result for the weight
filtration of a Mixed Hodge Structure. In the category of
Mixed Hodge Structures,
the short exact sequence <math id="S3.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\to Gr^{W}_{n-1}\to W_{n}/W_{n-2}\to Gr^{W}_{n}\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\to Gr^{W}_{n-1}\to W_{n}/W_{n-2}\to Gr^{W}_{n}\to 0</annotation></semantics></math> is a non-split extension
of the two pure Hodge structures <math id="S3.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr^{W}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr^{W}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr^{W}_{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr^{W}_{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
To construct the Hodge decomposition,
we introduce for each
pair of integers
<math id="S3.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p,q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p,q)</annotation></semantics></math>, the subspaces of <math id="S3.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H=H_{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H=H_{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I^{p,q}=(F^{p}\cap W_{p+q})\cap(\overline{F^{q}}\cap W_{p+q}+\overline{F^{q-1}}\cap W_{p+q-2}+\overline{F^{q-2}}\cap W_{p+q-3}+\cdots)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mover accent="true"><msup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mover accent="true"><msup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I^{p,q}=(F^{p}\cap W_{p+q})\cap(\overline{F^{q}}\cap W_{p+q}+\overline{F^{q-1}}\cap W_{p+q-2}+\overline{F^{q-2}}\cap W_{p+q-3}+\cdots)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p9.2" class="ltx_p">By construction they are related for <math id="S3.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p+q=n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p+q=n</annotation></semantics></math> to the components
<math id="S3.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{p,q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{p,q}</annotation></semantics></math> of the Hodge decomposition: <math id="S3.p9.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="Gr^{W}_{n}H\simeq\oplus_{n=p+q}H^{p,q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mi>r</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>W</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>H</mi><mo rspace="0em">≃</mo><mo lspace="0em">⊕</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mi>H</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr^{W}_{n}H\simeq\oplus_{n=p+q}H^{p,q}</annotation></semantics></math>;</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3.3</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The projection <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\varphi:W_{p+q}\rightarrow Gr^{W}_{p+q}H\simeq\oplus_{p^{\prime}+q^{\prime}=p+q}H^{p^{\prime},q^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>W</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>G</mi><mi>r</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>W</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mi>H</mi><mo rspace="0em">≃</mo><mo lspace="0em">⊕</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mi>H</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi:W_{p+q}\rightarrow Gr^{W}_{p+q}H\simeq\oplus_{p^{\prime}+q^{\prime}=p+q}H^{p^{\prime},q^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> induces an
isomorphism <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I^{p,q}\xrightarrow{\sim}H^{p,q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo></mover><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I^{p,q}\xrightarrow{\sim}H^{p,q}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Moreover,</p>
<table id="S3.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="W_{n}=\oplus_{p+q\leq n}I^{p,q},\quad F^{p}=\oplus_{p^{\prime}\geq p}I^{p^{\prime},q^{\prime}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">⊕</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mi>I</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mi>F</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></msup><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">⊕</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>≥</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mi>I</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{n}=\oplus_{p+q\leq n}I^{p,q},\quad F^{p}=\oplus_{p^{\prime}\geq p}I^{p^{\prime},q^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 3.4</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">i) For three decreasing opposed filtrations in an abelian
category, Deligne uses in the proof an inductive argument based on
the formula for <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math></p>
<table id="S3.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{p_{i}}\oplus\overline{F^{q_{i}}}\simeq Gr^{W}_{n-i}H,\quad p_{i}+q_{i}=n-i+1," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msup><mo>⊕</mo><mover accent="true"><msup><mi>F</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msup><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{p_{i}}\oplus\overline{F^{q_{i}}}\simeq Gr^{W}_{n-i}H,\quad p_{i}+q_{i}=n-i+1,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">to construct for <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> a decreasing family <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p_{i},q_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p_{i},q_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> starting
with <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p_{0}=p,q_{0}=q):p+q=n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p_{0}=p,q_{0}=q):p+q=n</annotation></semantics></math> and deduces the existence of a subspace
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{p,q}\subset W_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{p,q}\subset W_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> projecting isomorphically onto the subspace
of type <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p,q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p,q)</annotation></semantics></math> of the Hodge Structure <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr^{W}_{n}H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr^{W}_{n}H</annotation></semantics></math>.
To simplify, we choose here, for <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> increasing, a sequence
of the following type <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p_{0},q_{0})=(p,q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p_{0},q_{0})=(p,q)</annotation></semantics></math>, and for <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{i}=p,q_{i}=q-i+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{i}=p,q_{i}=q-i+1</annotation></semantics></math> which explains theasymmetry in the formula
(see also <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite> Lemma-Definition 3.4),
including the fact that for <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{F^{q}}\cap W_{p+q-1}\subset\overline{F^{q}}\cap W_{p+q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{F^{q}}\cap W_{p+q-1}\subset\overline{F^{q}}\cap W_{p+q}</annotation></semantics></math>.
<br class="ltx_break">ii) In general <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I^{p,q}\not=\overline{I^{q,p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo>≠</mo><mover accent="true"><msup><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I^{p,q}\not=\overline{I^{q,p}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have only
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I^{p,q}\equiv\overline{I^{q,p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo>≡</mo><mover accent="true"><msup><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I^{p,q}\equiv\overline{I^{q,p}}</annotation></semantics></math> modulo <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{p+q-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{p+q-2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
<br class="ltx_break">iii) A morphism of Mixed Hodge Structures is necessarily
compatible with the decomposition, which will be the main
ingredient to prove the strictness with respect to <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>
(see <a href="#S3.SS1.SSS3" title="3.1.3. Strictness ‣ 3.1. Filtrations ‣ 3. Mixed Hodge Structures (MHS) ‣ Mixed Hodge Structures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1.3</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p10.1" class="ltx_p">The proof of this proposition is given below. This proposition is
used to prove Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 3.2 (Deligne). ‣ 3. Mixed Hodge Structures (MHS) ‣ Mixed Hodge Structures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p11.1" class="ltx_p">The knowledge of the linear algebra underlying
Mixed Hodge Structures is supposed to help the reader before the reader is confronted
with their construction. The striking result to remember is that morphisms
of Mixed Hodge Structures are <span id="S3.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">strict</span> (see <a href="#S3.SS1.SSS3" title="3.1.3. Strictness ‣ 3.1. Filtrations ‣ 3. Mixed Hodge Structures (MHS) ‣ Mixed Hodge Structures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1.3</span></a>)
for both filtrations, the weight <math id="S3.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> and Hodge <math id="S3.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>.
The complete proofs are purely algebraic <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>; the corresponding theory
in an abelian category is developed for objects with opposite filtrations.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1. </span>Filtrations </h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given a morphism in an additive category,
the isomorphism between the image and co-image is one of the
conditions to define an abelian category. In the abelian
category of vector spaces endowed with finite filtrations by
subspaces, if we consider a morphism compatible with
filtrations <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:(H,F)\to(H^{\prime},F^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:(H,F)\to(H^{\prime},F^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(F^{j})\subset F^{{}^{\prime}j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mmultiscripts><mi>j</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></mmultiscripts></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(F^{j})\subset F^{{}^{\prime}j}</annotation></semantics></math>,
the induced filtration on the image by <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> does not coincide
with the induced filtration by <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> on the co-image. In this case we say that
the morphism is strict if they coincide (see <a href="#S3.SS1.SSS3" title="3.1.3. Strictness ‣ 3.1. Filtrations ‣ 3. Mixed Hodge Structures (MHS) ‣ Mixed Hodge Structures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1.3</span></a>).
This kind of problem will occur for various repeated
restrictions of filtrations and we need here to define with
precision the properties of induced filtrations, since this is at
the heart of the subject of Mixed Hodge Structures.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">On a sub-quotient of a filtered vector
space and in general of an object of an abelian category, there are two ways
to restrict the filtration (first on the sub-object, then on the quotient and
the reciprocal way). On an object <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> with two filtrations <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>,
we can repeat the restriction for each
filtration and we get different objects if we start with <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> then <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>
or we inverse the order.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We need to know precise relations between
the induced filtrations in these various ways, and to know the precise behavior
of a linear morphism with respect to such filtrations. For
example, we will indicate in section <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math>, three different ways to induce a
filtration on the terms of a spectral sequence. A central result
for Mixed Hodge Structures is to give conditions on the complex such that the three
induced filtrations coincide.
Hence, we recall here preliminaries on filtrations after Deligne.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔸</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> denote an abelian category.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.5</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A decreasing (resp. increasing) filtration <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> of an object
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔸</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a family of sub-objects of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔸</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{A}}</annotation></semantics></math>, satisfying</p>
<table id="S3.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\forall n,m,\quad\quad n\leq m\Longrightarrow F^{m}(A)\subset F^{n}(A)\quad(resp.\,n\leq m\Longrightarrow F_{n}(A)\subset F_{m}(A))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟹</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>s</mi><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.337em">.</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟹</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\forall n,m,\quad\quad n\leq m\Longrightarrow F^{m}(A)\subset F^{n}(A)\quad(resp.\,n\leq m\Longrightarrow F_{n}(A)\subset F_{m}(A))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The pair <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(A,F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(A,F)</annotation></semantics></math> of an object of <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔸</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{A}</annotation></semantics></math> and a decreasing
(resp. increasing) filtration
will be called a filtered object.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> is a decreasing filtration (resp. <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> an
increasing filtration), a shifted filtration <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F[n]</annotation></semantics></math> by an integer
<math id="S3.SS1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as</p>
<table id="S3.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(F[n])^{p}(A)=F^{n+p}(A)\quad(W[n])_{p}(A)=W_{n-p}(A)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(F[n])^{p}(A)=F^{n+p}(A)\quad(W[n])_{p}(A)=W_{n-p}(A)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Decreasing filtrations <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> are considered for a general
study. Statements for increasing filtrations <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> are deduced by
the change of indices <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{n}(A)=F^{-n}(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{n}(A)=F^{-n}(A)</annotation></semantics></math>. A filtration is
finite if there exist integers <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>
such that <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{n}(A)=A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{n}(A)=A</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{m}(A)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{m}(A)=0</annotation></semantics></math>.
<br class="ltx_break">A morphism of
filtered objects
<math id="S3.SS1.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(A,F)\xrightarrow{f}(B,F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑓</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(A,F)\xrightarrow{f}(B,F)</annotation></semantics></math> is a morphism <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\xrightarrow{f}B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑓</mo></mover><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\xrightarrow{f}B</annotation></semantics></math>
satisfying <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(F^{n}(A))\subset F^{n}(B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(F^{n}(A))\subset F^{n}(B)</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\in{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\in{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Filtered objects (resp. of finite filtration) form an
additive category
with existence of <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="kernel" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">kernel</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="cokernel" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">cokernel</annotation></semantics></math> of a morphism
with natural induced filtrations
as well image and coimage, however the image and co-image
will not be necessarily filtered-isomorphic, which is the main
obstruction to obtain an abelian category.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">To lift this obstruction, we are lead to define the notion of strictness for
compatible morphims (see <a href="#S3.SS1.SSS3" title="3.1.3. Strictness ‣ 3.1. Filtrations ‣ 3. Mixed Hodge Structures (MHS) ‣ Mixed Hodge Structures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1.3</span></a> below).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.6</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The graded object associated to <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(A,F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(A,F)</annotation></semantics></math>
is defined as:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="Gr_{F}(A)=\,\,\oplus_{n\in{\mathbb{Z}}}Gr_{F}^{n}(A)\quad\mbox{where}\quad Gr_{F}^{n}(A)=F^{n}(A)/F^{n+1}(A)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.118em">=</mo><msub><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></msub><mi>G</mi><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>F</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mtext>where</mtext><mi>G</mi><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>F</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr_{F}(A)=\,\,\oplus_{n\in{\mathbb{Z}}}Gr_{F}^{n}(A)\quad\mbox{where}\quad Gr_{F}^{n}(A)=F^{n}(A)/F^{n+1}(A).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.1.1. </span>Induced filtration</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A filtered object <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(A,F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(A,F)</annotation></semantics></math> induces
a filtration on a sub-object <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i:B\rightarrow A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i:B\rightarrow A</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>, defined by
<math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{n}(B)=B\cap F^{n}(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{n}(B)=B\cap F^{n}(A)</annotation></semantics></math>. Dually, the quotient filtration on <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A/B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A/B</annotation></semantics></math> is defined by:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{n}(A/B)=p(F^{n}(A))=(B+F^{n}(A))/B\simeq F^{n}(A)/(B\cap F^{n}(A))," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{n}(A/B)=p(F^{n}(A))=(B+F^{n}(A))/B\simeq F^{n}(A)/(B\cap F^{n}(A)),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p:A\to A/B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p:A\to A/B</annotation></semantics></math> is the projection.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.1.2. </span></h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The cohomology of a sequence of filtered morphisms:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(A,F)\xrightarrow{f}(B,F)\xrightarrow{g}(C,F)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑓</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑔</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(A,F)\xrightarrow{f}(B,F)\xrightarrow{g}(C,F)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">satisfying <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\circ f=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\circ f=0</annotation></semantics></math>
is defined as <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H=Ker\,g/Im\,f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H=Ker\,g/Im\,f</annotation></semantics></math>; it is <span id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">filtered</span> and endowed
with the quotient filtration of the induced filtration on
<math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Ker\,g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Ker\,g</annotation></semantics></math>. It is
equal to the induced filtration on <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> by the
quotient filtration on <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B/Im\,f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B/Im\,f</annotation></semantics></math>, since <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H\subset(B/Im\,f)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H\subset(B/Im\,f)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.1.3. </span>Strictness</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For filtered modules over a ring, a morphism
of filtered objects <math id="S3.SS1.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:(A,F)\rightarrow(B,F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:(A,F)\rightarrow(B,F)</annotation></semantics></math> is called
strict if the relation:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(F^{n}(A))=f(A)\cap F^{n}(B)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(F^{n}(A))=f(A)\cap F^{n}(B)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">is satisfied; that is, any element <math id="S3.SS1.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\in F^{n}(B)\cap ImA" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\in F^{n}(B)\cap ImA</annotation></semantics></math>
is already in <math id="S3.SS1.SSS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Im\,F^{n}(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Im\,F^{n}(A)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This is not the case for any morphism.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">A filtered morphism in an additive category
<math id="S3.SS1.SSS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:(A,F)\rightarrow(B,F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:(A,F)\rightarrow(B,F)</annotation></semantics></math>
is called strict if it induces a filtered isomorphism
<math id="S3.SS1.SSS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Coim(f),F)\rightarrow(Im(f),F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Coim(f),F)\rightarrow(Im(f),F)</annotation></semantics></math>
from the coimage to the image of <math id="S3.SS1.SSS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> with their induced
filtrations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">This concept is basic to the theory, so we mention the following
criteria:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3.7</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">i) A filtered morphism <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:(A,F)\to(B,F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:(A,F)\to(B,F)</annotation></semantics></math> of objects
with finite filtrations is strict if and only if the following
exact sequence of graded objects is exact:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\to Gr_{F}(Kerf)\to Gr_{F}(A)\to Gr_{F}(B)\to Gr_{F}(Cokerf)\to 0" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\to Gr_{F}(Kerf)\to Gr_{F}(A)\to Gr_{F}(B)\to Gr_{F}(Cokerf)\to 0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.2" class="ltx_p">ii) Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S:\,\,(A,F)\xrightarrow{f}(B,F)\xrightarrow{g}(C,F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.618em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑓</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑔</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S:\,\,(A,F)\xrightarrow{f}(B,F)\xrightarrow{g}(C,F)</annotation></semantics></math> be a sequence <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> of strict morphisms such that <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\circ f=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\circ f=0</annotation></semantics></math>, then the cohomology <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> with induced filtration satisfies:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(Gr_{F}(S))\simeq Gr_{F}(H(S))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(Gr_{F}(S))\simeq Gr_{F}(H(S)).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.1.4. </span>Two filtrations</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.SS1.SSS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> be an object of <math id="S3.SS1.SSS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔸</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> with two
filtrations <math id="S3.SS1.SSS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.SSS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. By definition, <math id="S3.SS1.SSS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr^{n}_{F}(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>F</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr^{n}_{F}(A)</annotation></semantics></math> is a quotient
of a sub-object of <math id="S3.SS1.SSS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>, and as such, it is endowed with an induced
filtration by <math id="S3.SS1.SSS4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. Its associated graded object defines a
bigraded object <math id="S3.SS1.SSS4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr^{n}_{G}Gr^{m}_{F}(A)_{n,m\in{\mathbb{Z}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>G</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>F</mi><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr^{n}_{G}Gr^{m}_{F}(A)_{n,m\in{\mathbb{Z}}}</annotation></semantics></math>. We refer to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>
for:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.8</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>( Zassenhaus’ lemma)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The objects <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr^{n}_{G}Gr^{m}_{F}(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>G</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>F</mi><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr^{n}_{G}Gr^{m}_{F}(A)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr^{m}_{F}Gr^{n}_{G}(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>F</mi><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>G</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr^{m}_{F}Gr^{n}_{G}(A)</annotation></semantics></math> are isomorphic.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 3.9</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> be a third filtration on <math id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>. It induces a
filtration on <math id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr_{F}(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr_{F}(A)</annotation></semantics></math>, hence on <math id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr_{G}Gr_{F}(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr_{G}Gr_{F}(A)</annotation></semantics></math>. It induces also
a filtration on <math id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr_{F}Gr_{G}(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr_{F}Gr_{G}(A)</annotation></semantics></math>. These filtrations do not
correspond in general under the above isomorphism.
In the formula <math id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr_{H}Gr_{G}Gr_{F}(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr_{H}Gr_{G}Gr_{F}(A)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> et <math id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> have
symmetric role, but not <math id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS5" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.1.5. </span>Hom and tensor functors</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S3.SS1.SSS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.SSS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> are
two filtered objects of <math id="S3.SS1.SSS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔸</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{A}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we define a filtration on the
left exact functor <math id="S3.SS1.SSS5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Hom" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Hom</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="F^{k}Hom(A,B)=\{f:A\rightarrow B:\forall n,f(F^{n}(A))\subset F^{n+k}(B)\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mi>H</mi><mi>o</mi><mi>m</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{k}Hom(A,B)=\{f:A\rightarrow B:\forall n,f(F^{n}(A))\subset F^{n+k}(B)\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS5.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Hence:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Hom((A,F),(B,F))=F^{0}(Hom(A,B))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Hom((A,F),(B,F))=F^{0}(Hom(A,B)).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS5.p1.3" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S3.SS1.SSS5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.SSS5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> are modules on some ring, we define:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{k}(A\otimes B)=\sum_{m}Im(F^{m}(A)\otimes F^{k-m}(B)\rightarrow A\otimes B)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>m</mi></munder><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{k}(A\otimes B)=\sum_{m}Im(F^{m}(A)\otimes F^{k-m}(B)\rightarrow A\otimes B)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS6" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.1.6. </span>Multifunctor</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In general if <math id="S3.SS1.SSS6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H:{\mathbb{A}}_{1}\times\ldots\times{\mathbb{A}}_{n}\rightarrow\mathbb{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔸</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>𝔸</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝔹</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H:{\mathbb{A}}_{1}\times\ldots\times{\mathbb{A}}_{n}\rightarrow\mathbb{B}</annotation></semantics></math>
is a right exact multi-additive functor, we define:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{k}(H(A_{1},...,A_{n}))=\sum_{\sum{k_{i}=k}}Im(H(F^{k_{1}}A_{1},...,F^{k_{n}}A_{n})\rightarrow H(A_{1},...,A_{n}))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mstyle scriptlevel="-1"><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" stretchy="true">∑</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">k</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">i</mi></msub></mrow></mstyle><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{k}(H(A_{1},...,A_{n}))=\sum_{\sum{k_{i}=k}}Im(H(F^{k_{1}}A_{1},...,F^{k_{n}}A_{n})\rightarrow H(A_{1},...,A_{n}))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS6.p1.2" class="ltx_p">and dually if <math id="S3.SS1.SSS6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is left exact:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{k}(H(A_{1},...,A_{n})=\bigcap_{\sum k_{i}=k}Ker(H(A_{1},...,A_{n})\rightarrow H(A_{1}/F^{k_{1}}A_{1},...,A_{n}/F^{k_{n}}A_{n}))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⋂</mo><mrow><mstyle scriptlevel="-1"><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" stretchy="true">∑</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">k</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">i</mi></msub></mrow></mstyle><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{k}(H(A_{1},...,A_{n})=\bigcap_{\sum k_{i}=k}Ker(H(A_{1},...,A_{n})\rightarrow H(A_{1}/F^{k_{1}}A_{1},...,A_{n}/F^{k_{n}}A_{n}))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS6.p1.3" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S3.SS1.SSS6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is exact, both definitions are equivalent.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2. </span>Opposite filtrations</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We review here the definition of Hodge Structure in terms of filtrations, as it
is adapted to its generalization to Mixed Hodge Structure. For any <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>-module
<math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{A}</annotation></semantics></math>, the complex conjugation extends to a conjugation on the
space <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{{\mathbb{C}}}=H_{A}\otimes_{A}{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>A</mi></msub><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{{\mathbb{C}}}=H_{A}\otimes_{A}{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>. A filtration <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> on
<math id="S3.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{{\mathbb{C}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{{\mathbb{C}}}</annotation></semantics></math> has a conjugate filtration <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{F}</annotation></semantics></math> such that
<math id="S3.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\overline{F}})^{j}H_{\mathbb{C}}=\overline{F^{j}H_{\mathbb{C}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\overline{F}})^{j}H_{\mathbb{C}}=\overline{F^{j}H_{\mathbb{C}}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.10</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p1.1" class="ltx_p">([HS1]) An A-Hodge structure <math id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> of weight <math id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>
consists of:
<br class="ltx_break">i) an <math id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>-module of finite type <math id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{A}</annotation></semantics></math>,
<br class="ltx_break">ii) a finite filtration <math id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{{\mathbb{C}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{{\mathbb{C}}}</annotation></semantics></math> (the Hodge filtration) such that
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> and its conjugate <math id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{F}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfy the relation:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr^{p}_{F}Gr^{q}_{\overline{F}}(H_{\mathbb{C}})=0,\quad\mbox{for}\,\,p+q\neq n" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>q</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>for</mtext><mo lspace="0.340em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr^{p}_{F}Gr^{q}_{\overline{F}}(H_{\mathbb{C}})=0,\quad\mbox{for}\,\,p+q\neq n</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The module <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{A}</annotation></semantics></math>, or its image in <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\mathbb{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℚ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\mathbb{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math> is called the lattice.
The Hodge Structure is real when <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A={\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A={\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math>, rational when <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> and integral
when <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.1. </span>Opposite filtrations</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The linear
algebra of Mixed Hodge Structures applies to an abelian category <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔸</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{A}</annotation></semantics></math> if we use the
following definition where
no conjugation map appears. 
<br class="ltx_break">Two finite filtrations <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>
on an object <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔸</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> are n-opposite if:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr^{p}_{F}Gr^{q}_{G}(A)=0\quad\mbox{for}\,\,p+q\neq n." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>G</mi><mi>q</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>for</mtext><mo lspace="0.340em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr^{p}_{F}Gr^{q}_{G}(A)=0\quad\mbox{for}\,\,p+q\neq n.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">hence the Hodge filtration <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> on a Hodge Sructure of weight <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is
<math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-opposite to its conjugate
<math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{F}</annotation></semantics></math>. The following constructions
will define an equivalence of categories between objects of
<math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔸</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> with two <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-opposite filtrations and bigraded objects of
<math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔸</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> of the following type.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem11" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem11.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 3.11</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{p,q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{p,q}</annotation></semantics></math> be a bigraded object of
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔸</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> such that
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{p,q}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{p,q}=0</annotation></semantics></math>, for all but a finite number of
pairs <math id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p,q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p,q)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{p,q}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{p,q}=0</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p+q\neq n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p+q\neq n</annotation></semantics></math>; then we define
two <math id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-opposite filtrations on <math id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A={\sum_{p,q}}A^{p,q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A={\sum_{p,q}}A^{p,q}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
<table id="S3.Ex20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{p}(A)={\sum_{p^{\prime}\geq p}}A^{p^{\prime},q^{\prime}},\quad G^{q}(A)={\sum_{q^{\prime}\geq q}}A^{p^{\prime},q^{\prime}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>≥</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></munder><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>≥</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></munder><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{p}(A)={\sum_{p^{\prime}\geq p}}A^{p^{\prime},q^{\prime}},\quad G^{q}(A)={\sum_{q^{\prime}\geq q}}A^{p^{\prime},q^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p1.2" class="ltx_p">We have <math id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr^{p}_{F}Gr^{q}_{G}(A)=A^{p,q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>G</mi><mi>q</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr^{p}_{F}Gr^{q}_{G}(A)=A^{p,q}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We state reciprocally <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem12" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem12.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3.12</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem12.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem12.p1.1" class="ltx_p">i) Two finite filtrations <math id="S3.Thmtheorem12.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem12.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> on an object <math id="S3.Thmtheorem12.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>
are <math id="S3.Thmtheorem12.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-opposite, if and only if:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\forall\,p,q,\quad p+q=n+1\Rightarrow F^{p}(A)\oplus G^{q}(A)\simeq A." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">∀</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⇒</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\forall\,p,q,\quad p+q=n+1\Rightarrow F^{p}(A)\oplus G^{q}(A)\simeq A.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem12.p1.2" class="ltx_p">ii) If <math id="S3.Thmtheorem12.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem12.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> are <math id="S3.Thmtheorem12.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-opposite, and if we put
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem12.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{p,q}=F^{p}(A)\cap G^{q}(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{p,q}=F^{p}(A)\cap G^{q}(A)</annotation></semantics></math>, for <math id="S3.Thmtheorem12.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p+q=n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p+q=n</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.Thmtheorem12.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{p,q}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{p,q}=0</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.Thmtheorem12.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p+q\neq n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p+q\neq n</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S3.Thmtheorem12.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is a direct sum of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem12.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{p,q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{p,q}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Moreover, <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> can be deduced from the
bigraded object <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{p,q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{p,q}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> by the above procedure.
We recall the previous equivalent definition of Hodge Structure:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem13" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem13.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.13</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem13.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem13.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem13.p1.1" class="ltx_p">([HS2])
An <math id="S3.Thmtheorem13.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>-Hodge Structure on <math id="S3.Thmtheorem13.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> of weight <math id="S3.Thmtheorem13.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is a pair of
a finite A-module <math id="S3.Thmtheorem13.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{A}</annotation></semantics></math> and a decomposition into a direct sum on <math id="S3.Thmtheorem13.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{{\mathbb{C}}}=H_{A}\otimes_{A}\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>A</mi></msub><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{{\mathbb{C}}}=H_{A}\otimes_{A}\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="H_{{\mathbb{C}}}={\oplus_{p+q=n}}H^{p,q}\quad\mbox{such that}\quad{\overline{H}^{p,q}}=H^{q,p}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">⊕</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mi>H</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mtext>such that</mtext><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>H</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{{\mathbb{C}}}={\oplus_{p+q=n}}H^{p,q}\quad\mbox{such that}\quad{\overline{H}^{p,q}}=H^{q,p}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The relation with the previous definition is given by <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{p,q}=F^{p}(H_{{\mathbb{C}}})\cap\overline{F}^{q}(H_{{\mathbb{C}}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>q</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{p,q}=F^{p}(H_{{\mathbb{C}}})\cap\overline{F}^{q}(H_{{\mathbb{C}}})</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p+q=n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p+q=n</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.2. </span>Complex Hodge Structures</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For some arguments in analysis, we
don’t need to know that <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\overline{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\overline{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the conjugate of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem14" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem14.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.14</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem14.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem14.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem14.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A complex Hodge Structure of weight <math id="S3.Thmtheorem14.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> on a complex vector space
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem14.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is given by a pair of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem14.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n-</annotation></semantics></math>opposite
filtrations <math id="S3.Thmtheorem14.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem14.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\overline{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\overline{F}}</annotation></semantics></math>, hence a
decomposition into a direct sum of subspaces:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="H=\oplus_{p+q=n}H^{p,q},\quad\mbox{where}\,\,H^{p,q}=F^{p}\cap{\overline{F}}^{q}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">⊕</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mi>H</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mtext>where</mtext><mi>H</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>F</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>∩</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>q</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H=\oplus_{p+q=n}H^{p,q},\quad\mbox{where}\,\,H^{p,q}=F^{p}\cap{\overline{F}}^{q}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The two <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n-</annotation></semantics></math>opposite
filtrations <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\overline{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\overline{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> on a complex Hodge Structure of weight <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>
can be recovered from the decomposition by the formula:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex24.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="F^{p}=\oplus_{i\geq p}H^{i,n-i}\quad{\overline{F}}^{q}=\oplus_{i\leq n-q}H^{i,n-i}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></msup><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">⊕</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mi>H</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow></msup><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>q</mi></msup><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">⊕</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mi>H</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{p}=\oplus_{i\geq p}H^{i,n-i}\quad{\overline{F}}^{q}=\oplus_{i\leq n-q}H^{i,n-i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Here we don’t assume the existence of conjugation although we
keep the notation <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\overline{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\overline{F}}</annotation></semantics></math>. An <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>-Hodge
Structure of weight <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> defines a complex Hodge Structure
on <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H=H_{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H=H_{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To define polarization, we recall that the conjugate space
<math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\overline{H}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\overline{H}}</annotation></semantics></math> of a complex vector space <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>, is the same group
<math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> with a different complex structure, such that the identity map
on the group <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> defines a real linear map <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma:H\rightarrow{\overline{H}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma:H\rightarrow{\overline{H}}</annotation></semantics></math> and the
product by scalars satisfy the relation <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\forall\lambda\in\mathbb{C},v\in H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\forall\lambda\in\mathbb{C},v\in H</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\times_{{\overline{H}}}\sigma(v):=\sigma({\overline{\lambda}}\times_{H}v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></msub><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>H</mi></msub><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\times_{{\overline{H}}}\sigma(v):=\sigma({\overline{\lambda}}\times_{H}v)</annotation></semantics></math>, then the complex structure on
<math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\overline{H}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\overline{H}}</annotation></semantics></math> is unique. On the other hand a complex linear
morphism <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:V\to V^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:V\to V^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> defines a
complex linear conjugate morphism <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\overline{f}}:{\overline{V}}\to{\overline{V}^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\overline{f}}:{\overline{V}}\to{\overline{V}^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>
satisfying <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\overline{f}}(\sigma(v))=\sigma(f(v))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\overline{f}}(\sigma(v))=\sigma(f(v))</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem15" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem15.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.15</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem15.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem15.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem15.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A polarization of a complex Hodge Structure of weight <math id="S3.Thmtheorem15.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is a bilinear morphism
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem15.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S:H\otimes{\overline{H}}\to\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S:H\otimes{\overline{H}}\to\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math> such that:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S(x,\sigma(y))=(-1)^{n}\overline{S(y,\sigma(x))}\,\hbox{ for }\,x,y\in L\hbox{ and }S(F^{p},\sigma({\overline{F}}^{q}))=0\,\,{\rm for}\,\,p+q&gt;n." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> for </mtext><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> and </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>q</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.340em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>for</mi><mo lspace="0.340em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S(x,\sigma(y))=(-1)^{n}\overline{S(y,\sigma(x))}\,\hbox{ for }\,x,y\in L\hbox{ and }S(F^{p},\sigma({\overline{F}}^{q}))=0\,\,{\rm for}\,\,p+q&gt;n.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem15.p1.2" class="ltx_p">and moreover <math id="S3.Thmtheorem15.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S(C(H)u,\sigma(v))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S(C(H)u,\sigma(v))</annotation></semantics></math> is a positive definite Hermitian
form on <math id="S3.Thmtheorem15.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S3.Thmtheorem15.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C(H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(H)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the Weil action on <math id="S3.Thmtheorem15.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem16" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem16.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 3.16</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem16.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem16.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem16.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A complex Hodge Structure of weight <math id="S3.Thmtheorem16.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> on a complex vector space
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem16.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is given by a decomposition into a direct sum of subspaces
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem16.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="H=\oplus_{p\in\mathbb{Z}}H^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">⊕</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></msub><mi>H</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H=\oplus_{p\in\mathbb{Z}}H^{p}</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S3.Thmtheorem16.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{i,j}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{i,j}=0</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.Thmtheorem16.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i+j\not=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i+j\not=0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.Thmtheorem16.p1.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="F^{p}=\oplus_{i\geq p}H^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></msup><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">⊕</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mi>H</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{p}=\oplus_{i\geq p}H^{p}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem16.p1.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\overline{F}}^{q}=\oplus_{i\leq-q}H^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>q</mi></msup><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">⊕</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0em">≤</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mi>H</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\overline{F}}^{q}=\oplus_{i\leq-q}H^{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem16.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem16.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A polarization is an Hermitian form on <math id="S3.Thmtheorem16.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> for which the
decomposition is orthogonal and whose restriction to <math id="S3.Thmtheorem16.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{p}</annotation></semantics></math> is
definite for <math id="S3.Thmtheorem16.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> even and negative definite for odd <math id="S3.Thmtheorem16.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.3. </span>Examples of Mixed Hodge Structure</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">1) A Hodge Structure <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> of weight <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, is a Mixed Hodge Structure
with weight filtration:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex26" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{i}(H_{{\mathbb{Q}}})=0\quad\mbox{for}\quad i&lt;n\qquad\mbox{and}\quad W_{i}(H_{\mathbb{Q}})=H_{{\mathbb{Q}}}\quad\mbox{for}\quad i\geq n." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℚ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>for</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℚ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℚ</mi></msub></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>for</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{i}(H_{{\mathbb{Q}}})=0\quad\mbox{for}\quad i&lt;n\qquad\mbox{and}\quad W_{i}(H_{\mathbb{Q}})=H_{{\mathbb{Q}}}\quad\mbox{for}\quad i\geq n.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">2) Let <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(H^{i},F_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(H^{i},F_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> be a finite family of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>-Hodge Structures of weight <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math>; then <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="H=\oplus_{i}H^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">⊕</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>H</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H=\oplus_{i}H^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is endowed with the following Mixed Hodge Structure:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex27" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex27.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="H_{A}=\oplus_{i}H^{i}_{A},\quad W_{n}=\oplus_{i\leq n}H^{i}_{A}\otimes\mathbb{Q},\quad F^{p}=\oplus_{i}F^{p}_{i}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>A</mi></msub><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">⊕</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>H</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>A</mi></msub><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mi>W</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">⊕</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mi>H</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>A</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mi>F</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></msup><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">⊕</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>F</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{A}=\oplus_{i}H^{i}_{A},\quad W_{n}=\oplus_{i\leq n}H^{i}_{A}\otimes\mathbb{Q},\quad F^{p}=\oplus_{i}F^{p}_{i}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">3) Let <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\mathbb{Z}}=i\mathbb{Z}^{n}\subset\mathbb{C}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\mathbb{Z}}=i\mathbb{Z}^{n}\subset\mathbb{C}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, then we consider the isomorphism
<math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\mathbb{Z}}\otimes\mathbb{C}\simeq\mathbb{C}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo>≃</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\mathbb{Z}}\otimes\mathbb{C}\simeq\mathbb{C}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> defined with respect to the
canonical basis <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> by:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex28" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\mathbb{C}}\xrightarrow{\sim}\mathbb{C}^{n}:ie_{j}\otimes(a_{j}+ib_{j})\mapsto i(a_{j}+ib_{j})e_{j}=(-b_{j}+ia_{j})e_{j}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo></mover><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\mathbb{C}}\xrightarrow{\sim}\mathbb{C}^{n}:ie_{j}\otimes(a_{j}+ib_{j})\mapsto i(a_{j}+ib_{j})e_{j}=(-b_{j}+ia_{j})e_{j}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.4" class="ltx_p">hence the conjugation <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(ie_{j}\otimes(a_{j}+ib_{j}))=ie_{j}\otimes(a_{j}-ib_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(ie_{j}\otimes(a_{j}+ib_{j}))=ie_{j}\otimes(a_{j}-ib_{j})</annotation></semantics></math> on
<math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>, corresponds to the following conjugation on <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>:
<math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(-b_{j}+ia_{j})e_{j}=(b_{j}+ia_{j})e_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(-b_{j}+ia_{j})e_{j}=(b_{j}+ia_{j})e_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>.
<br class="ltx_break">4) Let <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H=(H_{\mathbb{Z}},F,W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H=(H_{\mathbb{Z}},F,W)</annotation></semantics></math> be a Mixed Hodge Structure;
its <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m-</annotation></semantics></math>twist is a Mixed Hodge Structure
denoted by <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(m)</annotation></semantics></math> and defined by:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex29" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(m)_{\mathbb{Z}}:=H_{\mathbb{Z}}\otimes(2i\pi)^{m}\mathbb{Z},\,W_{r}H(m):=(W_{r+2m}H_{\mathbb{Q}})\otimes(2i\pi)^{m}\mathbb{Q},\,F_{r}H(m):=F^{r+m}H_{\mathbb{C}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>ℤ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℚ</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(m)_{\mathbb{Z}}:=H_{\mathbb{Z}}\otimes(2i\pi)^{m}\mathbb{Z},\,W_{r}H(m):=(W_{r+2m}H_{\mathbb{Q}})\otimes(2i\pi)^{m}\mathbb{Q},\,F_{r}H(m):=F^{r+m}H_{\mathbb{C}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.4. </span>Tensor product and Hom </h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be
two Mixed Hodge Structures.
<br class="ltx_break">1) We define their Mixed Hodge Structure tensor product <math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H\otimes H^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H\otimes H^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, by applying
the above general rules of filtrations, as follows:
<br class="ltx_break">i) <math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(H\otimes H^{\prime})_{\mathbb{Z}}=H_{\mathbb{Z}}\otimes H^{\prime}_{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(H\otimes H^{\prime})_{\mathbb{Z}}=H_{\mathbb{Z}}\otimes H^{\prime}_{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math> 
<br class="ltx_break">ii) <math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{r}(H\otimes H^{\prime})_{{\mathbb{Q}}}:=\sum_{p+p^{\prime}=r}W^{p}H_{\mathbb{Q}}\otimes W^{p^{\prime}}H^{\prime}_{\mathbb{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>ℚ</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℚ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{r}(H\otimes H^{\prime})_{{\mathbb{Q}}}:=\sum_{p+p^{\prime}=r}W^{p}H_{\mathbb{Q}}\otimes W^{p^{\prime}}H^{\prime}_{\mathbb{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math>
<br class="ltx_break">iii) <math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{r}(H\otimes H^{\prime})_{{\mathbb{C}}}:=\sum_{p+p^{\prime}=r}F^{p}H_{\mathbb{C}}\otimes F^{p^{\prime}}H^{\prime}_{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>ℂ</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{r}(H\otimes H^{\prime})_{{\mathbb{C}}}:=\sum_{p+p^{\prime}=r}F^{p}H_{\mathbb{C}}\otimes F^{p^{\prime}}H^{\prime}_{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
<br class="ltx_break">2) The Mixed Hodge Structure on <math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Hom(H,H^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Hom(H,H^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as follows:
<br class="ltx_break">i) <math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Hom(H,H^{\prime})_{\mathbb{Z}}:=Hom_{\mathbb{Z}}(H_{\mathbb{Z}},H^{\prime}_{\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>ℤ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Hom(H,H^{\prime})_{\mathbb{Z}}:=Hom_{\mathbb{Z}}(H_{\mathbb{Z}},H^{\prime}_{\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math> 
<br class="ltx_break">ii) <math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p1.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="W_{r}Hom(H,H^{\prime})_{\mathbb{Q}}:=\{f:Hom_{\mathbb{Q}}(H_{\mathbb{Q}},H^{\prime}_{\mathbb{Q}}):\forall n,f(W_{n}H)\subset W_{n+r}H^{\prime}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mi>H</mi><mi>o</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>ℚ</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>H</mi><mi>o</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>ℚ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℚ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{r}Hom(H,H^{\prime})_{\mathbb{Q}}:=\{f:Hom_{\mathbb{Q}}(H_{\mathbb{Q}},H^{\prime}_{\mathbb{Q}}):\forall n,f(W_{n}H)\subset W_{n+r}H^{\prime}\}</annotation></semantics></math>
<br class="ltx_break">iii) <math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p1.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="F^{r}Hom(H,H^{\prime})_{\mathbb{C}}:=\{f:Hom_{\mathbb{C}}(H_{\mathbb{C}},H^{\prime}_{\mathbb{C}}):\forall n,f(F^{n}H)\subset F^{n+r}H^{\prime}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><mi>H</mi><mi>o</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>H</mi><mi>o</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{r}Hom(H,H^{\prime})_{\mathbb{C}}:=\{f:Hom_{\mathbb{C}}(H_{\mathbb{C}},H^{\prime}_{\mathbb{C}}):\forall n,f(F^{n}H)\subset F^{n+r}H^{\prime}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
<br class="ltx_break">In particular the dual <math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>
is a Mixed Hodge Structure.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3. </span>Proof of the canonical decomposition of the weight filtration</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Injectivity of <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math>.</span> Let <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=p+q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=p+q</annotation></semantics></math>, then
we have the inclusion
modulo <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{m-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{m-1}</annotation></semantics></math> of the image
<math id="S3.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(I^{p,q})\subset H^{p,q}=(F^{p}\cap\overline{F^{q}})(Gr^{W}_{m}H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>∩</mo><mover accent="true"><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(I^{p,q})\subset H^{p,q}=(F^{p}\cap\overline{F^{q}})(Gr^{W}_{m}H)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in I^{p,q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in I^{p,q}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(v)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(v)=0</annotation></semantics></math>,
then <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in F^{p}\cap W_{m-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in F^{p}\cap W_{m-1}</annotation></semantics></math> and the class <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="cl(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">cl(v)</annotation></semantics></math>
in <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(F^{p}\cap\overline{F^{q}})(Gr^{W}_{m-1}H)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>∩</mo><mover accent="true"><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(F^{p}\cap\overline{F^{q}})(Gr^{W}_{m-1}H)=0</annotation></semantics></math> since <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p+q&gt;m-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p+q&gt;m-1</annotation></semantics></math>, hence <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="cl(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">cl(v)</annotation></semantics></math> must
vanish; so we deduce that <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in F^{p}\cap W_{m-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in F^{p}\cap W_{m-2}</annotation></semantics></math>. This is a step
in an inductive argument based on the formula <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{p}\oplus\overline{F^{q-r+1}}\simeq Gr^{W}_{m-r}H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>⊕</mo><mover accent="true"><msup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{p}\oplus\overline{F^{q-r+1}}\simeq Gr^{W}_{m-r}H</annotation></semantics></math>. We want to prove <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in F^{p}\cap W_{m-r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in F^{p}\cap W_{m-r}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. We just proved this for <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=2</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence we write</p>
<table id="S3.Ex30" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in\overline{F^{q}}\cap W_{m}+\sum_{r-1\geq i\geq 1}\overline{F^{q-i}}\cap W_{m-i-1}+\sum_{i&gt;r-1\geq 1}\overline{F^{q-i}}\cap W_{m-i-1}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder><mover accent="true"><msup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder><mover accent="true"><msup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in\overline{F^{q}}\cap W_{m}+\sum_{r-1\geq i\geq 1}\overline{F^{q-i}}\cap W_{m-i-1}+\sum_{i&gt;r-1\geq 1}\overline{F^{q-i}}\cap W_{m-i-1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where the right term is in <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{m-r-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{m-r-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, since <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{m-i-1}\subset W_{m-r-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{m-i-1}\subset W_{m-r-1}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&gt;r-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&gt;r-1</annotation></semantics></math>, hence
<math id="S3.SS3.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in F^{p}\cap\overline{F^{q-r+1}}\cap W_{m-r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>∩</mo><mover accent="true"><msup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in F^{p}\cap\overline{F^{q-r+1}}\cap W_{m-r}</annotation></semantics></math> modulo <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{m-r-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{m-r-1}</annotation></semantics></math> since <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{F}</annotation></semantics></math> is decreasing.
As <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(F^{p}\cap\overline{F^{q-r+1}})Gr^{W}_{m-r}H=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>∩</mo><mover accent="true"><msup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(F^{p}\cap\overline{F^{q-r+1}})Gr^{W}_{m-r}H=0</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, the class <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="cl(v)=0\in Gr^{W}_{m-r}H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">cl(v)=0\in Gr^{W}_{m-r}H</annotation></semantics></math>, then
<math id="S3.SS3.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in F^{p}\cap W_{m-r-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in F^{p}\cap W_{m-r-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Finally, as
<math id="S3.SS3.p1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{m-r}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{m-r}=0</annotation></semantics></math> for large <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>, we deduce <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v=0</annotation></semantics></math>.
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S3.SS3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Surjectivity.</span> Let <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in H^{p,q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in H^{p,q}</annotation></semantics></math>; there exists
<math id="S3.SS3.p1.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{0}\in F^{p}\cap W_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{0}\in F^{p}\cap W_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> (resp. <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{0}\in{F^{q}}\cap W_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{0}\in{F^{q}}\cap W_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>) such that
<math id="S3.SS3.p1.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(v_{0})=\alpha=\varphi({\overline{u}_{0}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(v_{0})=\alpha=\varphi({\overline{u}_{0}})</annotation></semantics></math>, hence <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{0}={\overline{u}_{0}}+w_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{0}={\overline{u}_{0}}+w_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{0}\in W_{m-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{0}\in W_{m-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Applying the formula
<math id="S3.SS3.p1.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{p}\oplus\overline{F^{q}}\simeq Gr^{W}_{m-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>⊕</mo><mover accent="true"><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{p}\oplus\overline{F^{q}}\simeq Gr^{W}_{m-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, the class of
<math id="S3.SS3.p1.m37" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> decomposes as <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m38" class="ltx_Math" alttext="cl(w_{0})=cl(v^{\prime})+cl({\overline{u}^{\prime}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">cl(w_{0})=cl(v^{\prime})+cl({\overline{u}^{\prime}})</annotation></semantics></math>
with <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m39" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v^{\prime}\in F^{p}\cap W_{m-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v^{\prime}\in F^{p}\cap W_{m-1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m40" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{\prime}\in F^{q}\cap W_{m-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{\prime}\in F^{q}\cap W_{m-1}</annotation></semantics></math>; hence
there exists <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m41" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{1}\in W_{m-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{1}\in W_{m-2}</annotation></semantics></math> such that
<math id="S3.SS3.p1.m42" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{0}={\overline{u}_{0}}+v^{\prime}+{\overline{u}^{\prime}}+w_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{0}={\overline{u}_{0}}+v^{\prime}+{\overline{u}^{\prime}}+w_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let
<math id="S3.SS3.p1.m43" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{1}:=v_{0}-v^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{1}:=v_{0}-v^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m44" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{1}=u_{0}+u^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{1}=u_{0}+u^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, then</p>
<table id="S3.Ex31" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex31.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="v_{1}={\overline{u}_{1}}+w_{1},\quad{\rm where}\,\,u_{1}\in F^{q}\cap W_{m},F^{p}\cap W_{m},w_{1}\in W_{m-2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>w</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mi>where</mi><mi>u</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>F</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>q</mi></msup><mo>∩</mo><mi>W</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>∩</mo><mi>W</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>W</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{1}={\overline{u}_{1}}+w_{1},\quad{\rm where}\,\,u_{1}\in F^{q}\cap W_{m},F^{p}\cap W_{m},w_{1}\in W_{m-2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">By an increasing inductive argument on <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m45" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, we apply the formula:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex32" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{p}\oplus\overline{F^{q-k+1}}\simeq Gr^{W}_{m-k}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>⊕</mo><mover accent="true"><msup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{p}\oplus\overline{F^{q-k+1}}\simeq Gr^{W}_{m-k}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.4" class="ltx_p">to find vectors <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m46" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{k},u_{k},w_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{k},u_{k},w_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex33" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}&amp;v_{k}\in F^{p}\cap W_{m},\,\,w_{k}\in W_{m-1-k},\,\,\varphi(v_{k})=\alpha,\,\,v_{k}={\overline{u}_{k}}+w_{k}\\
&amp;u_{k}\in F^{q}\cap W_{m}+F^{q-1}\cap W_{m-2}+F^{q-2}\cap W_{n-3}+...+F^{q+1-k}\cap W_{m-k}\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.677em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.677em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.677em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}&amp;v_{k}\in F^{p}\cap W_{m},\,\,w_{k}\in W_{m-1-k},\,\,\varphi(v_{k})=\alpha,\,\,v_{k}={\overline{u}_{k}}+w_{k}\\
&amp;u_{k}\in F^{q}\cap W_{m}+F^{q-1}\cap W_{m-2}+F^{q-2}\cap W_{n-3}+...+F^{q+1-k}\cap W_{m-k}\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.5" class="ltx_p">then we decompose the class of
<math id="S3.SS3.p1.m47" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m48" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr^{W}_{m-k-1}H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr^{W}_{m-k-1}H</annotation></semantics></math> in the inductive step as above.
For large <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m49" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m50" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{m-1-k}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{m-1-k}=0</annotation></semantics></math> and we
represent <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m51" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m52" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I^{p,q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I^{p,q}</annotation></semantics></math>. 
<br class="ltx_break">Moreover <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m53" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="W_{n}=W_{n-1}\oplus(\oplus_{p+q=n}I^{p,q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mo lspace="0em">⊕</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{n}=W_{n-1}\oplus(\oplus_{p+q=n}I^{p,q})</annotation></semantics></math>,
hence by induction we decompose <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m54" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> as direct sum of
<math id="S3.SS3.p1.m55" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I^{p,q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I^{p,q}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m56" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p+q\geq n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p+q\geq n</annotation></semantics></math>. 
<br class="ltx_break">Next we suppose, by induction, the formula for <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m57" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{p}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfied
for all <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m58" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in W_{n-1}\cap F^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∩</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in W_{n-1}\cap F^{p}</annotation></semantics></math>. The image of an element
<math id="S3.SS3.p1.m59" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in F^{p}\cap W_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in F^{p}\cap W_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m60" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr^{W}_{n}H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr^{W}_{n}H</annotation></semantics></math> decomposes into Hodge components
of type <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m61" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i,n-i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i,n-i)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m62" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\geq p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\geq p</annotation></semantics></math> since <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m63" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in F^{p}\cap W_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in F^{p}\cap W_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Hence the decomposition of <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m64" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> may be writen as <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m65" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v=v_{1}+v_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v=v_{1}+v_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>
with <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m66" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="v_{1}\in\oplus_{i&lt;p}I^{i,n-i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0em">∈</mo><mo lspace="0em">⊕</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mi>I</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{1}\in\oplus_{i&lt;p}I^{i,n-i}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S3.SS3.p1.m67" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="v_{2}\in\oplus_{i\geq p}I^{i,n-i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo rspace="0em">∈</mo><mo lspace="0em">⊕</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mi>I</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{2}\in\oplus_{i\geq p}I^{i,n-i}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m68" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{1}\in W_{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{1}\in W_{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math>
since its image vanish in <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m69" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr^{W}_{n}H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr^{W}_{n}H</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence, the formula for <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m70" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{p}</annotation></semantics></math>
follows by the inductive step.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.3.1. </span>Proof of the abelianness of the category of MHS and strictness</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The definition of Mixed Hodge Structure has a surprising strong property, since any
morphism of Mixed Hodge Structures is necessarily strict for each filtration <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>. In consequence, the category is abelian.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem17" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem17.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.17</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem17.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem17.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem17.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem17.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The kernel (resp. cokernel) of a morphism <math id="S3.Thmtheorem17.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> of Mixed Hodge Structures:
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem17.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H\rightarrow H^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H\rightarrow H^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is a Mixed Hodge Structure <math id="S3.Thmtheorem17.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> with underlying module <math id="S3.Thmtheorem17.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{A}</annotation></semantics></math> equal
to the kernel (resp. cokernel) of
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem17.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{A}:H_{A}\rightarrow{H^{\prime}}_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>H</mi><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo><mi>A</mi><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{A}:H_{A}\rightarrow{H^{\prime}}_{A}</annotation></semantics></math>; moreover <math id="S3.Thmtheorem17.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{A}\otimes{\mathbb{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{A}\otimes{\mathbb{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem17.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{A}\otimes{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{A}\otimes{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>
are endowed with induced filtrations (resp. quotient
filtrations) by <math id="S3.Thmtheorem17.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.Thmtheorem17.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math> (resp. <math id="S3.Thmtheorem17.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{H^{\prime}}_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mmultiscripts><mi>H</mi><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{H^{\prime}}_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math>) and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem17.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.Thmtheorem17.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{{\mathbb{C}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{{\mathbb{C}}}</annotation></semantics></math> (resp. <math id="S3.Thmtheorem17.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{H^{\prime}}_{{\mathbb{C}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mmultiscripts><mi>H</mi><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{H^{\prime}}_{{\mathbb{C}}}</annotation></semantics></math>).</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS1.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">A morphism compatible with
the filtrations is necessarily compatible with the canonical
decomposition of the Mixed Hodge Structure
into <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\oplus I^{p,q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>⊕</mo><msup><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\oplus I^{p,q}</annotation></semantics></math>.
It is enough to check the statement on <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>, hence we drop the
index by <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℂ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>. We consider on <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=Ker(f)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=Ker(f)</annotation></semantics></math> the induced filtrations from
<math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>. The morphism <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr^{W}K\rightarrow Gr^{W}H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>W</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>W</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr^{W}K\rightarrow Gr^{W}H</annotation></semantics></math> is injective, since it
is injective on the corresponding terms <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I^{p,q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I^{p,q}</annotation></semantics></math>; moreover, the
filtration <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> (resp. <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{F}</annotation></semantics></math>) of <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> induces on <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr^{W}K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>W</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr^{W}K</annotation></semantics></math>
the inverse image of the filtration <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> (resp. <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{F}</annotation></semantics></math>) on
<math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr^{W}H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>W</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr^{W}H</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex34" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex34.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="Gr^{W}K=\oplus_{p,q}(Gr^{W}K)\cap H^{p,q}(Gr^{W}H)\,\,\mbox{and}\,\,H^{p,q}(Gr^{W}K)=(Gr^{W}K)\cap H^{p,q}(Gr^{W}H)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>W</mi></msup><mi>K</mi><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><msub><mo lspace="0em">⊕</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>W</mi></msup><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∩</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>W</mi></msup><mi>H</mi><mo rspace="0.340em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mtext>and</mtext><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>W</mi></msup><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>W</mi></msup><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∩</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>W</mi></msup><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr^{W}K=\oplus_{p,q}(Gr^{W}K)\cap H^{p,q}(Gr^{W}H)\,\,\mbox{and}\,\,H^{p,q}(Gr^{W}K)=(Gr^{W}K)\cap H^{p,q}(Gr^{W}H)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">Hence the filtrations <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W,F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W,F</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> define a Mixed Hodge Structure on
<math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> which is a kernel of <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> in the category of Mixed Hodge Structures.
The statement on the cokernel follows by duality. ∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We still need to prove that for a morphism <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> of Mixed Hodge Structures, the canonical
morphism <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Coim(f)\to Im(f)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Coim(f)\to Im(f)</annotation></semantics></math> is an isomorphism of Mixed Hodge Structures. Since by
the above lemma <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Coim(f)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Coim(f)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Im(f)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Im(f)</annotation></semantics></math> are endowed
with natural Mixed Hodge Structure, the result follows from the statement:
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A morphism of Mixed Hodge Structures which induces an isomorphism on the
lattices, is an isomorphism of Mixed Hodge Structures.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem18" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem18.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 3.18</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem18.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem18.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem18.p1.1" class="ltx_p">i) Each morphism <math id="S3.Thmtheorem18.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:H\rightarrow H^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:H\rightarrow H^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is strictly compatible with the
filtrations <math id="S3.Thmtheorem18.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.Thmtheorem18.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{H}_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{H}_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem18.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{H^{\prime}}_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mmultiscripts><mi>H</mi><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{H^{\prime}}_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math>
as well the filtrations <math id="S3.Thmtheorem18.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.Thmtheorem18.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{{\mathbb{C}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{{\mathbb{C}}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem18.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{H^{\prime}}_{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mmultiscripts><mi>H</mi><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{H^{\prime}}_{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>. It
induces morphisms <math id="S3.Thmtheorem18.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr^{W}_{n}(f):Gr^{W}_{n}(H_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}})\rightarrow Gr^{W}_{n}({H^{\prime}}_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>H</mi><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr^{W}_{n}(f):Gr^{W}_{n}(H_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}})\rightarrow Gr^{W}_{n}({H^{\prime}}_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math> compatible with the
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem18.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math>-Hodge Structures, and morphisms <math id="S3.Thmtheorem18.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr^{p}_{F}(f):Gr^{p}_{F}(H_{{\mathbb{C}}})\rightarrow Gr^{p}_{F}({H^{\prime}}_{{\mathbb{C}}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>H</mi><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr^{p}_{F}(f):Gr^{p}_{F}(H_{{\mathbb{C}}})\rightarrow Gr^{p}_{F}({H^{\prime}}_{{\mathbb{C}}})</annotation></semantics></math> strictly
compatible with the induced filtrations by <math id="S3.Thmtheorem18.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{{\mathbb{C}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{{\mathbb{C}}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
<br class="ltx_break">ii) The functor <math id="S3.Thmtheorem18.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr^{W}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr^{W}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> from the category of Mixed Hodge Structures to the category
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem18.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\otimes{\mathbb{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\otimes{\mathbb{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math>-Hodge Structures of weight <math id="S3.Thmtheorem18.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is exact and the functor <math id="S3.Thmtheorem18.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr^{p}_{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr^{p}_{F}</annotation></semantics></math>
is also exact.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem19" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem19.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 3.19</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem19.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem19.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem19.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The above result shows that any exact sequence of
Mixed Hodge Structures gives rise to various exact sequences which, in the case of
Mixed Hodge Structures on cohomology of algebraic varieties that we are going to
construct, have in general interesting geometrical interpretation,
since we deduce from any long exact sequence of Mixed Hodge Structures:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex35" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex35.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="H^{\prime i}\to H^{i}\to H^{\prime\prime i}\to H^{\prime i+1}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">′</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>H</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>H</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">′</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">′</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>H</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">′</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\prime i}\to H^{i}\to H^{\prime\prime i}\to H^{\prime i+1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem19.p1.2" class="ltx_p">various exact sequences:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex36" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex36.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="Gr^{W}_{n}H^{\prime i}\to Gr^{W}_{n}H^{i}\to Gr^{W}_{n}H^{\prime\prime i}\to Gr^{W}_{n}H^{\prime i+1}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mi>r</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>W</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>H</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">′</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>G</mi><mi>r</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>W</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>H</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>G</mi><mi>r</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>W</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>H</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">′</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">′</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>G</mi><mi>r</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>W</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>H</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">′</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr^{W}_{n}H^{\prime i}\to Gr^{W}_{n}H^{i}\to Gr^{W}_{n}H^{\prime\prime i}\to Gr^{W}_{n}H^{\prime i+1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem19.p1.3" class="ltx_p">for <math id="S3.Thmtheorem19.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> (resp. <math id="S3.Thmtheorem19.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℂ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>) coefficients, and: 
<br class="ltx_break"></p>
<table id="S3.Ex37" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex37.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="Gr_{F}^{n}H^{\prime i}\to Gr_{F}^{n}H^{i}\to Gr_{F}^{n}H^{\prime\prime i}\to Gr_{F}^{n}H^{\prime i+1}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mi>r</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><mi>H</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">′</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>G</mi><mi>r</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><mi>H</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>G</mi><mi>r</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><mi>H</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">′</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">′</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>G</mi><mi>r</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><mi>H</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">′</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr_{F}^{n}H^{\prime i}\to Gr_{F}^{n}H^{i}\to Gr_{F}^{n}H^{\prime\prime i}\to Gr_{F}^{n}H^{\prime i+1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S3.Ex38" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex38.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="Gr_{F}^{m}Gr^{W}_{n}H^{\prime i}\to Gr_{F}^{m}Gr^{W}_{n}H^{i}\to Gr_{F}^{m}Gr^{W}_{n}H^{\prime\prime i}\to Gr_{F}^{m}Gr^{W}_{n}H^{\prime i+1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mi>r</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>m</mi></msup><mi>G</mi><mi>r</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>W</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>H</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">′</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>G</mi><mi>r</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>m</mi></msup><mi>G</mi><mi>r</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>W</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>H</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>G</mi><mi>r</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>m</mi></msup><mi>G</mi><mi>r</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>W</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>H</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">′</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">′</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>G</mi><mi>r</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>m</mi></msup><mi>G</mi><mi>r</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>W</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>H</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">′</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr_{F}^{m}Gr^{W}_{n}H^{\prime i}\to Gr_{F}^{m}Gr^{W}_{n}H^{i}\to Gr_{F}^{m}Gr^{W}_{n}H^{\prime\prime i}\to Gr_{F}^{m}Gr^{W}_{n}H^{\prime i+1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.3.2. </span>Hodge numbers</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> be a Mixed Hodge Structure and set:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex39" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex39.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{pq}=Gr^{p}_{F}Gr^{q}_{\overline{F}}Gr^{W}_{p+q}H_{\mathbb{C}}=(Gr^{W}_{p+q}H_{{\mathbb{C}}})^{p,q}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>q</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{pq}=Gr^{p}_{F}Gr^{q}_{\overline{F}}Gr^{W}_{p+q}H_{\mathbb{C}}=(Gr^{W}_{p+q}H_{{\mathbb{C}}})^{p,q}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The Hodge numbers of <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> are the integers
<math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{pq}=\dim_{{\mathbb{C}}}H^{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{pq}=\dim_{{\mathbb{C}}}H^{pq}</annotation></semantics></math>, that
is the Hodge numbers <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{pq}</annotation></semantics></math> of the Hodge Structure <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr^{W}_{p+q}H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr^{W}_{p+q}H</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.3.3. </span>Opposite filtrations</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Most of the proofs on the
algebraic
structure of Mixed Hodge Structure may be carried for three filtrations in an
abelian category defined as follows <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem20" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem20.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.20</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem20.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Opposite filtrations)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem20.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem20.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem20.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Three
finite filtrations <math id="S3.Thmtheorem20.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> (increasing), <math id="S3.Thmtheorem20.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem20.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> on an object <math id="S3.Thmtheorem20.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>
of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem20.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔸</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> are opposite if</p>
<table id="S3.Ex40" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex40.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr^{p}_{F}Gr^{q}_{G}Gr^{W}_{n}(A)=0\quad{\mbox{f}or}\,\,p+q\not=n." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>G</mi><mi>q</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>f</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.340em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr^{p}_{F}Gr^{q}_{G}Gr^{W}_{n}(A)=0\quad{\mbox{f}or}\,\,p+q\not=n.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This condition is symmetric in <math id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>.
It means that <math id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> induce on <math id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{n}(A)/W_{n-1}(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{n}(A)/W_{n-1}(A)</annotation></semantics></math> two
<math id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-opposite filtrations, then <math id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr^{W}_{n}(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr^{W}_{n}(A)</annotation></semantics></math> is bigraded</p>
<table id="S3.Ex41" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex41.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="W_{n}(A)/W_{n-1}(A)=\oplus_{p+q=n}A^{p,q}\quad{\mbox{w}here}\,\,A^{p,q}=Gr^{p}_{F}Gr^{q}_{G}Gr^{W}_{p+q}(A)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><msub><mo lspace="0em">⊕</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mtext>w</mtext><mi>h</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>e</mi><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>G</mi><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mi>G</mi><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>G</mi><mi>q</mi></msubsup><mi>G</mi><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{n}(A)/W_{n-1}(A)=\oplus_{p+q=n}A^{p,q}\quad{\mbox{w}here}\,\,A^{p,q}=Gr^{p}_{F}Gr^{q}_{G}Gr^{W}_{p+q}(A)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem21" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem21.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 3.21</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem21.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p1.1" class="ltx_p">i) A bigraded object <math id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=\oplus A^{p,q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">⊕</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=\oplus A^{p,q}</annotation></semantics></math> of finite bigrading has
the following three opposite filtrations</p>
<table id="S3.Ex42" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex42.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="W_{n}=\oplus_{p+q\leq n}A^{p,q}\quad,F^{p}=\oplus_{p^{\prime}\geq p}A^{p^{\prime}q^{\prime}}\quad,G^{q}=\oplus_{q^{\prime}\geq q}A^{p^{\prime}q^{\prime}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">⊕</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mi>A</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></msup><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">⊕</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>≥</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mi>A</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>G</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>q</mi></msup><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">⊕</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>≥</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mi>A</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{n}=\oplus_{p+q\leq n}A^{p,q}\quad,F^{p}=\oplus_{p^{\prime}\geq p}A^{p^{\prime}q^{\prime}}\quad,G^{q}=\oplus_{q^{\prime}\geq q}A^{p^{\prime}q^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p1.2" class="ltx_p">ii) In the definition of an <math id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>-Mixed Hodge Structure, the filtration <math id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>
deduced from <math id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> by saclar extension, the filtration F and its
complex conjugate , form a
system <math id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(W_{{\mathbb{C}}},F,\overline{F})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(W_{{\mathbb{C}}},F,\overline{F})</annotation></semantics></math> of three opposite filtrations.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem22" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem22.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.22</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem22.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Deligne)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem22.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem22.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem22.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem22.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem22.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔸</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{A}</annotation></semantics></math> be an abelian category and
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem22.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{A}}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝔸</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{A}}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> the category of objects of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem22.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔸</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{A}}</annotation></semantics></math>
endowed with three opposite filtrations <math id="S3.Thmtheorem22.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> (increasing),
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem22.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem22.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. The morphisms of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem22.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{A}}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝔸</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{A}}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>
are morphisms in <math id="S3.Thmtheorem22.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔸</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> compatible with the three filtrations.
<br class="ltx_break">i) <math id="S3.Thmtheorem22.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{A}}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝔸</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{A}}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is an abelian category.
<br class="ltx_break">ii) The kernel (resp. cokernel)
of a morphism <math id="S3.Thmtheorem22.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:A\rightarrow B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:A\rightarrow B</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.Thmtheorem22.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{A}}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝔸</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{A}}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is the kernel (resp.
cokernel ) of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem22.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.Thmtheorem22.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔸</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{A}}</annotation></semantics></math>, endowed with the three induced filtrations
from <math id="S3.Thmtheorem22.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> (resp. quotient of the filtrations on <math id="S3.Thmtheorem22.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>). 
<br class="ltx_break">iii) Any morphism <math id="S3.Thmtheorem22.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:A\rightarrow B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:A\rightarrow B</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.Thmtheorem22.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{A}}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝔸</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{A}}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is strictly
compatible with the filtrations <math id="S3.Thmtheorem22.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W,F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W,F</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem22.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>; the morphism <math id="S3.Thmtheorem22.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr^{W}(f)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>W</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr^{W}(f)</annotation></semantics></math> is compatible with the bigradings of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem22.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr^{W}(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>W</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr^{W}(A)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem22.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr^{W}(B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>W</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr^{W}(B)</annotation></semantics></math>;
the morphisms
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem22.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr_{F}(f)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr_{F}(f)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem22.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr_{G}(f)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr_{G}(f)</annotation></semantics></math> are strictly compatible with the
induced filtration by <math id="S3.Thmtheorem22.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math>.
<br class="ltx_break">iv) The forget the filtrations functors, as well
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem22.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr^{W},Gr_{F},Gr_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>W</mi></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>F</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>G</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr^{W},Gr_{F},Gr_{G}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.Thmtheorem22.p1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr^{W}Gr_{F}\simeq Gr_{F}Gr^{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>W</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>F</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>W</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr^{W}Gr_{F}\simeq Gr_{F}Gr^{W}</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem22.p1.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr_{F}Gr_{G}Gr^{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>W</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr_{F}Gr_{G}Gr^{W}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem22.p1.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr_{G}Gr^{W}\simeq Gr^{W}Gr_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>W</mi></msup></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>W</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>G</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr_{G}Gr^{W}\simeq Gr^{W}Gr_{G}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem22.p1.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{A}}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝔸</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{A}}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.Thmtheorem22.p1.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔸</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> are exact.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4. </span>Complex Mixed Hodge Structures</h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Although the cohomology of algebraic varieties
carries a Mixed Hodge Structure defined over <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>, we may need to work in analysis
without such structure over <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem23" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem23.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.23</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem23.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem23.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem23.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A complex Mixed Hodge Structure of weight <math id="S3.Thmtheorem23.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> on a complex vector space
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem23.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is given by an increasing filtration <math id="S3.Thmtheorem23.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> and two
decreasing filtrations <math id="S3.Thmtheorem23.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem23.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> such that
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem23.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Gr^{W}_{k}H,F,G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Gr^{W}_{k}H,F,G)</annotation></semantics></math>, with the induced filtrations, is a complex HS
of weight <math id="S3.Thmtheorem23.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n+k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+k</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=0</annotation></semantics></math>, we say a complex Mixed Hodge Structure.
The definition of complex Hodge Structure
of weight <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is obtained in the particular case when <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{n}=H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{n}=H</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{n-1}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{n-1}=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS4.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.4.1. </span>Variation of complex Mixed Hodge Structures</h4>

<div id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The structure which appears in deformation theory
on the cohomology of the fibers of a
morphism of algebraic varieties leads one to introduce the concept
of variation of Mixed Hodge Structure.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem24" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem24.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.24</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem24.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem24.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem24.p1.1" class="ltx_p">i) A variation (VHS) of complex Hodge Structures on a complex
manifold <math id="S3.Thmtheorem24.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> of weight <math id="S3.Thmtheorem24.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>
is given by a data
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem24.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{H},F,{\overline{F}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{H},F,{\overline{F}})</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S3.Thmtheorem24.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math> is a complex local system,
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem24.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> (resp.
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem24.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\overline{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\overline{F}}</annotation></semantics></math>) is a
decreasing filtration varying holomorphically (resp. anti-holomorphically)
by sub-bundles of the vector bundle
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem24.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{X}\otimes_{\mathbb{C}}\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{X}\otimes_{\mathbb{C}}\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math> (resp. <math id="S3.Thmtheorem24.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\overline{X}}\otimes_{\mathbb{C}}\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></msub><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\overline{X}}\otimes_{\mathbb{C}}\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math> on the
conjugate variety <math id="S3.Thmtheorem24.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\overline{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\overline{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> with anti-holomorphic structural sheaf)
such that for each point <math id="S3.Thmtheorem24.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in X</annotation></semantics></math>, data
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem24.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{H}(x),F(x),{\overline{F}}(x))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{H}(x),F(x),{\overline{F}}(x))</annotation></semantics></math> form a Hodge Structure of weight <math id="S3.Thmtheorem24.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>.
Moreover, the connection <math id="S3.Thmtheorem24.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∇</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla</annotation></semantics></math> defined by the local system
satisfies Griffiths tranversality: for tangent vectors <math id="S3.Thmtheorem24.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>
holomorphic and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem24.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\overline{u}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\overline{u}}</annotation></semantics></math> anti-holomorphic</p>
<table id="S3.Ex43" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex43.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\nabla_{v}F^{p})\subset F^{p-1},\quad(\nabla_{{\overline{u}}}{\overline{F}}^{p})\subset{\overline{F}}^{p-1}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>v</mi></msub><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></msub><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\nabla_{v}F^{p})\subset F^{p-1},\quad(\nabla_{{\overline{u}}}{\overline{F}}^{p})\subset{\overline{F}}^{p-1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem24.p1.2" class="ltx_p">ii) A variation (VMHS) of complex Mixed Hodge Structures
of weight <math id="S3.Thmtheorem24.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.Thmtheorem24.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>
is given by the following data</p>
<table id="S3.Ex44" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex44.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{H},W,F,{\overline{F}})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{H},W,F,{\overline{F}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem24.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.Thmtheorem24.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math> is a complex local system, <math id="S3.Thmtheorem24.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math>
an increasing filtration by sub-local systems (resp.
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem24.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\overline{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\overline{F}}</annotation></semantics></math>) is a
decreasing filtration varying holomorphically (resp. anti-holomorphically)
satisfying Griffiths tranversality</p>
<table id="S3.Ex45" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex45.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\nabla_{v}F^{p})\subset F^{p-1},\quad(\nabla_{{\overline{u}}}{\overline{F}}^{p})\subset{\overline{F}}^{p-1}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>v</mi></msub><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></msub><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\nabla_{v}F^{p})\subset F^{p-1},\quad(\nabla_{{\overline{u}}}{\overline{F}}^{p})\subset{\overline{F}}^{p-1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem24.p1.4" class="ltx_p">such that <math id="S3.Thmtheorem24.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Gr^{W}_{k}\mathcal{H},F,{\overline{F}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Gr^{W}_{k}\mathcal{H},F,{\overline{F}})</annotation></semantics></math>, with the induced
filtrations, is a complex VHS of weight <math id="S3.Thmtheorem24.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n+k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+k</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=0</annotation></semantics></math> we just say a complex Variation of Mixed Hodge Structures.
Let <math id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{H}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{H}}</annotation></semantics></math> be
the conjugate local system of <math id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math>. A linear morphism <math id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S:\mathcal{H}\otimes_{\mathbb{C}}\overline{\mathcal{H}}\to\mathbb{C}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S:\mathcal{H}\otimes_{\mathbb{C}}\overline{\mathcal{H}}\to\mathbb{C}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> defines a polarization
of a VHS if it defines a polarization at each point <math id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in X</annotation></semantics></math>.
A complex Mixed Hodge Structure of weight
<math id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is graded polarisable if <math id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Gr^{W}_{k}\mathcal{H},F,{\overline{F}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Gr^{W}_{k}\mathcal{H},F,{\overline{F}})</annotation></semantics></math> is a
polarized Variation Hodge Structure.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4. </span>Hypercohomology and spectral sequence of a Filtered Complex</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The abstract definition of Mixed Hodge Structures is intended to
describe global topological and geometrical properties of
algebraic varieties and will be established by constructing
special
complexes of sheaves <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> endowed with two filtrations <math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>.
As in the
definition of Mixed Hodge Structures, the filtration <math id="S4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> must be
defined on the rational level, while <math id="S4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> exists only on the
complex level.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The topological techniques used to construct <math id="S4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> on the rational
level are different from the geometrical techniques represented by de Rham complex
used to
construct the filtration <math id="S4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> on the complex level.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Comparison morphisms between the rational and
complex levels must be added in order to obtain a satisfactory
functorial theory of Mixed Hodge Structures with respect to
algebraic morphisms. This functoriality on the level of complexes
cannot be obtained in the actual abelian category of complexes of
sheaves but in a modified category called the derived category
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>, that we describe in this
section.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In this section we are going to introduce the proper terminology.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In the next section we use this
language in order to state rigorously the hypotheses
on the weight
filtration <math id="S4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> and Hodge filtration <math id="S4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S4.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> needed to induce a
Mixed Hodge Structure on the <math id="S4.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>th-cohomology <math id="S4.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(H^{i}(K),W,F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(H^{i}(K),W,F)</annotation></semantics></math>
of the complex <math id="S4.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The proof of the existence of the Mixed Hodge Structure is based
on the use of the spectral sequence of the filtered complex <math id="S4.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(K,W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(K,W)</annotation></semantics></math>. In this section we recall what a spectral sequence is and
discuss its behavior with respect to morphisms of filtered
complexes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p7.1" class="ltx_p">The filtration <math id="S4.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> induces different filtrations on the spectral
sequence in various ways. A detailed study in the next section
will show that these filtrations coincide under adequate
hypotheses and define a Hodge Structure on the terms of the
spectral sequence.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.0.1. </span>Spectral sequence defined by a filtered complex <math id="S4.SS0.SSS1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(K,F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(K,F)</annotation></semantics></math>
in an abelian category</h4>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We consider decreasing filtrations. A
change of the sign of the indices transforms an increasing
filtration into a decreasing one, hence any result has a meaning
for both filtrations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 4.1</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> be a complex of objects of an abelian
category <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔸</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{A}</annotation></semantics></math>, with a decreasing filtration by subcomplexes
<math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>. It induces
a filtration <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> on the cohomology <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}(K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}(K)</annotation></semantics></math>, defined by:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{i}H^{j}(K)=Im(H^{j}(F^{i}K)\rightarrow H^{j}(K)),\quad\forall i,j\in\mathbb{Z}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{i}H^{j}(K)=Im(H^{j}(F^{i}K)\rightarrow H^{j}(K)),\quad\forall i,j\in\mathbb{Z}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The spectral sequence defined by the filtered complex <math id="S4.SS0.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(K,F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(K,F)</annotation></semantics></math>
is a method to compute the graded object <math id="S4.SS0.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr^{*}_{F}H^{*}(K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr^{*}_{F}H^{*}(K)</annotation></semantics></math>.
It consists of indexed objects of <math id="S4.SS0.SSS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔸</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{A}</annotation></semantics></math> endowed with differentials
(see <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3. The spectral sequence defined by a filtered complex ‣ 4. Hypercohomology and spectral sequence of a Filtered Complex ‣ Mixed Hodge Structures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>
for explicit definitions):</p>
<ol id="S4.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">terms <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{r}^{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{r}^{pq}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r&gt;0,p,q\in\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r&gt;0,p,q\in\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">differentials
<math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{r}:E_{r}^{pq}\rightarrow E_{r}^{p+r,q-r+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{r}:E_{r}^{pq}\rightarrow E_{r}^{p+r,q-r+1}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\,d_{r}\circ d_{r}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\,d_{r}\circ d_{r}=0</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">isomorphisms:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{r+1}^{pq}\simeq H(E_{r}^{p-r,q+r-1}\xrightarrow{d_{r}}E_{r}^{pq}\xrightarrow{d_{r}}E_{r}^{p+r,q-r+1})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">d</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">r</mi></msub></mover><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">d</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">r</mi></msub></mover><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{r+1}^{pq}\simeq H(E_{r}^{p-r,q+r-1}\xrightarrow{d_{r}}E_{r}^{pq}\xrightarrow{d_{r}}E_{r}^{p+r,q-r+1})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">of the <math id="S4.SS0.SSS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p,q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p,q)</annotation></semantics></math>-term of index <math id="S4.SS0.SSS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r+1</annotation></semantics></math> with
the corresponding cohomology of the sequence with index <math id="S4.SS0.SSS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>. To
avoid ambiguity we may write <math id="S4.SS0.SSS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{}_{F}E_{r}^{pq}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mmultiscripts><mi>E</mi><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mi>r</mi><mrow></mrow><mi>F</mi><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{}_{F}E_{r}^{pq}}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S4.SS0.SSS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{r}^{pq}(K,F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{r}^{pq}(K,F)</annotation></semantics></math>.
The first term is defined as:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{1}^{pq}=H^{p+q}(Gr_{F}^{p}(K))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{1}^{pq}=H^{p+q}(Gr_{F}^{p}(K)).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS1.p2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS0.SSS1.p2.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The aim of the spectral sequence</span> is to compute the terms:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{\infty}^{pq}:=Gr_{F}^{p}(H^{p+q}(K))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{\infty}^{pq}:=Gr_{F}^{p}(H^{p+q}(K))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS1.p2.4" class="ltx_p">The spectral sequence is said to
degenerate if:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\forall\,p,q,\,\,\exists\,\,r_{0}(p,q)\,\,\hbox{ such that }\,\,\forall r\geq r_{0},\quad E_{r}^{pq}\simeq E_{\infty}^{pq}:=Gr^{F}_{p}H^{p+q}(K)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">∀</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo rspace="0.507em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.507em">∃</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.340em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> such that </mtext><mo lspace="0.507em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>≃</mo><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>p</mi><mi>F</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\forall\,p,q,\,\,\exists\,\,r_{0}(p,q)\,\,\hbox{ such that }\,\,\forall r\geq r_{0},\quad E_{r}^{pq}\simeq E_{\infty}^{pq}:=Gr^{F}_{p}H^{p+q}(K).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS1.p2.5" class="ltx_p">It degenerates at rank <math id="S4.SS0.SSS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> if the
differentials <math id="S4.SS0.SSS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.SS0.SSS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{i}^{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{i}^{pq}</annotation></semantics></math> vanish for <math id="S4.SS0.SSS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\geq r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\geq r</annotation></semantics></math>.
<br class="ltx_break">In Deligne-Hodge theory, the spectral sequences of the next section degenerates
at rank less than <math id="S4.SS0.SSS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>. Most known
applications are in the case of degenerate spectral sequences
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>, for example we assume the filtration biregular, that
is finite in each degree of <math id="S4.SS0.SSS1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>. It is often convenient to
locate the terms on the coordinates <math id="S4.SS0.SSS1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p,q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p,q)</annotation></semantics></math> in some region
of the plan <math id="S4.SS0.SSS1.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>; it degenerates for example when for <math id="S4.SS0.SSS1.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> increasing, the
differential <math id="S4.SS0.SSS1.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> has source or target outside the region of non vanishing
terms.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Formulas for the terms <math id="S4.SS0.SSS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math> are mentioned later in this
section, but we
will work hard to introduce sufficient conditions to imply specifically
in our case
that <math id="S4.SS0.SSS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{r}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{r}=0</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S4.SS0.SSS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, hence the terms <math id="S4.SS0.SSS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{r}^{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{r}^{pq}</annotation></semantics></math> are identical for all
<math id="S4.SS0.SSS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math> and isomorphic to the
cohomology of the sequence <math id="S4.SS0.SSS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{1}^{p-1,q}\xrightarrow{d_{1}}E_{1}^{p,q}\xrightarrow{d_{1}}E_{1}^{p+1,q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">d</mi><mn mathsize="0.710em">1</mn></msub></mover><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">d</mi><mn mathsize="0.710em">1</mn></msub></mover><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{1}^{p-1,q}\xrightarrow{d_{1}}E_{1}^{p,q}\xrightarrow{d_{1}}E_{1}^{p+1,q}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS0.SSS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Morphisms of spectral sequences.</span> A morphism of filtered
complexes <math id="S4.SS0.SSS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:(K,F)\rightarrow(K^{\prime},F^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:(K,F)\rightarrow(K^{\prime},F^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> compatible with the
filtration induces a morphism of the corresponding spectral
sequences.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS0.SSS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Filtered resolutions</span>. In presence of two filtrations by
subcomplexes <math id="S4.SS0.SSS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS0.SSS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> on a complex <math id="S4.SS0.SSS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> of
objects of an abelian category
<math id="S4.SS0.SSS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔸</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{A}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the filtration <math id="S4.SS0.SSS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> induces by restriction a new
filtration <math id="S4.SS0.SSS1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> on the terms <math id="S4.SS0.SSS1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{i}K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{i}K</annotation></semantics></math>, which also induces a quotient
filtration <math id="S4.SS0.SSS1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S4.SS0.SSS1.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr^{i}_{W}K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr^{i}_{W}K</annotation></semantics></math>. We define in this way the graded
complexes <math id="S4.SS0.SSS1.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr_{F}K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr_{F}K</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS0.SSS1.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr_{W}K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr_{W}K</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS0.SSS1.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr_{F}Gr_{W}K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr_{F}Gr_{W}K</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 4.2</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">i) A filtration <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>
on a complex <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>
is called biregular if it is finite in each degree of <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>.
<br class="ltx_break">ii) A morphism <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:X\xrightarrow{\approx}Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">≈</mo></mover><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:X\xrightarrow{\approx}Y</annotation></semantics></math> of complexes of
objects of <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔸</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{A}</annotation></semantics></math> is a quasi-isomorphism denoted by <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≈</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx</annotation></semantics></math> if
the induced morphisms on cohomology <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}(f):H^{*}(X)\xrightarrow{\sim}H^{*}(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo></mover><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}(f):H^{*}(X)\xrightarrow{\sim}H^{*}(Y)</annotation></semantics></math> are isomorphisms for all degrees.
<br class="ltx_break">iii) A morphism <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:(K,F)\xrightarrow{\approx}(K^{\prime},F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">≈</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:(K,F)\xrightarrow{\approx}(K^{\prime},F)</annotation></semantics></math> of
complexes with biregular filtrations is a filtered
quasi-isomorphism if it is compatible with the filtrations and
induces a quasi-isomorphism on the graded object <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr^{*}_{F}(f):Gr^{*}_{F}(K)\xrightarrow{\approx}Gr^{*}_{F}(K^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">≈</mo></mover><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr^{*}_{F}(f):Gr^{*}_{F}(K)\xrightarrow{\approx}Gr^{*}_{F}(K^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>.
<br class="ltx_break">iv) A morphism <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:(K,F,W)\xrightarrow{\approx}(K^{\prime},F,W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">≈</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:(K,F,W)\xrightarrow{\approx}(K^{\prime},F,W)</annotation></semantics></math> of
complexes
with biregular filtrations <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> is a bi-filtered quasi-isomorphism
if <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr^{*}_{F}Gr_{W}^{*}(f)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>W</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr^{*}_{F}Gr_{W}^{*}(f)</annotation></semantics></math> is a quasi-isomorphism.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">In the case iii) we call <math id="S4.SS0.SSS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(K^{\prime},F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(K^{\prime},F)</annotation></semantics></math> a filtered resolution of <math id="S4.SS0.SSS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(K,F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(K,F)</annotation></semantics></math>
and in iv) we say a bi-filtered resolution, while in i) it is just
a resolution.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 4.3</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let
<math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:(K,F)\rightarrow(K^{\prime},F^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:(K,F)\rightarrow(K^{\prime},F^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> be a filtered morphism with
biregular filtrations, then the following assertions are
equivalent:
<br class="ltx_break">i) <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is a filtered quasi-isomorphism.
<br class="ltx_break">ii) <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{1}^{pq}(f):E_{1}^{pq}(K,F)\rightarrow E_{1}^{pq}(K^{\prime},F^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{1}^{pq}(f):E_{1}^{pq}(K,F)\rightarrow E_{1}^{pq}(K^{\prime},F^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> is
an isomorphism for all <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p,q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p,q</annotation></semantics></math>.
<br class="ltx_break">iii) <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{r}^{pq}(f):E_{r}^{pq}(K,F)\rightarrow E_{r}^{pq}(K^{\prime},F^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{r}^{pq}(f):E_{r}^{pq}(K,F)\rightarrow E_{r}^{pq}(K^{\prime},F^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> is
an isomorphism for all <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> and all <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p,q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p,q</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">By definition of the terms <math id="S4.SS0.SSS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{1}^{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{1}^{pq}</annotation></semantics></math>, (ii) is equivalent to (i).
We deduce iii) from ii) by induction. If we suppose the
isomorphism in iii) satisfied for <math id="S4.SS0.SSS1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\leq r_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\leq r_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, the isomorphism
for <math id="S4.SS0.SSS1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{0}+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{0}+1</annotation></semantics></math> follows since <math id="S4.SS0.SSS1.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{r_{0}}^{pq}(f)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>E</mi><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{r_{0}}^{pq}(f)</annotation></semantics></math> is compatible with
<math id="S4.SS0.SSS1.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{r_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{r_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 4.4</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">(Prop. 1.3.2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>) Let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> be a complex with a biregular
filtration <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>. The following conditions are equivalent: 
<br class="ltx_break">i) The spectral sequence defined by <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> degenerates at rank <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>
(<math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{1}=E_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{1}=E_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math>)
<br class="ltx_break">ii) The differentials <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d:K^{i}\rightarrow K^{i+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>K</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d:K^{i}\rightarrow K^{i+1}</annotation></semantics></math> are strictly
compatible with the filtrations.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.0.2. </span>Diagrams of morphisms</h4>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the next section, we call
morphism of filtered complexes <math id="S4.SS0.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:(K_{1},F_{1})\rightarrow(K_{2},F_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:(K_{1},F_{1})\rightarrow(K_{2},F_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>, a class
of diagrams of
morphisms:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(K_{1},F_{1})\xleftarrow{g_{1}\approx}(K^{\prime}_{1},F^{\prime}_{1})\xrightarrow{f_{1}}(K_{2},F_{2})\quad,\quad(K_{1},F_{1})\xrightarrow{f_{2}}(K^{\prime}_{2},,F^{\prime}_{2})\xleftarrow{g_{2}\approx}(K_{2},,F_{2})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>←</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">g</mi><mn mathsize="0.710em">1</mn></msub><mo mathsize="0.700em">≈</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">f</mi><mn mathsize="0.710em">1</mn></msub></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">f</mi><mn mathsize="0.710em">2</mn></msub></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>←</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">g</mi><mn mathsize="0.710em">2</mn></msub><mo mathsize="0.700em">≈</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(K_{1},F_{1})\xleftarrow{g_{1}\approx}(K^{\prime}_{1},F^{\prime}_{1})\xrightarrow{f_{1}}(K_{2},F_{2})\quad,\quad(K_{1},F_{1})\xrightarrow{f_{2}}(K^{\prime}_{2},,F^{\prime}_{2})\xleftarrow{g_{2}\approx}(K_{2},,F_{2})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS0.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS0.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are filtered
quasi-isomorphisms. We think of <math id="S4.SS0.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S4.SS0.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1}\circ g_{1}^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1}\circ g_{1}^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> (resp.
<math id="S4.SS0.SSS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f=g_{2}^{-1}\circ f_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f=g_{2}^{-1}\circ f_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>) by adding the inverse of a quasi-isomorphism
to the morphisms in the category. This formal construction is explained
below.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">It follows that a diagram of filtered morphisms of complexes
induces a morphism of the corresponding spectral sequences, but
the reciprocal statement is not true: the existence of a
quasi-isomorphism is stronger than the existence of an
isomorphism of spectral sequences.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The idea to add the inverse of quasi-isomorphisms to the
morphisms of complexes is due to Grothendieck (inspired by the
construction of the inverse of a multiplicative system in a ring).
It is an advantage to use such
diagrams on the complex level, rather than to work with morphisms
on the cohomology level, as the theory of derived category
will show.
The derived filtered categories described below have been used
extensively in the theory of perverse sheaves and developments of
Hodge theory in an essential way.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1. </span>Derived filtered category</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We will use in the next section the language of <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">filtered and
bi-filtered derived categories</span>,
to define Mixed Hodge Structure with compatibility between the various data and
functoriality compatible with the filtrations.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.1.1. </span>Background on derived category</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The idea of Grothendieck is to construct a new category where the
class of all quasi-isomorphisms become isomorphisms in the new
category. We follow here the construction given by Verdier <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite> in two
steps. In the first step we construct the homotopy category where
the morphisms are classes defined up to homotopy (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite>, see
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>,<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>), and in the second step, a process of inverting all
quasi-isomorphisms called localization is carried by a calculus
of fractions similar to the process of inverting a
multiplicative system in a ring, although in this case the system
of quasi-isomorphisms is not commutative so that a set of diagram
relations must be carefully added in the definition of
a multiplicative system (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>,<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.1.2. </span>The homotopy category <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K(\mathbb{A})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔸</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K(\mathbb{A})</annotation></semantics></math></h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔸</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{A}</annotation></semantics></math> be an abelian category and let <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C(\mathbb{A})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔸</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(\mathbb{A})</annotation></semantics></math> (resp. <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{+}(\mathbb{A})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔸</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{+}(\mathbb{A})</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{-}(\mathbb{A})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔸</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{-}(\mathbb{A})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{b}(\mathbb{A})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mi>b</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔸</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{b}(\mathbb{A})</annotation></semantics></math>) denotes the abelian category of complexes of
objects in <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔸</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{A}</annotation></semantics></math> (resp. complexes <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{j}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{j}=0</annotation></semantics></math>, for
<math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j&lt;&lt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&lt;&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j&lt;&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and for <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j&gt;&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&gt;&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j&gt;&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., for <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> outside a finite interval). 
<br class="ltx_break">An homotopy between two morphisms of complexes <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f,g:X^{*}\to Y^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f,g:X^{*}\to Y^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>
is a family of morphisms <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{j}:X^{j}\to Y^{j-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{j}:X^{j}\to Y^{j-1}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔸</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{A}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying
<math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{j}-g^{j}=d_{Y}^{j-1}\circ h^{j}+h^{j+1}\circ d_{X}^{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mi>j</mi></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mi>j</mi></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{j}-g^{j}=d_{Y}^{j-1}\circ h^{j}+h^{j+1}\circ d_{X}^{j}</annotation></semantics></math>. Homotopy defines
an equivalence relation on
the additive group <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Hom_{C(\mathbb{A})}(X^{*},Y^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔸</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Hom_{C(\mathbb{A})}(X^{*},Y^{*})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 4.5</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The category <math id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K(\mathbb{A})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔸</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K(\mathbb{A})</annotation></semantics></math> has the same object as the
category of complexes <math id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C(\mathbb{A})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔸</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(\mathbb{A})</annotation></semantics></math>, while the group of morphisms
<math id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Hom_{K(\mathbb{A})}(X^{*},Y^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔸</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Hom_{K(\mathbb{A})}(X^{*},Y^{*})</annotation></semantics></math> is the group of morphisms of the two
complexes of <math id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔸</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{A}</annotation></semantics></math> modulo the homotopy equivalence relation.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.1.3. </span>Injective resolutions</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">. An abelian category <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔸</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{A}</annotation></semantics></math> is said to
have enough injectives if each object <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\in\mathbb{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\in\mathbb{A}</annotation></semantics></math> is embedded in an
injective object of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔸</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{A}</annotation></semantics></math>. In this case we can give another
description of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Hom_{K(\mathbb{A})}(X^{*},Y^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔸</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Hom_{K(\mathbb{A})}(X^{*},Y^{*})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Any complex <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔸</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{A}</annotation></semantics></math>
bounded below is quasi-isomorphic to a complex of injective
objects <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I^{*}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>I</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I^{*}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> called its injective resolution <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 4.6</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given a morphism <math id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:A_{1}\to A_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:A_{1}\to A_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{+}(\mathbb{A})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔸</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{+}(\mathbb{A})</annotation></semantics></math>
and two injective resolutions <math id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}\xrightarrow{\approx}I^{*}(A_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">≈</mo></mover><mrow><msup><mi>I</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}\xrightarrow{\approx}I^{*}(A_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>,
there exists an extension of <math id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> as a morphism of resolutions
<math id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I^{*}(f):I^{*}(A_{1})\to I^{*}(A_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>I</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>I</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>I</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I^{*}(f):I^{*}(A_{1})\to I^{*}(A_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>; moreover two extensions of <math id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> are
homotopic. In particular <math id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Hom_{K^{+}(\mathbb{A})}(A_{1},A_{2})\simeq Hom_{K^{+}(\mathbb{A})}(I^{*}(A_{1}),I^{*}(A_{2}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔸</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔸</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>I</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>I</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Hom_{K^{+}(\mathbb{A})}(A_{1},A_{2})\simeq Hom_{K^{+}(\mathbb{A})}(I^{*}(A_{1}),I^{*}(A_{2}))</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">See lemma <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4.4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4.4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4.4</annotation></semantics></math> in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>. Hence, an
injective resolution of an object in <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔸</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{A}</annotation></semantics></math> becomes unique up to an
isomorphism and functorial in the category
<math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K^{+}(\mathbb{A})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔸</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K^{+}(\mathbb{A})</annotation></semantics></math>.
<br class="ltx_break">The category <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K^{+}(\mathbb{A})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔸</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K^{+}(\mathbb{A})</annotation></semantics></math> is only additive, even if <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔸</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{A}</annotation></semantics></math> is abelian.
Although we keep the same objects as in <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{+}(\mathbb{A})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔸</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{+}(\mathbb{A})</annotation></semantics></math>, the
transformation on <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Hom" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Hom</annotation></semantics></math> is an important change in the category
since an homotopy equivalence between two complexes (i.e <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:X\to Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:X\to Y</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g:Y\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g:Y\to X</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\circ f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\circ f</annotation></semantics></math> (resp.<math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\circ g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\circ g</annotation></semantics></math>) is homotopic to the identity)
becomes an isomorphism.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 4.7</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The <math id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>th cohomology of a sheaf <math id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> on a
topological space <math id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>, is defined up to an isomorphism as the
cohomology of the global section of an injective resolution <math id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{i}(I^{*}(\mathcal{F})(V))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>I</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{i}(I^{*}(\mathcal{F})(V))</annotation></semantics></math>. The complex of global sections <math id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I^{*}(\mathcal{F})(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>I</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I^{*}(\mathcal{F})(V)</annotation></semantics></math> is
defined up to an homotopy in the category of groups <math id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{+}(\mathbb{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{+}(\mathbb{Z})</annotation></semantics></math>,
while it is defined up to an
isomorphism in the homotopy category of groups <math id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K^{+}(\mathbb{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K^{+}(\mathbb{Z})</annotation></semantics></math>,
and called the
higher direct image of <math id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> by the global section functor
<math id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> or the image <math id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\Gamma(V,\mathcal{F})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\Gamma(V,\mathcal{F})</annotation></semantics></math> by the derived functor.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.1.4. </span>The derived category <math id="S4.SS1.SSS4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(\mathbb{A})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔸</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(\mathbb{A})</annotation></semantics></math></h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The resolutions of a given
complex are quasi-isomorphic. If we want to consider all
resolutions as isomorphic, we must invert quasi-isomorphisms of
complexes. We construct now a new category
<math id="S4.SS1.SSS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(\mathbb{A})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔸</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(\mathbb{A})</annotation></semantics></math> with the same objects as <math id="S4.SS1.SSS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K(\mathbb{A})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔸</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K(\mathbb{A})</annotation></semantics></math> but with a different
additive group of morphisms of two
objects <math id="S4.SS1.SSS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Hom_{D(\mathbb{A})}(X,Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔸</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Hom_{D(\mathbb{A})}(X,Y)</annotation></semantics></math> that we describe.
<br class="ltx_break">Let <math id="S4.SS1.SSS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes
the category whose objects are quasi-isomorphisms <math id="S4.SS1.SSS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s^{\prime}:Y\xrightarrow{\approx}Y^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">≈</mo></mover><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s^{\prime}:Y\xrightarrow{\approx}Y^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S4.SS1.SSS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K(\mathbb{A})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔸</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K(\mathbb{A})</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S4.SS1.SSS4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s^{\prime\prime}:Y\xrightarrow{\approx}Y^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">≈</mo></mover><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s^{\prime\prime}:Y\xrightarrow{\approx}Y^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be another object. A morphism
<math id="S4.SS1.SSS4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y^{\prime}\xrightarrow{h}Y^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">ℎ</mo></mover><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y^{\prime}\xrightarrow{h}Y^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying <math id="S4.SS1.SSS4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\circ s^{\prime}=s^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\circ s^{\prime}=s^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>
defines a morphism <math id="S4.SS1.SSS4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h:s^{\prime}\to s^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h:s^{\prime}\to s^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The key property is that we can take limits in <math id="S4.SS1.SSS4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K(\mathbb{A})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔸</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K(\mathbb{A})</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Hom_{D(\mathbb{A})}(X,Y):=\lim_{\hskip 8.19447pt\longrightarrow\,I_{Y}}Hom_{K(\mathbb{A})}(X,Y^{\prime})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔸</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">:=</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">lim</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="1.098em" rspace="0.448em" stretchy="false">⟶</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔸</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Hom_{D(\mathbb{A})}(X,Y):=\lim_{\hskip 8.19447pt\longrightarrow\,I_{Y}}Hom_{K(\mathbb{A})}(X,Y^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">By definition, <math id="S4.SS1.SSS4.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\xrightarrow{f}Y^{\prime}\xleftarrow{s^{\prime}}Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑓</mo></mover><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mover accent="true"><mo>←</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.700em">s</mi><mo mathsize="0.710em">′</mo></msup></mover><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\xrightarrow{f}Y^{\prime}\xleftarrow{s^{\prime}}Y</annotation></semantics></math> is equal to
<math id="S4.SS1.SSS4.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\xrightarrow{g}Y^{\prime\prime}\xleftarrow{s^{\prime\prime}}Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑔</mo></mover><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mover accent="true"><mo>←</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.700em">s</mi><mo mathsize="0.710em">′′</mo></msup></mover><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\xrightarrow{g}Y^{\prime\prime}\xleftarrow{s^{\prime\prime}}Y</annotation></semantics></math> in the inductive limit
if and only if there exists a diagram <math id="S4.SS1.SSS4.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y^{\prime}\xrightarrow{u}Y^{\prime\prime\prime}\xleftarrow{v}Y^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑢</mo></mover><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′′′</mo></msup><mover accent="true"><mo>←</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑣</mo></mover><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y^{\prime}\xrightarrow{u}Y^{\prime\prime\prime}\xleftarrow{v}Y^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S4.SS1.SSS4.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\circ s^{\prime}=v\circ s^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\circ s^{\prime}=v\circ s^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is a quasi
isomorphism <math id="S4.SS1.SSS4.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s^{\prime\prime\prime}:Y\to Y^{\prime\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′′′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′′′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s^{\prime\prime\prime}:Y\to Y^{\prime\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS4.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\circ f=v\circ g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\circ f=v\circ g</annotation></semantics></math> is the
same morphism <math id="S4.SS1.SSS4.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\to Y^{\prime\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′′′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\to Y^{\prime\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. In this case, an element of the group
at right may be represented by a symbol <math id="S4.SS1.SSS4.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{s^{\prime}}^{-1}\circ f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>s</mi><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{s^{\prime}}^{-1}\circ f</annotation></semantics></math> and
this representation is not unique since in the above limit
<math id="S4.SS1.SSS4.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{s^{\prime}}^{-1}\circ f={s^{\prime\prime}}^{-1}\circ g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>s</mi><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>s</mi><mrow></mrow><mo>′′</mo><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{s^{\prime}}^{-1}\circ f={s^{\prime\prime}}^{-1}\circ g</annotation></semantics></math>.
<br class="ltx_break">We summarize what we need to know here by few remarks:
<br class="ltx_break">1) A morphism <math id="S4.SS1.SSS4.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:X\to Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:X\to Y</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S4.SS1.SSS4.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(\mathbb{A})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔸</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(\mathbb{A})</annotation></semantics></math> is represented by a diagram
of morphisms:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\xleftarrow{u\approx}Z\xrightarrow{g}Y\quad{\rm or}\quad X\xleftarrow{g}Z\xrightarrow{u\approx}Y" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mover accent="true"><mo>←</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">u</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">≈</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mover><mi>Z</mi><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑔</mo></mover><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mi>or</mi><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mi>X</mi><mover accent="true"><mo>←</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑔</mo></mover><mi>Z</mi><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">u</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">≈</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mover><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\xleftarrow{u\approx}Z\xrightarrow{g}Y\quad{\rm or}\quad X\xleftarrow{g}Z\xrightarrow{u\approx}Y</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS4.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS1.SSS4.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> is a quasi-isomorphism in <math id="S4.SS1.SSS4.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K(\mathbb{A})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔸</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K(\mathbb{A})</annotation></semantics></math> or by a sequence
of such diagrams.
<br class="ltx_break">2) When there are enough injectives, the <math id="S4.SS1.SSS4.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Hom" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Hom</annotation></semantics></math> of two objects <math id="S4.SS1.SSS4.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS1.SSS4.p1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>
in
<math id="S4.SS1.SSS4.p1.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{+}(\mathbb{A})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔸</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{+}(\mathbb{A})</annotation></semantics></math> is defined by their injective resolutions (lemma 4.5,
Prop. 4.7 in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>):</p>
<table id="S4.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Hom_{D^{+}(\mathbb{A})}(A_{1},A_{2})\simeq Hom_{D^{+}(\mathbb{A})}(I^{*}(A_{1}),I^{*}(A_{2}))\simeq Hom_{K^{+}(\mathbb{A})}(I^{*}(A_{1}),I^{*}(A_{2}))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔸</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔸</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>I</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>I</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔸</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>I</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>I</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Hom_{D^{+}(\mathbb{A})}(A_{1},A_{2})\simeq Hom_{D^{+}(\mathbb{A})}(I^{*}(A_{1}),I^{*}(A_{2}))\simeq Hom_{K^{+}(\mathbb{A})}(I^{*}(A_{1}),I^{*}(A_{2}))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS4.p1.4" class="ltx_p">In particular, all resolutions of
a complex are isomorphic in the derived category.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS5" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.1.5. </span>Triangles</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We define the shift of a complex <math id="S4.SS1.SSS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(K,d_{K})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(K,d_{K})</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted by <math id="S4.SS1.SSS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="TK" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">TK</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S4.SS1.SSS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K[1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K[1]</annotation></semantics></math>,
by shifting the degrees:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(TK)^{i}=K^{i+1},\quad d_{TK}=-d_{K}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(TK)^{i}=K^{i+1},\quad d_{TK}=-d_{K}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS5.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.SS1.SSS5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u:K\rightarrow K^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u:K\rightarrow K^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be a morphism of
<math id="S4.SS1.SSS5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{+}{\mathbb{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{+}{\mathbb{A}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the cone <math id="S4.SS1.SSS5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C(u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(u)</annotation></semantics></math> is the complex <math id="S4.SS1.SSS5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="TK\oplus K^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">TK\oplus K^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> with the
differential <math id="S4.SS1.SSS5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left({-d_{K}\atop u}{0\atop d_{K^{\prime}}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow><mi>u</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mn>0</mn><msub><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left({-d_{K}\atop u}{0\atop d_{K^{\prime}}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> . The
exact sequence associated to <math id="S4.SS1.SSS5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C(u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(u)</annotation></semantics></math> is:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\rightarrow K^{\prime}\rightarrow C(u)\rightarrow TK\rightarrow 0" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\rightarrow K^{\prime}\rightarrow C(u)\rightarrow TK\rightarrow 0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS5.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.SS1.SSS5.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> denotes an homotopy from a morphism <math id="S4.SS1.SSS5.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u:K\to K^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u:K\to K^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> to
<math id="S4.SS1.SSS5.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, we define an isomorphism <math id="S4.SS1.SSS5.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{h}:C(u)\xrightarrow{\sim}C(u^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo></mover><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{h}:C(u)\xrightarrow{\sim}C(u^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> by the matrix <math id="S4.SS1.SSS5.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left({Id\atop h}{0\atop Id}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mi>h</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mn>0</mn><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left({Id\atop h}{0\atop Id}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>
acting on <math id="S4.SS1.SSS5.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="TK\oplus K^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">TK\oplus K^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, which commute with the injections of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS5.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>
in
<math id="S4.SS1.SSS5.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C(u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(u)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS5.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C(u^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(u^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>, and with the projections on <math id="S4.SS1.SSS5.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="TK" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">TK</annotation></semantics></math>. 
<br class="ltx_break">Let <math id="S4.SS1.SSS5.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS5.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be two homotopies of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS5.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.SS1.SSS5.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. A second
homotopy of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS5.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.SS1.SSS5.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, that is a family of morphisms <math id="S4.SS1.SSS5.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{j+2}:K^{j+2}\rightarrow K^{{}^{\prime}j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mmultiscripts><mi>j</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></mmultiscripts></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{j+2}:K^{j+2}\rightarrow K^{{}^{\prime}j}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S4.SS1.SSS5.p1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\in{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\in{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math>, satisfying <math id="S4.SS1.SSS5.p1.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h-h^{\prime}=d_{K^{\prime}}\circ k-k\circ d_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h-h^{\prime}=d_{K^{\prime}}\circ k-k\circ d_{K}</annotation></semantics></math>, defines an homotopy of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS5.p1.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{h}</annotation></semantics></math>
to <math id="S4.SS1.SSS5.p1.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{h^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{h^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
<br class="ltx_break">A distinguished (or exact) triangle in <math id="S4.SS1.SSS5.p1.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K({\mathbb{A}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔸</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K({\mathbb{A}})</annotation></semantics></math> is a sequence
of complexes isomorphic to the image of an exact sequence
associated to a cone in <math id="S4.SS1.SSS5.p1.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C({\mathbb{A}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔸</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C({\mathbb{A}})</annotation></semantics></math>. We remark:
<br class="ltx_break">1) The cone over the identity morphism of a complex <math id="S4.SS1.SSS5.p1.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is
homotopic to zero.
<br class="ltx_break">2) Using the construction of the mapping cylinder complex over a
morphism of complexes <math id="S4.SS1.SSS5.p1.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u:X\to Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u:X\to Y</annotation></semantics></math>, one can transform
<math id="S4.SS1.SSS5.p1.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math>, up to an homotopy equivalence into an injective morphism of
complexes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>).
<br class="ltx_break">3) The derived category
is a triangulated category (that is endowed with a class of
distinguished triangles).
A distinguished triangle in <math id="S4.SS1.SSS5.p1.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D({\mathbb{A}})\," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔸</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D({\mathbb{A}})\,</annotation></semantics></math>
is a sequence of complexes isomorphic to the image of a
distinguished triangle in <math id="S4.SS1.SSS5.p1.m37" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K({\mathbb{A}})\," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔸</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K({\mathbb{A}})\,</annotation></semantics></math>. Long exact sequences of
cohomologies are associated to triangles.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS6" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.1.6. </span>Derived functor</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.SS1.SSS6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T:\mathbb{A}\rightarrow\mathbb{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>𝔸</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝔹</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T:\mathbb{A}\rightarrow\mathbb{B}</annotation></semantics></math> be a functor
of abelian categories. We denote also by <math id="S4.SS1.SSS6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T:C^{+}{\mathbb{A}}\to C^{+}{\mathbb{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔹</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T:C^{+}{\mathbb{A}}\to C^{+}{\mathbb{B}}</annotation></semantics></math>
the corresponding functor on complexes, and by <math id="S4.SS1.SSS6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="can:C^{+}{\mathbb{A}}\to D^{+}{\mathbb{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">can:C^{+}{\mathbb{A}}\to D^{+}{\mathbb{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> the canonical functor, then we construct a derived
functor:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RT:D^{+}{\mathbb{A}}\to D^{+}{\mathbb{B}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔹</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RT:D^{+}{\mathbb{A}}\to D^{+}{\mathbb{B}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS6.p1.2" class="ltx_p">satisfying <math id="S4.SS1.SSS6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RT\circ can=can\circ T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RT\circ can=can\circ T</annotation></semantics></math> under the following
conditions:
<br class="ltx_break">1) the functor <math id="S4.SS1.SSS6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T:\mathbb{A}\rightarrow\mathbb{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>𝔸</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝔹</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T:\mathbb{A}\rightarrow\mathbb{B}</annotation></semantics></math> is left exact; 
<br class="ltx_break">2) the category
<math id="S4.SS1.SSS6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔸</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> has enough injective objects.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The construction is dual with the following condition: <math id="S4.SS1.SSS6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is
right exact and there exists enough projective.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS6.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">a) Given a complex <math id="S4.SS1.SSS6.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S4.SS1.SSS6.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{+}({\mathbb{A}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔸</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{+}({\mathbb{A}})</annotation></semantics></math>, we start by
choosing an injective resolution of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS6.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>, that is a
quasi-isomorphism <math id="S4.SS1.SSS6.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i:K\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\approx}}{{\rightarrow}}I(K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mo>≈</mo></mover><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i:K\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\approx}}{{\rightarrow}}I(K)</annotation></semantics></math>
where the components of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS6.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> are injectives in each degree
(see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite> Lemma 4.6 p. 42).
<br class="ltx_break">b) We define <math id="S4.SS1.SSS6.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RT(K)=T(I(K))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RT(K)=T(I(K))</annotation></semantics></math>. 
<br class="ltx_break">c) A morphism <math id="S4.SS1.SSS6.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:K\to K^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:K\to K^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> gives rise to a morphism
<math id="S4.SS1.SSS6.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RT(K)\to RT(K^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RT(K)\to RT(K^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> functorially, since <math id="S4.SS1.SSS6.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>
can be extended
to a morphism <math id="S4.SS1.SSS6.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(f):T(I(K))\to T(I(K^{\prime}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(f):T(I(K))\to T(I(K^{\prime}))</annotation></semantics></math>, defined on
the injective resolutions uniquely up to homotopy.
<br class="ltx_break">In particular, for a
different choice of an injective resolution <math id="S4.SS1.SSS6.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J(K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J(K)</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS6.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>, we
have an isomorphism <math id="S4.SS1.SSS6.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T(I(K))\simeq T(J(K))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(I(K))\simeq T(J(K))</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S4.SS1.SSS6.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{+}(\mathbb{B})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔹</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{+}(\mathbb{B})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 4.8</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">i) The cohomology <math id="S4.Thmtheorem8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{j}(RT(K))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{j}(RT(K))</annotation></semantics></math> is called the hypercohomology
<math id="S4.Thmtheorem8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{j}T(K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{j}T(K)</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.Thmtheorem8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="S4.Thmtheorem8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>.
<br class="ltx_break">ii) An object <math id="S4.Thmtheorem8.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\in\mathbb{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\in\mathbb{A}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S4.Thmtheorem8.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>-acyclic if
<math id="S4.Thmtheorem8.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{j}T(A)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{j}T(A)=0</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S4.Thmtheorem8.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 4.9</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">i) If <math id="S4.Thmtheorem9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\xrightarrow{\approx}K^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">≈</mo></mover><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\xrightarrow{\approx}K^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is a quasi-isomorphism of
complexes, <math id="S4.Thmtheorem9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="TK" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">TK</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="TK^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">TK^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> are not quasi-isomorphisc in general,
while <math id="S4.Thmtheorem9.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RT(K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RT(K)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem9.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RT(K^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RT(K^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> must be quasi-isomorphic since the image of
an isomorphism in the derived category must
be an isomorphism.
<br class="ltx_break">ii) It is important to know that we can use acyclic objects to
compute <math id="S4.Thmtheorem9.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RT" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RT</annotation></semantics></math>: for any resolution <math id="S4.Thmtheorem9.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A(K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A(K)</annotation></semantics></math> of a complex <math id="S4.Thmtheorem9.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S4.Thmtheorem9.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext=":K\xrightarrow{\approx}A(K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">≈</mo></mover><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">:K\xrightarrow{\approx}A(K)</annotation></semantics></math>), by acyclic objects, <math id="S4.Thmtheorem9.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="TA(K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">TA(K)</annotation></semantics></math> is
isomorphic to the complex <math id="S4.Thmtheorem9.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RT(K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RT(K)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 4.10</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p1.1" class="ltx_p">1) The hypercohomology of the global section functor <math id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>
in the case of
sheaves on a topological space, is equal to the cohomology
defined via flasque resolutions or any “acyclic ” resolution.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p2.1" class="ltx_p">2) <span id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Extension groups.</span> The group of morphisms of two
complexes <math id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Hom_{D(\mathbb{A})}(X^{*},Y^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔸</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Hom_{D(\mathbb{A})}(X^{*},Y^{*})</annotation></semantics></math>
obtained in the new category <math id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(\mathbb{A})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔸</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(\mathbb{A})</annotation></semantics></math> called derived category
has a significant interpretation as an extension group:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Hom_{D(\mathbb{A})}(X^{*},Y^{*}[n])=Ext^{n}(X^{*},Y^{*})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔸</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Hom_{D(\mathbb{A})}(X^{*},Y^{*}[n])=Ext^{n}(X^{*},Y^{*})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p2.2" class="ltx_p">In presence of enough injectives, these groups are derived from
the <math id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Hom" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Hom</annotation></semantics></math> functor. In general the group <math id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Hom_{D(\mathbb{A})}(A,B[n])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔸</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Hom_{D(\mathbb{A})}(A,B[n])</annotation></semantics></math> of
two objects in <math id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔸</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{A}</annotation></semantics></math> may be still interpreted as the Yoneda
<math id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n-</annotation></semantics></math>extension group <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS7" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.1.7. </span>Filtered homotopy categories <math id="S4.SS1.SSS7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K^{+}F(\mathbb{A}),K^{+}F_{2}(\mathbb{A})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔸</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔸</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K^{+}F(\mathbb{A}),K^{+}F_{2}(\mathbb{A})</annotation></semantics></math></h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For an abelian category <math id="S4.SS1.SSS7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔸</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{A}}</annotation></semantics></math>, let
<math id="S4.SS1.SSS7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F{\mathbb{A}}\," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F{\mathbb{A}}\,</annotation></semantics></math> (resp. <math id="S4.SS1.SSS7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{2}{\mathbb{A}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{2}{\mathbb{A}})</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the category of
filtered objects (resp. bi-filtered) of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔸</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> with finite
filtration(s),
<math id="S4.SS1.SSS7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{+}F{\mathbb{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{+}F{\mathbb{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> (resp. <math id="S4.SS1.SSS7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{+}F_{2}{\mathbb{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{+}F_{2}{\mathbb{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> ) the
category of complexes of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS7.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F{\mathbb{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F{\mathbb{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> (resp. <math id="S4.SS1.SSS7.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{2}{\mathbb{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{2}{\mathbb{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> ) bounded on
the left ( zero in degrees near <math id="S4.SS1.SSS7.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\infty</annotation></semantics></math>) with morphisms of
complexes respecting the filtration(s).
<br class="ltx_break">Two morphisms <math id="S4.SS1.SSS7.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u,u^{\prime}:(K,F,W)\to(K^{\prime},F,W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u,u^{\prime}:(K,F,W)\to(K^{\prime},F,W)</annotation></semantics></math> to
are homotopic if there exists an homotopy from <math id="S4.SS1.SSS7.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.SS1.SSS7.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>
compatible with the filtrations, then it induces an homotopy on
each term <math id="S4.SS1.SSS7.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{i+1}:F^{j}K^{i+1}\rightarrow F^{\prime j}K^{{}^{\prime}i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">′</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mmultiscripts><mi>i</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></mmultiscripts></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{i+1}:F^{j}K^{i+1}\rightarrow F^{\prime j}K^{{}^{\prime}i}</annotation></semantics></math> (resp. for
<math id="S4.SS1.SSS7.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math>) and in particular <math id="S4.SS1.SSS7.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr_{F}(u-u^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr_{F}(u-u^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> (resp. <math id="S4.SS1.SSS7.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr_{F}Gr_{W}(u-u^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr_{F}Gr_{W}(u-u^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>) is
homotopic to <math id="S4.SS1.SSS7.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>.
<br class="ltx_break">The homotopy category whose objects are bounded below complexes
of filtered (resp. bi-filtered) objects
of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS7.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔸</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{A}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and whose morphisms are equivalence classes modulo
homotopy compatible with the filtration(s) is denoted by <math id="S4.SS1.SSS7.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K^{+}F{\mathbb{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K^{+}F{\mathbb{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> (resp. <math id="S4.SS1.SSS7.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K^{+}F_{2}{\mathbb{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K^{+}F_{2}{\mathbb{A}}</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS8" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.1.8. </span>Derived filtered categories <math id="S4.SS1.SSS8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{+}F(\mathbb{A}),D^{+}F_{2}(\mathbb{A})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔸</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔸</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{+}F(\mathbb{A}),D^{+}F_{2}(\mathbb{A})</annotation></semantics></math></h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">They are deduced from <math id="S4.SS1.SSS8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K^{+}F{\mathbb{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K^{+}F{\mathbb{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> (resp. <math id="S4.SS1.SSS8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K^{+}F_{2}{\mathbb{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K^{+}F_{2}{\mathbb{A}}</annotation></semantics></math>) by
inverting the filtered quasi-isomorphisms (resp. bi-filtered
quasi-isomorphisms).
The objects of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{+}F{\mathbb{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{+}F{\mathbb{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> (resp.<math id="S4.SS1.SSS8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{+}F_{2}{\mathbb{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{+}F_{2}{\mathbb{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> ) are complexes of
filtered objects of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS8.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔸</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{A}</annotation></semantics></math>
as of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS8.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K^{+}F{\mathbb{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K^{+}F{\mathbb{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> (resp. <math id="S4.SS1.SSS8.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K^{+}F_{2}{\mathbb{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K^{+}F_{2}{\mathbb{A}}</annotation></semantics></math>). Hence, the morphisms are
represented by diagrams with filtered (resp. bi-filtered)
quasi-isomorphisms.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS9" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.1.9. </span>Triangles</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The complex <math id="S4.SS1.SSS9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T(K,F,W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(K,F,W)</annotation></semantics></math> and the cone <math id="S4.SS1.SSS9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C(u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(u)</annotation></semantics></math> of a morphism
<math id="S4.SS1.SSS9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u:(K,F,W)\rightarrow(K^{\prime},F,W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u:(K,F,W)\rightarrow(K^{\prime},F,W)</annotation></semantics></math> are endowed naturally with
filtrations <math id="S4.SS1.SSS9.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS9.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math>. The exact sequence associated to <math id="S4.SS1.SSS9.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C(u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(u)</annotation></semantics></math> is
compatible with the filtrations. A filtered homotopy <math id="S4.SS1.SSS9.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> of
morphisms <math id="S4.SS1.SSS9.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS9.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> defines a filtered isomorphism of cones
<math id="S4.SS1.SSS9.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{h}:C(u)\xrightarrow{\sim}C(u^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo></mover><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{h}:C(u)\xrightarrow{\sim}C(u^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>.
<br class="ltx_break">Distinguished (or exact) triangles are defined similarly in <math id="S4.SS1.SSS9.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K^{+}F{\mathbb{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K^{+}F{\mathbb{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS9.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K^{+}F_{2}{\mathbb{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K^{+}F_{2}{\mathbb{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> as well in <math id="S4.SS1.SSS9.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{+}F{\mathbb{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{+}F{\mathbb{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS9.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{+}F_{2}{\mathbb{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{+}F_{2}{\mathbb{A}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Long filtered (resp. bi- filtered) exact sequences of
cohomologies are associated to triangles.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2. </span>Derived functor on a filtered complex</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T:\mathbb{A}\to\mathbb{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>𝔸</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝔹</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T:\mathbb{A}\to\mathbb{B}</annotation></semantics></math>
be a left exact functor of abelian
categories with enough injectives in <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔸</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{A}</annotation></semantics></math>. We want to construct a
derived functor <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RT:D^{+}F{\mathbb{A}}\to D^{+}F{\mathbb{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔹</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RT:D^{+}F{\mathbb{A}}\to D^{+}F{\mathbb{B}}</annotation></semantics></math> (resp. <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RT:D^{+}F_{2}{\mathbb{A}}\to D^{+}F_{2}{\mathbb{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔹</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RT:D^{+}F_{2}{\mathbb{A}}\to D^{+}F_{2}{\mathbb{B}}</annotation></semantics></math>). Given a filtered complex with biregular
filtration(s) we define first the image of the filtrations
via acyclic
filtered resolutions.
Then, we remark that the image of a filtered quasi-isomorphism
is a filtered quasi-isomorphism, hence the construction factors by <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RT" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RT</annotation></semantics></math>
through the derived filtered category.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We need to introduce the concept of <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>-acyclic filtered
resolutions.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.2.1. </span>Image of a filtration by a left exact functor</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(A,F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(A,F)</annotation></semantics></math> be a filtered object in <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔸</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{A}</annotation></semantics></math>, with a finite
filtration. Since <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is left exact, a filtration <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="TF" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">TF</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="TA" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">TA</annotation></semantics></math>
is defined by the sub-objects <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="TF^{p}(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">TF^{p}(A)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr_{F}(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr_{F}(A)</annotation></semantics></math> is
<math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>-acyclic, then the objects <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{p}(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{p}(A)</annotation></semantics></math> are <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>-acyclic as
successive extensions of <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>-acyclic objects. Hence, the image by
<math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> of the sequence of acyclic objects:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\rightarrow F^{p+1}(A)\rightarrow F^{p}(A)\rightarrow Gr^{p}_{F}(A)\rightarrow 0" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\rightarrow F^{p+1}(A)\rightarrow F^{p}(A)\rightarrow Gr^{p}_{F}(A)\rightarrow 0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">is exact; then:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem11" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem11.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 4.11</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem11.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem11.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="S4.Thmtheorem11.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr_{F}A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr_{F}A</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S4.Thmtheorem11.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T-</annotation></semantics></math>acyclic object, we have
<math id="S4.Thmtheorem11.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr_{TF}TA\simeq TGr_{F}A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr_{TF}TA\simeq TGr_{F}A</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.2.2. </span></h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> be an object with two finite filtrations
<math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr_{F}Gr_{W}A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr_{F}Gr_{W}A</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-acyclic</span>, then the
objects <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr_{F}A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr_{F}A</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr_{W}A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr_{W}A</annotation></semantics></math> are <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>-acyclic, as well <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{q}(A)\cap W^{p}(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{q}(A)\cap W^{p}(A)</annotation></semantics></math>. As a consequence of acyclicity, the sequences:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\rightarrow T(F^{q}\cap W^{p+1})\rightarrow T(F^{q}\cap W^{p})\rightarrow T((F^{q}\cap W^{p})/(F^{q}\cap W^{p+1}))\rightarrow 0" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo>∩</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo>∩</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo>∩</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo>∩</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\rightarrow T(F^{q}\cap W^{p+1})\rightarrow T(F^{q}\cap W^{p})\rightarrow T((F^{q}\cap W^{p})/(F^{q}\cap W^{p+1}))\rightarrow 0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">are exact, and <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T(F^{q}(Gr^{p}_{W}A))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>W</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(F^{q}(Gr^{p}_{W}A))</annotation></semantics></math> is the image in <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T(Gr^{p}_{W}(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>W</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(Gr^{p}_{W}(A)</annotation></semantics></math> of
<math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T(F^{q}\cap W^{p})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo>∩</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(F^{q}\cap W^{p})</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, the isomorphism <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr_{TW}TA\simeq TGr_{W}A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr_{TW}TA\simeq TGr_{W}A</annotation></semantics></math> transforms the filtration <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr_{TW}(TF)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr_{TW}(TF)</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr_{TW}TA" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr_{TW}TA</annotation></semantics></math>
into the filtration <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T(Gr_{W}(F))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(Gr_{W}(F))</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="TGr_{W}A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">TGr_{W}A</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.2.3. </span><math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RT:D^{+}F({\mathbb{A}})\rightarrow D^{+}F({\mathbb{B}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔸</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔹</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RT:D^{+}F({\mathbb{A}})\rightarrow D^{+}F({\mathbb{B}})</annotation></semantics></math></h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> be a biregular
filtration of <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A filtered <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>-acyclic resolution of <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> is given by a
filtered quasi-isomorphism <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\colon(K,F)\rightarrow(K^{\prime},F^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\colon(K,F)\rightarrow(K^{\prime},F^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>
to a complex with a biregular filtration such that <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr^{p}_{F}(K^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr^{p}_{F}(K^{n})</annotation></semantics></math>
are acyclic for <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem12" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem12.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 4.12</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Filtered derived functor of
a left exact functor <math id="S4.Thmtheorem12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T:\mathbb{A}\to\mathbb{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>𝔸</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝔹</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T:\mathbb{A}\to\mathbb{B}</annotation></semantics></math>)<span id="S4.Thmtheorem12.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem12.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem12.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem12.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose we are given
functorially for each filtered
complex <math id="S4.Thmtheorem12.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(K,F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(K,F)</annotation></semantics></math> a filtered <math id="S4.Thmtheorem12.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>-acyclic resolution <math id="S4.Thmtheorem12.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i:(K,F)\rightarrow(K^{\prime},F^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i:(K,F)\rightarrow(K^{\prime},F^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>, we define <math id="S4.Thmtheorem12.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}:C^{+}F({\mathbb{A}})\rightarrow D^{+}F({\mathbb{B}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔸</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔹</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}:C^{+}F({\mathbb{A}})\rightarrow D^{+}F({\mathbb{B}})</annotation></semantics></math>
by the formula <math id="S4.Thmtheorem12.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}(K,F)=(TK^{\prime},TF^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}(K,F)=(TK^{\prime},TF^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>.
A filtered quasi-isomorphism <math id="S4.Thmtheorem12.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:(K_{1},F_{1})\rightarrow(K_{2},F_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:(K_{1},F_{1})\rightarrow(K_{2},F_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>
induces an isomorphism <math id="S4.Thmtheorem12.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}(f):T(K_{1},F_{1})\simeq T(K_{2},F_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}(f):T(K_{1},F_{1})\simeq T(K_{2},F_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> in
<math id="S4.Thmtheorem12.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{+}F({\mathbb{B}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔹</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{+}F({\mathbb{B}})</annotation></semantics></math>, hence <math id="S4.Thmtheorem12.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> factors through a derived functor
<math id="S4.Thmtheorem12.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RT:D^{+}F({\mathbb{A}})\rightarrow D^{+}F({\mathbb{B}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔸</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔹</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RT:D^{+}F({\mathbb{A}})\rightarrow D^{+}F({\mathbb{B}})</annotation></semantics></math> such that
<math id="S4.Thmtheorem12.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RT(K,F)=(TK^{\prime},TF^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RT(K,F)=(TK^{\prime},TF^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>, and we have <math id="S4.Thmtheorem12.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr_{F}RT(K)\simeq RT(Gr_{F}K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr_{F}RT(K)\simeq RT(Gr_{F}K)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In particular for a different choice <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(K^{\prime\prime},F^{\prime\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(K^{\prime\prime},F^{\prime\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(K^{\prime},F^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(K^{\prime},F^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> we have an isomorphism <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(TK^{\prime\prime},TF^{\prime\prime})\simeq(TK^{\prime},TF^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(TK^{\prime\prime},TF^{\prime\prime})\simeq(TK^{\prime},TF^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{+}F({\mathbb{B}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔹</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{+}F({\mathbb{B}})</annotation></semantics></math> and</p>
<table id="S4.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RT(Gr_{F}K)\simeq Gr_{TF^{\prime}}T(K^{\prime})\simeq Gr_{TF^{\prime\prime}}T(K^{\prime\prime})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RT(Gr_{F}K)\simeq Gr_{TF^{\prime}}T(K^{\prime})\simeq Gr_{TF^{\prime\prime}}T(K^{\prime\prime}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem13" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem13.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 4.13</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem13.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem13.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem13.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the particular case of interest, where <math id="S4.Thmtheorem13.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔸</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the category
of sheaves of <math id="S4.Thmtheorem13.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>-modules on a topological space <math id="S4.Thmtheorem13.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, and where
<math id="S4.Thmtheorem13.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is the global section functor <math id="S4.Thmtheorem13.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.Thmtheorem13.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔸</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> to the
category of modules over the ring <math id="S4.Thmtheorem13.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>, an example of filtered
<math id="S4.Thmtheorem13.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>-acyclic resolution of <math id="S4.Thmtheorem13.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> is the simple complex <math id="S4.Thmtheorem13.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}^{*}(K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}^{*}(K)</annotation></semantics></math>, associated to the double complex defined by Godement
resolution (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite> Chap.II, §3.6 p.95 or
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> Chap.II, §4.3 p.167) <math id="S4.Thmtheorem13.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> in each degree of <math id="S4.Thmtheorem13.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>,
filtered by <math id="S4.Thmtheorem13.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}^{*}(F^{p}K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}^{*}(F^{p}K)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem13.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem13.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This example will apply to the next result for bi-filtered
complexes <math id="S4.Thmtheorem13.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(K,W,F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(K,W,F)</annotation></semantics></math> and the resolution <math id="S4.Thmtheorem13.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathcal{G}}^{*}K,{\mathcal{G}}^{*}W,{\mathcal{G}}^{*}F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathcal{G}}^{*}K,{\mathcal{G}}^{*}W,{\mathcal{G}}^{*}F)</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying</p>
<table id="S4.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr_{{\mathcal{G}}^{*}F}Gr_{{\mathcal{G}}^{*}W}({\mathcal{G}}^{*}K)\simeq{\mathcal{G}}^{*}(Gr_{F}Gr_{W}K)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr_{{\mathcal{G}}^{*}F}Gr_{{\mathcal{G}}^{*}W}({\mathcal{G}}^{*}K)\simeq{\mathcal{G}}^{*}(Gr_{F}Gr_{W}K)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.2.4. </span><math id="S4.SS2.SSS4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RT:D^{+}F_{2}({\mathbb{A}})\rightarrow D^{+}F_{2}({\mathbb{B}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔸</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔹</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RT:D^{+}F_{2}({\mathbb{A}})\rightarrow D^{+}F_{2}({\mathbb{B}})</annotation></semantics></math></h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.SS2.SSS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.SSS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> be two
biregular filtrations of <math id="S4.SS2.SSS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A bi-filtered <math id="S4.SS2.SSS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>-acyclic resolution of <math id="S4.SS2.SSS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> is a bi-filtered quasi-
isomorphism <math id="S4.SS2.SSS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i:(K,W,F)\rightarrow(K^{\prime},W^{\prime},F^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i:(K,W,F)\rightarrow(K^{\prime},W^{\prime},F^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.SS2.SSS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(K,W,F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(K,W,F)</annotation></semantics></math> to a
bi-filtered complex biregular for each filtration
such that <math id="S4.SS2.SSS4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr^{p}_{F}Gr^{q}_{W}(K^{{}^{\prime}n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>W</mi><mi>q</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mmultiscripts><mi>n</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></mmultiscripts></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr^{p}_{F}Gr^{q}_{W}(K^{{}^{\prime}n})</annotation></semantics></math> are acyclic for <math id="S4.SS2.SSS4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S4.SS2.SSS4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.SSS4.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.SSS4.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem14" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem14.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 4.14</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem14.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem14.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem14.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem14.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose we are given
functorially for each bi-filtered
complex <math id="S4.Thmtheorem14.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(K,F,W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(K,F,W)</annotation></semantics></math> a bi-filtered <math id="S4.Thmtheorem14.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>-acyclic resolution <math id="S4.Thmtheorem14.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i:(K,F,W)\rightarrow(K^{\prime},F^{\prime},W^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i:(K,F,W)\rightarrow(K^{\prime},F^{\prime},W^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>, we define <math id="S4.Thmtheorem14.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}:C^{+}F({\mathbb{A}})\rightarrow D^{+}F({\mathbb{B}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔸</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔹</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}:C^{+}F({\mathbb{A}})\rightarrow D^{+}F({\mathbb{B}})</annotation></semantics></math> by the formula <math id="S4.Thmtheorem14.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}(K,F,W)=(TK^{\prime},TF^{\prime},TW^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}(K,F,W)=(TK^{\prime},TF^{\prime},TW^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>.
<br class="ltx_break">A bi-filtered quasi-isomorphism <math id="S4.Thmtheorem14.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:(K_{1},F_{1},W_{1})\rightarrow(K_{2},F_{2},W_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:(K_{1},F_{1},W_{1})\rightarrow(K_{2},F_{2},W_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> induces an isomorphism <math id="S4.Thmtheorem14.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}(f):T^{\prime}(K_{1},F_{1},W_{1})\simeq T^{\prime}(K_{2},F_{2},W_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}(f):T^{\prime}(K_{1},F_{1},W_{1})\simeq T^{\prime}(K_{2},F_{2},W_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S4.Thmtheorem14.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{+}F_{2}({\mathbb{B}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔹</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{+}F_{2}({\mathbb{B}})</annotation></semantics></math>, hence
<math id="S4.Thmtheorem14.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> factors through a derived functor
<math id="S4.Thmtheorem14.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RT:D^{+}F_{2}({\mathbb{A}})\rightarrow D^{+}F_{2}({\mathbb{B}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔸</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔹</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RT:D^{+}F_{2}({\mathbb{A}})\rightarrow D^{+}F_{2}({\mathbb{B}})</annotation></semantics></math> such that
<math id="S4.Thmtheorem14.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RT(K,F,W)=(TK^{\prime},TF^{\prime},TW^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RT(K,F,W)=(TK^{\prime},TF^{\prime},TW^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>, and we have
<math id="S4.Thmtheorem14.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr_{F}Gr_{W}RT(K)\simeq RT(Gr_{F}Gr_{W}K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr_{F}Gr_{W}RT(K)\simeq RT(Gr_{F}Gr_{W}K)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In particular for a different choice <math id="S4.SS2.SSS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(K^{\prime\prime},F^{\prime\prime},W^{\prime\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(K^{\prime\prime},F^{\prime\prime},W^{\prime\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.SS2.SSS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(K^{\prime},F^{\prime},W^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(K^{\prime},F^{\prime},W^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> we have an isomorphism <math id="S4.SS2.SSS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(TK^{\prime\prime},TF^{\prime\prime},TW^{\prime\prime})\simeq(TK^{\prime},TF^{\prime},TW^{\prime\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(TK^{\prime\prime},TF^{\prime\prime},TW^{\prime\prime})\simeq(TK^{\prime},TF^{\prime},TW^{\prime\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S4.SS2.SSS4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{+}F_{2}({\mathbb{B}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔹</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{+}F_{2}({\mathbb{B}})</annotation></semantics></math> and</p>
<table id="S4.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RT(Gr_{F}Gr_{W}K)\simeq Gr_{TF^{\prime}}Gr_{TW^{\prime}}T(K^{\prime})\simeq Gr_{TF^{\prime\prime}}T(K^{\prime\prime})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RT(Gr_{F}Gr_{W}K)\simeq Gr_{TF^{\prime}}Gr_{TW^{\prime}}T(K^{\prime})\simeq Gr_{TF^{\prime\prime}}T(K^{\prime\prime})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3. </span>The spectral sequence defined by a filtered complex</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A decreasing filtration <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> of a complex <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> by sub-complexes
induces a filtration still denoted <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> on its cohomology
<math id="S4.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}(K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}(K)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The aim of a spectral sequence is to compute the
associated graded cohomology <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr_{F}^{*}H^{*}(K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr_{F}^{*}H^{*}(K)</annotation></semantics></math> of the filtered group
(<math id="S4.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}(K),F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}(K),F</annotation></semantics></math>), out of the cohomology <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}(F^{i}K/F^{j}K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>j</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}(F^{i}K/F^{j}K)</annotation></semantics></math> of the
various indices of the filtration. The spectral sequence
<math id="S4.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E^{p,q}_{r}(K,F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E^{p,q}_{r}(K,F)</annotation></semantics></math> associated to <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>) leads
for large <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> and under mild conditions, to such graded cohomology
defined by the filtration.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">A morphism in the derived filtered
category defines a natural morphism of associated spectral
sequences; in particular a quasi-isomorphism defines an
isomorphism.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Later we shall study Mixed Hodge Complex where the
weight spectral sequence
becomes interesting and meaningful since it contains geometrical
information and degenerates at rank <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>. Now, we give the definition of the terms of the
spectral sequence and some examples.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">To define the spectral
terms <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{r}^{pq}(K,F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{r}^{pq}(K,F)</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{}_{F}E}_{r}^{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mmultiscripts><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mi>F</mi><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{}_{F}E}_{r}^{pq}</annotation></semantics></math> or simply <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{r}^{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{r}^{pq}</annotation></semantics></math> with
respect to <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>, we put for <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p,q\in\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p,q\in\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}&amp;Z_{r}^{pq}=Ker(d:F^{p}K^{p+q}\rightarrow K^{p+q+1}/F^{p+r}K^{p+q+1})\\
&amp;B_{r}^{pq}=F^{p+1}K^{p+q}+d(F^{p-r+1}K^{p+q-1})\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>Z</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>r</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>K</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>F</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></msup><mi>K</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>K</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>F</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup><mi>K</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}&amp;Z_{r}^{pq}=Ker(d:F^{p}K^{p+q}\rightarrow K^{p+q+1}/F^{p+r}K^{p+q+1})\\
&amp;B_{r}^{pq}=F^{p+1}K^{p+q}+d(F^{p-r+1}K^{p+q-1})\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p5.2" class="ltx_p">Such formula still makes sense for <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=\infty</annotation></semantics></math> if we set, for a filtered
object
<math id="S4.SS3.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(A,F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(A,F)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{-\infty}(A)=A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{-\infty}(A)=A</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{\infty}(A)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{\infty}(A)=0</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}&amp;Z_{\infty}^{pq}=Ker(d:F^{p}K^{p+q}\rightarrow K^{p+q+1})\\
&amp;B_{\infty}^{pq}=F^{p+1}K^{p+q}+d(K^{p+q+1})\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>Z</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>K</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>F</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></msup><mi>K</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>K</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}&amp;Z_{\infty}^{pq}=Ker(d:F^{p}K^{p+q}\rightarrow K^{p+q+1})\\
&amp;B_{\infty}^{pq}=F^{p+1}K^{p+q}+d(K^{p+q+1})\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p5.3" class="ltx_p">We set by definition:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{r}^{pq}=Z_{r}^{pq}/(B_{r}^{pq}\cap Z_{r}^{pq})\quad E_{\infty}^{pq}=Z_{\infty}^{pq}/B_{\infty}^{pq}\cap Z_{\infty}^{pq}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>∩</mo><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>/</mo><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>∩</mo><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{r}^{pq}=Z_{r}^{pq}/(B_{r}^{pq}\cap Z_{r}^{pq})\quad E_{\infty}^{pq}=Z_{\infty}^{pq}/B_{\infty}^{pq}\cap Z_{\infty}^{pq}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p5.4" class="ltx_p">The
notations are similar to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> but different from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem15" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem15.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 4.15</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem15.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem15.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem15.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In our case, in order to obtain some arguments by duality, we note
the following equivalent dual definitions to <math id="S4.Thmtheorem15.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{r}^{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{r}^{pq}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S4.Thmtheorem15.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{\infty}^{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{\infty}^{pq}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}&amp;K^{p+q}/B_{r}^{pq}=coker(d:F^{p-r+1}K^{p+q-1}\rightarrow K^{p+q}/F^{p+1}(K^{p+q}))\\
&amp;K^{p+q}/B_{\infty}^{pq}=coker(d:K^{p+q+1}\rightarrow K^{p+q}/F^{p+1}K^{p+q})\\
&amp;E_{r}^{pq}=Im(Z_{r}^{pq}\rightarrow K^{p+q}/B_{r}^{pq})=Ker(K^{p+q}/B_{r}^{pq}\rightarrow K^{p+q}/(Z_{r}^{pq}+B_{r}^{pq})).\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>K</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>B</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>r</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>c</mi><mi>o</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>F</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>K</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>K</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>F</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>K</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>B</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>c</mi><mi>o</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>K</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>K</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>F</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>K</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo>/</mo><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo>/</mo><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}&amp;K^{p+q}/B_{r}^{pq}=coker(d:F^{p-r+1}K^{p+q-1}\rightarrow K^{p+q}/F^{p+1}(K^{p+q}))\\
&amp;K^{p+q}/B_{\infty}^{pq}=coker(d:K^{p+q+1}\rightarrow K^{p+q}/F^{p+1}K^{p+q})\\
&amp;E_{r}^{pq}=Im(Z_{r}^{pq}\rightarrow K^{p+q}/B_{r}^{pq})=Ker(K^{p+q}/B_{r}^{pq}\rightarrow K^{p+q}/(Z_{r}^{pq}+B_{r}^{pq})).\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The term
<math id="S4.SS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr_{F}^{p}(H^{p+q}(K))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr_{F}^{p}(H^{p+q}(K))</annotation></semantics></math> is said to be the limit of the spectral
sequence. If the filtration is biregular
the terms <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{r}^{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{r}^{pq}</annotation></semantics></math> compute this limit after a
finite number of steps; for each <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p,q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p,q</annotation></semantics></math> there exists <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> such
that:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{r}^{pq}=Z_{\infty}^{pq},\quad B_{r}^{pq}=B_{\infty}^{pq},\quad E_{r}^{pq}=E_{\infty}^{pq},\quad\forall r&gt;r_{0}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{r}^{pq}=Z_{\infty}^{pq},\quad B_{r}^{pq}=B_{\infty}^{pq},\quad E_{r}^{pq}=E_{\infty}^{pq},\quad\forall r&gt;r_{0}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p6.2" class="ltx_p">Note that in some cases, it is not satisfactory to get only the
graded cohomology and
this is one motivation to be not happy with spectral sequences and
prefer to keep the complex as in the derived category. 
<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem16" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem16.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 4.16</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem16.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem16.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem16.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem16.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For each <math id="S4.Thmtheorem16.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists a differential <math id="S4.Thmtheorem16.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> on the terms
<math id="S4.Thmtheorem16.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{r}^{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{r}^{pq}</annotation></semantics></math> with the property that its cohomology is exactly
<math id="S4.Thmtheorem16.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{r+1}^{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{r+1}^{pq}</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{r+1}^{pq}=H(E_{r}^{p-r,q+r-1}\xrightarrow{d_{r}}E_{r}^{pq}\xrightarrow{d_{r}}E_{r}^{p+r,q-r+1})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">d</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">r</mi></msub></mover><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">d</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">r</mi></msub></mover><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{r+1}^{pq}=H(E_{r}^{p-r,q+r-1}\xrightarrow{d_{r}}E_{r}^{pq}\xrightarrow{d_{r}}E_{r}^{p+r,q-r+1}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem16.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem16.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="S4.Thmtheorem16.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> is induced by the differential <math id="S4.Thmtheorem16.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d:Z_{r}^{pq}\rightarrow Z_{r}^{p+r,q-r+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d:Z_{r}^{pq}\rightarrow Z_{r}^{p+r,q-r+1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">We define <math id="S4.SS3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> later with new notations for an increasing filtration. 
<br class="ltx_break">For <math id="S4.SS3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r&lt;\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r&lt;\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, the terms <math id="S4.SS3.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> form a complex of
objects, graded by the degree <math id="S4.SS3.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p-r(p+q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p-r(p+q)</annotation></semantics></math> (or a
direct sum of complexes with index <math id="S4.SS3.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p-r(p+q))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p-r(p+q))</annotation></semantics></math>.
The first term may be written as:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{1}^{pq}=H^{p+q}(Gr_{F}^{p}(K))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{1}^{pq}=H^{p+q}(Gr_{F}^{p}(K))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p7.2" class="ltx_p">so that the differentials <math id="S4.SS3.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> are obtained as connecting
morphisms defined by the short exact sequences of complexes</p>
<table id="S4.Ex26" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\rightarrow Gr_{F}^{p+1}K\rightarrow F^{p}K/F^{p+2}K\rightarrow Gr_{F}^{p}K\rightarrow 0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\rightarrow Gr_{F}^{p+1}K\rightarrow F^{p}K/F^{p+2}K\rightarrow Gr_{F}^{p}K\rightarrow 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p7.3" class="ltx_p">It will be convenient to set for <math id="S4.SS3.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS3.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{0}^{pq}=Gr_{F}^{p}(K^{p+q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{0}^{pq}=Gr_{F}^{p}(K^{p+q})</annotation></semantics></math>.
<br class="ltx_break">The spectral sequence <math id="S4.SS3.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{i}^{p,q}(K,F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{i}^{p,q}(K,F)</annotation></semantics></math> is
said to degenerate at rank <math id="S4.SS3.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> if the differentials <math id="S4.SS3.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are zero
for <math id="S4.SS3.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\geq r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\geq r</annotation></semantics></math> independently of <math id="S4.SS3.p7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p,q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p,q</annotation></semantics></math>, then we have in this case</p>
<table id="S4.Ex27" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{r}^{pq}=E_{i}^{pq}=E_{\infty}^{pq}\quad for\ i\geq r." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{r}^{pq}=E_{i}^{pq}=E_{\infty}^{pq}\quad for\ i\geq r.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p7.4" class="ltx_p">There is no easy general construction for (<math id="S4.SS3.p7.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{r},d_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{r},d_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>) for <math id="S4.SS3.p7.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>;
however we will see that in the case of Mixed Hodge Structures of
interest to us the terms with respect to the weight filtration <math id="S4.SS3.p7.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math>
have a geometric meaning and degenerate at rank <math id="S4.SS3.p7.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>:
<math id="S4.SS3.p7.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{}_{W}E}_{2}^{pq}={{}_{W}E}_{\infty}^{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>E</mi><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mi>W</mi><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo>=</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>E</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mi>W</mi><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{}_{W}E}_{2}^{pq}={{}_{W}E}_{\infty}^{pq}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.3.1. </span>Equivalent notations for increasing filtrations</h4>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For an increasing filtration
<math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>, the precedent formulas are transformed by the usual
change of indices to pass from <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> to a decreasing filtration <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>,
that is <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{i}=W_{-i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{i}=W_{-i}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> be an increasing filtration on <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>. We set for all <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leq m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leq m</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leq i\leq m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leq i\leq m</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex28" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{i}H^{j}(W_{m}K/W_{n}K)=Im(H^{j}(W_{i}K/W_{n}K)\rightarrow H^{j}(W_{m}K/W_{n}K)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{i}H^{j}(W_{m}K/W_{n}K)=Im(H^{j}(W_{i}K/W_{n}K)\rightarrow H^{j}(W_{m}K/W_{n}K)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">then we adopt the following new notations for the terms, for all
<math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\geq 1,p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\geq 1,p</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem17" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem17.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 4.17</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem17.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem17.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem17.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem17.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The terms of the spectral sequence for (<math id="S4.Thmtheorem17.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K,W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K,W</annotation></semantics></math>)
are equal to:</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex29" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{r}^{pq}(K,W))=Gr_{-p}^{W}H^{p+q}(W_{-p+r-1}K/W_{-p-r}K)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{r}^{pq}(K,W))=Gr_{-p}^{W}H^{p+q}(W_{-p+r-1}K/W_{-p-r}K).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS1.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let (<math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{r}^{p},W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{r}^{p},W</annotation></semantics></math>) denotes the quotient complex <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K^{p}_{r}:=W_{-p+r-1}K/W_{-p-r}K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K^{p}_{r}:=W_{-p+r-1}K/W_{-p-r}K</annotation></semantics></math>
with the induced filtration by subcomplexes <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math>; we put:</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex30" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}&amp;Z_{\infty}^{pq}(K^{p}_{r},W):=Ker\,(d:(W_{-p}K^{p+q}/W_{-p-r}K^{p+q})\rightarrow(W_{-p+r-1}K^{p+q+1}/W_{-p-r}K^{p+q+1}))\\
&amp;B_{\infty}^{pq}(K^{p}_{r},W):=(W_{-p-1}K^{p+q}+dW_{-p+r-1}K^{p+q-1})/W_{-p-r}K^{p+q}\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>Z</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>:=</mo><mi>K</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.340em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mi>K</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>W</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mi>K</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mi>K</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>W</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mi>K</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}&amp;Z_{\infty}^{pq}(K^{p}_{r},W):=Ker\,(d:(W_{-p}K^{p+q}/W_{-p-r}K^{p+q})\rightarrow(W_{-p+r-1}K^{p+q+1}/W_{-p-r}K^{p+q+1}))\\
&amp;B_{\infty}^{pq}(K^{p}_{r},W):=(W_{-p-1}K^{p+q}+dW_{-p+r-1}K^{p+q-1})/W_{-p-r}K^{p+q}\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text">which coincide, </span><span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p3.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">up to the quotient by </span><math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{-p-r}K^{p+q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{-p-r}K^{p+q}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p3.2.3" class="ltx_text">,
with <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{r}^{pq}(K,W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{r}^{pq}(K,W)</annotation></semantics></math> (resp. <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{r}^{pq}(K,W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{r}^{pq}(K,W)</annotation></semantics></math>) with:</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex31" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}&amp;Z^{p,q}_{r}:=Ker\,(d:W_{-p}K^{p+q}\rightarrow K^{p+q+1}/W_{-p-r}K^{p+q+1})\\
&amp;B^{p,q}_{r}:=W_{-p-1}K^{p+q}+dW_{-p+r-1}K^{p+q-1}\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>Z</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mi>K</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.340em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>W</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mi>K</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>K</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>W</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mi>K</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}&amp;Z^{p,q}_{r}:=Ker\,(d:W_{-p}K^{p+q}\rightarrow K^{p+q+1}/W_{-p-r}K^{p+q+1})\\
&amp;B^{p,q}_{r}:=W_{-p-1}K^{p+q}+dW_{-p+r-1}K^{p+q-1}\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p3.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p3.3.1" class="ltx_text">then, we define:</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex32" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{\infty}^{pq}(K^{p}_{r},W)={Z_{\infty}^{pq}(K^{p}_{r},W)\over B_{\infty}^{pq}(K^{p}_{r},W)\cap Z_{\infty}^{pq}(K^{p}_{r},W)}=Gr_{-p}^{W}H^{pq}(W_{-p+r-1}K/W_{-p-r}K)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{\infty}^{pq}(K^{p}_{r},W)={Z_{\infty}^{pq}(K^{p}_{r},W)\over B_{\infty}^{pq}(K^{p}_{r},W)\cap Z_{\infty}^{pq}(K^{p}_{r},W)}=Gr_{-p}^{W}H^{pq}(W_{-p+r-1}K/W_{-p-r}K)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p3.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p3.4.1" class="ltx_text">and find:</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex33" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}&amp;E_{r}^{pq}(K,W)=Z_{r}^{pq}/(B_{r}^{pq}\cap Z_{r}^{pq})=Z_{\infty}^{pq}(K^{p}_{r},W)/(B_{\infty}^{pq}(K^{p}_{r},W)\cap Z_{\infty}^{pq}(K^{p}_{r},W))\\
&amp;=E_{\infty}^{pq}(K^{p}_{r},W)=Gr_{-p}^{W}H^{pq}(W_{-p+r-1}K/W_{-p-r}K)\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>∩</mo><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}&amp;E_{r}^{pq}(K,W)=Z_{r}^{pq}/(B_{r}^{pq}\cap Z_{r}^{pq})=Z_{\infty}^{pq}(K^{p}_{r},W)/(B_{\infty}^{pq}(K^{p}_{r},W)\cap Z_{\infty}^{pq}(K^{p}_{r},W))\\
&amp;=E_{\infty}^{pq}(K^{p}_{r},W)=Gr_{-p}^{W}H^{pq}(W_{-p+r-1}K/W_{-p-r}K)\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p3.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p3.5.1" class="ltx_text">∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To define the differential <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>,
we consider the exact sequence:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex34" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\rightarrow W_{-p-r}K/W_{-p-2r}K\rightarrow W_{-p+r-1}K/W_{-p-2r}K\rightarrow W_{-p+r-1}K/W_{-p-r}K\rightarrow 0" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\rightarrow W_{-p-r}K/W_{-p-2r}K\rightarrow W_{-p+r-1}K/W_{-p-2r}K\rightarrow W_{-p+r-1}K/W_{-p-r}K\rightarrow 0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">and the connecting morphism:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex35" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex35.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{p+q}(W_{-p+r-1}K/W_{-p-r}K){\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\rightarrow}\limits^{\partial}}}H^{p+q+1}(W_{-p-r}K/W_{-p-2r}K)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∂</mo></msup></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{p+q}(W_{-p+r-1}K/W_{-p-r}K){\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\rightarrow}\limits^{\partial}}}H^{p+q+1}(W_{-p-r}K/W_{-p-2r}K)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p4.3" class="ltx_p">the injection <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{-p-r}K\rightarrow W_{-p-1}K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{-p-r}K\rightarrow W_{-p-1}K</annotation></semantics></math> induces
a morphism:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex36" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex36.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi:H^{p+q+1}(W_{-p-r}K/W_{-p-2r}K)\rightarrow W_{-p-r}H^{p+q+1}(W_{-p-1}K/W_{-p-2r}K)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi:H^{p+q+1}(W_{-p-r}K/W_{-p-2r}K)\rightarrow W_{-p-r}H^{p+q+1}(W_{-p-1}K/W_{-p-2r}K)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p4.4" class="ltx_p">.
Let <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> denote the projection on the right term below, equal to
<math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{r}^{p+r,q-r+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{r}^{p+r,q-r+1}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex37" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex37.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{-p-r}H^{p+q+1}(W_{-p-1}K/W_{-p-2r}K)\xrightarrow{\pi}Gr_{-p-r}^{W}H^{p+q+1}(W_{-p-1}K/W_{-p-2r}K)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝜋</mo></mover><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{-p-r}H^{p+q+1}(W_{-p-1}K/W_{-p-2r}K)\xrightarrow{\pi}Gr_{-p-r}^{W}H^{p+q+1}(W_{-p-1}K/W_{-p-2r}K)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p4.5" class="ltx_p">the composition of morphisms <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi\circ\varphi\circ\partial" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.055em">∘</mo><mo>∂</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi\circ\varphi\circ\partial</annotation></semantics></math> restricted to <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{-p}H^{p+q}(W_{-p+r-1}K/W_{-p-r}K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{-p}H^{p+q}(W_{-p+r-1}K/W_{-p-r}K)</annotation></semantics></math>
induces the differential:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex38" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex38.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{r}:E_{r}^{pq}\rightarrow E_{r}^{p+r,q-r+1}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{r}:E_{r}^{pq}\rightarrow E_{r}^{p+r,q-r+1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p4.6" class="ltx_p">while the injection <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{-p+r-1}\rightarrow W_{-p+r}K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{-p+r-1}\rightarrow W_{-p+r}K</annotation></semantics></math> induces the
isomorphism:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex39" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex39.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(E_{r}^{pq},d_{r})\xrightarrow{\sim}E_{r+1}^{pq}=Gr_{-p}^{W}H^{p+q}(W_{-p+r}K/W_{-p-r-1}K)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo></mover><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(E_{r}^{pq},d_{r})\xrightarrow{\sim}E_{r+1}^{pq}=Gr_{-p}^{W}H^{p+q}(W_{-p+r}K/W_{-p-r-1}K).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.3.2. </span>Hypercohomology spectral sequence</h4>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T:{\mathbb{A}}\rightarrow{\mathbb{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>𝔸</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝔹</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T:{\mathbb{A}}\rightarrow{\mathbb{B}}</annotation></semantics></math> be a left exact functor of abelian categories,
and <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(K,F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(K,F)</annotation></semantics></math> an object of <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{+}F{\mathbb{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{+}F{\mathbb{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RT(K,F):D^{+}F{\mathbb{A}}\to D^{+}F{\mathbb{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔹</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RT(K,F):D^{+}F{\mathbb{A}}\to D^{+}F{\mathbb{B}}</annotation></semantics></math> its derived functor. The spectral sequence defined by
the complex <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RT(K,F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RT(K,F)</annotation></semantics></math> is written as:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex40" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex40.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{}_{F}E}_{1}^{p,q}=R^{p+q}T(Gr_{F}^{p})\Rightarrow Gr_{F}^{p}R^{p+q}T(K)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mi>F</mi><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⇒</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{}_{F}E}_{1}^{p,q}=R^{p+q}T(Gr_{F}^{p})\Rightarrow Gr_{F}^{p}R^{p+q}T(K)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">This is the hypercohomology spectral sequence of the filtered
complex <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>. For an increasing filtration <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>, we have:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex41" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex41.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{}_{W}E}_{1}^{p,q}=R^{p+q}T(Gr_{-p}^{W})\Rightarrow Gr_{-p}^{W}R^{p+q}T(K)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mi>W</mi><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⇒</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{}_{W}E}_{1}^{p,q}=R^{p+q}T(Gr_{-p}^{W})\Rightarrow Gr_{-p}^{W}R^{p+q}T(K)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">It depends functorially on <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> and a filtered quasi-isomorphism
induces an isomorphism of spectral sequences.
The differentials <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> of this spectral sequence are the image
by <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> of the
connecting morphisms defined by the short exact sequences:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex42" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex42.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\rightarrow Gr_{F}^{p+1}K\rightarrow F^{p}K/F^{p+2}K\rightarrow Gr_{F}^{p}K\rightarrow 0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\rightarrow Gr_{F}^{p+1}K\rightarrow F^{p}K/F^{p+2}K\rightarrow Gr_{F}^{p}K\rightarrow 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.3.3. </span>Examples</h4>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">1) Let <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> be a complex, the canonical
filtration <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> is the increasing filtration by sub-complexes:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex43" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex43.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\tau_{\leq p}\ =(\cdots\to K^{p-1}\to Kerd\to 0\cdots\to 0)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.778em">=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>K</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{\leq p}\ =(\cdots\to K^{p-1}\to Kerd\to 0\cdots\to 0)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">then:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex44" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex44.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr^{\tau}_{\leq p}K\xrightarrow{\approx}H^{p}(K)[-p],\quad H^{i}(\tau_{\leq p}(K))=H^{i}(K)\,\,{\rm if}\,\ i\leq p,\,\,{\,\rm and\,\,}0\,\,{\rm if\,}\,i&gt;p." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mi>τ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">≈</mo></mover><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.340em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>if</mi><mo lspace="0.670em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.677em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>and</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>  0</mn><mo lspace="0.340em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>if</mi><mo lspace="0.340em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr^{\tau}_{\leq p}K\xrightarrow{\approx}H^{p}(K)[-p],\quad H^{i}(\tau_{\leq p}(K))=H^{i}(K)\,\,{\rm if}\,\ i\leq p,\,\,{\,\rm and\,\,}0\,\,{\rm if\,}\,i&gt;p.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">2) The sub-complexes of <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex45" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex45.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\geq p}K:=K^{*\geq p}=(0\to\cdots\to 0\to K^{p}\to K^{p+1}\to\cdots)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>:=</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.1389em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.1389em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\geq p}K:=K^{*\geq p}=(0\to\cdots\to 0\to K^{p}\to K^{p+1}\to\cdots)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p1.4" class="ltx_p">define a decreasing biregular filtration, the trivial
filtration of <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr^{p}_{\sigma}K=K^{p}[-p]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>σ</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr^{p}_{\sigma}K=K^{p}[-p]</annotation></semantics></math>,
i.e., it coincides with the Hodge filtration on de Rham complex.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A quasi-isomorphism
<math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:K\rightarrow K^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:K\rightarrow K^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is necessarily a filtered
quasi-isomorphism for both, the trivial and the canonical
filtrations. The hypercohomology spectral sequences of a left
exact functor attached to the trivial and canonical filtrations of
<math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> are the two natural hypercohomology spectral sequences of
<math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>.
<br class="ltx_break">3) Let <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:X\rightarrow Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:X\rightarrow Y</annotation></semantics></math> be a continued map
of topological spaces. Let <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> be an abelian sheaf
on <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> a resolution of <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> by
<math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{*}-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>−</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{*}-</annotation></semantics></math>acyclic sheaves, then <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{i}f_{*}{\mathcal{F}}\cong H^{i}(f_{*}{\mathcal{F}}^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{i}f_{*}{\mathcal{F}}\cong H^{i}(f_{*}{\mathcal{F}}^{*})</annotation></semantics></math>. The hypercohomology spectral sequence
of the global section functor <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma(Y,*)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma(Y,*)</annotation></semantics></math> of the complex <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Rf_{*}{\mathcal{F}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Rf_{*}{\mathcal{F}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> with its canonical filtration, is:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex46" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex46.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{}_{\tau}E}_{1}^{pq}=\mathbb{H}^{p+q}(Y,R^{-p}f_{*}{\mathcal{F}}[p])\simeq H^{2p+q}(Y,R^{-p}f_{*}{\mathcal{F}})\Rightarrow Gr^{\tau}_{-p}H^{p+q}(X,{\mathcal{F}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mi>τ</mi><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℍ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⇒</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mi>τ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{}_{\tau}E}_{1}^{pq}=\mathbb{H}^{p+q}(Y,R^{-p}f_{*}{\mathcal{F}}[p])\simeq H^{2p+q}(Y,R^{-p}f_{*}{\mathcal{F}})\Rightarrow Gr^{\tau}_{-p}H^{p+q}(X,{\mathcal{F}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">This formula illustrates basic difference in Deligne’s notation:
the sheaf <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{-p}f_{*}{\mathcal{F}}[p]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{-p}f_{*}{\mathcal{F}}[p]</annotation></semantics></math> is in degree <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-p</annotation></semantics></math>.
<br class="ltx_break">In classical notations, Leray’s spectral
sequence for <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> starts at:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex47" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex47.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{2}^{pq}=H^{p}(Y,R^{q}f_{*}{\mathcal{F}})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{2}^{pq}=H^{p}(Y,R^{q}f_{*}{\mathcal{F}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p2.3" class="ltx_p">To relate both notations we need to renumber the classical term
<math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{r+1}^{2p+q,-p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{r+1}^{2p+q,-p}</annotation></semantics></math> into the new term <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{r}^{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{r}^{pq}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5. </span>Mixed Hodge Complex (MHC)</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we give the definition of a Mixed Hodge Complex
(MHC) and prove Deligne’s fundamental theorem that the
cohomology of a Mixed Hodge Complex is endowed with a Mixed Hodge
Structure.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">First, on an algebraic variety <math id="S5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>, we define a cohomological version of
a Mixed Hodge Complex, that we call Cohomological
Mixed Hodge Complex (CMHC), which is defined essentially
by a bi-filtered complex of sheaves <math id="S5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(K_{\mathbb{C}},F,W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(K_{\mathbb{C}},F,W)</annotation></semantics></math> where the filtration <math id="S5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> is
rationally defined and satisfies precise conditions sufficient
to define a Mixed Hodge Complex structure on
the global section functor <math id="S5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\Gamma(V,K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\Gamma(V,K)</annotation></semantics></math>. The results proved by Deligne
are technically difficult and so strong that
the theory is reduced to constructing such a Cohomological Mixed Hodge
Complex for all algebraic
varieties in the remaining sections; hence the theoretical path to
construct a Mixed Hodge Structure on a variety follows the pattern:</p>
<table id="S5.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hbox{CMHC}\Rightarrow\hbox{ MHC}\Rightarrow\hbox{MHS}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>CMHC</mtext><mo stretchy="false">⇒</mo><mtext> MHC</mtext><mo stretchy="false">⇒</mo><mtext>MHS</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hbox{CMHC}\Rightarrow\hbox{ MHC}\Rightarrow\hbox{MHS}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p2.2" class="ltx_p">It is true that a direct
study of the logarithmic complex
by Griffiths and Schmid <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite> is very attractive, but the initial work of
Deligne
is easy to apply, flexible and helps to go beyond this case
towards a general theory.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The de Rham complex of a smooth compact complex
variety is a special case of a Mixed Hodge Complex, called a Hodge
complex (HC) with the characteristic property that it induces a
Hodge Structure on its hypercohomology. We start by rewriting the
Hodge theory that we know, with terminology that is fitted to
our generalization.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> denote <math id="S5.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S5.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℝ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\otimes\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\otimes\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> the field
<math id="S5.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S5.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℝ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math> accordingly as in section <math id="S5.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{+}(\mathbb{Z}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{+}(\mathbb{Z}))</annotation></semantics></math> (resp.
<math id="S5.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{+}(\mathbb{C}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{+}(\mathbb{C}))</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{+}(V,\mathbb{Z}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{+}(V,\mathbb{Z}))</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{+}(V,\mathbb{C}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{+}(V,\mathbb{C}))</annotation></semantics></math>)
denotes the derived category of <math id="S5.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>−</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}-</annotation></semantics></math>modules (resp. <math id="S5.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>−</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}-</annotation></semantics></math>vector
spaces and corresponding sheaves on <math id="S5.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 5.1</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Hodge Complex (HC))<span id="S5.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A Hodge
<math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>-complex <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> of weight <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> consists of:
<br class="ltx_break">i) A complex <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{A}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>-modules, such that <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(K_{A})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(K_{A})</annotation></semantics></math>
is an <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>-module of finite type for all <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>;
<br class="ltx_break">ii) A filtered complex <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(K_{\mathbb{C}},F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(K_{\mathbb{C}},F)</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>−</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}-</annotation></semantics></math>vector spaces;
<br class="ltx_break">iii) An isomorphism
<math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha:K_{A}\otimes\mathbb{C}\simeq K_{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo>≃</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha:K_{A}\otimes\mathbb{C}\simeq K_{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{+}(\mathbb{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{+}(\mathbb{C})</annotation></semantics></math>.
<br class="ltx_break">The following axioms must be satisfied:
<br class="ltx_break">(HC 1) the differential <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math> is strictly compatible
with the filtration <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{i}:(K_{A}^{i},F)\to(K_{A}^{i+1},F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{i}:(K_{A}^{i},F)\to(K_{A}^{i+1},F)</annotation></semantics></math> is
strict, for all <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>; 
<br class="ltx_break">(HC 2) for all <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>,
the filtration <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(K_{\mathbb{C}})\simeq H^{k}(K_{A})\otimes\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(K_{\mathbb{C}})\simeq H^{k}(K_{A})\otimes\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>
defines an <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>-Hodge Structure of weight <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n+k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+k</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(K_{A})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(K_{A})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Equivalently, in (HC1) the
spectral sequence defined by <math id="S5.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(K_{\mathbb{C}},F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(K_{\mathbb{C}},F)</annotation></semantics></math> degenerates at
<math id="S5.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{1}(E_{1}=E_{\infty})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{1}(E_{1}=E_{\infty})</annotation></semantics></math> (see <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem4" title="Proposition 4.4. ‣ 4.0.1. Spectral sequence defined by a filtered complex 
          
            
              
                
                
                
              
              
                (
                K
                ,
                F
                )
              
            
          
        
in an abelian category ‣ 4. Hypercohomology and spectral sequence of a Filtered Complex ‣ Mixed Hodge Structures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a> or <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>), and in (HC2) the
filtration <math id="S5.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> is
(<math id="S5.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n+k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+k</annotation></semantics></math>)-opposed to its complex conjugate (conjugation makes sense since
<math id="S5.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\subset{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\subset{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 5.2</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> be a topological space. An
<math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>-Cohomological Hodge Complex (CHC) <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> of weight <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>,
consists of: 
<br class="ltx_break">i) A complex of sheaves <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{A}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A-</annotation></semantics></math>modules on <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>;
<br class="ltx_break">ii) A filtered complex of sheaves <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(K_{\mathbb{C}},F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(K_{\mathbb{C}},F)</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℂ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>-vector spaces
on <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>;
<br class="ltx_break">iii) An isomorphism <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha:K_{A}\otimes\mathbb{C}\xrightarrow{\approx}K_{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">≈</mo></mover><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha:K_{A}\otimes\mathbb{C}\xrightarrow{\approx}K_{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{+}(X,\mathbb{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{+}(X,\mathbb{C})</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>−</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}-</annotation></semantics></math>sheaves on <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.
<br class="ltx_break">Moreover, the following axiom must be satisfied:
<br class="ltx_break">(CHC) The triple <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(R\Gamma(K_{A}),R\Gamma(K_{\mathbb{C}},F),R\Gamma(\alpha))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(R\Gamma(K_{A}),R\Gamma(K_{\mathbb{C}},F),R\Gamma(\alpha))</annotation></semantics></math> is a Hodge Complex of weight <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p6.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S5.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(K,F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(K,F)</annotation></semantics></math> is a Hodge Complex (resp. Cohomological Hodge
Complex) of weight <math id="S5.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S5.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(K[m],F[p])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(K[m],F[p])</annotation></semantics></math>
is a Hodge Complex (resp. Cohomological Hodge
Complex) of weight <math id="S5.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n+m-2p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+m-2p</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p7.1" class="ltx_p">The following statement is a new version of Hodge decomposition Theorem:</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 5.3</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> be a compact complex
algebraic manifold and consider:
<br class="ltx_break">i) <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math> the complex reduced to a constant sheaf <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>
in degree zero; 
<br class="ltx_break">ii) <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math> the analytic de Rham
complex <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega^{*}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega^{*}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> with its trivial filtration <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{p}=\Omega^{*\geq p}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{p}=\Omega^{*\geq p}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> by subcomplexes:</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="F^{p}\Omega^{*}_{X}:=0\rightarrow 0\cdots 0\rightarrow\Omega^{p}_{X}\rightarrow\Omega^{p+1}_{X}\rightarrow\cdots\rightarrow\Omega^{n}_{X}\rightarrow 0;" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>X</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{p}\Omega^{*}_{X}:=0\rightarrow 0\cdots 0\rightarrow\Omega^{p}_{X}\rightarrow\Omega^{p+1}_{X}\rightarrow\cdots\rightarrow\Omega^{n}_{X}\rightarrow 0;</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">iii) The quasi-isomorphism <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\colon K_{\mathbb{Z}}\otimes\mathbb{C}\xrightarrow{\approx}\Omega^{*}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">≈</mo></mover><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\colon K_{\mathbb{Z}}\otimes\mathbb{C}\xrightarrow{\approx}\Omega^{*}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> (Poincaré lemma).
<br class="ltx_break">Then <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(K_{\mathbb{Z}},(K_{\mathbb{C}},F),\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(K_{\mathbb{Z}},(K_{\mathbb{C}},F),\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math> is a Cohomological Hodge Complex
of weight <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>; its
hypercohomology on <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, isomorphic to the cohomology of <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, carries a
functorial Hodge Structure for morphisms of algebraic varieties.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p8.1" class="ltx_p">The
new idea here is to observe the degeneracy of the spectral sequence
of <math id="S5.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\Omega^{*}_{X},F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\Omega^{*}_{X},F)</annotation></semantics></math> and deduce the definition of the Hodge filtration
from the trivial filtration <math id="S5.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> on the de Rham complex without any
reference to harmonic forms, although the proof of the
decomposition is given via a reduction to the case of a
projective variety, hence a compact Kähler manifold:</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 5.4</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The Hodge filtration <math id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> is defined on
the cohomology as follows:</p>
<table id="S5.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{p}H^{i}(X,\mathbb{C}):=F^{p}\mathbb{H}^{i}(X,\Omega^{*}_{X}):=Im(\mathbb{H}^{i}(X,F^{p}\Omega^{*}_{X})\rightarrow\mathbb{H}^{i}(X,\Omega^{*}_{X}))," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℍ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ℍ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℍ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{p}H^{i}(X,\mathbb{C}):=F^{p}\mathbb{H}^{i}(X,\Omega^{*}_{X}):=Im(\mathbb{H}^{i}(X,F^{p}\Omega^{*}_{X})\rightarrow\mathbb{H}^{i}(X,\Omega^{*}_{X})),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where the first equality follows from holomorphic Poincaré
lemma on the resolution of the constant sheaf <math id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℂ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math> by the analytic
de Rham complex <math id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega^{*}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega^{*}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 5.5</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Deligne <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>)<span id="S5.Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S5.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> be a smooth compact complex algebraic
variety, then the filtration <math id="S5.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> induced on cohomology by the
filtration <math id="S5.Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> on the de Rham complex is a Hodge filtration.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p9.1" class="ltx_p">The proof is based on the degeneration at rank one of the spectral
sequence with respect to <math id="S5.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> defined as follows:</p>
<table id="S5.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{}_{F}E}_{1}^{p,q}:=\mathbb{H}^{p+q}(X,Gr^{p}_{F}\Omega^{*}_{X})\simeq H^{q}(X,\Omega^{p}_{X})\Rightarrow Gr^{p}_{F}\mathbb{H}^{p+q}(X,\Omega^{*}_{X})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mi>F</mi><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℍ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⇒</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℍ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{}_{F}E}_{1}^{p,q}:=\mathbb{H}^{p+q}(X,Gr^{p}_{F}\Omega^{*}_{X})\simeq H^{q}(X,\Omega^{p}_{X})\Rightarrow Gr^{p}_{F}\mathbb{H}^{p+q}(X,\Omega^{*}_{X}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p9.2" class="ltx_p">The degeneration at rank one may be deduced from classical Hodge
decomposition, but it has been also obtained by direct algebraic
methods by Deligne and Illusie in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite> for the algebraic
de Rham complex with respect to Zariski topology on which the
filtration <math id="S5.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> is also defined.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p10.1" class="ltx_p">The isomorphism on complex smooth algebraic
varieties between analytic and algebraic de Rham hypercohomology
defined respectively with analytic and algebraic de Rham complexes
is given by Grothendieck’s comparison theorem (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p11.1" class="ltx_p">Now, we define the structure including two filtrations by weight <math id="S5.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S5.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> needed on a complex, in order to
define a Mixed Hodge Structure on its cohomology.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 5.6</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(MHC)<span id="S5.Thmtheorem6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">An <math id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>-Mixed Hodge Complex (MHC) <math id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> consists
of:
<br class="ltx_break">i) A complex <math id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{A}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A-</annotation></semantics></math>modules such that <math id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(K_{A})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(K_{A})</annotation></semantics></math> is an
<math id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>-module of finite type for all  <math id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>;
<br class="ltx_break">ii) A filtered complex
<math id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(K_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}},W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(K_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}},W)</annotation></semantics></math> of
<math id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(A\otimes\mathbb{Q})-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(A\otimes\mathbb{Q})-</annotation></semantics></math>vector spaces with an increasing filtration <math id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math>;
<br class="ltx_break">iii) An isomorphism <math id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{A}\otimes\mathbb{Q}\xrightarrow{\sim}K_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo></mover><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{A}\otimes\mathbb{Q}\xrightarrow{\sim}K_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{+}(A\otimes\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{+}(A\otimes\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math>;
<br class="ltx_break">iv) A bi-filtered
complex (<math id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{\mathbb{C}},W,F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{\mathbb{C}},W,F)</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>−</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}-</annotation></semantics></math>vector spaces with an increasing
(resp. decreasing) filtration <math id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> (resp. <math id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>) and an
isomorphism:</p>
<table id="S5.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha:(K_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}},W)\otimes\mathbb{C}\xrightarrow{\sim}(K_{\mathbb{C}},W)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha:(K_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}},W)\otimes\mathbb{C}\xrightarrow{\sim}(K_{\mathbb{C}},W)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.2" class="ltx_p">in <math id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{+}F(\mathbb{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{+}F(\mathbb{C})</annotation></semantics></math>.
<br class="ltx_break">Moreover, the following axiom is satisfied: 
<br class="ltx_break">(MHC) For all <math id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, the system consisting of
<br class="ltx_break">- the complex <math id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr_{n}^{W}(K_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr_{n}^{W}(K_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(A\otimes\mathbb{Q})-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(A\otimes\mathbb{Q})-</annotation></semantics></math>vector spaces,
<br class="ltx_break">- the complex <math id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr_{n}^{W}(K_{\mathbb{C}},F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr_{n}^{W}(K_{\mathbb{C}},F)</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>−</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}-</annotation></semantics></math>vector spaces with
induced <math id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> and
<br class="ltx_break">- the isomorphism
<math id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr_{n}^{W}(\alpha):Gr_{n}^{W}(K_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}})\otimes\mathbb{C}\xrightarrow{\sim}Gr_{n}^{W}(K_{\mathbb{C}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo></mover><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr_{n}^{W}(\alpha):Gr_{n}^{W}(K_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}})\otimes\mathbb{C}\xrightarrow{\sim}Gr_{n}^{W}(K_{\mathbb{C}})</annotation></semantics></math>,
<br class="ltx_break">is an <math id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\otimes\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\otimes\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-Hodge Complex of weight <math id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p12.1" class="ltx_p">The above structure has
a corresponding structure on a complex of sheaves on <math id="S5.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> called a
Cohomological Mixed Hodge Complex:</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 5.7</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(CMHC)<span id="S5.Thmtheorem7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">An <math id="S5.Thmtheorem7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>-Cohomological Mixed Hodge Complex <math id="S5.Thmtheorem7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>
(CMHC) on a topological space <math id="S5.Thmtheorem7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> consists of:
<br class="ltx_break">i) A complex of sheaves <math id="S5.Thmtheorem7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{A}</annotation></semantics></math> of sheaves of <math id="S5.Thmtheorem7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>-modules on <math id="S5.Thmtheorem7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>
such that <math id="S5.Thmtheorem7.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(X,K_{A})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(X,K_{A})</annotation></semantics></math> are <math id="S5.Thmtheorem7.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>-modules of finite type;
<br class="ltx_break">ii) A filtered complex <math id="S5.Thmtheorem7.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(K_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}},W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(K_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}},W)</annotation></semantics></math> of sheaves of <math id="S5.Thmtheorem7.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(A\otimes\mathbb{Q})-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(A\otimes\mathbb{Q})-</annotation></semantics></math>vector spaces on <math id="S5.Thmtheorem7.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>
with an increasing filtration <math id="S5.Thmtheorem7.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> and an
isomorphism
<math id="S5.Thmtheorem7.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{A}\otimes\mathbb{Q}\simeq K_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><mo>≃</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{A}\otimes\mathbb{Q}\simeq K_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S5.Thmtheorem7.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{+}(X,A\otimes\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{+}(X,A\otimes\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math>;
<br class="ltx_break">iii) A bi-filitered complex of sheaves <math id="S5.Thmtheorem7.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(K_{\mathbb{C}},W,F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(K_{\mathbb{C}},W,F)</annotation></semantics></math> of
<math id="S5.Thmtheorem7.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>−</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}-</annotation></semantics></math>vector spaces on <math id="S5.Thmtheorem7.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> with an increasing
(resp. decreasing) filtration <math id="S5.Thmtheorem7.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> (resp. <math id="S5.Thmtheorem7.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> ) and an
isomorphism:</p>
<table id="S5.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha:(K_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}},W)\otimes\mathbb{C}\xrightarrow{\sim}(K_{\mathbb{C}},W)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha:(K_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}},W)\otimes\mathbb{C}\xrightarrow{\sim}(K_{\mathbb{C}},W)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem7.p1.2" class="ltx_p">in <math id="S5.Thmtheorem7.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{+}F(X,\mathbb{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{+}F(X,\mathbb{C})</annotation></semantics></math>.
<br class="ltx_break">Moreover, the following axiom is satisfied: 
<br class="ltx_break">(CMHC) For all <math id="S5.Thmtheorem7.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, the system consisting of: 
<br class="ltx_break">- the complex <math id="S5.Thmtheorem7.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr_{n}^{W}(K_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr_{n}^{W}(K_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math> of sheaves of <math id="S5.Thmtheorem7.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(A\otimes\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(A\otimes\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math>-vector spaces on <math id="S5.Thmtheorem7.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>; 
<br class="ltx_break">- the complex <math id="S5.Thmtheorem7.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr_{n}^{W}(K_{\mathbb{C}},F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr_{n}^{W}(K_{\mathbb{C}},F)</annotation></semantics></math> of sheaves of <math id="S5.Thmtheorem7.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℂ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>-vector
spaces on <math id="S5.Thmtheorem7.p1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> with induced <math id="S5.Thmtheorem7.p1.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>;
<br class="ltx_break">- the isomorphism
<math id="S5.Thmtheorem7.p1.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr_{n}^{W}(\alpha):Gr_{n}^{W}(K_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}})\otimes\mathbb{C}\xrightarrow{\sim}Gr_{n}^{W}(K_{\mathbb{C}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo></mover><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr_{n}^{W}(\alpha):Gr_{n}^{W}(K_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}})\otimes\mathbb{C}\xrightarrow{\sim}Gr_{n}^{W}(K_{\mathbb{C}})</annotation></semantics></math>,
<br class="ltx_break">is an <math id="S5.Thmtheorem7.p1.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\otimes\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\otimes\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-Cohomological Hodge Complex of weight <math id="S5.Thmtheorem7.p1.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p13.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S5.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(K,W,F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(K,W,F)</annotation></semantics></math> is a Mixed Hodge Complex (resp. Cohomological Mixed Hodge Complex),
then for all <math id="S5.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\in\mathbb{Z},(K[m],W[m-2n],F[n])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\in\mathbb{Z},(K[m],W[m-2n],F[n])</annotation></semantics></math> is a Mixed
Hodge Complex (resp. Cohomological Mixed Hodge Complex).</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p14.1" class="ltx_p">In fact, any Hodge Complex or Mixed Hodge Complex
described here is obtained from de Rham complexes with
modifications (at infinity) as the logarithmic complex described
later in the next section. A new construction of Hodge Complex has
been later introduced with the theory of differential modules and
perverse sheaves <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite> but will not be covered
in these
lectures.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p15.1" class="ltx_p">Now we describe how we deduce first the Mixed Hodge Complex from
a Cohomological Mixed Hodge Complex, then a Mixed Hodge
Structure from a Mixed Hodge Complex.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 5.8</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S5.Thmtheorem8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=(K_{A},(K_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}},W),(K_{\mathbb{C}},W,F))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=(K_{A},(K_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}},W),(K_{\mathbb{C}},W,F))</annotation></semantics></math> and the isomorphism <math id="S5.Thmtheorem8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> is an
<math id="S5.Thmtheorem8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>-Cohomological Mixed Hodge Complex
then:</p>
<table id="S5.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\Gamma K=(R\Gamma K_{A},R\Gamma(K_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}},W),R\Gamma(K_{\mathbb{C}},W,F))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\Gamma K=(R\Gamma K_{A},R\Gamma(K_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}},W),R\Gamma(K_{\mathbb{C}},W,F))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem8.p1.2" class="ltx_p">with <math id="S5.Thmtheorem8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\Gamma(\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\Gamma(\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math> is an
<math id="S5.Thmtheorem8.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>-Mixed Hodge Complex.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p16.1" class="ltx_p">The main result of Deligne in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>
is algebraic and states in short:</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 5.9</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Deligne)<span id="S5.Thmtheorem9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The cohomology of a Mixed Hodge
Complex carries a Mixed Hodge Structure.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p17.1" class="ltx_p">The proof of this result requires a detailed study of spectral
sequences and will occupy the rest of this section. We give
here, before the proof, the
properties of the various spectral sequences which
may be of interest as independent results.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p18" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p18.1" class="ltx_p">Precisely, the weight
spectral sequence of a Mixed Hodge complex is in the category of
Hodge Structure.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p19" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p19.1" class="ltx_p">So, the Mixed Hodge Structure on cohomology is approached step by step by Hodge
Structures on the terms of the weight spectral sequence
<math id="S5.p19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{}_{W}E_{r}^{pq}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mmultiscripts><mi>E</mi><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mi>r</mi><mrow></mrow><mi>W</mi><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{}_{W}E_{r}^{pq}}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S5.p19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(K_{\mathbb{C}},W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(K_{\mathbb{C}},W)</annotation></semantics></math>. However, the big surprise is that
the spectral sequence degenerates quickly, at rank two for <math id="S5.p19.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> and
at rank one for <math id="S5.p19.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>: this is all which is needed.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p20" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p20.1" class="ltx_p">A careful study shows that there are various induced filtrations
by <math id="S5.p20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> on the
weight spectral sequence <math id="S5.p20.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{}_{W}E_{r}^{pq}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mmultiscripts><mi>E</mi><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mi>r</mi><mrow></mrow><mi>W</mi><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{}_{W}E_{r}^{pq}}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S5.p20.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(K_{\mathbb{C}},W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(K_{\mathbb{C}},W)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Two direct filtrations
<math id="S5.p20.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p20.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{d^{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{d^{*}}</annotation></semantics></math> and one recurrent <math id="S5.p20.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{rec}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{rec}</annotation></semantics></math> are induced by
<math id="S5.p20.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> on the terms <math id="S5.p20.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{}_{W}E_{r}^{pq}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mmultiscripts><mi>E</mi><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mi>r</mi><mrow></mrow><mi>W</mi><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{}_{W}E_{r}^{pq}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Under the conditions imposed on
the two filtrations <math id="S5.p20.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p20.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> in the definition of a Mixed Hodge Complex, the
three filtrations coincide, the spectral sequence with respect to
<math id="S5.p20.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> degenerates at rank <math id="S5.p20.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> and the induced filtration by <math id="S5.p20.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> on
the first terms <math id="S5.p20.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E^{p,q}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E^{p,q}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> define a Hodge Structure of weight <math id="S5.p20.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>
(lemma on two filtrations). The filtration
<math id="S5.p20.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is a morphism of <math id="S5.p20.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="HS" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">HS</annotation></semantics></math>, hence the terms <math id="S5.p20.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E^{p,q}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E^{p,q}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> carry a
Hodge Structures of weight <math id="S5.p20.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>. The proof consists to show that <math id="S5.p20.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> is
compatible with the induced Hodge Structure, but for <math id="S5.p20.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math> it is a morphism
between two Hodge Structures of different weight, hence it must
vanish.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 5.10</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(MHS on the cohomology of a MHC)<span id="S5.Thmtheorem10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem10.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S5.Thmtheorem10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> be an <math id="S5.Thmtheorem10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>-MHC.
<br class="ltx_break">i)The filtration <math id="S5.Thmtheorem10.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W[n]</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S5.Thmtheorem10.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{n}(K_{A})\otimes\mathbb{Q}\simeq H^{n}(K_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{n}(K_{A})\otimes\mathbb{Q}\simeq H^{n}(K_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S5.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(W[n])_{q}(H^{n}(K_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}}):=\,Im\,(H^{n}(W_{q-n}K_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}})\to H^{n}(K_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}}))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.448em">:=</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(W[n])_{q}(H^{n}(K_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}}):=\,Im\,(H^{n}(W_{q-n}K_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}})\to H^{n}(K_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}}))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem10.p1.2" class="ltx_p">and the filtration <math id="S5.Thmtheorem10.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.Thmtheorem10.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{n}(K_{\mathbb{C}})\simeq H^{n}(K_{A})\otimes_{A}\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>A</mi></msub><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{n}(K_{\mathbb{C}})\simeq H^{n}(K_{A})\otimes_{A}\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S5.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{p}(H^{n}(K_{\mathbb{C}}):=\,Im\,(H^{n}(F^{p}K_{\mathbb{C}})\to H^{n}(K_{\mathbb{C}}))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.448em">:=</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{p}(H^{n}(K_{\mathbb{C}}):=\,Im\,(H^{n}(F^{p}K_{\mathbb{C}})\to H^{n}(K_{\mathbb{C}}))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem10.p1.3" class="ltx_p">define on <math id="S5.Thmtheorem10.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{n}(K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{n}(K)</annotation></semantics></math> an <math id="S5.Thmtheorem10.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>-Mixed Hodge Structure, i.e.:
<br class="ltx_break"></p>
<table id="S5.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(H^{n}(K_{A}),(H^{n}(K_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}}),W),(H^{n}(K_{\mathbb{C}}),W,F))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(H^{n}(K_{A}),(H^{n}(K_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}}),W),(H^{n}(K_{\mathbb{C}}),W,F))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem10.p1.4" class="ltx_p">is an <math id="S5.Thmtheorem10.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>-Mixed Hodge Structure.
<br class="ltx_break">ii) On the terms <math id="S5.Thmtheorem10.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}_{W}E_{r}^{pq}(K_{\mathbb{C}},W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>E</mi><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mi>r</mi><mrow></mrow><mi>W</mi><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{W}E_{r}^{pq}(K_{\mathbb{C}},W)</annotation></semantics></math>, the recurrent filtration
and the two direct filtrations
coincide <math id="S5.Thmtheorem10.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{d}=F_{rec}=F_{d^{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{d}=F_{rec}=F_{d^{*}}</annotation></semantics></math> and define the Hodge filtration <math id="S5.Thmtheorem10.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> of a Hodge Sructure of weight
<math id="S5.Thmtheorem10.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.Thmtheorem10.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> is compatible with <math id="S5.Thmtheorem10.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>.
<br class="ltx_break">iii) The morphisms <math id="S5.Thmtheorem10.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{1}:\,{{}_{W}E}_{1}^{p,q}\,\rightarrow\,{{}_{W}E}_{1}^{p+1,q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.448em">:</mo><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mi>W</mi><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo rspace="0.448em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mi>W</mi><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{1}:\,{{}_{W}E}_{1}^{p,q}\,\rightarrow\,{{}_{W}E}_{1}^{p+1,q}</annotation></semantics></math> are strictly compatible with <math id="S5.Thmtheorem10.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>.
<br class="ltx_break">iv) The spectral sequence of <math id="S5.Thmtheorem10.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(K_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(K_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math>,
W) degenerates at rank <math id="S5.Thmtheorem10.p1.m19" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="2\,(_{W}E_{2}={{}_{W}E}_{\infty})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi></msub><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>E</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mrow></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mi>W</mi><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\,(_{W}E_{2}={{}_{W}E}_{\infty})</annotation></semantics></math>.
<br class="ltx_break">v) The spectral sequence of Hodge Structures of <math id="S5.Thmtheorem10.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(K_{\mathbb{C}},F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(K_{\mathbb{C}},F)</annotation></semantics></math> degenerates at
rank <math id="S5.Thmtheorem10.p1.m21" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="1\,(_{F}E_{1}={{}_{F}E}_{\infty})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi></msub><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>E</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mrow></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mi>F</mi><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\,(_{F}E_{1}={{}_{F}E}_{\infty})</annotation></semantics></math>.
<br class="ltx_break">vi) The spectral sequence of the complex
<math id="S5.Thmtheorem10.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr_{F}^{p}(K_{\mathbb{C}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr_{F}^{p}(K_{\mathbb{C}})</annotation></semantics></math>,
with the induced filtration <math id="S5.Thmtheorem10.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math>, degenerates at rank <math id="S5.Thmtheorem10.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p21" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p21.1" class="ltx_p">Notice that the index of the weight is not given by the index of
the induced filtration <math id="S5.p21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> on cohomology, but shifted by <math id="S5.p21.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[n]</annotation></semantics></math>.
One should recall that the weight of the Hodge Structure on the terms <math id="S5.p21.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{}_{W}E}_{r}^{p,q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mmultiscripts><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mi>W</mi><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{}_{W}E}_{r}^{p,q}</annotation></semantics></math>
is always <math id="S5.p21.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>, hence the weight of <math id="S5.p21.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr^{W}_{-p}H^{p+q}(K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr^{W}_{-p}H^{p+q}(K)</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S5.p21.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>, that is
<math id="S5.p21.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(W[p+q])_{q}=W_{-p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(W[p+q])_{q}=W_{-p}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p22" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p22.1" class="ltx_p">Now, we are going to give the proofs of the statements of this introduction.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1. </span>HS on the cohomology of a smooth compact algebraic variety</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Proof of the proposition (5.5)</span>. i) For <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> projective, we
use the Dolbeault resolution of
the sub-complex <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{p}\Omega^{*}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{p}\Omega^{*}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> by the sub-complex <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{p}\mathcal{E}^{*}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{p}\mathcal{E}^{*}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>
defined as a simple complex associated to a double complex:</p>
<table id="S5.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="F^{p}\mathcal{E}^{*}_{X}=s((\mathcal{E}_{X}^{r,*},\overline{\partial}),\partial)_{r\geq p}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mi>s</mi><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">∗</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mover accent="true"><mo>∂</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo>∂</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{p}\mathcal{E}^{*}_{X}=s((\mathcal{E}_{X}^{r,*},\overline{\partial}),\partial)_{r\geq p}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">This is a fine resolution (see <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem17" title="Lemma 1.17. ‣ 1.2.5. Dolbeault cohomology ‣ 1.2. Complex manifolds and decomposition into types ‣ 1. Hodge decomposition ‣ Mixed Hodge Structures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.17</span></a>), which compute de
Rham hypercohomology:</p>
<table id="S5.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{H}^{n}(X,F^{p}\Omega^{*}_{X})\simeq H^{n}(X,F^{p}\mathcal{E}^{*})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ℍ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{H}^{n}(X,F^{p}\Omega^{*}_{X})\simeq H^{n}(X,F^{p}\mathcal{E}^{*}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Then we can identify the terms <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E^{pq}_{1}=H^{q}(X,\Omega^{p}_{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E^{pq}_{1}=H^{q}(X,\Omega^{p}_{X})</annotation></semantics></math> with the harmonic forms
of type <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p,q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p,q)</annotation></semantics></math> to deduce the
degeneration of the spectral sequence <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({{}_{F}E}^{p,q}_{r},d_{r})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mi>F</mi><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({{}_{F}E}^{p,q}_{r},d_{r})</annotation></semantics></math>
of the filtered de Rham complex at rank <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> from classical Hodge theory,
and moreover
we obtain the Hodge decomposition on <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">ii) If <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is not projective, by
Chow’s lemma (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite> p.69), there exists a projective variety and a projective
birational morphism <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:X^{\prime}\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:X^{\prime}\to X</annotation></semantics></math>. By Hironaka’s
desingularization (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>) we can suppose <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> smooth, hence <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>
is a Kähler manifold. Then, by Grothendieck’s duality theory
(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> § 4 or <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>), there
exists for all integers <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> a trace map <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Tr(f):Rf_{*}\Omega^{p}_{X^{\prime}}\to\Omega^{p}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mi>p</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Tr(f):Rf_{*}\Omega^{p}_{X^{\prime}}\to\Omega^{p}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> inducing a map on cohomology
<math id="S5.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Tr(f):H^{q}(X^{\prime},\Omega^{p}_{X^{\prime}})\to H^{q}(X,\Omega^{p}_{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Tr(f):H^{q}(X^{\prime},\Omega^{p}_{X^{\prime}})\to H^{q}(X,\Omega^{p}_{X})</annotation></semantics></math>,
because <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{H}^{q}(X,Rf_{*}\Omega^{p}_{X^{\prime}})\simeq H^{q}(X^{\prime},\Omega^{p}_{X^{\prime}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ℍ</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mi>p</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{H}^{q}(X,Rf_{*}\Omega^{p}_{X^{\prime}})\simeq H^{q}(X^{\prime},\Omega^{p}_{X^{\prime}})</annotation></semantics></math>,
such that the composition with
the canonical reciprocal morphism
<math id="S5.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}:H^{q}(X,\Omega^{p}_{X})\to H^{q}(X^{\prime},\Omega^{p}_{X^{\prime}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}:H^{q}(X,\Omega^{p}_{X})\to H^{q}(X^{\prime},\Omega^{p}_{X^{\prime}})</annotation></semantics></math> is the identity:</p>
<table id="S5.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Tr(f)\circ f^{*}=Id:H^{q}(X,\Omega^{p}_{X})\to H^{q}(X^{\prime},\Omega^{p}_{X^{\prime}})\to H^{q}(X,\Omega^{p}_{X})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Tr(f)\circ f^{*}=Id:H^{q}(X,\Omega^{p}_{X})\to H^{q}(X^{\prime},\Omega^{p}_{X^{\prime}})\to H^{q}(X,\Omega^{p}_{X})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">In particular we deduce that <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is injective. Since the map:</p>
<table id="S5.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}:f^{*}(\Omega^{p}_{X},F)\to(\Omega^{p}_{X^{\prime}},F)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}:f^{*}(\Omega^{p}_{X},F)\to(\Omega^{p}_{X^{\prime}},F)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">is compatible
with filtrations, we deduce a map of spectral sequences:</p>
<table id="S5.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{1}^{pq}=H^{q}(X,\Omega^{p}_{X})\xrightarrow{f^{*}}E_{1}^{\prime pq}=H^{q}(X^{\prime},\Omega^{p}_{X^{\prime}}),\quad f^{*}:E_{r}^{pq}(X)\hookrightarrow E_{r}^{pq}(X^{\prime})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.700em">f</mi><mo mathsize="0.710em">∗</mo></msup></mover><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo rspace="0em">′</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{1}^{pq}=H^{q}(X,\Omega^{p}_{X})\xrightarrow{f^{*}}E_{1}^{\prime pq}=H^{q}(X^{\prime},\Omega^{p}_{X^{\prime}}),\quad f^{*}:E_{r}^{pq}(X)\hookrightarrow E_{r}^{pq}(X^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.4" class="ltx_p">which is injective on all terms and commute with the differentials <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The proof is by induction on <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> as follows.
It is true for <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=1</annotation></semantics></math>, as we have just noticed. The differential <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> vanishes on <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>;
it must vanish on <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, then the terms for <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=2</annotation></semantics></math> coincide with the terms
for <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=1</annotation></semantics></math> and we can repeat the argument for all <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The degeneration of the
Hodge spectral sequence on <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> at rank <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> follows, and it is
equivalent to the isomorphism:</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S5.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{H}^{n}(X,F^{p}\Omega^{*}_{X})\xrightarrow{\sim}F^{p}\mathbb{H}^{n}(X,\Omega^{*}_{X})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ℍ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo></mover><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℍ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{H}^{n}(X,F^{p}\Omega^{*}_{X})\xrightarrow{\sim}F^{p}\mathbb{H}^{n}(X,\Omega^{*}_{X}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Equivalently, the dimension of
the hypercohomology of the de Rham complex <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{H}^{j}(X,\Omega^{*}_{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℍ</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{H}^{j}(X,\Omega^{*}_{X})</annotation></semantics></math>
is equal to the dimension of Hodge cohomology
<math id="S5.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\oplus_{p+q=j}H^{q}(X,\Omega^{p}_{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\oplus_{p+q=j}H^{q}(X,\Omega^{p}_{X})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">However, Hodge theory tells more. From the Hodge filtration, we deduce
the definition of the subspaces:</p>
<table id="S5.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{p,q}(X):=F^{p}H^{i}(X,\mathbb{C})\cap\overline{F^{q}}H^{i}(X,\mathbb{C}),\quad{\rm for}\,p+q=i" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>for</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{p,q}(X):=F^{p}H^{i}(X,\mathbb{C})\cap\overline{F^{q}}H^{i}(X,\mathbb{C}),\quad{\rm for}\,p+q=i</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.p6.2" class="ltx_p">satisfying <math id="S5.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{p,q}(X)=\overline{H^{q,p}(X)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{p,q}(X)=\overline{H^{q,p}(X)}</annotation></semantics></math>. We must check
the decomposition:</p>
<table id="S5.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="H^{i}(X,\mathbb{C})=\oplus_{p+q=i}H^{p,q}(X)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><msub><mo lspace="0em">⊕</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{i}(X,\mathbb{C})=\oplus_{p+q=i}H^{p,q}(X),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.p6.3" class="ltx_p">and moreover, we will deduce <math id="S5.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{p,q}(X)\simeq H^{q}(X,\Omega^{p}_{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{p,q}(X)\simeq H^{q}(X,\Omega^{p}_{X})</annotation></semantics></math>.
We have:</p>
<table id="S5.Ex19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{p}H^{n}(X)\cap\overline{F^{n-p+1}}H^{n}(X)\subset F^{p}H^{n}(X^{\prime})\cap\overline{F^{n-p+1}}H^{n}(X^{\prime})=0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><msup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><msup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{p}H^{n}(X)\cap\overline{F^{n-p+1}}H^{n}(X)\subset F^{p}H^{n}(X^{\prime})\cap\overline{F^{n-p+1}}H^{n}(X^{\prime})=0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.p6.4" class="ltx_p">This shows that <math id="S5.SS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{p}H^{n}(X)+\overline{F^{n-p+1}}H^{n}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><msup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{p}H^{n}(X)+\overline{F^{n-p+1}}H^{n}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> is a direct sum.
We want to prove that this sum equals <math id="S5.SS1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{n}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{n}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S5.SS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{p,q}={\dim}H^{p,q}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{p,q}={\dim}H^{p,q}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>, since the spectral sequence
degenerates at rank <math id="S5.SS1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>, we have:</p>
<table id="S5.Ex20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\dim}F^{p}H^{n}(X)=\sum_{i\geq p}h^{i,n-i},\,{\dim}\overline{F^{n-p+1}}H^{n}(X)=\sum_{i\geq n-p+1}h^{i,n-i}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></munder><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.059em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><msup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></munder><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\dim}F^{p}H^{n}(X)=\sum_{i\geq p}h^{i,n-i},\,{\dim}\overline{F^{n-p+1}}H^{n}(X)=\sum_{i\geq n-p+1}h^{i,n-i},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.p7.2" class="ltx_p">then:</p>
<table id="S5.Ex21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i\geq p}h^{i,n-i}+\sum_{i\geq n-p+1}h^{i,n-i}\leq{\dim}H^{n}(X)=\sum_{i}h^{i,n-i}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></munder><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></munder><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">≤</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>i</mi></munder><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i\geq p}h^{i,n-i}+\sum_{i\geq n-p+1}h^{i,n-i}\leq{\dim}H^{n}(X)=\sum_{i}h^{i,n-i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.p7.3" class="ltx_p">from which we deduce <math id="S5.SS1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i\geq p}h^{i,n-i}\leq\sum_{i\leq n-p}h^{i,n-i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≤</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i\geq p}h^{i,n-i}\leq\sum_{i\leq n-p}h^{i,n-i}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">By Serre duality on <math id="S5.SS1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> of dimension <math id="S5.SS1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S5.SS1.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{i,j}=h^{N-i,N-j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{i,j}=h^{N-i,N-j}</annotation></semantics></math>, which transforms the inequality into:
<math id="S5.SS1.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{N-i\leq N-p}h^{N-i,N-n+i}\leq\sum_{N-i\geq N-n+p}h^{N-i,N-n+i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≤</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{N-i\leq N-p}h^{N-i,N-n+i}\leq\sum_{N-i\geq N-n+p}h^{N-i,N-n+i}</annotation></semantics></math>,
from which we deduce on <math id="S5.SS1.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{m}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{m}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S5.SS1.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=2N-n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=2N-n</annotation></semantics></math>, the opposite
inequality by setting <math id="S5.SS1.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j=N-i,q=N-n+p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j=N-i,q=N-n+p</annotation></semantics></math> shows that, for all <math id="S5.SS1.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS1.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S5.Ex22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{j\geq q}h^{j,m-j}\geq\sum_{j\leq m-q}h^{j,m-j},\quad" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></munder><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≥</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mrow></munder><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{j\geq q}h^{j,m-j}\geq\sum_{j\leq m-q}h^{j,m-j},\quad</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.p8.2" class="ltx_p">In particular:</p>
<table id="S5.Ex23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i\geq p}h^{i,n-i}\geq\sum_{i\leq n-p}h^{i,n-i}\quad\hbox{hence}\quad\sum_{i\geq p}h^{i,n-i}=\sum_{i\leq n-p}h^{i,n-i}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></munder><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≥</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow></munder><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>hence</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></munder><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow></munder><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i\geq p}h^{i,n-i}\geq\sum_{i\leq n-p}h^{i,n-i}\quad\hbox{hence}\quad\sum_{i\geq p}h^{i,n-i}=\sum_{i\leq n-p}h^{i,n-i}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.p8.3" class="ltx_p">This implies <math id="S5.SS1.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\dim}F^{p}+{\dim}\overline{F^{n-p+1}}={\dim}H^{n}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mover accent="true"><msup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\dim}F^{p}+{\dim}\overline{F^{n-p+1}}={\dim}H^{n}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence:</p>
<table id="S5.Ex24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{n}(X)=F^{p}H^{n}(X)\oplus\overline{F^{n-p+1}}H^{n}(X)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><msup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{n}(X)=F^{p}H^{n}(X)\oplus\overline{F^{n-p+1}}H^{n}(X)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.p8.4" class="ltx_p">which,
in particular, induces a decomposition:</p>
<table id="S5.Ex25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{n-1}H^{n}(X)=F^{p}H^{n}(X)\oplus H^{n-1,n-p+1}(X)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{n-1}H^{n}(X)=F^{p}H^{n}(X)\oplus H^{n-1,n-p+1}(X).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem11" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem11.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 5.11</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem11.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If we use distinct notations for <math id="S5.Thmtheorem11.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> with Zariski topology and
<math id="S5.Thmtheorem11.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{an}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{an}</annotation></semantics></math> for the analytic associated manifold, then the filtration
<math id="S5.Thmtheorem11.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> is defined on the algebraic de Rham hypercohomology groups and
the comparison theorem (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>) is compatible with the
filtrations: <math id="S5.Thmtheorem11.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{H}^{i}(X,F^{p}\Omega^{*}_{X})\simeq\mathbb{H}^{i}(X^{an},F^{p}\Omega^{*}_{X^{an}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ℍ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℍ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{H}^{i}(X,F^{p}\Omega^{*}_{X})\simeq\mathbb{H}^{i}(X^{an},F^{p}\Omega^{*}_{X^{an}})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.1.1. </span></h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> be a rational local system with a
polarization, rationally defined, on the associated local system
<math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathbb{C}}=\mathcal{L}\otimes_{\mathbb{Q}}\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></msub><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathbb{C}}=\mathcal{L}\otimes_{\mathbb{Q}}\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, then the spectral sequence
defined by the Hodge filtration on de Rham complex with
coefficients <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega^{*}_{X}\otimes_{\mathbb{C}}\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega^{*}_{X}\otimes_{\mathbb{C}}\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> degenerates at rank <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S5.Ex26" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E^{pq}_{1}=H^{q}(X,\Omega^{p}_{X}(\mathcal{L}))\Rightarrow H^{p+q}(X,\mathcal{L}_{\mathbb{C}}),\quad E^{pq}_{1}=E^{pq}_{\infty}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⇒</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E^{pq}_{1}=H^{q}(X,\Omega^{p}_{X}(\mathcal{L}))\Rightarrow H^{p+q}(X,\mathcal{L}_{\mathbb{C}}),\quad E^{pq}_{1}=E^{pq}_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">and the induced filtration by <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> on cohomology defines a Hodge Structure. The
proof by Deligne proceeds as in Hodge theory.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2. </span>MHS on the cohomology of a Mixed Hodge Complex </h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The proof is based on a delicate study of the induced filtration
<math id="S5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> on the cohomology.
To explain the difficulty, imagine for a moment that we want to give
a proof by induction on the length of <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose that the weights of a
Mixed Hodge Complex: <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(K,W,F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(K,W,F)</annotation></semantics></math> vary from
<math id="S5.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{0}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{0}=0</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{l}=K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{l}=K</annotation></semantics></math> and suppose we did construct the Mixed Hodge Structure on
cohomology for <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l-1</annotation></semantics></math>, then we consider the long exact sequence
of cohomology:</p>
<table id="S5.Ex27" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{i-1}(Gr^{W}_{l}K)\to H^{i}(W_{l-1}K)\to H^{i}(K)\to H^{i}(Gr^{W}_{l}K)\to H^{i+1}(W_{l-1}K)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>l</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>l</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{i-1}(Gr^{W}_{l}K)\to H^{i}(W_{l-1}K)\to H^{i}(K)\to H^{i}(Gr^{W}_{l}K)\to H^{i+1}(W_{l-1}K)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">If the result was established then the
morphisms of the sequence are strict, hence the difficulty
is a question of relative
positions of the subspaces <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{q}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{i}(K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{i}(K)</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to
Im<math id="S5.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{i}(W_{l-1}K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{i}(W_{l-1}K)</annotation></semantics></math> and the projection on <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{i}(Gr^{W}_{l}K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>l</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{i}(Gr^{W}_{l}K)</annotation></semantics></math>. This
study is known as the two filtrations lemma.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.2.1. </span>Two filtrations</h4>

<div id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section relates results on
various induced filtrations on terms of a spectral sequence,
contained in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> (lemma on two
filtrations). A filtration <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> of a complex by subcomplexes define
a spectral sequence <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{r}(K,W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{r}(K,W)</annotation></semantics></math>. The filtration <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> induces in
various ways filtrations on <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{r}(K,W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{r}(K,W)</annotation></semantics></math>, different
in general. We discuss here axioms on <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>, at the level of complexes, in order to get compatibility
of the various induced filtrations by <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> on the spectral
sequence of <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(K,W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(K,W)</annotation></semantics></math>. What we have in mind is to find axioms
leading to define the Mixed Hodge Structure with induced
filtrations <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> on cohomology. The proof is technical,
hence we emphasize here the main ideas as a guide to Deligne’s
proof.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.2.2. </span></h4>

<div id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(K,F,W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(K,F,W)</annotation></semantics></math> be a bi-filtered
complex of objects of an abelian category, bounded below. The
filtration <math id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>,
assumed to be biregular, induces on the terms <math id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{r}^{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{r}^{pq}</annotation></semantics></math> of
the spectral sequence <math id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(K,W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(K,W)</annotation></semantics></math> various filtrations as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem12" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem12.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 5.12</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<math id="S5.Thmtheorem12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{d},F_{d^{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{d},F_{d^{*}}</annotation></semantics></math>)<span id="S5.Thmtheorem12.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem12.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem12.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The first direct filtration
on <math id="S5.Thmtheorem12.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{r}(K,W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{r}(K,W)</annotation></semantics></math> is the filtration <math id="S5.Thmtheorem12.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> defined for <math id="S5.Thmtheorem12.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> finite or
<math id="S5.Thmtheorem12.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, by the image:</p>
<table id="S5.Ex28" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{d}^{p}(E_{r}(K,W))=Im(E_{r}(F^{p}K,W)\rightarrow E_{r}(K,W))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{d}^{p}(E_{r}(K,W))=Im(E_{r}(F^{p}K,W)\rightarrow E_{r}(K,W)).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem12.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Dually, the second direct filtration <math id="S5.Thmtheorem12.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{d^{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{d^{*}}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.Thmtheorem12.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{r}(K,W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{r}(K,W)</annotation></semantics></math> is
defined by the kernel:</p>
<table id="S5.Ex29" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{d^{*}}^{p}(E_{r}(K,W))=Ker(E_{r}(K,W)\rightarrow E_{r}(K/F^{p}K,W))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>F</mi><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{d^{*}}^{p}(E_{r}(K,W))=Ker(E_{r}(K,W)\rightarrow E_{r}(K/F^{p}K,W)).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This definition is temporarily convenient for the next computation,
since the filtrations <math id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{d},F_{d^{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{d},F_{d^{*}}</annotation></semantics></math> are naturally induced by <math id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>,
hence compatible with the differentials <math id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Since for <math id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=0,\,1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn> 1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=0,\,1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{pq}_{r}\subset Z^{pq}_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>⊂</mo><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{pq}_{r}\subset Z^{pq}_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem13" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem13.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 5.13</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem13.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem13.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem13.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem13.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We have <math id="S5.Thmtheorem13.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{d}=F_{d^{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{d}=F_{d^{*}}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.Thmtheorem13.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{0}^{pq}=Gr_{F}^{p}(K^{p+q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{0}^{pq}=Gr_{F}^{p}(K^{p+q})</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S5.Thmtheorem13.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{1}^{pq}=H^{p+q}(Gr^{W}_{p}K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>p</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{1}^{pq}=H^{p+q}(Gr^{W}_{p}K)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem14" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem14.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 5.14</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem14.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<math id="S5.Thmtheorem14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{rec}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{rec}</annotation></semantics></math>)<span id="S5.Thmtheorem14.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem14.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem14.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The recurrent filtration <math id="S5.Thmtheorem14.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{rec}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{rec}</annotation></semantics></math>
on <math id="S5.Thmtheorem14.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{r}^{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{r}^{pq}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as follows 
<br class="ltx_break">i) On <math id="S5.Thmtheorem14.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{0}^{pq},F_{rec}=F_{d}=F_{d^{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{0}^{pq},F_{rec}=F_{d}=F_{d^{*}}</annotation></semantics></math>. 
<br class="ltx_break">ii) The recurrent filtration <math id="S5.Thmtheorem14.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{rec}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{rec}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.Thmtheorem14.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{r}^{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{r}^{pq}</annotation></semantics></math> induces a
filtration on ker <math id="S5.Thmtheorem14.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>, which induces on its turn the recurrent
filtration <math id="S5.Thmtheorem14.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{rec}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{rec}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.Thmtheorem14.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{r+1}^{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{r+1}^{pq}</annotation></semantics></math> as a quotient of ker
<math id="S5.Thmtheorem14.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.2.3. </span></h4>

<div id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The precedent definitions of direct filtrations
apply to <math id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{\infty}^{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{\infty}^{pq}</annotation></semantics></math> as well and they are compatible with
the isomorphism <math id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{r}^{pq}\simeq E_{\infty}^{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>≃</mo><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{r}^{pq}\simeq E_{\infty}^{pq}</annotation></semantics></math> for large <math id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>.
We deduce, via this isomorphism a recurrent filtration <math id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{rec}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{rec}</annotation></semantics></math>
on <math id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{\infty}^{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{\infty}^{pq}</annotation></semantics></math>. The filtrations <math id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> induce each a
filtration on <math id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{p+q}(K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{p+q}(K)</annotation></semantics></math>. We want to prove that the isomorphism
<math id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{\infty}^{pq}\simeq Gr_{W}^{-p}H^{p+q}(K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>W</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{\infty}^{pq}\simeq Gr_{W}^{-p}H^{p+q}(K)</annotation></semantics></math> is compatible with
<math id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> at right and <math id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{rec}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{rec}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{\infty}^{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{\infty}^{pq}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2.SSS4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.2.4. </span>Comparison of <math id="S5.SS2.SSS4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{d},F_{rec},F_{d^{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{d},F_{rec},F_{d^{*}}</annotation></semantics></math></h4>

<div id="S5.SS2.SSS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In general we have only the inclusions</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem15" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem15.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 5.15</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem15.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem15.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem15.p1.1" class="ltx_p">i) On <math id="S5.Thmtheorem15.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{r}^{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{r}^{pq}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have the inclusions</p>
<table id="S5.Ex30" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{d}(E_{r}^{pq})\subset F_{rec}(E_{r}^{pq})\subset F_{d^{*}}(E_{r}^{pq})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{d}(E_{r}^{pq})\subset F_{rec}(E_{r}^{pq})\subset F_{d^{*}}(E_{r}^{pq})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem15.p1.2" class="ltx_p">ii) On <math id="S5.Thmtheorem15.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{\infty}^{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{\infty}^{pq}</annotation></semantics></math>, the filtration induced by the
filtration <math id="S5.Thmtheorem15.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.Thmtheorem15.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}(K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}(K)</annotation></semantics></math> satisfy</p>
<table id="S5.Ex31" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{d}(E_{\infty}^{pq})\subset F(E_{\infty}^{pq})\subset F_{d^{*}}(E_{\infty}^{pq})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{d}(E_{\infty}^{pq})\subset F(E_{\infty}^{pq})\subset F_{d^{*}}(E_{\infty}^{pq}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem15.p1.3" class="ltx_p">iii) The differential <math id="S5.Thmtheorem15.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> is compatible with <math id="S5.Thmtheorem15.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S5.Thmtheorem15.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{d^{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{d^{*}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.SSS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We want to show that these three filtrations coincide under the
conditions on the Mixed Hodge Complex, for this we need to
know the compatibility of <math id="S5.SS2.SSS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S5.SS2.SSS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{rec}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{rec}</annotation></semantics></math> which will be the
Hodge filtration of a Hodge Structure.
In fact, Deligne proves an intermediary statement, that will apply
inductively for Mixed Hodge Complexes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem16" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem16.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 5.16</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem16.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Deligne)<span id="S5.Thmtheorem16.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem16.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem16.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem16.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S5.Thmtheorem16.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> be a complex with
two filtrations <math id="S5.Thmtheorem16.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.Thmtheorem16.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S5.Thmtheorem16.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> is biregular and suppose
that for each non negative integer <math id="S5.Thmtheorem16.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r&lt;r_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r&lt;r_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, the differentials
<math id="S5.Thmtheorem16.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> of the graded complex <math id="S5.Thmtheorem16.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{r}(K,W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{r}(K,W)</annotation></semantics></math> are strictly compatible
with <math id="S5.Thmtheorem16.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{rec}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{rec}</annotation></semantics></math>, then
<br class="ltx_break">i) For <math id="S5.Thmtheorem16.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\leq r_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\leq r_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> the sequence</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex32" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\rightarrow E_{r}(F^{p}K,W)\rightarrow E_{r}(K,W)\rightarrow E_{r}(K/F^{p}K,W)\rightarrow 0" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\rightarrow E_{r}(F^{p}K,W)\rightarrow E_{r}(K,W)\rightarrow E_{r}(K/F^{p}K,W)\rightarrow 0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem16.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem16.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">is exact, and for <math id="S5.Thmtheorem16.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=r_{0}+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=r_{0}+1</annotation></semantics></math>, the sequence</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex33" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{r}(F^{p}K,W)\rightarrow E_{r}(K,W)\rightarrow E_{r}(K/F^{p}K,W)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{r}(F^{p}K,W)\rightarrow E_{r}(K,W)\rightarrow E_{r}(K/F^{p}K,W)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem16.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem16.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">is exact. In particular for <math id="S5.Thmtheorem16.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\leq r_{0}+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\leq r_{0}+1</annotation></semantics></math>, the two direct and
the recurrent filtration on <math id="S5.Thmtheorem16.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{r}(K,W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{r}(K,W)</annotation></semantics></math> coincide <math id="S5.Thmtheorem16.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{d}=F_{rec}=F_{d^{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{d}=F_{rec}=F_{d^{*}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
<br class="ltx_break">ii) For <math id="S5.Thmtheorem16.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a&lt;b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a&lt;b</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.Thmtheorem16.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r&lt;r_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r&lt;r_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, the differentials
<math id="S5.Thmtheorem16.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> of the graded complex <math id="S5.Thmtheorem16.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{r}(F^{a}K/F^{b}K,W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>a</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>b</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{r}(F^{a}K/F^{b}K,W)</annotation></semantics></math> are strictly compatible
with <math id="S5.Thmtheorem16.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{rec}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{rec}</annotation></semantics></math>.
<br class="ltx_break">iii) If the above condition is satisfied for all <math id="S5.Thmtheorem16.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>, then
the spectral sequence <math id="S5.Thmtheorem16.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(K,F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(K,F)</annotation></semantics></math> degenerates at <math id="S5.Thmtheorem16.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>
<math id="S5.Thmtheorem16.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(E_{1}=E_{\infty})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(E_{1}=E_{\infty})</annotation></semantics></math> and the filtrations <math id="S5.Thmtheorem16.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{d},F_{rec},F_{d^{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{d},F_{rec},F_{d^{*}}</annotation></semantics></math>
coincide into a unique filtration called <math id="S5.Thmtheorem16.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, the isomorphism
<math id="S5.Thmtheorem16.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{\infty}^{pq}\simeq Gr_{W}^{-p}H^{p+q}(K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>W</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{\infty}^{pq}\simeq Gr_{W}^{-p}H^{p+q}(K)</annotation></semantics></math> is compatible
with the filtrations <math id="S5.Thmtheorem16.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> and we
have an isomorphism of spectral sequences</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex34" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr^{p}_{F}E_{r}(K,W)\simeq E_{r}(Gr^{p}_{F}K,W)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr^{p}_{F}E_{r}(K,W)\simeq E_{r}(Gr^{p}_{F}K,W)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.SSS4.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.SS2.SSS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.SSS4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">This surprising statement looks natural only
if we have in mind the degeneration of <math id="S5.SS2.SSS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(K,F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(K,F)</annotation></semantics></math> at rank <math id="S5.SS2.SSS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> and
the strictness in the category of Mixed Hodge Structures.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.SSS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.SSS4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">For fixed <math id="S5.SS2.SSS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>, we consider the following property:</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex35" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex35.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(P_{r})\,E_{i}(F^{p}K,W)\hbox{ injects into }E_{i}(K,W)\hbox{ for }i\leq r\hbox{ and its
image is }F^{p}_{rec}\hbox{ for }i\leq r+1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mtext> injects into </mtext><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mtext> for </mtext><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>r</mi><mtext> and its
image is </mtext><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mtext> for </mtext><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(P_{r})\,E_{i}(F^{p}K,W)\hbox{ injects into }E_{i}(K,W)\hbox{ for }i\leq r\hbox{ and its
image is }F^{p}_{rec}\hbox{ for }i\leq r+1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.SSS4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS4.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.SSS4.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">We already noted that <math id="S5.SS2.SSS4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(P_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(P_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> is satisfied. The proof by induction
on <math id="S5.SS2.SSS4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> will apply as long as <math id="S5.SS2.SSS4.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> remains <math id="S5.SS2.SSS4.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq r_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq r_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose <math id="S5.SS2.SSS4.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r&lt;r_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r&lt;r_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.SSS4.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(P_{s})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(P_{s})</annotation></semantics></math> true for all
<math id="S5.SS2.SSS4.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\leq r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\leq r</annotation></semantics></math>, we prove <math id="S5.SS2.SSS4.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(P_{r+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(P_{r+1})</annotation></semantics></math>.
The sequence:</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex36" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex36.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{r}(F^{p}K,W)\xrightarrow{d_{r}}E_{r}(F^{p}K,W)\xrightarrow{d_{r}}E_{r}(F^{p}K,W)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">d</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">r</mi></msub></mover><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">d</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">r</mi></msub></mover><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{r}(F^{p}K,W)\xrightarrow{d_{r}}E_{r}(F^{p}K,W)\xrightarrow{d_{r}}E_{r}(F^{p}K,W)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS4.p5.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.SSS4.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text">injects into:</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex37" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex37.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{r}(K,W)\xrightarrow{d_{r}}E_{r}(K,W)\xrightarrow{d_{r}}E_{r}(K,W)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">d</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">r</mi></msub></mover><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">d</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">r</mi></msub></mover><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{r}(K,W)\xrightarrow{d_{r}}E_{r}(K,W)\xrightarrow{d_{r}}E_{r}(K,W)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS4.p5.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.SSS4.p5.3.1" class="ltx_text">with image <math id="S5.SS2.SSS4.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{d}=F_{rec}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{d}=F_{rec}</annotation></semantics></math>, then, the image of <math id="S5.SS2.SSS4.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{p}_{rec}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mi>p</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{p}_{rec}</annotation></semantics></math> in
<math id="S5.SS2.SSS4.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{r+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{r+1}</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex38" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex38.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="F^{p}_{rec}E_{r+1}=Im[Ker(F_{rec}^{p}E_{r}(K,W)\xrightarrow{d_{r}}E_{r}(K,W))\to E_{r+1}(K,W)]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mi>p</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>I</mi><mi>m</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>K</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mi>p</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">d</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">r</mi></msub></mover><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{p}_{rec}E_{r+1}=Im[Ker(F_{rec}^{p}E_{r}(K,W)\xrightarrow{d_{r}}E_{r}(K,W))\to E_{r+1}(K,W)]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS4.p5.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.SSS4.p5.4.1" class="ltx_text">coincides with the image of <math id="S5.SS2.SSS4.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{p}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{p}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> which is by definition <math id="S5.SS2.SSS4.p5.m13" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="Im[E_{r+1}(F^{p}K,W)\to E_{r+1}(K,W)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mi>m</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Im[E_{r+1}(F^{p}K,W)\to E_{r+1}(K,W)]</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.SSS4.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS4.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.SSS4.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="S5.SS2.SSS4.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> is strictly compatible with <math id="S5.SS2.SSS4.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{rec}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{rec}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have:</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex39" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex39.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{r}E_{r}(K,W)\cap E_{r}(F^{p}K,W)=d_{r}E_{r}(F^{p}K,W)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{r}E_{r}(K,W)\cap E_{r}(F^{p}K,W)=d_{r}E_{r}(F^{p}K,W)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS4.p6.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.SSS4.p6.2.1" class="ltx_text">which means that <math id="S5.SS2.SSS4.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{r+1}(F^{p}K,W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{r+1}(F^{p}K,W)</annotation></semantics></math> injects into <math id="S5.SS2.SSS4.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{r+1}(K,W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{r+1}(K,W)</annotation></semantics></math>, hence we deduce the injectivity
for <math id="S5.SS2.SSS4.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r+1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since ker <math id="S5.SS2.SSS4.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.SS2.SSS4.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{p}_{rec}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mi>p</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{p}_{rec}</annotation></semantics></math> is equal to ker <math id="S5.SS2.SSS4.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.SS2.SSS4.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{r+1}(F^{p}K,W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{r+1}(F^{p}K,W)</annotation></semantics></math>, we deduce <math id="S5.SS2.SSS4.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{p}_{rec}=F^{p}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{p}_{rec}=F^{p}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.SS2.SSS4.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{r+2}(K,W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{r+2}(K,W)</annotation></semantics></math>, which proves
<math id="S5.SS2.SSS4.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(P_{r+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(P_{r+1})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.SSS4.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS4.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.SSS4.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">Then, i) follows from a dual statement applied to
<math id="S5.SS2.SSS4.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{d^{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{d^{*}}</annotation></semantics></math> and ii) follows, because <math id="S5.SS2.SSS4.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{r}(F^{b}K,W)\hookrightarrow E_{r}(F^{a}K,W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>b</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>a</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{r}(F^{b}K,W)\hookrightarrow E_{r}(F^{a}K,W)</annotation></semantics></math>
is injective as they are both in <math id="S5.SS2.SSS4.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{r}(K,W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{r}(K,W)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.SSS4.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS4.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.SSS4.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">iii) We deduce from i) the first exact sequence followed by its
dual:</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex40" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex40.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}&amp;0\rightarrow E_{r}(F^{p+1}K,W)\rightarrow E_{r}(F^{p}K,W)\rightarrow E_{r}(Gr_{F}^{p}K,W)\rightarrow 0\\
&amp;0\leftarrow E_{r}(K/F^{p}K,W)\leftarrow E_{r}(F^{p+1}K,W)\leftarrow E_{r}(Gr_{F}^{p}K,W)\leftarrow 0.\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}&amp;0\rightarrow E_{r}(F^{p+1}K,W)\rightarrow E_{r}(F^{p}K,W)\rightarrow E_{r}(Gr_{F}^{p}K,W)\rightarrow 0\\
&amp;0\leftarrow E_{r}(K/F^{p}K,W)\leftarrow E_{r}(F^{p+1}K,W)\leftarrow E_{r}(Gr_{F}^{p}K,W)\leftarrow 0.\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS4.p8.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.SSS4.p8.2.1" class="ltx_text">In view of the injections in i) and the coincidence of <math id="S5.SS2.SSS4.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{d}=F_{rec}=F_{d^{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{d}=F_{rec}=F_{d^{*}}</annotation></semantics></math> we have a unique filtration <math id="S5.SS2.SSS4.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>, the quotient
of the first two terms in the first exact sequence above is
isomorphic to <math id="S5.SS2.SSS4.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr_{F}^{p}E_{r}(K,W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr_{F}^{p}E_{r}(K,W)</annotation></semantics></math>, hence we deduce an isomorphism</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex41" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex41.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr_{F}^{p}E_{r}(K,W)\simeq E_{r}(Gr_{F}^{p}K,W)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr_{F}^{p}E_{r}(K,W)\simeq E_{r}(Gr_{F}^{p}K,W)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS4.p8.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.SSS4.p8.3.1" class="ltx_text">compatible with <math id="S5.SS2.SSS4.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> and autodual. If the hypothesis is now true
for all <math id="S5.SS2.SSS4.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>, we deduce an exact sequence:</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex42" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex42.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\rightarrow E_{\infty}(F^{p}K,W)\rightarrow E_{\infty}(K,W)\rightarrow E_{\infty}(K/F^{p}K,W)\rightarrow 0" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\rightarrow E_{\infty}(F^{p}K,W)\rightarrow E_{\infty}(K,W)\rightarrow E_{\infty}(K/F^{p}K,W)\rightarrow 0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS4.p8.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.SSS4.p8.4.1" class="ltx_text">which is identical to:</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex43" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex43.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\rightarrow Gr_{W}H^{*}(F^{p}K)\rightarrow Gr_{W}H^{*}(K)\rightarrow Gr_{W}H^{*}(K/F^{p}K)\rightarrow 0" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\rightarrow Gr_{W}H^{*}(F^{p}K)\rightarrow Gr_{W}H^{*}(K)\rightarrow Gr_{W}H^{*}(K/F^{p}K)\rightarrow 0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS4.p8.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.SSS4.p8.5.1" class="ltx_text">from which we deduce, for all <math id="S5.SS2.SSS4.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex44" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex44.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\rightarrow H^{i}(F^{p}K)\rightarrow H^{i}(K)\rightarrow H^{i}(K/F^{p}K)\rightarrow 0" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\rightarrow H^{i}(F^{p}K)\rightarrow H^{i}(K)\rightarrow H^{i}(K/F^{p}K)\rightarrow 0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS4.p8.6" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.SSS4.p8.6.1" class="ltx_text">and that the filtrations <math id="S5.SS2.SSS4.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> induced on <math id="S5.SS2.SSS4.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{i}(F^{p}K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{i}(F^{p}K)</annotation></semantics></math> from
<math id="S5.SS2.SSS4.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(F^{p}K,W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(F^{p}K,W)</annotation></semantics></math> and from <math id="S5.SS2.SSS4.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(H^{i}(K),W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(H^{i}(K),W)</annotation></semantics></math> coincide.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2.SSS5" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.2.5. </span>Proof of the existence of a MHS on cohomology of a MHC</h4>

<div id="S5.SS2.SSS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The above theorem <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem16" title="Theorem 5.16 (Deligne). ‣ 5.2.4. Comparison of 
            
              
                
                  
                  
                  
                  
                
                
                  
                    
                    F
                    d
                  
                  ,
                  
                    
                    F
                    
                      ⁢
                      r
                      e
                      c
                    
                  
                  ,
                  
                    
                    F
                    
                      
                      d
                      ∗ ‣ 5.2. MHS on the cohomology of a Mixed Hodge Complex ‣ 5. Mixed Hodge Complex (MHC) ‣ Mixed Hodge Structures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.16</span></a> applies to Mixed Hodge Complexes, since the
hypothesis of the induction on <math id="S5.SS2.SSS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> in the theorem will be
satisfied as follows. If we assume that the filtrations <math id="S5.SS2.SSS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{d}=F_{rec}=F_{d^{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{d}=F_{rec}=F_{d^{*}}</annotation></semantics></math> coincide for <math id="S5.SS2.SSS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r&lt;r_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r&lt;r_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and moreover define the
same Hodge filtration <math id="S5.SS2.SSS5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> of a Hodge Structure of weight <math id="S5.SS2.SSS5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> on
<math id="S5.SS2.SSS5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E^{p,q}_{r}(K,W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E^{p,q}_{r}(K,W)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.SSS5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{r}:E^{p,q}_{r}\to E^{p+r,q-r+1}_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{r}:E^{p,q}_{r}\to E^{p+r,q-r+1}_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> is
compatible with such Hodge Structure, then in particular <math id="S5.SS2.SSS5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> is
strictly compatible with <math id="S5.SS2.SSS5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>, hence the induction apply.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem17" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem17.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 5.17</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem17.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem17.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem17.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem17.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For <math id="S5.Thmtheorem17.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, the differentials
<math id="S5.Thmtheorem17.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> of the spectral sequence <math id="S5.Thmtheorem17.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}_{W}E_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mmultiscripts><mi>E</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mi>r</mi><mrow></mrow><mi>W</mi><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{W}E_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> are strictly compatible
to the recurrent filtration <math id="S5.Thmtheorem17.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F=F_{rec}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F=F_{rec}</annotation></semantics></math>. For <math id="S5.Thmtheorem17.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>, they
vanish.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.SSS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The initial statement applies for <math id="S5.SS2.SSS5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=1</annotation></semantics></math> by definition of a Mixed
Hodge Complex since the complex <math id="S5.SS2.SSS5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr_{-p}^{W}K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr_{-p}^{W}K</annotation></semantics></math> is a Hodge Complex
of weight <math id="S5.SS2.SSS5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-p</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence,
the two direct filtrations and the recurrent filtration <math id="S5.SS2.SSS5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{rec}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{rec}</annotation></semantics></math>
coincide with the Hodge filtration <math id="S5.SS2.SSS5.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.SS2.SSS5.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}_{W}E_{1}^{pq}=H^{p+q}(Gr_{-p}^{W}K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>E</mi><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mn>1</mn><mrow></mrow><mi>W</mi><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{W}E_{1}^{pq}=H^{p+q}(Gr_{-p}^{W}K)</annotation></semantics></math>.
The differential <math id="S5.SS2.SSS5.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is compatible with the direct filtrations,
hence with <math id="S5.SS2.SSS5.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{rec}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{rec}</annotation></semantics></math>, and commutes
with complex conjugation since it is defined on
<math id="S5.SS2.SSS5.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\otimes\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\otimes\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>, hence it is compatible with
<math id="S5.SS2.SSS5.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{F}_{rec}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{F}_{rec}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then it is strictly compatible
with the Hodge filtration <math id="S5.SS2.SSS5.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F=F_{rec}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F=F_{rec}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.SSS5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The filtration <math id="S5.SS2.SSS5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{rec}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{rec}</annotation></semantics></math> defined in this way is <math id="S5.SS2.SSS5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>-opposed
to its complex conjugate and defines a Hodge Structure of weight <math id="S5.SS2.SSS5.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> on
<math id="S5.SS2.SSS5.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}_{W}E_{2}^{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mmultiscripts><mi>E</mi><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mn>2</mn><mrow></mrow><mi>W</mi><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{W}E_{2}^{pq}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.SSS5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We suppose by induction that the two direct filtrations and the
recurrent filtration coincide on <math id="S5.SS2.SSS5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}_{W}E_{s}(s\leq r+1):F_{d}=F_{rec}=F_{d^{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>E</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mi>s</mi><mrow></mrow><mi>W</mi><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{W}E_{s}(s\leq r+1):F_{d}=F_{rec}=F_{d^{*}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.SSS5.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}_{W}E_{r}=\,{{}_{W}E_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>E</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mi>r</mi><mrow></mrow><mi>W</mi><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo rspace="0.448em">=</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>E</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mn>2</mn><mrow></mrow><mi>W</mi><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{W}E_{r}=\,{{}_{W}E_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>. On <math id="S5.SS2.SSS5.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}_{W}E_{2}^{p,q}={{}_{W}E}_{r}^{p,q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>E</mi><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mn>2</mn><mrow></mrow><mi>W</mi><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo>=</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mi>W</mi><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{W}E_{2}^{p,q}={{}_{W}E}_{r}^{p,q}</annotation></semantics></math>, the filtration <math id="S5.SS2.SSS5.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{rec}:=F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo>:=</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{rec}:=F</annotation></semantics></math> is compatible with
<math id="S5.SS2.SSS5.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.SSS5.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>-opposed to its complex conjugate. Hence the morphism
<math id="S5.SS2.SSS5.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{r}:\,{{}_{W}E}_{r}^{p,q}\rightarrow\,{{}_{W}E}_{r}^{p+r,q-r+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.448em">:</mo><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mi>W</mi><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo rspace="0.448em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mi>W</mi><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{r}:\,{{}_{W}E}_{r}^{p,q}\rightarrow\,{{}_{W}E}_{r}^{p+r,q-r+1}</annotation></semantics></math>
is a morphism of a Hodge Structure of weight <math id="S5.SS2.SSS5.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> to a Hodge Structure
of weight <math id="S5.SS2.SSS5.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q-r+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q-r+1</annotation></semantics></math>
and must vanish for <math id="S5.SS2.SSS5.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>.
In particular, we deduce that the weight spectral sequence
degenerates at rank <math id="S5.SS2.SSS5.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.SSS5.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS5.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The filtration on <math id="S5.SS2.SSS5.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}_{W}E_{\infty}^{p,q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mmultiscripts><mi>E</mi><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mrow></mrow><mi>W</mi><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{W}E_{\infty}^{p,q}</annotation></semantics></math> induced by the filtration
<math id="S5.SS2.SSS5.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.SS2.SSS5.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{p+q}(K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{p+q}(K)</annotation></semantics></math> coincides with the filtration <math id="S5.SS2.SSS5.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{rec}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{rec}</annotation></semantics></math> on
<math id="S5.SS2.SSS5.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}_{W}E_{2}^{p,q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mmultiscripts><mi>E</mi><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mn>2</mn><mrow></mrow><mi>W</mi><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{W}E_{2}^{p,q}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6. </span>Logarithmic complex, normal crossing divisor and the mixed
cone</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the next two sections, we show how the previous abstract
algebraic study applies to algebraic varieties by constructing
explicitly the Cohomological Mixed Hodge Complex. First, on a smooth complex
variety <math id="S6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> containing a Normal Crossing Divisor (NCD) <math id="S6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> with
smooth irreducible components, we introduce the complex of
sheaves of differential forms with logarithmic singularities
along <math id="S6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> denoted <math id="S6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega^{*}_{X}(LogY)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega^{*}_{X}(LogY)</annotation></semantics></math> and sometimes
<math id="S6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega^{*}_{X}&lt;Y&gt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega^{*}_{X}&lt;Y&gt;</annotation></semantics></math>. Its hypercohomology is isomorphic to the cohomology of <math id="S6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X-Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X-Y</annotation></semantics></math>
with coefficients in <math id="S6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℂ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math> and it is naturally endowed with two
filtrations <math id="S6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>. It is only when <math id="S6.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is compact that the
bi-filtered complex <math id="S6.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\Omega^{*}_{X}(LogY),W,F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\Omega^{*}_{X}(LogY),W,F)</annotation></semantics></math> underlies a
Cohomological Mixed Hodge complex which defines a Mixed Hodge Structure
on the cohomology of <math id="S6.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X-Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X-Y</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The Mixed Hodge Structure of a smooth variety <math id="S6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> depends on the properties at
infinity of <math id="S6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., we have to consider a
compactification of <math id="S6.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> by a compact algebraic variety <math id="S6.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, which
is always possible by a result of Nagata <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>. Moreover, by
Hironaka’s desingularization theorem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>, we can suppose <math id="S6.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is
smooth and the complement <math id="S6.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y=X-V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y=X-V</annotation></semantics></math> is a Normal Crossing Divisor
(NCD) with smooth irreducible components. Hence we can use
<math id="S6.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\Omega^{*}_{X}(LogY),W,F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\Omega^{*}_{X}(LogY),W,F)</annotation></semantics></math> to define a Mixed Hodge Structure
on the cohomology of <math id="S6.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X-Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X-Y</annotation></semantics></math> and then carry
this Mixed Hodge Structure onto the cohomology of <math id="S6.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>. It is not
difficult to show that such a Mixed Hodge Structure does not depend on the
compactification <math id="S6.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and will be referred to as the
Mixed Hodge Structure on <math id="S6.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>. We
stress that the weight <math id="S6.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> of the cohomology <math id="S6.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{i}(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{i}(V)</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S6.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq i</annotation></semantics></math>, to be precise <math id="S6.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{i-1}=0,W_{2i}=H^{i}(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{i-1}=0,W_{2i}=H^{i}(V)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The dual of the logarithmic complex <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> is more natural to construct
and is an example of the general construction in the next section.
Similar to the basic Mayer-Vietoris construction in cohomology,
in the case of the normal crossing divisor <math id="S6.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>, we define a
resolution of the complex <math id="S6.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}_{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}_{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> by a complex
<math id="S6.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}_{Y_{{\underline{*}}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><msub><mi>Y</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mo>∗</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}_{Y_{{\underline{*}}}}</annotation></semantics></math> defined in degree <math id="S6.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j-1</annotation></semantics></math> by the constant sheaf <math id="S6.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> on the
disjoint sum <math id="S6.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{\underline{j}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{\underline{j}}</annotation></semantics></math> of the intersections of <math id="S6.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>
distinct components of <math id="S6.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>. The interest in such resolution is its
construction from the spaces <math id="S6.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{\underline{j}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{\underline{j}}</annotation></semantics></math> forming what we
call a strict simplicial resolution of <math id="S6.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>. In such case we will
develop a natural procedure to deduce a canonical Mixed
Hodge Complex on <math id="S6.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>. This construction is relatively
easy to understand in this case, hence it is the best illustration
of the construction in the following section. The weight <math id="S6.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> of the
cohomology <math id="S6.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{j}(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{j}(Y)</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S6.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq j</annotation></semantics></math>, to be precise <math id="S6.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{-1}=0,W_{j}=H^{j}(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{-1}=0,W_{j}=H^{j}(V)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We associate to the natural morphism <math id="S6.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}_{X}\to\mathbb{Z}_{Y_{\underline{*}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><msub><mi>Y</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mo>∗</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}_{X}\to\mathbb{Z}_{Y_{\underline{*}}}</annotation></semantics></math>
a complex called the cone defining the relative cohomology
isomorphic to the cohomology with compact supports of the
complement <math id="S6.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X-Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X-Y</annotation></semantics></math>, Poincaré dual to the cohomology of <math id="S6.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X-Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X-Y</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The combination of the two cases, when we consider a
sub-normal Crossing Divisor <math id="S6.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>, a union of some components of <math id="S6.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>,
and consider the complement <math id="S6.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y-Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y-Z</annotation></semantics></math>, we obtain an open
Normal Crossing Divisor which
will be a model for the general case with the most general type
of Mixed Hodge Structure on the cohomology
<math id="S6.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{j}(Y-Z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{j}(Y-Z)</annotation></semantics></math> of weights varying from <math id="S6.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>
to <math id="S6.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2j</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Finally we discuss the technique of the mixed cone which
associates a new Mixed Hodge Complex to a morphism
of Mixed Hodge Complexes at the level of the
homotopy category, which leads to a Mixed Hodge Structure on the relative cohomology
and a long exact sequence of Mixed Hodge Structures. However there exists an
ambiguity on the Mixed Hodge Structure obtained since it depends on an homotopy
between various resolutions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>. In this case the ambiguity is
related to the embedding of the rational cohomology into the
complex cohomology and equivalently related to the problem of
extension of Mixed Hodge Structure in general.</p>
</div>
<section id="S6.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.1. </span>MHS on the cohomology of smooth varieties</h3>

<div id="S6.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Now we construct the Mixed Hodge Structure on the
cohomology of a smooth complex
algebraic variety <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>.
The algebraicity of <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> allows us to use a result of Nagata <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>
to embed <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> as an open Zariski subset of a
complete variety <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the singularities of <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> are included
in <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D:=Z-V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D:=Z-V</annotation></semantics></math>. Since Hironaka’s desingularization process
in characteristic zero (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>) is carried out by blowing up smooth centers above
<math id="S6.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>,
there exists a variety <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\to Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\to Z</annotation></semantics></math> above <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> such that the inverse image
of <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is a normal crossing divisor <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> with
smooth components in <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X-Y\simeq Z-D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X-Y\simeq Z-D</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Hence we may start with the hypothesis that <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V=X^{*}:=X-Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V=X^{*}:=X-Y</annotation></semantics></math>
is the complement of a normal crossing divisor <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> in a
smooth proper variety <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>,
so the construction of the Mixed Hodge Structure is reduced to this situation,
under the condition that we prove its independence of the choice of <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce now
the logarithmic complex and prove that it underlies a
Cohomological Mixed Hodge Complex
on <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and computes the cohomology of <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S6.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">6.1.1. </span>Logarithmic complex</h4>

<div id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> be a complex manifold and <math id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> be
a Normal Crossing Divisor in <math id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. We denote
by <math id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j:X^{*}\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j:X^{*}\to X</annotation></semantics></math> the embedding of <math id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{*}:=X-Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{*}:=X-Y</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.
A meromorphic form <math id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> has a pole of order at most <math id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> along <math id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>
if at each point <math id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\omega</annotation></semantics></math> is holomorphic for some
local equation <math id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Omega^{*}_{X}(*Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega^{*}_{X}(*Y)</annotation></semantics></math> denote the
sub-complex of <math id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j_{*}\Omega_{X^{*}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j_{*}\Omega_{X^{*}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> defined by
meromorphic forms along <math id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>, holomorphic on <math id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 6.1</span></span><span id="S6.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The logarithmic de Rham complex of <math id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>
along a Normal Crossing Divisor <math id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> is the subcomplex
<math id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega_{X}^{*}(Log\,Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{X}^{*}(Log\,Y)</annotation></semantics></math> of the complex
<math id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Omega^{*}_{X}(*Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega^{*}_{X}(*Y)</annotation></semantics></math> defined by the sections <math id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> such that
<math id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\omega</annotation></semantics></math> both have a pole of order
at most <math id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> along <math id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">By definition, at each point <math id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in Y</annotation></semantics></math>, there exist local coordinates
<math id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(z_{i})_{i\in[1,n]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(z_{i})_{i\in[1,n]}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I\subset[1,n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I\subset[1,n]</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> is
defined at <math id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> by the equation <math id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi_{i\in I}z_{i}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{i\in I}z_{i}=0</annotation></semantics></math>. Then
<math id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> has logarithmic poles along <math id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if it can be
written locally as:</p>
<table id="S6.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega=\sum_{i_{1},\cdots,i_{r}}\omega_{i_{1},\cdots,i_{r}}\frac{dz_{i_{1}}}{z_{i_{1}}}\wedge\cdots\wedge\frac{dz_{i_{r}}}{z_{i_{r}}}\quad\hbox{ where }\,\;\omega_{i_{1},\cdots,i_{r}}\;\hbox{ is holomorphic.}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub></mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>∧</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></msub></mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></msub></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mtext> where </mtext><mo lspace="0.450em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> is holomorphic.</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega=\sum_{i_{1},\cdots,i_{r}}\omega_{i_{1},\cdots,i_{r}}\frac{dz_{i_{1}}}{z_{i_{1}}}\wedge\cdots\wedge\frac{dz_{i_{r}}}{z_{i_{r}}}\quad\hbox{ where }\,\;\omega_{i_{1},\cdots,i_{r}}\;\hbox{ is holomorphic.}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">This formula may be used as a definition but then we have to prove
its independence of the choice of coordinates, that is <math id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> may be
written in this form with respect to any set of local
coordinates at <math id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The <math id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{O}}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{O}}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>-module <math id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega_{X}^{1}(Log\,Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{X}^{1}(Log\,Y)</annotation></semantics></math> is locally free with
basis <math id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(dz_{i}/z_{i})_{i\in I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(dz_{i}/z_{i})_{i\in I}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(dz_{j})_{j\in[1,n]-I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(dz_{j})_{j\in[1,n]-I}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Omega_{X}^{p}(Log\,Y)\simeq\wedge^{p}\Omega_{X}^{1}(Log\,Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mi>o</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">≃</mo><msup><mo lspace="0em">∧</mo><mi>p</mi></msup><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mi>o</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{X}^{p}(Log\,Y)\simeq\wedge^{p}\Omega_{X}^{1}(Log\,Y)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:X_{1}\rightarrow X_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:X_{1}\rightarrow X_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> be a morphism of complex manifolds,
with normal crossing divisors <math id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,2</annotation></semantics></math>, such that
<math id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{-1}(Y_{2})=Y_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{-1}(Y_{2})=Y_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, the reciprocal morphism <math id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="f^{*}:f^{*}(j_{2*}\Omega_{X_{2}^{*}}^{*})\rightarrow j_{1*}\Omega_{X_{1}^{*}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}:f^{*}(j_{2*}\Omega_{X_{2}^{*}}^{*})\rightarrow j_{1*}\Omega_{X_{1}^{*}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> induces a morphism on logarithmic
complexes:</p>
<table id="S6.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}:f^{*}\Omega_{X_{2}}^{*}(Log\,Y_{2})\rightarrow\Omega_{X_{1}}^{*}(Log\,Y_{1})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}:f^{*}\Omega_{X_{2}}^{*}(Log\,Y_{2})\rightarrow\Omega_{X_{1}}^{*}(Log\,Y_{1}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">6.1.2. </span>Weight filtration <math id="S6.SS1.SSS2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math></h4>

<div id="S6.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S6.SS1.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="Y=\cup_{i\in I}Y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">∪</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></msub><mi>Y</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y=\cup_{i\in I}Y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> be the union of smooth irreducible divisors. Let <math id="S6.SS1.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{q}</annotation></semantics></math>
denotes the set of strictly increasing sequences <math id="S6.SS1.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma=(\sigma_{1},...,\sigma_{q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma=(\sigma_{1},...,\sigma_{q})</annotation></semantics></math> in the set of indices <math id="S6.SS1.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I,\,Y_{\sigma}=Y_{\sigma_{1}...\sigma_{q}}=Y_{\sigma_{1}}\cap...\cap Y_{\sigma_{q}},\,Y^{q}=\coprod_{\sigma\in S^{q}}Y_{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>∩</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Y</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∐</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mi>q</mi></msup></mrow></msub><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I,\,Y_{\sigma}=Y_{\sigma_{1}...\sigma_{q}}=Y_{\sigma_{1}}\cap...\cap Y_{\sigma_{q}},\,Y^{q}=\coprod_{\sigma\in S^{q}}Y_{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math> the
disjoint union of <math id="S6.SS1.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math>. Set <math id="S6.SS1.SSS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y^{0}=X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Y</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y^{0}=X</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.SS1.SSS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi:Y^{q}\rightarrow Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Y</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi:Y^{q}\rightarrow Y</annotation></semantics></math> the canonical projection. An increasing filtration
<math id="S6.SS1.SSS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math>, called the weight, is defined as follows:</p>
<table id="S6.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{m}(\Omega_{X}^{p}(Log\,Y))=\sum_{\sigma\in S^{m}}\Omega_{X}^{p-m}\wedge dz_{\sigma_{1}}/z_{\sigma_{1}}\wedge...\wedge dz_{\sigma_{m}}/z_{\sigma_{m}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow></munder><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{m}(\Omega_{X}^{p}(Log\,Y))=\sum_{\sigma\in S^{m}}\Omega_{X}^{p-m}\wedge dz_{\sigma_{1}}/z_{\sigma_{1}}\wedge...\wedge dz_{\sigma_{m}}/z_{\sigma_{m}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS1.SSS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The sub-<math id="S6.SS1.SSS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{O}}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{O}}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>-module <math id="S6.SS1.SSS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{m}(\Omega_{X}^{p}(Log\,Y))\subset\Omega_{X}^{p}(Log\,Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{m}(\Omega_{X}^{p}(Log\,Y))\subset\Omega_{X}^{p}(Log\,Y)</annotation></semantics></math> is the smallest sub-module stable by exterior
multiplication with local sections of <math id="S6.SS1.SSS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega_{X}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{X}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> and containing
the products <math id="S6.SS1.SSS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="dz_{i_{1}}/z_{i_{1}}\wedge...\wedge dz_{i_{k}}/z_{i_{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">dz_{i_{1}}/z_{i_{1}}\wedge...\wedge dz_{i_{k}}/z_{i_{k}}</annotation></semantics></math>
for <math id="S6.SS1.SSS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\leq m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\leq m</annotation></semantics></math> for local equations <math id="S6.SS1.SSS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> of the components of <math id="S6.SS1.SSS2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS1.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">6.1.3. </span>The Residue isomorphism</h4>

<div id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To define the Poincaré residue
along a component of an intersection of <math id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, we need to fix and
order a set of hypersurfaces <math id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{i_{1}},\ldots,Y_{i_{m}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{i_{1}},\ldots,Y_{i_{m}}</annotation></semantics></math> to
intersect. The following composed Poincaré residue is defined on
<math id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr^{W}_{m}\Omega_{X}^{*}(Log\,Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr^{W}_{m}\Omega_{X}^{*}(Log\,Y)</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S6.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Res:Gr^{W}_{m}(\Omega_{X}^{p}(Log\,Y))\rightarrow\Pi_{*}\Omega_{Y^{m}}^{p-m}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><msup><mi>Y</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Res:Gr^{W}_{m}(\Omega_{X}^{p}(Log\,Y))\rightarrow\Pi_{*}\Omega_{Y^{m}}^{p-m}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">given by <math id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="Res([\alpha\wedge(dz_{i_{1}}/z_{i_{1}}\wedge...\wedge(dz_{i_{m}}/z_{i_{m}})])=\alpha/Y_{i_{1},\ldots,i_{m}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>z</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>∧</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>z</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Res([\alpha\wedge(dz_{i_{1}}/z_{i_{1}}\wedge...\wedge(dz_{i_{m}}/z_{i_{m}})])=\alpha/Y_{i_{1},\ldots,i_{m}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In the case <math id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=1</annotation></semantics></math>, it defines:</p>
<table id="S6.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Res:\Omega_{X}^{*}(Log\,Y)\rightarrow\Pi_{*}\Omega^{*}_{Y^{1}}[-1]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><msup><mi>Y</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Res:\Omega_{X}^{*}(Log\,Y)\rightarrow\Pi_{*}\Omega^{*}_{Y^{1}}[-1]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Y</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is the disjoint union of the irreducible components
of <math id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>.
In general it is a composition map of such residue
in the one codimensional case up to a sign.
We need to prove
it is well defined, independent of the
coordinates, compatible with the differentials and
induces an isomorphism:</p>
<table id="S6.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Res:Gr_{m}^{W}(\Omega_{X}^{*}(Log\,Y))\xrightarrow{\sim}\Pi_{*}\Omega_{Y^{m}}^{*}[-m]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><msup><mi>Y</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Res:Gr_{m}^{W}(\Omega_{X}^{*}(Log\,Y))\xrightarrow{\sim}\Pi_{*}\Omega_{Y^{m}}^{*}[-m]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p2.3" class="ltx_p">We construct its inverse. Consider, for each sequence of indices
<math id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma=(i_{1},\ldots,i_{m})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma=(i_{1},\ldots,i_{m})</annotation></semantics></math>
in <math id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, the morphism <math id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rho}_{\sigma}:\Omega_{X}^{p}\rightarrow Gr_{m}^{W}(\Omega_{X}^{p+m}(Log\,Y))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rho}_{\sigma}:\Omega_{X}^{p}\rightarrow Gr_{m}^{W}(\Omega_{X}^{p+m}(Log\,Y))</annotation></semantics></math>,
defined locally as:</p>
<table id="S6.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{\sigma}(\alpha)=\alpha\wedge dz_{\sigma_{1}}/z_{\sigma_{1}}\wedge...\wedge dz_{i_{m}}/z_{i_{m}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{\sigma}(\alpha)=\alpha\wedge dz_{\sigma_{1}}/z_{\sigma_{1}}\wedge...\wedge dz_{i_{m}}/z_{i_{m}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p2.4" class="ltx_p">It does not depend on the choice of <math id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, since for another
choice of coordinates <math id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z^{\prime}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z^{\prime}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{i}/z^{\prime}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{i}/z^{\prime}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are holomorphic and the
difference <math id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(dz_{i}/z_{i})-(dz^{\prime}_{i}/z^{\prime}_{i})=d(z_{i}/z^{\prime}_{i})/(z_{i}/z^{\prime}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>/</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(dz_{i}/z_{i})-(dz^{\prime}_{i}/z^{\prime}_{i})=d(z_{i}/z^{\prime}_{i})/(z_{i}/z^{\prime}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> is holomorphic.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Then <math id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{\sigma}(\alpha)-\alpha\wedge dz^{\prime}_{i_{1}}/z^{\prime}_{i_{1}}\wedge...\wedge dz^{\prime}_{i_{m}}/z^{\prime}_{i_{m}}\in W_{m-1}\Omega_{X}^{p+m}(LogY)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>/</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>/</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{\sigma}(\alpha)-\alpha\wedge dz^{\prime}_{i_{1}}/z^{\prime}_{i_{1}}\wedge...\wedge dz^{\prime}_{i_{m}}/z^{\prime}_{i_{m}}\in W_{m-1}\Omega_{X}^{p+m}(LogY)</annotation></semantics></math>, and successively <math id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{\sigma}(\alpha)-\rho^{\prime}_{\sigma}(\alpha)\in W_{m-1}\Omega_{X}^{p+m}(Log\,Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mi>σ</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{\sigma}(\alpha)-\rho^{\prime}_{\sigma}(\alpha)\in W_{m-1}\Omega_{X}^{p+m}(Log\,Y)</annotation></semantics></math>. We have <math id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{\sigma}(z_{i_{j}}\cdot\beta)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{\sigma}(z_{i_{j}}\cdot\beta)=0</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{\sigma}(dz_{i_{j}}\wedge\beta^{\prime})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo>∧</mo><msup><mi>β</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{\sigma}(dz_{i_{j}}\wedge\beta^{\prime})=0</annotation></semantics></math>
for sections <math id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega_{X}^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{X}^{p}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>β</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> of
<math id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega_{X}^{p-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{X}^{p-1}</annotation></semantics></math>; hence <math id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math> factors by
<math id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\rho}_{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>σ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\rho}_{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi_{*}\Omega_{Y_{\sigma}}^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mi>p</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{*}\Omega_{Y_{\sigma}}^{p}</annotation></semantics></math>
defined locally and glue globally into
a morphism of complexes on <math id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S6.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\rho}_{\sigma}:\Pi_{*}\Omega_{Y_{\sigma}}^{p}\rightarrow Gr_{m}^{W}(\Omega_{X}^{p+m}(Log\,Y)),\quad\overline{\rho}:\Pi_{*}\Omega_{Y^{m}}^{*}[-m]\rightarrow Gr_{m}^{W}\Omega_{X}^{*}(Log\,Y)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mi>p</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><msup><mi>Y</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\rho}_{\sigma}:\Pi_{*}\Omega_{Y_{\sigma}}^{p}\rightarrow Gr_{m}^{W}(\Omega_{X}^{p+m}(Log\,Y)),\quad\overline{\rho}:\Pi_{*}\Omega_{Y^{m}}^{*}[-m]\rightarrow Gr_{m}^{W}\Omega_{X}^{*}(Log\,Y).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 6.2</span></span><span id="S6.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We have the following isomorphisms of sheaves:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i) <math id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{i}(Gr_{m}^{W}\Omega_{X}^{*}(Log\,Y))\simeq\Pi_{*}\hbox{\bf C}_{Y^{m}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">C</mtext><msup><mi>Y</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{i}(Gr_{m}^{W}\Omega_{X}^{*}(Log\,Y))\simeq\Pi_{*}\hbox{\bf C}_{Y^{m}}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=m</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\neq m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\neq m</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">ii) <math id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{i}(W_{r}\Omega_{X}^{*}(Log\,Y))\simeq\Pi_{*}\hbox{\bf C}_{Y^{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">C</mtext><msup><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{i}(W_{r}\Omega_{X}^{*}(Log\,Y))\simeq\Pi_{*}\hbox{\bf C}_{Y^{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>
for <math id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\leq r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\leq r</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{i}(W_{r}\Omega_{X}^{*}(Log\,Y))=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{i}(W_{r}\Omega_{X}^{*}(Log\,Y))=0</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&gt;r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&gt;r</annotation></semantics></math>, 
<br class="ltx_break">and in particular <math id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{i}(\Omega_{X}^{*}(Log\,Y))\simeq\Pi_{*}{\mathbb{C}}_{Y^{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℂ</mi><msup><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{i}(\Omega_{X}^{*}(Log\,Y))\simeq\Pi_{*}{\mathbb{C}}_{Y^{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.SSS3.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">The statement in i) follows from the residue
isomorphism.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">The statement in ii) follows easily by induction
on <math id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>, from i) and the long exact sequence associated to the short exact
sequence <math id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\rightarrow W_{r}\rightarrow W_{r+1}\rightarrow Gr_{r+1}^{W}\rightarrow 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\rightarrow W_{r}\rightarrow W_{r+1}\rightarrow Gr_{r+1}^{W}\rightarrow 0</annotation></semantics></math>, written as</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{i}(W_{r})\rightarrow H^{i}(W_{r+1})\rightarrow H^{i}(Gr_{r+1}^{W})\rightarrow H^{i+1}(W_{r})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{i}(W_{r})\rightarrow H^{i}(W_{r+1})\rightarrow H^{i}(Gr_{r+1}^{W})\rightarrow H^{i+1}(W_{r})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p5.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text">∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 6.3</span></span><span id="S6.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Weight filtration <math id="S6.Thmtheorem3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math>)<span id="S6.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The morphisms of filtered complexes:</p>
<table id="S6.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\Omega_{X}^{*}(Log\,Y),W)\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\leftarrow}\limits^{\alpha}}(\Omega_{X}^{*}(Log\,Y),\tau)\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\rightarrow}\limits^{\beta}}(j_{*}\Omega_{X^{*}}^{*},\tau)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>α</mi></msup></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>β</mi></msup></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\Omega_{X}^{*}(Log\,Y),W)\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\leftarrow}\limits^{\alpha}}(\Omega_{X}^{*}(Log\,Y),\tau)\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\rightarrow}\limits^{\beta}}(j_{*}\Omega_{X^{*}}^{*},\tau)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">are filtered quasi-isomorphisms.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.SSS3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">The quasi-isomorphism <math id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> follows from the
lemma.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">The morphism <math id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> is Stein, since for each polydisc <math id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U(y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U(y)</annotation></semantics></math> in
<math id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> centered at a point <math id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in Y</annotation></semantics></math>, the inverse image <math id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{*}\cap U(y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{*}\cap U(y)</annotation></semantics></math> is Stein as the complement of an hypersurface, hence <math id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>
is acyclic for coherent sheaves, that is <math id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Rj_{*}\Omega_{X^{*}}^{*}\simeq j_{*}\Omega_{X^{*}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Rj_{*}\Omega_{X^{*}}^{*}\simeq j_{*}\Omega_{X^{*}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>. By Poincaré lemma <math id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\mathbb{C}}_{X^{*}}\simeq\Omega_{X^{*}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mo>≃</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\mathbb{C}}_{X^{*}}\simeq\Omega_{X^{*}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, so that <math id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Rj_{*}\underline{\mathbb{C}}_{X^{*}}\simeq j_{*}\Omega_{X^{*}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Rj_{*}\underline{\mathbb{C}}_{X^{*}}\simeq j_{*}\Omega_{X^{*}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>,
hence it is enough to prove <math id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr^{\tau}_{i}Rj_{*}{\mathbb{C}}_{X^{*}}\simeq\Pi_{*}{\mathbb{C}}_{Y^{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>τ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℂ</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℂ</mi><msup><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr^{\tau}_{i}Rj_{*}{\mathbb{C}}_{X^{*}}\simeq\Pi_{*}{\mathbb{C}}_{Y^{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is a local statement.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">For each polydisc <math id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U(y)=U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U(y)=U</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{*}=U-U\cap Y=(D^{*})^{m}\times D^{n-m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>U</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>m</mi></msup><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{*}=U-U\cap Y=(D^{*})^{m}\times D^{n-m}</annotation></semantics></math>,
the cohomology <math id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{H}^{i}(U,Rj_{*}\underline{\mathbb{C}}_{X^{*}})=H^{i}(U^{*},\mathbb{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ℍ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>U</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{H}^{i}(U,Rj_{*}\underline{\mathbb{C}}_{X^{*}})=H^{i}(U^{*},\mathbb{C})</annotation></semantics></math> can be
computed by
Künneth formula and is equal to <math id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p8.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\wedge^{i}H^{1}(U^{*},\mathbb{C})\simeq\wedge^{i}\Gamma(U,\Omega_{U}^{1}(Log\,Y)))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mo>∧</mo><mi>i</mi></msup><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>U</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">≃</mo><msup><mo lspace="0em">∧</mo><mi>i</mi></msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mi>o</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\wedge^{i}H^{1}(U^{*},\mathbb{C})\simeq\wedge^{i}\Gamma(U,\Omega_{U}^{1}(Log\,Y)))</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="dz_{i}/z_{i},i\in[1,m]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">dz_{i}/z_{i},i\in[1,m]</annotation></semantics></math> form a basis dual to the homology basis since <math id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(D^{*})^{m}\times D^{n-m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>m</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(D^{*})^{m}\times D^{n-m}</annotation></semantics></math>
is homotopic to an <math id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i-</annotation></semantics></math>dimensional torus <math id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(S^{1})^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>m</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(S^{1})^{m}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 6.4</span></span><span id="S6.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The weight filtration is rationally defined.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p9.1" class="ltx_p">The main point here is that the <math id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> filtration is defined with
rational coefficients as <math id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Rj_{*}\underline{\mathbb{Q}}_{X^{*}},\tau)\otimes\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Rj_{*}\underline{\mathbb{Q}}_{X^{*}},\tau)\otimes\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, which
gives the rational definition for <math id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS1.SSS4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">6.1.4. </span>Hodge filtration <math id="S6.SS1.SSS4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math></h4>

<div id="S6.SS1.SSS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.SSS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">It is defined
by the formula
<math id="S6.SS1.SSS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{p}=\Omega_{X}^{*\geq p}(Log\,Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{p}=\Omega_{X}^{*\geq p}(Log\,Y)</annotation></semantics></math>, which
includes all forms of type <math id="S6.SS1.SSS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p^{\prime},q^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p^{\prime},q^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S6.SS1.SSS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{\prime}\geq p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>≥</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{\prime}\geq p</annotation></semantics></math>. We have:</p>
<table id="S6.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Res:F^{p}(Gr_{m}^{W}\Omega_{X}^{*}(Log\,Y))\simeq\Pi_{*}F^{p-m}\Omega_{Y^{m}}^{*}[-m]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><msup><mi>Y</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Res:F^{p}(Gr_{m}^{W}\Omega_{X}^{*}(Log\,Y))\simeq\Pi_{*}F^{p-m}\Omega_{Y^{m}}^{*}[-m]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS1.SSS4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">hence a filtered isomorphism:</p>
<table id="S6.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Res:(Gr_{m}^{W}\Omega_{X}^{*}(Log\,Y),F)\simeq(\Pi_{*}\Omega_{Y^{m}}^{*}[-m],F[-m])." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><msup><mi>Y</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Res:(Gr_{m}^{W}\Omega_{X}^{*}(Log\,Y),F)\simeq(\Pi_{*}\Omega_{Y^{m}}^{*}[-m],F[-m]).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 6.5</span></span><span id="S6.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The system <span id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_bold">K</span>:</p>
<ol id="S6.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S6.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S6.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\hbox{\bf K}^{\mathbb{Q}},W)=(Rj_{*}\underline{\mathbb{Q}}_{X^{*}},\tau)\in ObD^{+}F(X,\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">K</mtext><mi>ℚ</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\hbox{\bf K}^{\mathbb{Q}},W)=(Rj_{*}\underline{\mathbb{Q}}_{X^{*}},\tau)\in ObD^{+}F(X,\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S6.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\hbox{\bf K}^{\mathbb{C}},W,F)=(\Omega_{X}^{*}(Log\,Y),W,F)\in ObD^{+}F_{2}(X,\mathbb{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">K</mtext><mi>ℂ</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\hbox{\bf K}^{\mathbb{C}},W,F)=(\Omega_{X}^{*}(Log\,Y),W,F)\in ObD^{+}F_{2}(X,\mathbb{C})</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The isomorphism <math id="S6.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\hbox{\bf K}^{\mathbb{Q}},W)\otimes\mathbb{C}\simeq(\hbox{\bf K}^{\mathbb{C}},W)\,\,{\rm in}\,\,D^{+}F(X,\mathbb{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">K</mtext><mi>ℚ</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">K</mtext><mi>ℂ</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.340em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>in</mi><mo lspace="0.340em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\hbox{\bf K}^{\mathbb{Q}},W)\otimes\mathbb{C}\simeq(\hbox{\bf K}^{\mathbb{C}},W)\,\,{\rm in}\,\,D^{+}F(X,\mathbb{C})</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p1.2" class="ltx_p">is a Cohomological Mixed Hodge Complex on <math id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 6.6</span></span><span id="S6.Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Deligne)<span id="S6.Thmtheorem6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The system <math id="S6.Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=R\Gamma(X,\hbox{\bf K})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">K</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=R\Gamma(X,\hbox{\bf K})</annotation></semantics></math> is a
Mixed Hodge Complex. It endows the cohomology
of <math id="S6.Thmtheorem6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{*}=X-Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{*}=X-Y</annotation></semantics></math> with a canonical Mixed Hodge Structure.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.SSS4.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S6.SS1.SSS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.SSS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS1.SSS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">The result follows directly from the general theory of
Cohomological Mixed Hodge Complex.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.SSS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.SSS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS1.SSS4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Nevertheless, it is interesting to understand what is needed for a
direct proof and compute the weight spectral sequence at rank
<math id="S6.SS1.SSS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}&{}_{W}E_{1}^{pq}(R\Gamma(X,\Omega_{X}^{*}(Log\,Y))=\mathbb{H}^{p+q}(X,Gr_{-p}^{W}\Omega_{X}^{*}(LogY))\simeq\mathbb{H}^{p+q}(X,\Pi_{*}\Omega_{Y^{-p}}^{*}[p])\\
&amp;\simeq H^{2p+q}(Y^{-p},\mathbb{C})\Rightarrow Gr_{q}^{W}H^{p+q}(X^{*},\mathbb{C}).\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>E</mi><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mn>1</mn><mrow></mrow><mi>W</mi><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.340em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℍ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℍ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><msup><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⇒</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>q</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}&amp;{}_{W}E_{1}^{pq}(R\Gamma(X,\Omega_{X}^{*}(Log\,Y))=\mathbb{H}^{p+q}(X,Gr_{-p}^{W}\Omega_{X}^{*}(LogY))\simeq\mathbb{H}^{p+q}(X,\Pi_{*}\Omega_{Y^{-p}}^{*}[p])\\
&amp;\simeq H^{2p+q}(Y^{-p},\mathbb{C})\Rightarrow Gr_{q}^{W}H^{p+q}(X^{*},\mathbb{C}).\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS1.SSS4.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS1.SSS4.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text">where the double arrow means that the spectral sequence
degenerates to the cohomology graded with respect to the filtration <math id="S6.SS1.SSS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> induced
by the weight on the complex level.
In fact, we recall the proof up to rank <math id="S6.SS1.SSS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>.
The differential <math id="S6.SS1.SSS4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{1}=\sum_{j=1}^{-p}(-1)^{j+1}G(\lambda_{j,-p})=G:H^{2p+q}(Y^{-p},\mathbb{C})\longrightarrow H^{2p+q+2}(Y^{-p-1},\mathbb{C})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></munderover><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{1}=\sum_{j=1}^{-p}(-1)^{j+1}G(\lambda_{j,-p})=G:H^{2p+q}(Y^{-p},\mathbb{C})\longrightarrow H^{2p+q+2}(Y^{-p-1},\mathbb{C})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS1.SSS4.p3.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS1.SSS4.p3.3.1" class="ltx_text">is equal to an alternate
Gysin morphism,
Poincaré dual to the alternate
restriction morphism:</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho=\sum_{j=1}^{-p}(-1)^{j+1}\lambda_{j,-p}^{*}:H^{2n-q}(Y^{-p-1},\mathbb{C})\rightarrow H^{2n-q}(Y^{-p},\mathbb{C})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></munderover><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho=\sum_{j=1}^{-p}(-1)^{j+1}\lambda_{j,-p}^{*}:H^{2n-q}(Y^{-p-1},\mathbb{C})\rightarrow H^{2n-q}(Y^{-p},\mathbb{C})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS1.SSS4.p3.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS1.SSS4.p3.4.1" class="ltx_text">hence the first term:</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(_{W}E_{1}^{pq},d_{1})_{p\in\mathbb{Z}}=(H^{2p+q}(Y^{-p},\mathbb{C}),d_{1})_{p\in\mathbb{Z}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(_{W}E_{1}^{pq},d_{1})_{p\in\mathbb{Z}}=(H^{2p+q}(Y^{-p},\mathbb{C}),d_{1})_{p\in\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS1.SSS4.p3.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS1.SSS4.p3.5.1" class="ltx_text">is viewed as a complex in the category of Hodge Structures of weight <math id="S6.SS1.SSS4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>. It follows that
the terms:</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{W}E_{2}^{pq}=H^{p}(_{W}E_{1}^{*,q},d_{1})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>E</mi><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mi>W</mi><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mrow><msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{W}E_{2}^{pq}=H^{p}(_{W}E_{1}^{*,q},d_{1})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS1.SSS4.p3.6" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS1.SSS4.p3.6.1" class="ltx_text">are endowed with a
Hodge Structure of weight <math id="S6.SS1.SSS4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.SSS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.SSS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS1.SSS4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">We need to prove that the differential
<math id="S6.SS1.SSS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is compatible with the induced Hodge filtration.
For this we introduced the
direct filtrations compatible with <math id="S6.SS1.SSS4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and proved that they coincide
with the induced Hodge filtration. The differential <math id="S6.SS1.SSS4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is necessarily
zero since it is a morphism of Hodge Structure of different weights:
the Hodge Structure of weight <math id="S6.SS1.SSS4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S6.SS1.SSS4.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{2}^{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{2}^{pq}</annotation></semantics></math> and
the Hodge Structure of weight <math id="S6.SS1.SSS4.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q-1</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S6.SS1.SSS4.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{2}^{p+2,q-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mn>2</mn><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{2}^{p+2,q-1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The proof is the same as any Mixed Hodge Complex and consists
of a recurrent argument to show in this way that the
differentials <math id="S6.SS1.SSS4.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S6.SS1.SSS4.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> are zero.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS1.SSS5" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">6.1.5. </span>Independence of the compactification and functoriality</h4>

<div id="S6.SS1.SSS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.SSS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S6.SS1.SSS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> be a complex smooth variety, <math id="S6.SS1.SSS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> (resp. <math id="S6.SS1.SSS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>) a
compactification of <math id="S6.SS1.SSS5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> by a Normal Crossing Divisor <math id="S6.SS1.SSS5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> (resp. <math id="S6.SS1.SSS5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>) at infinity, <math id="S6.SS1.SSS5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j:U\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j:U\to X</annotation></semantics></math> (resp. <math id="S6.SS1.SSS5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j^{\prime}:U\to X^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j^{\prime}:U\to X^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>) the open embedding; then <math id="S6.SS1.SSS5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\times j^{\prime}:U\to X\times X^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\times j^{\prime}:U\to X\times X^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is a locally closed embedding, with closure
<math id="S6.SS1.SSS5.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>. By desingularizing <math id="S6.SS1.SSS5.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> outside the image of <math id="S6.SS1.SSS5.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>, we are
reduced to the case where we have a smooth variety <math id="S6.SS1.SSS5.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\prime\prime}\xrightarrow{f}X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑓</mo></mover><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\prime\prime}\xrightarrow{f}X</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S6.SS1.SSS5.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y^{\prime\prime}:=f^{-1}(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y^{\prime\prime}:=f^{-1}(Y)</annotation></semantics></math> is a Normal Crossing Divisor and <math id="S6.SS1.SSS5.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\simeq X^{\prime\prime}-Y^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\simeq X^{\prime\prime}-Y^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, then we have an induced morphism <math id="S6.SS1.SSS5.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> on the
corresponding logarithmic complexes, compatible with the structure
of Mixed Hodge Complex. It follows that the induced morphism <math id="S6.SS1.SSS5.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> on
hypercohomology is compatible with the Mixed Hodge Structure
and is an isomorphism
on the hypercohomology
groups, hence it is an isomorphism of Mixed Hodge Structure.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.SSS5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS1.SSS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS1.SSS5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Functoriality.</span> Let <math id="S6.SS1.SSS5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:U\to V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:U\to V</annotation></semantics></math> be a morphism of smooth
varieties, <math id="S6.SS1.SSS5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> (resp. <math id="S6.SS1.SSS5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>) smooth compactifications of <math id="S6.SS1.SSS5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> (resp.
<math id="S6.SS1.SSS5.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>) by Normal Crossing Divisor at infinity, then taking the closure of the graph of
<math id="S6.SS1.SSS5.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S6.SS1.SSS5.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\times Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\times Z</annotation></semantics></math> and desingularizing, we are reduced to the
case where there exists a compactification <math id="S6.SS1.SSS5.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> with an extension
<math id="S6.SS1.SSS5.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{f}:X\to Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{f}:X\to Z</annotation></semantics></math> inducing <math id="S6.SS1.SSS5.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S6.SS1.SSS5.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>. The induced morphism
<math id="S6.SS1.SSS5.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{f}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{f}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> on the corresponding logarithmic
complexes is compatible with the filtrations <math id="S6.SS1.SSS5.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.SS1.SSS5.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> and with
the structure of Mixed Hodge Complex, hence it is compatible with
the Mixed Hodge Structure on hypercohomology.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 6.7</span></span><span id="S6.Thmtheorem7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S6.Thmtheorem7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> be a smooth complex algebraic variety.
<br class="ltx_break">i) The Hodge numbers <math id="S6.Thmtheorem7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{p,q}:=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo>:=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{p,q}:=</annotation></semantics></math> dim.<math id="S6.Thmtheorem7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{p,q}(Gr^{W}_{p+q}H^{i}(U))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{p,q}(Gr^{W}_{p+q}H^{i}(U))</annotation></semantics></math>
vanish for <math id="S6.Thmtheorem7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p,q\notin[0,i]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>∉</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p,q\notin[0,i]</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, the weight of the
cohomology <math id="S6.Thmtheorem7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{i}(U)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{i}(U)</annotation></semantics></math> vary from <math id="S6.Thmtheorem7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S6.Thmtheorem7.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2i</annotation></semantics></math>.
<br class="ltx_break">ii) let <math id="S6.Thmtheorem7.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> be a smooth compactification of <math id="S6.Thmtheorem7.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>, then:</p>
<table id="S6.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{i}H^{i}(U)=\hbox{Im }(H^{i}(X)\to H^{i}(U))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>Im </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{i}H^{i}(U)=\hbox{Im }(H^{i}(X)\to H^{i}(U)).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.SSS5.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S6.SS1.SSS5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.SSS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS1.SSS5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">i) The space <math id="S6.SS1.SSS5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr^{W}_{r}H^{i}(U)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr^{W}_{r}H^{i}(U)</annotation></semantics></math> is
isomorphic to the term
<math id="S6.SS1.SSS5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{2}^{i-r,r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mn>2</mn><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{2}^{i-r,r}</annotation></semantics></math> of the spectral sequence with a Hodge Structure of
weight <math id="S6.SS1.SSS5.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>, hence it is a sub-quotient of <math id="S6.SS1.SSS5.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{1}^{i-r,r}=H^{2i-r}(Y^{r-i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{1}^{i-r,r}=H^{2i-r}(Y^{r-i})</annotation></semantics></math> twisted by <math id="S6.SS1.SSS5.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}(i-r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}(i-r)</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence, we have
<math id="S6.SS1.SSS5.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{p,q}(H^{2i-r}(Y^{r-i}))=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{p,q}(H^{2i-r}(Y^{r-i}))=0</annotation></semantics></math>, for <math id="S6.SS1.SSS5.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p+q=r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p+q=r</annotation></semantics></math>, unless <math id="S6.SS1.SSS5.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r-i\geq 0,2i-r\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r-i\geq 0,2i-r\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> ( i.e <math id="S6.SS1.SSS5.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\leq r\leq 2i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\leq r\leq 2i</annotation></semantics></math> ) and <math id="S6.SS1.SSS5.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\in[0,2r-i]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\in[0,2r-i]</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S6.SS1.SSS5.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{p,q}(E_{1}^{i-r,r})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{p,q}(E_{1}^{i-r,r})=0</annotation></semantics></math> unless <math id="S6.SS1.SSS5.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\in[i-r,r]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\in[i-r,r]</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.SSS5.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS1.SSS5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">ii) Suppose <math id="S6.SS1.SSS5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> is the complement of a Normal Crossing
Divisor. Denote <math id="S6.SS1.SSS5.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j:U\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j:U\to X</annotation></semantics></math> the inclusion. By definition
<math id="S6.SS1.SSS5.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{i}H^{i}(U)=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{i}H^{i}(U)=</annotation></semantics></math> Im (<math id="S6.SS1.SSS5.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{H}^{i}(X,\tau_{\leq 0}Rj_{*}\mathbb{Q}_{U})\to H^{i}(U,\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ℍ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>U</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{H}^{i}(X,\tau_{\leq 0}Rj_{*}\mathbb{Q}_{U})\to H^{i}(U,\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math>), hence it is equal to the image
of <math id="S6.SS1.SSS5.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{i}(X,\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{i}(X,\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> since <math id="S6.SS1.SSS5.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> is quasi-isomorphic to <math id="S6.SS1.SSS5.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{\leq 0}Rj_{*}\mathbb{Q}_{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>U</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{\leq 0}Rj_{*}\mathbb{Q}_{U}</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S6.SS1.SSS5.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X-U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X-U</annotation></semantics></math> is not a Normal Crossing Divisor, there
exists a desingularization <math id="S6.SS1.SSS5.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi:X^{\prime}\rightarrow X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:X^{\prime}\rightarrow X</annotation></semantics></math> with an
embedding of <math id="S6.SS1.SSS5.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S6.SS1.SSS5.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> as the complement of a Normal Crossing
Divisor, then we use the trace map <math id="S6.SS1.SSS5.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Tr\,\pi:H^{i}(X^{\prime},\mathbb{Q})\to H^{i}(X,\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Tr\,\pi:H^{i}(X^{\prime},\mathbb{Q})\to H^{i}(X,\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying <math id="S6.SS1.SSS5.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Tr\pi)\circ\pi^{*}=Id" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Tr\pi)\circ\pi^{*}=Id</annotation></semantics></math> and compatible
with Hodge Structures. In fact the trace map is defined as a morphism of sheaves
<math id="S6.SS1.SSS5.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\pi_{*}\mathbb{Q}_{X^{\prime}}\to\mathbb{Q}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\pi_{*}\mathbb{Q}_{X^{\prime}}\to\mathbb{Q}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite>, hence commutes with the
restriction to <math id="S6.SS1.SSS5.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, the images of both cohomology
groups coincide in <math id="S6.SS1.SSS5.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{i}(U)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{i}(U)</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_xca">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Exercise 6.8</span></span><span id="S6.Thmtheorem8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Riemann Surface)<span id="S6.Thmtheorem8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S6.Thmtheorem8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{C}</annotation></semantics></math> be a connected compact Riemann surface of genus
<math id="S6.Thmtheorem8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.Thmtheorem8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y=\{x_{1},\ldots,x_{m}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y=\{x_{1},\ldots,x_{m}\}</annotation></semantics></math> a subset of <math id="S6.Thmtheorem8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> points, and <math id="S6.Thmtheorem8.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C=\overline{C}-Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C=\overline{C}-Y</annotation></semantics></math> the open surface with <math id="S6.Thmtheorem8.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> points in
<math id="S6.Thmtheorem8.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{C}</annotation></semantics></math> deleted.
Consider the long exact sequence:</p>
<table id="S6.Ex19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="0\rightarrow H^{1}(\overline{C},\mathbb{Z})\rightarrow H^{1}({C},\mathbb{Z})\rightarrow H^{2}_{Y}(\overline{C},\mathbb{Z})=\oplus_{i=1}^{i=m}\mathbb{Z}\rightarrow H^{2}(\overline{C},\mathbb{Z})\simeq\mathbb{Z}\rightarrow H^{2}(C,\mathbb{Z})=0" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>Y</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><msubsup><mo lspace="0em">⊕</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msubsup><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\rightarrow H^{1}(\overline{C},\mathbb{Z})\rightarrow H^{1}({C},\mathbb{Z})\rightarrow H^{2}_{Y}(\overline{C},\mathbb{Z})=\oplus_{i=1}^{i=m}\mathbb{Z}\rightarrow H^{2}(\overline{C},\mathbb{Z})\simeq\mathbb{Z}\rightarrow H^{2}(C,\mathbb{Z})=0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem8.p1.2" class="ltx_p">then:</p>
<table id="S6.Ex20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="0\rightarrow H^{1}(\overline{C},\mathbb{Z})\rightarrow H^{1}({C},\mathbb{Z})\rightarrow\mathbb{Z}^{m-1}\simeq Ker(\oplus_{i=1}^{i=m}\mathbb{Z}\to\mathbb{Z})\to 0" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>≃</mo><mi>K</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mo lspace="0em">⊕</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msubsup><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\rightarrow H^{1}(\overline{C},\mathbb{Z})\rightarrow H^{1}({C},\mathbb{Z})\rightarrow\mathbb{Z}^{m-1}\simeq Ker(\oplus_{i=1}^{i=m}\mathbb{Z}\to\mathbb{Z})\to 0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem8.p1.3" class="ltx_p">is a short exact sequence of Mixed Hodge Structures where <math id="S6.Thmtheorem8.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{1}({C},\mathbb{Z})=W_{2}H^{1}({C},\mathbb{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{1}({C},\mathbb{Z})=W_{2}H^{1}({C},\mathbb{Z})</annotation></semantics></math> is an extension of two different weights:
<math id="S6.Thmtheorem8.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{1}H^{1}({C},\mathbb{Z})=H^{1}(\overline{C},\mathbb{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{1}H^{1}({C},\mathbb{Z})=H^{1}(\overline{C},\mathbb{Z})</annotation></semantics></math> of rank <math id="S6.Thmtheorem8.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.Thmtheorem8.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr^{W}_{2}H^{1}({C},\mathbb{Z})\simeq\mathbb{Z}^{m-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr^{W}_{2}H^{1}({C},\mathbb{Z})\simeq\mathbb{Z}^{m-1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem8.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem8.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The Hodge filtration is given by <math id="S6.Thmtheorem8.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{0}H^{1}(C,\mathbb{C})=H^{1}(C,\mathbb{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{0}H^{1}(C,\mathbb{C})=H^{1}(C,\mathbb{C})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.Thmtheorem8.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{2}H^{1}(C,\mathbb{C})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{2}H^{1}(C,\mathbb{C})=0</annotation></semantics></math>, while:</p>
<table id="S6.Ex21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="F^{1}H^{1}(C,\mathbb{C})\simeq\mathbb{H}^{1}(\overline{C},(0\to\Omega^{1}_{\overline{C}}(Log\{x_{1},\ldots,x_{m}\})\simeq H^{0}(\overline{C},\Omega^{1}_{\overline{C}}(Log\{x_{1},\ldots,x_{m}\})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≃</mo><msup><mi>ℍ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mi>o</mi><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≃</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mi>o</mi><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{1}H^{1}(C,\mathbb{C})\simeq\mathbb{H}^{1}(\overline{C},(0\to\Omega^{1}_{\overline{C}}(Log\{x_{1},\ldots,x_{m}\})\simeq H^{0}(\overline{C},\Omega^{1}_{\overline{C}}(Log\{x_{1},\ldots,x_{m}\})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem8.p2.2" class="ltx_p">is of rank <math id="S6.Thmtheorem8.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g+m-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g+m-1</annotation></semantics></math> and fits into the exact sequence determined by the
residue morphism:</p>
<table id="S6.Ex22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\to\Omega^{1}_{\overline{C}}\to\Omega^{1}_{\overline{C}}(Log\{x_{1},\ldots,x_{m}\})\to\mathcal{O}_{\{x_{1},\ldots,x_{m}\}}\to 0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\to\Omega^{1}_{\overline{C}}\to\Omega^{1}_{\overline{C}}(Log\{x_{1},\ldots,x_{m}\})\to\mathcal{O}_{\{x_{1},\ldots,x_{m}\}}\to 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_xca">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Exercise 6.9</span></span><span id="S6.Thmtheorem9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Hypersurfaces)<span id="S6.Thmtheorem9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i:Y\hookrightarrow P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i:Y\hookrightarrow P</annotation></semantics></math> be a smooth
hypersurface in a projective variety <math id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>.
<br class="ltx_break">1) To describe the cohomology
of the affine open set <math id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U=P-Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U=P-Y</annotation></semantics></math> we may use, by Grothendieck’s
result on algebraic de Rham cohomology, algebraic forms on <math id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>
regular on <math id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> denoted <math id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p1.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Omega^{*}(U)=\Omega_{P}(*Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega^{*}(U)=\Omega_{P}(*Y)</annotation></semantics></math>, or
meromorphic forms along <math id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>, holomorphic on <math id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> denoted
<math id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p1.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Omega_{P^{an}}(*Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{P^{an}}(*Y)</annotation></semantics></math>, where the Hodge filtration is described by
the order of the pole (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> Prop. 3.1.11) (but not the
trivial filtration <math id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega^{*}(U)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega^{*}(U)</annotation></semantics></math> and by Deligne’s resulton
the
logarithmic complex with its trivial filtration <math id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>.
<br class="ltx_break">2) For example, in the case of a curve <math id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> in a plane <math id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>,
global holomorphic forms are all rational by Serre’s result on
cohomology of coherent sheaves. We have an exact sequence of
sheaves:</p>
<table id="S6.Ex23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\rightarrow\Omega^{2}_{P}\rightarrow i_{*}\Omega^{2}_{P}(LogY)\rightarrow\Omega^{1}_{Y}\rightarrow 0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>Y</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\rightarrow\Omega^{2}_{P}\rightarrow i_{*}\Omega^{2}_{P}(LogY)\rightarrow\Omega^{1}_{Y}\rightarrow 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Since <math id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{2,0}=h^{2,1}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{2,0}=h^{2,1}=0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{0}(P,\Omega^{2}_{P})=H^{1}(P,\Omega^{2}_{P})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{0}(P,\Omega^{2}_{P})=H^{1}(P,\Omega^{2}_{P})=0</annotation></semantics></math>, hence we deduce from the associated long
exact sequence, the isomorphism:</p>
<table id="S6.Ex24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Res:H^{0}(P,\Omega^{2}_{P}(LogY))\xrightarrow{\sim}H^{0}(Y,\Omega^{1}_{Y})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo></mover><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>Y</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Res:H^{0}(P,\Omega^{2}_{P}(LogY))\xrightarrow{\sim}H^{0}(Y,\Omega^{1}_{Y}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p1.3" class="ltx_p">that is <math id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-forms on <math id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> are residues
of rational <math id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2-</annotation></semantics></math>forms on <math id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> with simple pole along the curve.
<br class="ltx_break">In homogeneous coordinates, let <math id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F=0</annotation></semantics></math> be the homogeneous equation of
<math id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>. We take the residue along <math id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>
of the rational form:</p>
<table id="S6.Ex25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{A(z_{0}dz_{1}\wedge dz_{2}-z_{1}dz_{0}\wedge dz_{2}+z_{2}dz_{0}\wedge dz_{1})}{F}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>F</mi></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{A(z_{0}dz_{1}\wedge dz_{2}-z_{1}dz_{0}\wedge dz_{2}+z_{2}dz_{0}\wedge dz_{1})}{F}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p1.4" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is homogeneous of degree <math id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d-3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d-3</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> has
degree <math id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> example 3.2.8).
<br class="ltx_break">3) For <math id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p1.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> projective again, we consider the exact sequence for
relative cohomology (or cohomology with support in <math id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p1.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>):</p>
<table id="S6.Ex26" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k-1}(U)\xrightarrow{\partial}H_{Y}^{k}(P)\rightarrow H^{k}(P)\xrightarrow{j^{*}}H^{k}(U)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">∂</mo></mover><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>Y</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.700em">j</mi><mo mathsize="0.710em">∗</mo></msup></mover><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k-1}(U)\xrightarrow{\partial}H_{Y}^{k}(P)\rightarrow H^{k}(P)\xrightarrow{j^{*}}H^{k}(U)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p1.5" class="ltx_p">which reduces via Thom’s isomorphism, to:</p>
<table id="S6.Ex27" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k-1}(U)\xrightarrow{r}H^{k-2}(Y)\xrightarrow{i_{*}}H^{k}(P)\xrightarrow{j^{*}}H^{k}(U)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑟</mo></mover><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">i</mi><mo mathsize="0.710em">∗</mo></msub></mover><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.700em">j</mi><mo mathsize="0.710em">∗</mo></msup></mover><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k-1}(U)\xrightarrow{r}H^{k-2}(Y)\xrightarrow{i_{*}}H^{k}(P)\xrightarrow{j^{*}}H^{k}(U)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p1.6" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p1.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> is the topological Leray’s residue map dual to the tube
over cycle map <math id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p1.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau:H_{k-2}(Y)\rightarrow H_{k-1}(U)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau:H_{k-2}(Y)\rightarrow H_{k-1}(U)</annotation></semantics></math> associating to a cycle <math id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p1.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>
the boundary in <math id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p1.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> of a tube over <math id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p1.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>,
and <math id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p1.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is Gysin map, Poincaré dual to
the map <math id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p1.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> in cohomology.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P=\mathbb{P}^{n+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P=\mathbb{P}^{n+1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> odd, the map <math id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> is an isomorphism:</p>
<table id="S6.Ex28" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{n-1}(Y)\simeq H^{n+1}(P)\to H^{n+1}(U)\xrightarrow{r}H^{n}(Y)\xrightarrow{i_{*}}H^{n+2}(P)=0\xrightarrow{j^{*}}H^{n+2}(U)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑟</mo></mover><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">i</mi><mo mathsize="0.710em">∗</mo></msub></mover><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.700em">j</mi><mo mathsize="0.710em">∗</mo></msup></mover><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{n-1}(Y)\simeq H^{n+1}(P)\to H^{n+1}(U)\xrightarrow{r}H^{n}(Y)\xrightarrow{i_{*}}H^{n+2}(P)=0\xrightarrow{j^{*}}H^{n+2}(U)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p2.2" class="ltx_p">and for <math id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> even the map <math id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> is injective:</p>
<table id="S6.Ex29" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{n+1}(P)=0\to H^{n+1}(U)\xrightarrow{r}H^{n}(Y)\xrightarrow{i_{*}}H^{n+2}(P)=\mathbb{Q}\xrightarrow{j^{*}}H^{n+2}(U)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑟</mo></mover><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">i</mi><mo mathsize="0.710em">∗</mo></msub></mover><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.700em">j</mi><mo mathsize="0.710em">∗</mo></msup></mover><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{n+1}(P)=0\to H^{n+1}(U)\xrightarrow{r}H^{n}(Y)\xrightarrow{i_{*}}H^{n+2}(P)=\mathbb{Q}\xrightarrow{j^{*}}H^{n+2}(U)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p2.3" class="ltx_p">then <math id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> is surjective onto the primitive cohomology:</p>
<table id="S6.Ex30" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r:H^{n+1}(U)\xrightarrow{\hbox{\rm Im}}H_{\rm prim}^{n}(X)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mtext mathsize="0.700em">Im</mtext></mover><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>prim</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r:H^{n+1}(U)\xrightarrow{\hbox{\rm Im}}H_{\rm prim}^{n}(X)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.2. </span>MHS of a normal crossing divisor (NCD)</h3>

<div id="S6.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> be a Normal Crossing Divisor in a proper complex smooth algebraic variety.
We suppose the irreducible components
<math id="S6.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Y_{i})_{i\in I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Y_{i})_{i\in I}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> smooth and ordered.</p>
</div>
<section id="S6.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">6.2.1. </span>Mayer-Vietoris resolution</h4>

<div id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>
denotes the set of strictly increasing sequences <math id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma=(\sigma_{0},...,\sigma_{q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma=(\sigma_{0},...,\sigma_{q})</annotation></semantics></math> on the ordered set of indices <math id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{\sigma}=Y_{\sigma_{0}}\cap...\cap Y_{\sigma_{q}},Y_{\underline{q}}=\coprod_{\sigma\in S_{q}}Y_{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo>∩</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∐</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{\sigma}=Y_{\sigma_{0}}\cap...\cap Y_{\sigma_{q}},Y_{\underline{q}}=\coprod_{\sigma\in S_{q}}Y_{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math> is the
disjoint union, and for all <math id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\in[0,q],q\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\in[0,q],q\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> let
<math id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{j,\underline{q}}:Y_{\underline{q}}\rightarrow Y_{\underline{q}-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{j,\underline{q}}:Y_{\underline{q}}\rightarrow Y_{\underline{q}-1}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes a
map inducing for each <math id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> the embedding
<math id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{j,\sigma}:Y_{\sigma}\rightarrow Y_{\sigma(\widehat{j})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>j</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{j,\sigma}:Y_{\sigma}\rightarrow Y_{\sigma(\widehat{j})}</annotation></semantics></math>
where <math id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(\widehat{j})=(\sigma_{0},...,\widehat{\sigma_{j}},...,\sigma_{q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>j</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(\widehat{j})=(\sigma_{0},...,\widehat{\sigma_{j}},...,\sigma_{q})</annotation></semantics></math> is obtained by deleting <math id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi_{q}:Y_{\underline{q}}\rightarrow Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{q}:Y_{\underline{q}}\rightarrow Y</annotation></semantics></math> (or simply <math id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi</annotation></semantics></math>)
denotes the canonical projection and <math id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda^{*}_{j,\underline{q}}:\Pi_{*}\hbox{\bf Z}_{Y_{\underline{q-1}}}\rightarrow\Pi_{*}\hbox{\bf Z}_{Y_{\underline{q}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">Z</mtext><msub><mi>Y</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></msub></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">Z</mtext><msub><mi>Y</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></msub></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda^{*}_{j,\underline{q}}:\Pi_{*}\hbox{\bf Z}_{Y_{\underline{q-1}}}\rightarrow\Pi_{*}\hbox{\bf Z}_{Y_{\underline{q}}}</annotation></semantics></math> the
restriction map defined by <math id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{j,\underline{q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{j,\underline{q}}</annotation></semantics></math>
for <math id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\in[0,q]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\in[0,q]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 6.10</span></span><span id="S6.Thmtheorem10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Mayer-Vietoris resolution of
<math id="S6.Thmtheorem10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}_{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}_{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>)<span id="S6.Thmtheorem10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem10.p1.1" class="ltx_p">It is defined by the following complex of sheaves
<math id="S6.Thmtheorem10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi_{*}{\mathbb{Z}}_{Y{\underline{.}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mo>.</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{*}{\mathbb{Z}}_{Y{\underline{.}}}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S6.Ex31" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\rightarrow\Pi_{*}\mathbb{Z}_{Y_{\underline{0}}}\rightarrow\Pi_{*}\mathbb{Z}_{Y_{\underline{1}}}\rightarrow\cdots\rightarrow\Pi_{*}\mathbb{Z}_{Y_{\underline{q-1}}}{\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\rightarrow}\limits^{{\delta_{q-1}}}}}\Pi_{*}\mathbb{Z}_{Y_{\underline{q}}}\rightarrow\cdots" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><msub><mi>Y</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></msub></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><msub><mi>Y</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></msub></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><msub><mi>Y</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></msub></msub></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><msub><mi>Y</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></msub></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\rightarrow\Pi_{*}\mathbb{Z}_{Y_{\underline{0}}}\rightarrow\Pi_{*}\mathbb{Z}_{Y_{\underline{1}}}\rightarrow\cdots\rightarrow\Pi_{*}\mathbb{Z}_{Y_{\underline{q-1}}}{\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\rightarrow}\limits^{{\delta_{q-1}}}}}\Pi_{*}\mathbb{Z}_{Y_{\underline{q}}}\rightarrow\cdots</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem10.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S6.Thmtheorem10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{q-1}=\sum_{j\in[0,q]}(-1)^{j}\lambda^{*}_{j,{\underline{q}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{q-1}=\sum_{j\in[0,q]}(-1)^{j}\lambda^{*}_{j,{\underline{q}}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This resolution is associated to an hypercovering of
<math id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> by topological spaces in the following sense. Consider the
diagram of spaces over <math id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S6.Ex32" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex32.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="Y_{\underline{.}}=(Y_{\underline{0}}\quad\begin{matrix}\leftarrow\\
\leftarrow\end{matrix}\quad Y_{\underline{1}}\quad\begin{matrix}\leftarrow\\
\leftarrow\\
\leftarrow\end{matrix}\quad\cdots\quad Y_{\underline{q-1}}\quad\begin{matrix}\xleftarrow{\lambda_{j,\underline{q}}}\\
\vdots\\
\longleftarrow\end{matrix}\quad Y_{\underline{q}}\cdots)\ \xrightarrow{\Pi}Y" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mo>.</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></msub><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mo stretchy="false">←</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mo stretchy="false">←</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable><msub><mi>Y</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></msub><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mo stretchy="false">←</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mo stretchy="false">←</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mo stretchy="false">←</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></msub><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mover accent="true"><mo>←</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">λ</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.710em">j</mi><mo mathsize="0.710em">,</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi mathsize="0.710em">q</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></mrow></msub></mover></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mo stretchy="false">⟵</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable><msub><mi>Y</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">Π</mo></mover><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{\underline{.}}=(Y_{\underline{0}}\quad\begin{matrix}\leftarrow\\
\leftarrow\end{matrix}\quad Y_{\underline{1}}\quad\begin{matrix}\leftarrow\\
\leftarrow\\
\leftarrow\end{matrix}\quad\cdots\quad Y_{\underline{q-1}}\quad\begin{matrix}\xleftarrow{\lambda_{j,\underline{q}}}\\
\vdots\\
\longleftarrow\end{matrix}\quad Y_{\underline{q}}\cdots)\ \xrightarrow{\Pi}Y</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">This diagram is the strict simplicial scheme associated in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> to the
normal crossing divisor <math id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>, called here after
Mayer-Vietoris. The Mayer-Vietoris complex is canonically
associated as direct image by <math id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi</annotation></semantics></math> of the sheaf
<math id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Z}}_{Y_{\underline{*}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><msub><mi>Y</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mo>∗</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Z}}_{Y_{\underline{*}}}</annotation></semantics></math> equal to <math id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Z}}_{Y_{\underline{i}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><msub><mi>Y</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Z}}_{Y_{\underline{i}}}</annotation></semantics></math> on
<math id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{\underline{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{\underline{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The generalization of such resolution is the
basis of the later general construction of Mixed Hodge Structure using simplicial
covering of an algebraic variety.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">6.2.2. </span>The cohomological mixed Hodge complex of a NCD</h4>

<div id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The weight
filtration <math id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi_{*}{\mathbb{Q}}_{Y\underline{.}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mo>.</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{*}{\mathbb{Q}}_{Y\underline{.}}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined by:</p>
<table id="S6.Ex33" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{-q}(\Pi_{*}\mathbb{Q}_{Y_{\underline{*}}})={\sigma}_{\cdot\geq q}\Pi_{*}\mathbb{Q}_{Y_{\underline{*}}}=\Pi_{*}{\sigma}_{\cdot\geq q}\mathbb{Q}_{Y_{\underline{*}}},\quad Gr_{-q}^{W}(\Pi_{*}\mathbb{Q}_{Y_{\underline{*}}})\simeq\Pi_{*}\mathbb{Q}_{Y_{\underline{q}}}[-q]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><msub><mi>Y</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mo>∗</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></msub></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><msub><mi>Y</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mo>∗</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></msub></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><msub><mi>Y</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mo>∗</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></msub></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><msub><mi>Y</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mo>∗</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></msub></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><msub><mi>Y</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{-q}(\Pi_{*}\mathbb{Q}_{Y_{\underline{*}}})={\sigma}_{\cdot\geq q}\Pi_{*}\mathbb{Q}_{Y_{\underline{*}}}=\Pi_{*}{\sigma}_{\cdot\geq q}\mathbb{Q}_{Y_{\underline{*}}},\quad Gr_{-q}^{W}(\Pi_{*}\mathbb{Q}_{Y_{\underline{*}}})\simeq\Pi_{*}\mathbb{Q}_{Y_{\underline{q}}}[-q]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">We introduce the
complexes <math id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega^{*}_{Y_{\underline{i}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><msub><mi>Y</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega^{*}_{Y_{\underline{i}}}</annotation></semantics></math> of differential forms on
<math id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{\underline{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{\underline{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The simple complex <math id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s(\Omega^{*}_{Y_{\underline{*}}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><msub><mi>Y</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mo>∗</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s(\Omega^{*}_{Y_{\underline{*}}})</annotation></semantics></math> is associated to the double complex <math id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi_{*}\Omega^{*}_{Y_{\underline{*}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><msub><mi>Y</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mo>∗</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{*}\Omega^{*}_{Y_{\underline{*}}}</annotation></semantics></math> with the exterior differential <math id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> of forms
and the differential <math id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{*}</annotation></semantics></math> defined by <math id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{q-1}=\sum_{j\in[0,q]}(-1)^{j}\lambda^{*}_{j,{\underline{q}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{q-1}=\sum_{j\in[0,q]}(-1)^{j}\lambda^{*}_{j,{\underline{q}}}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi_{*}\Omega^{*}_{Y_{\underline{q-1}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><msub><mi>Y</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{*}\Omega^{*}_{Y_{\underline{q-1}}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The weight <math id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math>, and Hodge <math id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>
filtrations are defined as:</p>
<table id="S6.Ex34" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex34.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="W_{-q}=s(\sigma_{\cdot\geq q}\Omega^{*}_{Y_{\underline{*}}})=s(0\rightarrow\cdots 0\rightarrow\Pi_{*}\Omega^{*}_{Y_{\underline{q}}}\rightarrow\Pi_{*}\Omega^{*}_{Y_{\underline{q+1}}}\rightarrow\cdots)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><msub><mi>Y</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mo>∗</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.1389em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><msub><mi>Y</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><msub><mi>Y</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo rspace="0.1389em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{-q}=s(\sigma_{\cdot\geq q}\Omega^{*}_{Y_{\underline{*}}})=s(0\rightarrow\cdots 0\rightarrow\Pi_{*}\Omega^{*}_{Y_{\underline{q}}}\rightarrow\Pi_{*}\Omega^{*}_{Y_{\underline{q+1}}}\rightarrow\cdots)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S6.Ex35" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex35.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="F^{p}=s(\sigma_{*\geq p}\Omega_{Y_{\underline{*}}}^{*})=s(0\rightarrow\cdots 0\rightarrow\Pi_{*}\Omega^{p}_{Y_{\underline{*}}}\rightarrow\Pi_{*}\Omega_{Y_{\underline{*}}}^{p+1}\rightarrow\cdots)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><msub><mi>Y</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mo>∗</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.1389em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><msub><mi>Y</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mo>∗</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></msub><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><msub><mi>Y</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mo>∗</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></msub><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo rspace="0.1389em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{p}=s(\sigma_{*\geq p}\Omega_{Y_{\underline{*}}}^{*})=s(0\rightarrow\cdots 0\rightarrow\Pi_{*}\Omega^{p}_{Y_{\underline{*}}}\rightarrow\Pi_{*}\Omega_{Y_{\underline{*}}}^{p+1}\rightarrow\cdots)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We have a filtered isomorphism
in the filtered derived category of sheaves of abelian groups
<math id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{+}F(Y,\mathbb{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{+}F(Y,\mathbb{C})</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S6.Ex36" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex36.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Gr_{-q}^{W}s(\Omega_{Y_{\underline{*}}}^{*}),F)\simeq(\Pi_{*}\Omega_{Y_{\underline{q}}}^{*}[-q],F)\quad\hbox{in}\quad D^{+}F(Y,\mathbb{C})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><msub><mi>Y</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mo>∗</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><msub><mi>Y</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>in</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Gr_{-q}^{W}s(\Omega_{Y_{\underline{*}}}^{*}),F)\simeq(\Pi_{*}\Omega_{Y_{\underline{q}}}^{*}[-q],F)\quad\hbox{in}\quad D^{+}F(Y,\mathbb{C}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">inducing isomorphisms in <math id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{+}(Y,\mathbb{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{+}(Y,\mathbb{C})</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S6.Ex37" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex37.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\Pi}_{*}\mathbb{Q}_{Y_{\underline{*}}},W)\otimes\mathbb{C}=(\mathbb{C}_{Y\underline{.}},W)\xrightarrow{\alpha\,\sim}(s(\Omega_{Y_{\underline{*}}}^{*}),W)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><msub><mi>Y</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mo>∗</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></msub></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℂ</mi><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mo>.</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">α</mi><mo lspace="0.448em" mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><msub><mi>Y</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mo>∗</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\Pi}_{*}\mathbb{Q}_{Y_{\underline{*}}},W)\otimes\mathbb{C}=(\mathbb{C}_{Y\underline{.}},W)\xrightarrow{\alpha\,\sim}(s(\Omega_{Y_{\underline{*}}}^{*}),W)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S6.Ex38" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex38.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr_{-q}^{W}(\Pi_{*}\mathbb{C}_{Y_{\underline{*}}})\simeq\Pi_{*}\mathbb{C}_{Y_{\underline{q}}}[-q]{\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\rightarrow}\limits^{\sim}}}{\Pi}_{*}\Omega_{Y_{\underline{q}}}^{*}[-q]\simeq Gr_{-q}^{W}s(\Omega_{Y_{\underline{*}}}^{*})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℂ</mi><msub><mi>Y</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mo>∗</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></msub></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℂ</mi><msub><mi>Y</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∼</mo></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><msub><mi>Y</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><msub><mi>Y</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mo>∗</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr_{-q}^{W}(\Pi_{*}\mathbb{C}_{Y_{\underline{*}}})\simeq\Pi_{*}\mathbb{C}_{Y_{\underline{q}}}[-q]{\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\rightarrow}\limits^{\sim}}}{\Pi}_{*}\Omega_{Y_{\underline{q}}}^{*}[-q]\simeq Gr_{-q}^{W}s(\Omega_{Y_{\underline{*}}}^{*})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p2.3" class="ltx_p">Let <span id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p2.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_bold">K</span> be the system consisting of:</p>
<table id="S6.Ex39" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex39.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\Pi_{*}\mathbb{Q}_{Y_{\underline{*}}},W),\mathbb{Q}_{Y}\xrightarrow{\sim}\Pi_{*}\mathbb{Q}_{Y_{\underline{*}}},(s(\Omega_{Y_{\underline{*}}}^{*}),W,F),\quad(\Pi_{*}\mathbb{Q}_{Y_{\underline{*}}},W)\otimes\mathbb{C}\xrightarrow{\sim}(s(\Omega_{Y_{\underline{*}}}^{*}),W)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><msub><mi>Y</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mo>∗</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></msub></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><msub><mi>Y</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mo>∗</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></msub></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><msub><mi>Y</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mo>∗</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><msub><mi>Y</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mo>∗</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></msub></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><msub><mi>Y</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mo>∗</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\Pi_{*}\mathbb{Q}_{Y_{\underline{*}}},W),\mathbb{Q}_{Y}\xrightarrow{\sim}\Pi_{*}\mathbb{Q}_{Y_{\underline{*}}},(s(\Omega_{Y_{\underline{*}}}^{*}),W,F),\quad(\Pi_{*}\mathbb{Q}_{Y_{\underline{*}}},W)\otimes\mathbb{C}\xrightarrow{\sim}(s(\Omega_{Y_{\underline{*}}}^{*}),W)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem11" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem11.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 6.11</span></span><span id="S6.Thmtheorem11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem11.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The system <span id="S6.Thmtheorem11.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_bold">K</span>
associated to a normal crossing divisor <math id="S6.Thmtheorem11.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> with smooth proper irreducible
components, is a Cohomological Mixed Hodge Complex on <math id="S6.Thmtheorem11.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>.
It defines a functorial Mixed Hodge Structure on the
cohomology <math id="S6.Thmtheorem11.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{i}(Y,\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{i}(Y,\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math>, with weights varying between <math id="S6.Thmtheorem11.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S6.Thmtheorem11.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In terms of Dolbeault resolutions : <math id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(s(\mathcal{E}^{*,*}_{Y\underline{.}}),W,F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mo>.</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">∗</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(s(\mathcal{E}^{*,*}_{Y\underline{.}}),W,F)</annotation></semantics></math>, the statement means that
the complex of global sections <math id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma(Y,s(\mathcal{E}^{*,*}_{Y_{\underline{*}}}),W,F):=({\mathbb{R}}\Gamma(Y,\mathbb{C}),W,F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><msub><mi>Y</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mo>∗</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></msub><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">∗</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma(Y,s(\mathcal{E}^{*,*}_{Y_{\underline{*}}}),W,F):=({\mathbb{R}}\Gamma(Y,\mathbb{C}),W,F)</annotation></semantics></math> is a Mixed Hodge Complex in the following sense:</p>
<table id="S6.Ex40" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex40.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}&amp;(Gr^{W}_{-i}({\mathbb{R}}\Gamma(Y,\mathbb{C}),F):=(\Gamma(Y,W_{-i}s({\mathcal{E}}^{*,*}_{Y_{\underline{*}}})/\Gamma(Y,W_{-i-1}s({\mathcal{E}}^{*,*}_{Y_{\underline{*}}}),F)\\
&amp;\simeq(\Gamma(Y,Gr^{W}_{-i}s({\mathcal{E}}^{*,*}_{Y_{\underline{*}}}),F)\simeq({\mathbb{R}}\Gamma(Y_{i},\Omega_{Y_{\underline{i}}}^{*}[-i]),F)\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mi>r</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>W</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>:=</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">∗</mo></mrow></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mo>∗</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>/</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">∗</mo></mrow></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mo>∗</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><msub><mi>Y</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mo>∗</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></msub><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">∗</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><msub><mi>Y</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}&amp;(Gr^{W}_{-i}({\mathbb{R}}\Gamma(Y,\mathbb{C}),F):=(\Gamma(Y,W_{-i}s({\mathcal{E}}^{*,*}_{Y_{\underline{*}}})/\Gamma(Y,W_{-i-1}s({\mathcal{E}}^{*,*}_{Y_{\underline{*}}}),F)\\
&amp;\simeq(\Gamma(Y,Gr^{W}_{-i}s({\mathcal{E}}^{*,*}_{Y_{\underline{*}}}),F)\simeq({\mathbb{R}}\Gamma(Y_{i},\Omega_{Y_{\underline{i}}}^{*}[-i]),F)\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">is a Hodge Complex of weight <math id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-i</annotation></semantics></math> in the sense that:</p>
<table id="S6.Ex41" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex41.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(H^{n}(Gr^{W}_{-i}{\mathbb{R}}\Gamma(Y,\mathbb{C})),F)\simeq(H^{n-i}(Y_{\underline{i}},\mathbb{C}),F)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(H^{n}(Gr^{W}_{-i}{\mathbb{R}}\Gamma(Y,\mathbb{C})),F)\simeq(H^{n-i}(Y_{\underline{i}},\mathbb{C}),F)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p3.3" class="ltx_p">is a Hodge Structure of weight <math id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n-i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n-i</annotation></semantics></math>. 
<br class="ltx_break">The terms of the spectral sequence <math id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{1}(K,W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{1}(K,W)</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(K,W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(K,W)</annotation></semantics></math> are
written as:</p>
<table id="S6.Ex42" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex42.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}_{W}E_{1}^{pq}=\mathbb{H}^{p+q}(Y,Gr_{-p}^{W}(s\Omega_{Y_{\underline{*}}}^{*}))\simeq\mathbb{H}^{p+q}(Y,\Pi_{*}\Omega^{*}_{Y_{\underline{p}}}[-p])\simeq H^{q}(Y_{\underline{p}},\mathbb{C})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>E</mi><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mn>1</mn><mrow></mrow><mi>W</mi><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℍ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><msub><mi>Y</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mo>∗</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℍ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><msub><mi>Y</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{W}E_{1}^{pq}=\mathbb{H}^{p+q}(Y,Gr_{-p}^{W}(s\Omega_{Y_{\underline{*}}}^{*}))\simeq\mathbb{H}^{p+q}(Y,\Pi_{*}\Omega^{*}_{Y_{\underline{p}}}[-p])\simeq H^{q}(Y_{\underline{p}},\mathbb{C})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p3.4" class="ltx_p">They carry the Hodge Structure of the space <math id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{\underline{p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{\underline{p}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The differential is a combinatorial restriction map
inducing a morphism of Hodge Structures:</p>
<table id="S6.Ex43" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex43.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{1}=\sum_{j\leq p+1}(-1)^{j}\lambda_{j,p+1}^{*}:H^{q}(Y_{\underline{p}},\mathbb{C})\rightarrow H^{q}(Y_{\underline{p+1}},\mathbb{C})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{1}=\sum_{j\leq p+1}(-1)^{j}\lambda_{j,p+1}^{*}:H^{q}(Y_{\underline{p}},\mathbb{C})\rightarrow H^{q}(Y_{\underline{p+1}},\mathbb{C}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p3.5" class="ltx_p">The spectral sequence
degenerates at <math id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{2}\,(E_{2}=E_{\infty})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{2}\,(E_{2}=E_{\infty})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem12" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem12.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 6.12</span></span><span id="S6.Thmtheorem12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem12.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem12.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The Hodge Structure on <math id="S6.Thmtheorem12.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr^{W}_{q}H^{p+q}(Y,\mathbb{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>q</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr^{W}_{q}H^{p+q}(Y,\mathbb{C})</annotation></semantics></math> is the cohomology
of the complex of Hodge Structure defined by <math id="S6.Thmtheorem12.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(H^{q}(Y_{\underline{*}},\mathbb{C}),d_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mo>∗</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(H^{q}(Y_{\underline{*}},\mathbb{C}),d_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>
equal to <math id="S6.Thmtheorem12.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{q}(Y_{\underline{p}},\mathbb{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{q}(Y_{\underline{p}},\mathbb{C})</annotation></semantics></math> in degree <math id="S6.Thmtheorem12.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S6.Ex44" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex44.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Gr^{W}_{q}H^{p+q}(Y,\mathbb{C}),F)\simeq((H^{p}(H^{q}(Y_{\underline{*}},\mathbb{C}),d_{1}),F)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>q</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mo>∗</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Gr^{W}_{q}H^{p+q}(Y,\mathbb{C}),F)\simeq((H^{p}(H^{q}(Y_{\underline{*}},\mathbb{C}),d_{1}),F).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem12.p1.2" class="ltx_p">In particular, the weight of <math id="S6.Thmtheorem12.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{i}(Y,\mathbb{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{i}(Y,\mathbb{C})</annotation></semantics></math> vary in the interval
[<math id="S6.Thmtheorem12.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0,i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0,i</annotation></semantics></math>] (<math id="S6.Thmtheorem12.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr^{W}_{q}H^{i}(Y,\mathbb{C})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>q</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr^{W}_{q}H^{i}(Y,\mathbb{C})=0</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S6.Thmtheorem12.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\notin" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∉</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\notin</annotation></semantics></math> [<math id="S6.Thmtheorem12.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0,i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0,i</annotation></semantics></math>]).</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We will see that the last condition on the weight is true for all
complete varieties.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.3. </span>Relative cohomology and the mixed cone </h3>

<div id="S6.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The notion of morphism of Mixed Hodge Complex involves compatibility
between the rational level and complex level in the derived category,
hence up to quasi-isomorphism.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To define a Mixed Hodge Structure on
the relative cohomology, we define
the notion of mixed cone with respect to a representative of the morphism
on the level of complexes, hence depending on the representative.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The
isomorphism between two structures
obtained for two representatives depends on the choice of an homotopy, hence
it is not naturally defined. Nevertheless this notion is
interesting in applications. Later, one solution is to consider
Mixed Hodge Complex on a category of diagrams, then the diagonal filtration is a
natural example of such construction applied for example on
simplicial varieties.</p>
</div>
<section id="S6.SS3.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">6.3.1. </span></h4>

<div id="S6.SS3.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A morphism <math id="S6.SS3.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u:K\rightarrow K^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u:K\rightarrow K^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> of Mixed Hodge Complex (resp.
CMHC) consists of morphisms:</p>
<table id="S6.Ex45" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex45.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}&amp;u_{A}:K_{A}\rightarrow K^{\prime}_{A}\,\,{\rm in}\,\,D^{+}A({\rm resp.})D^{+}(X,A)),\\
&amp;u_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}}:(K_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}},W)\rightarrow({K^{\prime}}_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}},W)\,\,{\rm in}\,\,D^{+}F(A\otimes\mathbb{Q})(\,\,{\rm resp.}\,\,D^{+}F(X,A\otimes\mathbb{Q})),\\
&amp;u_{\mathbb{C}}:(K_{\mathbb{C}},W,F)\rightarrow(K^{\prime}_{\mathbb{C}},W,F)\,\,{\rm in}\,\,D^{+}F_{2}\mathbb{C}(\,\,{\rm resp.}\,\,D^{+}F_{2}(X,\mathbb{C}).)\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>u</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>A</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>K</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>A</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>K</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>A</mi></msub><mi>in</mi><mi>D</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo></msup><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>resp</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>D</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>u</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo rspace="0.510em" stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>in</mi><mi>D</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo></msup><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo rspace="0.340em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>resp</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.677em">.</mo><mi>D</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo></msup><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>u</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo rspace="0.510em" stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>in</mi><mi>D</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo></msup><mi>F</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>ℂ</mi><mo rspace="0.340em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>resp</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.677em">.</mo><mi>D</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo></msup><mi>F</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}&amp;u_{A}:K_{A}\rightarrow K^{\prime}_{A}\,\,{\rm in}\,\,D^{+}A({\rm resp.})D^{+}(X,A)),\\
&amp;u_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}}:(K_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}},W)\rightarrow({K^{\prime}}_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}},W)\,\,{\rm in}\,\,D^{+}F(A\otimes\mathbb{Q})(\,\,{\rm resp.}\,\,D^{+}F(X,A\otimes\mathbb{Q})),\\
&amp;u_{\mathbb{C}}:(K_{\mathbb{C}},W,F)\rightarrow(K^{\prime}_{\mathbb{C}},W,F)\,\,{\rm in}\,\,D^{+}F_{2}\mathbb{C}(\,\,{\rm resp.}\,\,D^{+}F_{2}(X,\mathbb{C}).)\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS3.SSS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">and commutative diagrams:</p>
<table id="S6.Ex46" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex46.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{matrix}K_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}}&amp;\xrightarrow{u_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}}}&amp;K^{\prime}_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}}\qquad&amp;K_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}}\otimes\mathbb{C}&amp;\xrightarrow{u_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}}\otimes\mathbb{C}}&amp;K^{\prime}_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}}\otimes\mathbb{C}\\
\wr\downarrow{\alpha}&amp;&amp;\wr\downarrow{\alpha^{\prime}}\qquad&amp;\wr\downarrow{\beta}&amp;&amp;\wr\downarrow{\beta^{\prime}}\\
K_{A}\otimes\mathbb{Q}&amp;\xrightarrow{u_{A}\otimes\mathbb{Q}}&amp;K^{\prime}_{A}\otimes\mathbb{Q}\qquad&amp;K_{\mathbb{C}}&amp;\xrightarrow{u_{\mathbb{C}}}&amp;K^{\prime}_{\mathbb{C}}\end{matrix}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">u</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.710em">A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" mathsize="0.710em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi mathsize="0.710em">ℚ</mi></mrow></msub></mover></mtd><mtd><mrow><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace></mrow></mtd><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow></mtd><mtd><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">u</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.710em">A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" mathsize="0.710em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi mathsize="0.710em">ℚ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" mathsize="0.700em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">ℂ</mi></mrow></mover></mtd><mtd><mrow><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">≀</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">≀</mo><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">≀</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">≀</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><msup><mi>β</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></mtd><mtd><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">u</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">A</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" mathsize="0.700em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">ℚ</mi></mrow></mover></mtd><mtd><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mi>A</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace></mrow></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub></mtd><mtd><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">u</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">ℂ</mi></msub></mover></mtd><mtd><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{matrix}K_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}}&amp;\xrightarrow{u_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}}}&amp;K^{\prime}_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}}\qquad&amp;K_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}}\otimes\mathbb{C}&amp;\xrightarrow{u_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}}\otimes\mathbb{C}}&amp;K^{\prime}_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}}\otimes\mathbb{C}\\
\wr\downarrow{\alpha}&amp;&amp;\wr\downarrow{\alpha^{\prime}}\qquad&amp;\wr\downarrow{\beta}&amp;&amp;\wr\downarrow{\beta^{\prime}}\\
K_{A}\otimes\mathbb{Q}&amp;\xrightarrow{u_{A}\otimes\mathbb{Q}}&amp;K^{\prime}_{A}\otimes\mathbb{Q}\qquad&amp;K_{\mathbb{C}}&amp;\xrightarrow{u_{\mathbb{C}}}&amp;K^{\prime}_{\mathbb{C}}\end{matrix}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS3.SSS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">in <math id="S6.SS3.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{+}(A\otimes\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{+}(A\otimes\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> (resp. <math id="S6.SS3.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{+}(X,A\otimes\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{+}(X,A\otimes\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> and in <math id="S6.SS3.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{+}F(\mathbb{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{+}F(\mathbb{C})</annotation></semantics></math>
(resp. <math id="S6.SS3.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{+}F(X,\mathbb{C}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{+}F(X,\mathbb{C}))</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to <math id="S6.SS3.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS3.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">6.3.2. </span></h4>

<div id="S6.SS3.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S6.SS3.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(K,W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(K,W)</annotation></semantics></math> be a complex of objects of an abelian category
<math id="S6.SS3.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔸</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{A}</annotation></semantics></math> with an increasing filtration <math id="S6.SS3.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math>. We denote by
<math id="S6.SS3.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(T_{M}K,W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(T_{M}K,W)</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S6.SS3.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(K[1],W[1])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(K[1],W[1])</annotation></semantics></math> the mixed shifted complex
with a translation on degrees
of <math id="S6.SS3.SSS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.SS3.SSS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="S6.SS3.SSS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{n}(T_{M}K^{i})=W_{n-1}K^{i+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{n}(T_{M}K^{i})=W_{n-1}K^{i+1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem13" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem13.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 6.13</span></span><span id="S6.Thmtheorem13.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Mixed cone)<span id="S6.Thmtheorem13.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem13.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem13.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S6.Thmtheorem13.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u:K\rightarrow K^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u:K\rightarrow K^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be a
morphism of complexes in <math id="S6.Thmtheorem13.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{+}F(\mathbb{A})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔸</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{+}F(\mathbb{A})</annotation></semantics></math> with an increasing
filtration. The mixed cone <math id="S6.Thmtheorem13.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{M}(u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{M}(u)</annotation></semantics></math> is defined by the complex
<math id="S6.Thmtheorem13.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{M}K\oplus K^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{M}K\oplus K^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> with the differential of the cone <math id="S6.Thmtheorem13.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C(u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(u)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS3.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">6.3.3. </span></h4>

<div id="S6.SS3.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S6.SS3.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u:K\rightarrow K^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u:K\rightarrow K^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be a morphism of a Mixed Hodge Complex. There exists a
quasi-isomorphism
<math id="S6.SS3.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v=(v_{A},v_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}},v_{\mathbb{C}}):\tilde{K}\xrightarrow{\approx}K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">≈</mo></mover><mi>K</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v=(v_{A},v_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}},v_{\mathbb{C}}):\tilde{K}\xrightarrow{\approx}K</annotation></semantics></math>
and a morphism <math id="S6.SS3.SSS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{u}=(\tilde{u}_{A},\tilde{u}_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}},\tilde{u}_{\mathbb{C}}):\tilde{K}\rightarrow K^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{u}=(\tilde{u}_{A},\tilde{u}_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}},\tilde{u}_{\mathbb{C}}):\tilde{K}\rightarrow K^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> of Mixed Hodge Complexes such that <math id="S6.SS3.SSS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S6.SS3.SSS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{u}</annotation></semantics></math> are defined successively in <math id="S6.SS3.SSS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{+}A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{+}A</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.SS3.SSS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{+}F(A\otimes\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{+}F(A\otimes\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.SS3.SSS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{+}F_{2}\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{+}F_{2}\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., we can find, by definition,
diagrams:</p>
<table id="S6.Ex47" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex47.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{A}\xleftarrow{\approx}\tilde{K}_{A}\rightarrow{K^{\prime}}_{A},\quad K_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}}\xleftarrow{\approx}\tilde{K}_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}}\rightarrow{K^{\prime}}_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}},\quad K_{\mathbb{C}}\xleftarrow{\approx}\tilde{K}_{\mathbb{C}}\rightarrow{K^{\prime}}_{\mathbb{C}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mover accent="true"><mo>←</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">≈</mo></mover><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>A</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>K</mi><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo><mi>A</mi><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></msub><mover accent="true"><mo>←</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">≈</mo></mover><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>K</mi><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mover accent="true"><mo>←</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">≈</mo></mover><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>K</mi><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{A}\xleftarrow{\approx}\tilde{K}_{A}\rightarrow{K^{\prime}}_{A},\quad K_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}}\xleftarrow{\approx}\tilde{K}_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}}\rightarrow{K^{\prime}}_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}},\quad K_{\mathbb{C}}\xleftarrow{\approx}\tilde{K}_{\mathbb{C}}\rightarrow{K^{\prime}}_{\mathbb{C}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS3.SSS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">or in short
<math id="S6.SS3.SSS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\xleftarrow{\approx v}\tilde{K}\xrightarrow{\tilde{u}}K^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mover accent="true"><mo>←</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo mathsize="0.700em">≈</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">v</mi></mrow></mover><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.700em">u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mover><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\xleftarrow{\approx v}\tilde{K}\xrightarrow{\tilde{u}}K^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> (or equivalently
<math id="S6.SS3.SSS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\xrightarrow{\tilde{u}}\tilde{K}^{\prime}\xleftarrow{\approx v}K^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.700em">u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mover><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>′</mo></msup><mover accent="true"><mo>←</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo mathsize="0.700em">≈</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">v</mi></mrow></mover><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\xrightarrow{\tilde{u}}\tilde{K}^{\prime}\xleftarrow{\approx v}K^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>) representing <math id="S6.SS3.SSS3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS3.SSS4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">6.3.4. </span>Dependence on homotopy</h4>

<div id="S6.SS3.SSS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS3.SSS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Consider a morphism <math id="S6.SS3.SSS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\colon K\rightarrow K^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\colon K\rightarrow K^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> of
Mixed Hodge Complexes, represented by a morphism of complexes <math id="S6.SS3.SSS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tilde{u}}:\,\tilde{K}\rightarrow K^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.448em">:</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tilde{u}}:\,\tilde{K}\rightarrow K^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.
<br class="ltx_break">To define the mixed cone <math id="S6.SS3.SSS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{M}({\tilde{u}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{M}({\tilde{u}})</annotation></semantics></math> out of: 
<br class="ltx_break">(i) the cones <math id="S6.SS3.SSS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C({\tilde{u}_{A}})\in C^{+}(A),\,C_{M}(\tilde{u}_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}})\in C^{+}F(A\otimes\mathbb{Q}),\,C_{M}(\tilde{u}_{\mathbb{C}})\in C^{+}F_{2}(\mathbb{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>A</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C({\tilde{u}_{A}})\in C^{+}(A),\,C_{M}(\tilde{u}_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}})\in C^{+}F(A\otimes\mathbb{Q}),\,C_{M}(\tilde{u}_{\mathbb{C}})\in C^{+}F_{2}(\mathbb{C})</annotation></semantics></math>, 
<br class="ltx_break">we still need to define compatibility
isomorphisms:</p>
<table id="S6.Ex48" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex48.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{1}:C_{M}(\tilde{u}_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}})\simeq C(\tilde{u}_{A})\otimes\mathbb{Q},\quad\gamma_{2}:(C_{M}(\tilde{u}_{\mathbb{C}},W)\simeq(C_{M}(\tilde{u}_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}}),W)\otimes\mathbb{C}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>A</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{1}:C_{M}(\tilde{u}_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}})\simeq C(\tilde{u}_{A})\otimes\mathbb{Q},\quad\gamma_{2}:(C_{M}(\tilde{u}_{\mathbb{C}},W)\simeq(C_{M}(\tilde{u}_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}}),W)\otimes\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS3.SSS4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">successively in <math id="S6.SS3.SSS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{+}(A\otimes\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{+}(A\otimes\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.SS3.SSS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{+}F(\mathbb{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{+}F(\mathbb{C})</annotation></semantics></math>. With the notations of <a href="#S6.SS3.SSS1" title="6.3.1. ‣ 6.3. Relative cohomology and the mixed cone ‣ 6. Logarithmic complex, normal crossing divisor and the mixed
cone ‣ Mixed Hodge Structures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.3.1</span></a>
the choice of isomorphisms <math id="S6.SS3.SSS4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{M}(\tilde{\alpha},\tilde{\alpha}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{M}(\tilde{\alpha},\tilde{\alpha}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S6.SS3.SSS4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{M}(\tilde{\beta},\tilde{\beta}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>β</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>β</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{M}(\tilde{\beta},\tilde{\beta}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> from the compatibility isomorphisms
in <math id="S6.SS3.SSS4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.SS3.SSS4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> does not define compatibility isomorphisms for the cone
since the diagrams of compatibility are commutative only up to
homotopy, that is there exists homotopies <math id="S6.SS3.SSS4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.SS3.SSS4.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>
 such that:</p>
<table id="S6.Ex49" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex49.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\alpha}^{\prime}\circ(\tilde{u}_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}})-(\tilde{u}_{A}\otimes\mathbb{Q})\circ\tilde{\alpha}=h_{1}\circ d+d\circ h_{1},\,\," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>A</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\alpha}^{\prime}\circ(\tilde{u}_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}})-(\tilde{u}_{A}\otimes\mathbb{Q})\circ\tilde{\alpha}=h_{1}\circ d+d\circ h_{1},\,\,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS3.SSS4.p1.3" class="ltx_p">and:</p>
<table id="S6.Ex50" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex50.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\beta}^{\prime}\circ\tilde{u}_{\mathbb{C}}-(\tilde{u}_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}}\otimes\mathbb{C})\circ\tilde{\beta}=h_{2}\circ d+d\circ h_{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>β</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>ℂ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>β</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\beta}^{\prime}\circ\tilde{u}_{\mathbb{C}}-(\tilde{u}_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}}\otimes\mathbb{C})\circ\tilde{\beta}=h_{2}\circ d+d\circ h_{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS3.SSS4.p1.4" class="ltx_p">ii) Then we can define the compatibility isomorphism as:</p>
<table id="S6.Ex51" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex51.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{M}(\tilde{\alpha},\tilde{\alpha}^{\prime},h_{1}):=\left({\tilde{\alpha}\atop{h_{1}}}{0\atop\tilde{\alpha}^{\prime}}\right):C_{M}(\tilde{u}_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}})\xrightarrow{\sim}C(\tilde{u}_{A})\otimes\mathbb{Q}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mn>0</mn><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>′</mo></msup></mfrac></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>A</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{M}(\tilde{\alpha},\tilde{\alpha}^{\prime},h_{1}):=\left({\tilde{\alpha}\atop{h_{1}}}{0\atop\tilde{\alpha}^{\prime}}\right):C_{M}(\tilde{u}_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}})\xrightarrow{\sim}C(\tilde{u}_{A})\otimes\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS3.SSS4.p1.5" class="ltx_p">and a similar formula for <math id="S6.SS3.SSS4.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{M}(\tilde{\beta},\tilde{\beta}^{\prime},h_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>β</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>β</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{M}(\tilde{\beta},\tilde{\beta}^{\prime},h_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem14" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem14.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 6.14</span></span><span id="S6.Thmtheorem14.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem14.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem14.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S6.Thmtheorem14.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\colon K\rightarrow K^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\colon K\rightarrow K^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be a morphism
of Mixed Hodge Complexes. The mixed cone <math id="S6.Thmtheorem14.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{M}(\tilde{u},h_{1},h_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{M}(\tilde{u},h_{1},h_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> constructed above
depends on the choices of the homotopies (<math id="S6.Thmtheorem14.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{1},h_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{1},h_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>) and the
choice of a representative <math id="S6.Thmtheorem14.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{u}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S6.Thmtheorem14.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math>, such that:</p>
<table id="S6.Ex52" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex52.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr_{n}^{W}(C_{M}(\tilde{u}),F)\simeq(Gr_{n-1}^{W}(T\tilde{K}),F)\oplus(Gr_{n}^{W}K^{\prime},F)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr_{n}^{W}(C_{M}(\tilde{u}),F)\simeq(Gr_{n-1}^{W}(T\tilde{K}),F)\oplus(Gr_{n}^{W}K^{\prime},F)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem14.p1.2" class="ltx_p">is a HC of weight
<math id="S6.Thmtheorem14.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>; hence <math id="S6.Thmtheorem14.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{M}(\tilde{u},h_{1},h_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{M}(\tilde{u},h_{1},h_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> is a Mixed Hodge Complex.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7. </span>MHS on the cohomology of a complex algebraic
variety</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The aim of this section is to prove:</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 7.1</span></span><span id="S7.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Deligne)<span id="S7.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The cohomology of a complex algebraic variety carries a natural
Mixed Hodge Structure.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The uniqueness and the functoriality will follow easily, once we have fixed
the case of Normal Crossing Divisors and the smooth case.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We need a construction based on a diagram of algebraic varieties:</p>
<table id="S7.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="X_{*}=(X_{0}\quad\begin{matrix}\leftarrow\\
\leftarrow\end{matrix}\quad X_{1}\quad\begin{matrix}\leftarrow\\
\leftarrow\\
\leftarrow\end{matrix}\quad\cdots\quad X_{q-1}\quad\begin{matrix}\xleftarrow{X_{*}(\delta_{i})}\\
\vdots\\
\longleftarrow\end{matrix}\quad X_{q}\cdots)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mo stretchy="false">←</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mo stretchy="false">←</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mo stretchy="false">←</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mo stretchy="false">←</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mo stretchy="false">←</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mover accent="true"><mo>←</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">X</mi><mo mathsize="0.710em">∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">δ</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">i</mi></msub><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mover></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mo stretchy="false">⟵</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{*}=(X_{0}\quad\begin{matrix}\leftarrow\\
\leftarrow\end{matrix}\quad X_{1}\quad\begin{matrix}\leftarrow\\
\leftarrow\\
\leftarrow\end{matrix}\quad\cdots\quad X_{q-1}\quad\begin{matrix}\xleftarrow{X_{*}(\delta_{i})}\\
\vdots\\
\longleftarrow\end{matrix}\quad X_{q}\cdots)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.p3.2" class="ltx_p">similar to the model in the case of a Normal Crossing Divisor. Here
the <math id="S7.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{*}(\delta_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{*}(\delta_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> are called the face maps
(see below <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem2" title="Definition 7.2. ‣ 7.1.1. Simplicial category ‣ 7.1. MHS on cohomology of simplicial varieties ‣ 7. MHS on the cohomology of a complex algebraic
variety ‣ Mixed Hodge Structures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.2</span></a> for the definition), one for each <math id="S7.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[0,q]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[0,q]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Moreover, we still need to state commutativity
relations when we compose the face maps. When we consider diagrams
of complexes of sheaves, we need resolutions of such sheaves, with
compatibility with respect to the maps <math id="S7.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{*}(\delta_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{*}(\delta_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> so as to avoid
the dependence on homotopy that we met
in the mixed cone construction.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The language of the simplicial category gives a rigorous setting to
state the compatibility relations needed, and leads to the
construction in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> of a simplicial hypercovering of an
algebraic variety <math id="S7.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>,
using a general simplicial technique combined with
desingularization at the various steps.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p6.1" class="ltx_p">We will take this topological construction here as granted and concentrate
on the construction of a
cohomological mixed Hodge complex on the variety <math id="S7.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. This construction is
based on a diagonal process,
out of various logarithmic complexes on the terms
of the simplicial hypercovering, as in the previous case
of normal crossing divisor,
without the ambiguity of the choice
of homotopy, because we carry resolutions of simplicial
complexes of sheaves, hence functorial in the simplicial
derived category.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p7.1" class="ltx_p">In particular, we should view the simplicial
category as a set of diagrams and the construction is carried out
in a “category of diagrams”. In fact, there exists another construction
based on the
“category of diagrams” of cubical schemes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite>, and
an alternative construction with diagrams with only four edges
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> for embedded varieties.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p8.1" class="ltx_p">In all cases, the Mixed Hodge Structure is constructed first
for smooth varieties and normal
crossing divisors, then it is deduced for general varieties. The
uniqueness follows from the compatibility of the Mixed Hodge
Structure with Poincaré duality and classical exact sequences on
cohomology.</p>
</div>
<section id="S7.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7.1. </span>MHS on cohomology of simplicial varieties</h3>

<div id="S7.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To construct
a natural Mixed Hodge Structure on the cohomology of an algebraic
variety <math id="S7.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, not necessarily smooth or compact, Deligne considers a
simplicial smooth variety <math id="S7.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi:U_{*}\to S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:U_{*}\to S</annotation></semantics></math> which is a
cohomological resolution of the original variety in the sense
that the direct image <math id="S7.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{*}\mathbb{Z}_{U_{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><msub><mi>U</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{*}\mathbb{Z}_{U_{*}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a resolution of <math id="S7.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}_{S}</annotation></semantics></math>
(descent theorem, see Theorem <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem7" title="Theorem 7.7. ‣ 7.1.8. MHS on cohomology of algebraic varieties ‣ 7.1. MHS on cohomology of simplicial varieties ‣ 7. MHS on the cohomology of a complex algebraic
variety ‣ Mixed Hodge Structures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.7</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">On each term of the simplicial resolution, which consists of the
complement of a normal crossing divisor in a smooth compact
complex variety, the various logarithmic complexes are connected
by functorial relations and form a simplicial Cohomological Mixed
Hodge Complex giving rise to the Cohomological Mixed Hodge Complex
defining the Mixed Hodge Structure we are looking for on the
cohomology of <math id="S7.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>. Although such a construction is technically
elaborate, the above abstract development of Mixed Hodge Complexes
leads easily to the result without further difficulty.</p>
</div>
<section id="S7.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">7.1.1. </span>Simplicial category</h4>

<div id="S7.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The simplicial category <math id="S7.SS1.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>
is defined by its objects, its morphisms and the composition of morphisms.
<br class="ltx_break">i) The objects of <math id="S7.SS1.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> are the subsets of integers <math id="S7.SS1.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\Delta}_{n}:=\{0,1,\ldots,n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\Delta}_{n}:=\{0,1,\ldots,n\}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S7.SS1.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\in{\hbox{\bf N}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">N</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\in{\hbox{\bf N}}</annotation></semantics></math>,
<br class="ltx_break">ii) The set of morphisms of <math id="S7.SS1.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> are the sets <math id="S7.SS1.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{p,q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{p,q}</annotation></semantics></math> of increasing mappings from
<math id="S7.SS1.SSS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\Delta}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\Delta}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> to
<math id="S7.SS1.SSS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\Delta}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\Delta}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> for integers <math id="S7.SS1.SSS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p,q\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p,q\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, with the natural
composition of mappings : <math id="S7.SS1.SSS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{pq}\times H_{qr}\rightarrow H_{pr}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{pq}\times H_{qr}\rightarrow H_{pr}</annotation></semantics></math>. 
<br class="ltx_break">Notice that <math id="S7.SS1.SSS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:{\Delta}_{p}\to{\Delta}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:{\Delta}_{p}\to{\Delta}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> is increasing in the non-strict sense
<math id="S7.SS1.SSS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\forall i&lt;j,\,f(i)\leq f(j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\forall i&lt;j,\,f(i)\leq f(j)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 7.2</span></span><span id="S7.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We define for <math id="S7.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq i\leq n+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq i\leq n+1</annotation></semantics></math> the
<math id="S7.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i-</annotation></semantics></math>th face map as the unique strictly increasing mapping such
that <math id="S7.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\not\in{\delta}_{i}({\Delta}_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∉</mo><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\not\in{\delta}_{i}({\Delta}_{n})</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="S7.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\delta}_{i}\colon{\Delta}_{n}\rightarrow{\Delta}_{n+1},\quad i\not\in{\delta}_{i}({\Delta}_{n}):=Im\,{\delta}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∉</mo><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\delta}_{i}\colon{\Delta}_{n}\rightarrow{\Delta}_{n+1},\quad i\not\in{\delta}_{i}({\Delta}_{n}):=Im\,{\delta}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS1.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The semi-simplicial category
<math id="S7.SS1.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{&gt;}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{&gt;}</annotation></semantics></math> is obtained when we consider only the strictly
increasing morphisms in <math id="S7.SS1.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>. In what follows we could
restrict the constructions to
semi-simplicial spaces which underly the simplicial spaces and
work only with semi-simplicial spaces, since we use only the face
maps.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 7.3</span></span><span id="S7.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A simplicial (resp.
co-simplicial) object <math id="S7.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{*}:=(X_{n})_{n\in\mathbb{N}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>:=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{*}:=(X_{n})_{n\in\mathbb{N}}</annotation></semantics></math> of a category
<math id="S7.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a contravariant (resp. covariant) functor from
<math id="S7.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S7.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
<br class="ltx_break">A morphism <math id="S7.Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a:X_{*}\to Y_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a:X_{*}\to Y_{*}</annotation></semantics></math> of simplicial (resp. co-simplicial)
objects is defined by its components <math id="S7.Thmtheorem3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{n}:X_{n}\to Y_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{n}:X_{n}\to Y_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> compatible
with the various maps image by the functor of simplicial morphisms
in <math id="S7.Thmtheorem3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S7.Thmtheorem3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p,q\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p,q\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS1.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The functor <math id="S7.SS1.SSS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma:{\Delta}\to{\mathcal{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma:{\Delta}\to{\mathcal{C}}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined by
<math id="S7.SS1.SSS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma(\Delta_{n}):=X_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma(\Delta_{n}):=X_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and for each <math id="S7.SS1.SSS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:{\Delta}_{p}\to{\Delta}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:{\Delta}_{p}\to{\Delta}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>, by <math id="S7.SS1.SSS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma(f):X_{q}\to X_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma(f):X_{q}\to X_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> (resp.
<math id="S7.SS1.SSS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma(f):X_{p}\to X_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma(f):X_{p}\to X_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>); <math id="S7.SS1.SSS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma(f)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma(f)</annotation></semantics></math> will be denoted by <math id="S7.SS1.SSS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{*}(f)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{*}(f)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">7.1.2. </span>Sheaves on a simplicial space</h4>

<div id="S7.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S7.SS1.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the category of topological spaces, we can define
simplicial topological spaces.
A sheaf <math id="S7.SS1.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> on a simplicial topological space
<math id="S7.SS1.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined by: 
<br class="ltx_break">1) A family of sheaves <math id="S7.SS1.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S7.SS1.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>,
<br class="ltx_break">2) For each <math id="S7.SS1.SSS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\Delta_{n}\to\Delta_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\Delta_{n}\to\Delta_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S7.SS1.SSS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{*}(f):X_{m}\to X_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{*}(f):X_{m}\to X_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>,
an <math id="S7.SS1.SSS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{*}(f)-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{*}(f)-</annotation></semantics></math>morphism <math id="S7.SS1.SSS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{*}(f)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{*}(f)</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S7.SS1.SSS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S7.SS1.SSS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, that is
<math id="S7.SS1.SSS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{*}(f)^{*}F^{n}\to F^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{*}(f)^{*}F^{n}\to F^{m}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S7.SS1.SSS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying for all <math id="S7.SS1.SSS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g:\Delta_{r}\to\Delta_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g:\Delta_{r}\to\Delta_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S7.SS1.SSS2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{*}(f\circ g)=F_{*}(f)\circ F_{*}(g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{*}(f\circ g)=F_{*}(f)\circ F_{*}(g)</annotation></semantics></math>.
<br class="ltx_break">A morphism
<math id="S7.SS1.SSS2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u:F^{*}\to G^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u:F^{*}\to G^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is a family of morphisms <math id="S7.SS1.SSS2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{n}:F^{n}\to G^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{n}:F^{n}\to G^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> such that
for all <math id="S7.SS1.SSS2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\Delta_{n}\to\Delta_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\Delta_{n}\to\Delta_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S7.SS1.SSS2.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{m}F^{*}(f)=G^{*}(f)u^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{m}F^{*}(f)=G^{*}(f)u^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>
where the left term is:</p>
<table id="S7.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{*}(f)^{*}F^{n}\rightarrow F^{m}\xrightarrow{u^{m}}G^{m}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.700em">u</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">m</mi></msup></mover><msup><mi>G</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{*}(f)^{*}F^{n}\rightarrow F^{m}\xrightarrow{u^{m}}G^{m}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">and the right term is:</p>
<table id="S7.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{*}(f)^{*}F^{n}\xrightarrow{X_{*}(f)^{*}(u_{n})}X_{*}(f)^{*}G^{n}\to G^{m}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">X</mi><mo mathsize="0.710em">∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">f</mi><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow><mo mathsize="0.710em">∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">u</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">n</mi></msub><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mover><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{*}(f)^{*}F^{n}\xrightarrow{X_{*}(f)^{*}(u_{n})}X_{*}(f)^{*}G^{n}\to G^{m}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<br class="ltx_break">
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">The image of the
<math id="S7.SS1.SSS2.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i-</annotation></semantics></math>th face map by a functor is also denoted abusively by the
same symbol
<math id="S7.SS1.SSS2.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{i}\colon X_{n+1}\rightarrow X_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{i}\colon X_{n+1}\rightarrow X_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.
<br class="ltx_break">For a ring <math id="S7.SS1.SSS2.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>, we will consider the derived category
of cosimplicial sheaves of <math id="S7.SS1.SSS2.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A-</annotation></semantics></math>modules.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS1.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">7.1.3. </span>Derived category on a simplicial space</h4>

<div id="S7.SS1.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The definition of a complex of sheaves <math id="S7.SS1.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> on a simplicial topological space
<math id="S7.SS1.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{*}</annotation></semantics></math> follows from the definition of sheaves, it has two degrees
<math id="S7.SS1.SSS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K:=K^{p,q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>:=</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K:=K^{p,q}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S7.SS1.SSS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> is the degree of the complex and <math id="S7.SS1.SSS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> is
the simplicial degree, hence for each <math id="S7.SS1.SSS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S7.SS1.SSS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K^{p,*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">∗</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K^{p,*}</annotation></semantics></math> is a
simplicial sheaf and for each <math id="S7.SS1.SSS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S7.SS1.SSS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K^{*,q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K^{*,q}</annotation></semantics></math> is a complex on
<math id="S7.SS1.SSS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS1.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A quasi-isomorphism (resp. filtered, bi-filtered) <math id="S7.SS1.SSS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma:K\to K^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma:K\to K^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> (resp. with filtrations) of simplicial complexes on <math id="S7.SS1.SSS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, is
a morphism of simplicial complexes inducing a quasi-isomorphism
<math id="S7.SS1.SSS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma^{*,q}:K^{*,q}\to{K^{\prime}}^{*,q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>K</mi><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mmultiscripts></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma^{*,q}:K^{*,q}\to{K^{\prime}}^{*,q}</annotation></semantics></math> (resp. filtered,
bi-filtered) for each space <math id="S7.SS1.SSS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS1.SSS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The definition of the derived category (resp. filtered,
bi-filtered) of the abelian category of abelian sheaves of groups
(resp. vector spaces) on a simplicial space is obtained by
inverting the quasi-isomorphisms ( resp. filtered, bi-filtered).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS1.SSS4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">7.1.4. </span></h4>

<div id="S7.SS1.SSS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A topological space <math id="S7.SS1.SSS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> defines a simplicial
constant space <math id="S7.SS1.SSS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{*}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S7.SS1.SSS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{n}=S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{n}=S</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S7.SS1.SSS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.SS1.SSS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{*}(f)=Id" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{*}(f)=Id</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S7.SS1.SSS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\in H^{p,q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\in H^{p,q}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS1.SSS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">An augmented simplicial space <math id="S7.SS1.SSS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi:X_{*}\to S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:X_{*}\to S</annotation></semantics></math>
is defined by a family of maps <math id="S7.SS1.SSS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{n}:X_{n}\to S_{n}=S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{n}:X_{n}\to S_{n}=S</annotation></semantics></math> defining a morphism of simplicial spaces.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS1.SSS5" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">7.1.5. </span></h4>

<div id="S7.SS1.SSS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If the target category <math id="S7.SS1.SSS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the category
of complex analytic spaces, we define a simplicial complex analytic space.
The structural sheaves <math id="S7.SS1.SSS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{X_{n}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{X_{n}}</annotation></semantics></math> of a simplicial
complex analytic space form a simplicial sheaf of rings.
Let <math id="S7.SS1.SSS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi:X_{*}\to S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:X_{*}\to S</annotation></semantics></math> be an augmentation to a complex analytic space <math id="S7.SS1.SSS5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>,
the de Rham complex of sheaves <math id="S7.SS1.SSS5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega^{*}_{X_{n}/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega^{*}_{X_{n}/S}</annotation></semantics></math> for various <math id="S7.SS1.SSS5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>
form
a complex of sheaves on <math id="S7.SS1.SSS5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{*}</annotation></semantics></math> denoted <math id="S7.SS1.SSS5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega^{*}_{X_{*}/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega^{*}_{X_{*}/S}</annotation></semantics></math> .</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS1.SSS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A simplicial sheaf <math id="S7.SS1.SSS5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> on a constant simplicial space <math id="S7.SS1.SSS5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{*}</annotation></semantics></math>
defined by <math id="S7.SS1.SSS5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to a co-simplicial sheaf on <math id="S7.SS1.SSS5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>; hence
if <math id="S7.SS1.SSS5.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is abelian, it defines a complex via the face maps,
with:</p>
<table id="S7.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=\sum_{i}(-1)^{i}\delta_{i}:F^{n}\to F^{n+1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mi>i</mi></munder><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=\sum_{i}(-1)^{i}\delta_{i}:F^{n}\to F^{n+1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS5.p2.2" class="ltx_p">A complex of abelian sheaves <math id="S7.SS1.SSS5.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> on
<math id="S7.SS1.SSS5.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted by <math id="S7.SS1.SSS5.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K^{n,m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K^{n,m}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S7.SS1.SSS5.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> the cosimplicial degree,
defines a simple complex <math id="S7.SS1.SSS5.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="sK" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">sK</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S7.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(sK)^{n}:=\oplus_{p+q=n}K^{pq};\quad d(x^{pq})=d_{K}(x^{pq})+\sum_{i}(-1)^{i}\delta_{i}x^{pq}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><mo rspace="0em">:=</mo><msub><mo lspace="0em">⊕</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo rspace="1.167em">;</mo><mi>d</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mi>i</mi></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(sK)^{n}:=\oplus_{p+q=n}K^{pq};\quad d(x^{pq})=d_{K}(x^{pq})+\sum_{i}(-1)^{i}\delta_{i}x^{pq}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS5.p2.3" class="ltx_p">The filtration <math id="S7.SS1.SSS5.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>
with respect to the second degree will be useful:</p>
<table id="S7.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{r}(sK)=s(K^{pq})_{q\geq r}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{r}(sK)=s(K^{pq})_{q\geq r}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS1.SSS6" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">7.1.6. </span>Direct image in the derived category of abelian sheaves
(resp. filtered, bi-filtered)</h4>

<div id="S7.SS1.SSS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For an augmented simplicial space
<math id="S7.SS1.SSS6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a:X_{*}\to S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a:X_{*}\to S</annotation></semantics></math>, we define a functor denoted <math id="S7.SS1.SSS6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Ra_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Ra_{*}</annotation></semantics></math> on
complexes <math id="S7.SS1.SSS6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> (resp. filtered <math id="S7.SS1.SSS6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(K,F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(K,F)</annotation></semantics></math>, bi-filtered <math id="S7.SS1.SSS6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(K,F,W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(K,F,W)</annotation></semantics></math>))
of abelian sheaves on <math id="S7.SS1.SSS6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{*}</annotation></semantics></math>. We may view <math id="S7.SS1.SSS6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> as a constant
simplicial scheme <math id="S7.SS1.SSS6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{*}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.SS1.SSS6.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> as a morphism <math id="S7.SS1.SSS6.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{*}:X_{*}\to S_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{*}:X_{*}\to S_{*}</annotation></semantics></math>.
In the first step we construct a complex <math id="S7.SS1.SSS6.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> (resp. <math id="S7.SS1.SSS6.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(I,F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(I,F)</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S7.SS1.SSS6.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(I,F,W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(I,F,W)</annotation></semantics></math>)of acyclic (for example flabby) sheaves,
quasi-isomorphic (resp. filtered, bi-filtered) to <math id="S7.SS1.SSS6.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> (resp.
<math id="S7.SS1.SSS6.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(K,F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(K,F)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S7.SS1.SSS6.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(K,F,W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(K,F,W)</annotation></semantics></math>)); we can always take Godement resolutions
(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite> Chap. II, §3.6 p. 95 or <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> Chap. II, §4.3
p.167) for example, then in each degree <math id="S7.SS1.SSS6.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S7.SS1.SSS6.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a_{q})_{*}I^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>I</mi><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a_{q})_{*}I^{p}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S7.SS1.SSS6.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{q}=S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{q}=S</annotation></semantics></math> defines for varying <math id="S7.SS1.SSS6.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> a cosimplicial sheaf on <math id="S7.SS1.SSS6.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> denoted
<math id="S7.SS1.SSS6.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a_{*})_{*}I^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>I</mi><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a_{*})_{*}I^{p}</annotation></semantics></math>, and a differential graded complex for varying <math id="S7.SS1.SSS6.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>,
which is a double complex whose associated
simple complex is denoted <math id="S7.SS1.SSS6.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s(a_{*})_{*}I:=Ra_{*}K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s(a_{*})_{*}I:=Ra_{*}K</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S7.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(Ra_{*}K)^{n}:=\oplus_{p+q=n}(a_{q})_{*}I^{p,q};\quad dx^{pq}=d_{I}(x^{pq})+(-1)^{p}\sum_{i=0}^{q+1}(-1)^{i}\delta_{i}x^{pq}\in(Ra_{*}K)^{n+1}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><msub><mi>a</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><mo rspace="0em">:=</mo><msub><mo lspace="0em">⊕</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msub><msup><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo rspace="1.167em">;</mo><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi></msup><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><msub><mi>a</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Ra_{*}K)^{n}:=\oplus_{p+q=n}(a_{q})_{*}I^{p,q};\quad dx^{pq}=d_{I}(x^{pq})+(-1)^{p}\sum_{i=0}^{q+1}(-1)^{i}\delta_{i}x^{pq}\in(Ra_{*}K)^{n+1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS6.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S7.SS1.SSS6.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> is the simplicial index ( <math id="S7.SS1.SSS6.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{i}(x^{pq})\in I^{p,q+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{i}(x^{pq})\in I^{p,q+1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.SS1.SSS6.p1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> is the degree. In particular for
<math id="S7.SS1.SSS6.p1.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> a point we define the hypercohomology of <math id="S7.SS1.SSS6.p1.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S7.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\Gamma(X_{*},K):=sR\Gamma^{*}(X_{*},K);\quad\mathbb{H}^{i}(X_{*},K):=H^{i}(R\Gamma(X_{*},K))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">;</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ℍ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\Gamma(X_{*},K):=sR\Gamma^{*}(X_{*},K);\quad\mathbb{H}^{i}(X_{*},K):=H^{i}(R\Gamma(X_{*},K)).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS6.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Respectively, the definition of <math id="S7.SS1.SSS6.p1.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Ra_{*}(K,F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Ra_{*}(K,F)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.SS1.SSS6.p1.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Ra_{*}(K,F,W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Ra_{*}(K,F,W)</annotation></semantics></math>is similar.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS1.SSS6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The filtration <math id="S7.SS1.SSS6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S7.SS1.SSS6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s(a_{*})_{*}I:=Ra_{*}K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s(a_{*})_{*}I:=Ra_{*}K</annotation></semantics></math> defines a
spectral sequence:</p>
<table id="S7.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="E^{pq}_{1}=R^{q}(a_{p})_{*}(K_{|X_{p}}):=H^{q}(R(a_{p})_{*}(K_{|X_{p}}))\Rightarrow H^{p+q}(Ra_{*}K):=R^{p+q}a_{*}K" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>X</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>X</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⇒</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><msub><mi>a</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><msub><mi>a</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E^{pq}_{1}=R^{q}(a_{p})_{*}(K_{|X_{p}}):=H^{q}(R(a_{p})_{*}(K_{|X_{p}}))\Rightarrow H^{p+q}(Ra_{*}K):=R^{p+q}a_{*}K</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 7.4</span></span><span id="S7.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> </span>(Topological realization)<span id="S7.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recall that a morphism of simplices <math id="S7.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\Delta_{n}\to\Delta_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\Delta_{n}\to\Delta_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> has a geometric
realization <math id="S7.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|f|:|\Delta_{n}|\to|\Delta_{m}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>f</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|f|:|\Delta_{n}|\to|\Delta_{m}|</annotation></semantics></math> as the affine map
defined when we identify a simplex <math id="S7.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> with the vertices of
its affine realization in <math id="S7.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}^{\Delta_{n}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}^{\Delta_{n}}</annotation></semantics></math>. We construct the
topological realization of a topological semi-simplicial space
<math id="S7.Thmtheorem4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{*}</annotation></semantics></math> as the quotient of the topological space <math id="S7.Thmtheorem4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y=\coprod_{n\geq 0}X_{n}\times|\Delta_{n}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∐</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y=\coprod_{n\geq 0}X_{n}\times|\Delta_{n}|</annotation></semantics></math> by the equivalence relation <math id="S7.Thmtheorem4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math>
generated by the identifications:</p>
<table id="S7.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\forall f:\Delta_{n}\to\Delta_{m},x\in X_{m},a\in|\Delta_{n}|,\quad(x,|f|(a))\equiv(X_{*}(f)(x),a)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\forall f:\Delta_{n}\to\Delta_{m},x\in X_{m},a\in|\Delta_{n}|,\quad(x,|f|(a))\equiv(X_{*}(f)(x),a)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The topological realization <math id="S7.Thmtheorem4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|X_{*}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|X_{*}|</annotation></semantics></math> is the quotient space of
<math id="S7.Thmtheorem4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>, modulo the relation <math id="S7.Thmtheorem4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math>, with its quotient topology.
The construction above of the cohomology amounts to the computation of the
cohomology of the topological space <math id="S7.Thmtheorem4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|X_{*}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|X_{*}|</annotation></semantics></math> with coefficient in
an abelian group <math id="S7.Thmtheorem4.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S7.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{i}(X_{*},A)\simeq H^{i}(|X_{*}|,A)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{i}(X_{*},A)\simeq H^{i}(|X_{*}|,A).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS1.SSS7" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">7.1.7. </span>Cohomological descent</h4>

<div id="S7.SS1.SSS7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S7.SS1.SSS7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a:X_{*}\to S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a:X_{*}\to S</annotation></semantics></math> be an
augmented simplicial scheme; any abelian sheaf <math id="S7.SS1.SSS7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S7.SS1.SSS7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, lifts
to a sheaf <math id="S7.SS1.SSS7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{*}F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{*}F</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S7.SS1.SSS7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{*}</annotation></semantics></math> and we have a natural morphism:</p>
<table id="S7.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(a):F\to Ra_{*}a^{*}F\,\,{\rm in}\,\,D^{+}(S)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0.340em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>in</mi><mo lspace="0.340em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(a):F\to Ra_{*}a^{*}F\,\,{\rm in}\,\,D^{+}(S).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 7.5</span></span><span id="S7.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(cohomological descent)<span id="S7.Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The morphism <math id="S7.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a:X_{*}\to S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a:X_{*}\to S</annotation></semantics></math> is of
cohomological descent if the natural morphism <math id="S7.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(a)</annotation></semantics></math> is an
isomorphism in <math id="S7.Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{+}(S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{+}(S)</annotation></semantics></math> for all abelian sheaves <math id="S7.Thmtheorem5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S7.Thmtheorem5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS1.SSS7.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS7.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The definition
amounts to the following conditions:</p>
<table id="S7.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="F\xrightarrow{\sim}Ker(a_{0*}a_{0}^{*}F\xrightarrow{\delta_{1}-\delta_{0}}a_{1}^{*}F);\quad R^{i}a_{*}a^{*}F=0\,\,{\rm for}\,\,i&gt;0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo></mover><mi>K</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mi>F</mi><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">δ</mi><mn mathsize="0.710em">1</mn></msub><mo mathsize="0.700em">−</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">δ</mi><mn mathsize="0.710em">0</mn></msub></mrow></mover><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">;</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><msub><mi>a</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>for</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\xrightarrow{\sim}Ker(a_{0*}a_{0}^{*}F\xrightarrow{\delta_{1}-\delta_{0}}a_{1}^{*}F);\quad R^{i}a_{*}a^{*}F=0\,\,{\rm for}\,\,i&gt;0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS7.p2.2" class="ltx_p">In this case for all complexes <math id="S7.SS1.SSS7.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S7.SS1.SSS7.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{+}(S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{+}(S)</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S7.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\Gamma(S,K)\simeq R\Gamma(X_{*},a^{*}K)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\Gamma(S,K)\simeq R\Gamma(X_{*},a^{*}K)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS7.p2.3" class="ltx_p">and we have a spectral sequence:</p>
<table id="S7.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E^{pq}_{1}=\mathbb{H}^{q}(X_{p},a_{p}^{*}K)\Rightarrow\mathbb{H}^{p+q}(S,K),\quad d_{1}=\sum_{i}(-1)^{i}\delta_{i}:E_{1}^{p,q}\to E_{1}^{p+1,q}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℍ</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⇒</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℍ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mi>i</mi></munder><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E^{pq}_{1}=\mathbb{H}^{q}(X_{p},a_{p}^{*}K)\Rightarrow\mathbb{H}^{p+q}(S,K),\quad d_{1}=\sum_{i}(-1)^{i}\delta_{i}:E_{1}^{p,q}\to E_{1}^{p+1,q}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS1.SSS8" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">7.1.8. </span>MHS on cohomology of algebraic varieties</h4>

<div id="S7.SS1.SSS8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A simplicial complex variety
<math id="S7.SS1.SSS8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth (resp. proper) if every <math id="S7.SS1.SSS8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth (resp.
compact).</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 7.6</span></span><span id="S7.Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(NCD)<span id="S7.Thmtheorem6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A simplicial Normal Crossing Divisor is a family <math id="S7.Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{n}\subset X_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{n}\subset X_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>
of normal crossing divisors such that the family of open subsets
<math id="S7.Thmtheorem6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{n}:=X_{n}-Y_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{n}:=X_{n}-Y_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> form a simplicial subvariety <math id="S7.Thmtheorem6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{*}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S7.Thmtheorem6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{*}</annotation></semantics></math>,
hence
the family of filtered logarithmic complexes <math id="S7.Thmtheorem6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\Omega^{*}_{X_{n}}(LogY_{n}))_{n\geq 0},W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\Omega^{*}_{X_{n}}(LogY_{n}))_{n\geq 0},W)</annotation></semantics></math> form a filtered complex on <math id="S7.Thmtheorem6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{*}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS1.SSS8.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS8.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The following theorem is admitted here:</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 7.7</span></span><span id="S7.Thmtheorem7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> 6.2.8) For each separated complex variety <math id="S7.Thmtheorem7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, 
<br class="ltx_break">i) There exist a simplicial variety proper and smooth <math id="S7.Thmtheorem7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{*}</annotation></semantics></math> over
<math id="S7.Thmtheorem7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℂ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>,
containing a normal crossing divisor <math id="S7.Thmtheorem7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{*}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S7.Thmtheorem7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{*}</annotation></semantics></math> and
an augmentation <math id="S7.Thmtheorem7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a:U_{*}=(X_{*}-Y_{*})\to S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a:U_{*}=(X_{*}-Y_{*})\to S</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying the cohomological descent property.
<br class="ltx_break">Hence for all abelian
sheaves <math id="S7.Thmtheorem7.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S7.Thmtheorem7.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, we have an isomorphism <math id="S7.Thmtheorem7.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\xrightarrow{\sim}Ra_{*}a^{*}F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo></mover><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\xrightarrow{\sim}Ra_{*}a^{*}F</annotation></semantics></math>.
<br class="ltx_break">ii) Moreover, for each morphism <math id="S7.Thmtheorem7.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:S\to S^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:S\to S^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists a
morphism
<math id="S7.Thmtheorem7.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{*}:X_{*}\to X^{\prime}_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{*}:X_{*}\to X^{\prime}_{*}</annotation></semantics></math> of simplicial varieties proper and smooth
with normal crossing divisors <math id="S7.Thmtheorem7.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{*}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.Thmtheorem7.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y^{\prime}_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mo>∗</mo><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y^{\prime}_{*}</annotation></semantics></math> and augmented complements
<math id="S7.Thmtheorem7.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a:U_{*}\to S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a:U_{*}\to S</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.Thmtheorem7.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{\prime}:U^{\prime}_{*}\to S^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mo>∗</mo><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{\prime}:U^{\prime}_{*}\to S^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying the
cohomological descent property, with
<math id="S7.Thmtheorem7.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(U_{*})\subset U^{\prime}_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mo>∗</mo><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(U_{*})\subset U^{\prime}_{*}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.Thmtheorem7.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{\prime}\circ f=a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{\prime}\circ f=a</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS1.SSS8.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS8.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The proof is based on Hironaka’s desingularisation theorem and on
a general contruction of hypercoverings described briefly by
Deligne in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> after preliminaries on the general theory
of hypercoverings. The desingularisation is carried at each step
of the construction by induction.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 7.8</span></span><span id="S7.Thmtheorem8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We can assume the Normal Crossing Divisor with smooth
irreducible components.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS1.SSS9" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">7.1.9. </span></h4>

<div id="S7.SS1.SSS9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">An <math id="S7.SS1.SSS9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>-Cohomological Mixed Hodge Complex <math id="S7.SS1.SSS9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> (CMHC) on a
topological simplicial space <math id="S7.SS1.SSS9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{*}</annotation></semantics></math> consists of:
<br class="ltx_break">i) A complex <math id="S7.SS1.SSS9.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{A}</annotation></semantics></math> of sheaves of <math id="S7.SS1.SSS9.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A-</annotation></semantics></math>modules on <math id="S7.SS1.SSS9.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{*}</annotation></semantics></math> such that
<math id="S7.SS1.SSS9.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{H}^{k}(X_{*},K_{A})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℍ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{H}^{k}(X_{*},K_{A})</annotation></semantics></math> are <math id="S7.SS1.SSS9.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>-modules of finite type, and <math id="S7.SS1.SSS9.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{H}^{*}(X_{*},K_{A})\otimes\mathbb{Q}\xrightarrow{\sim}\mathbb{H}^{*}(X_{*},K_{A}\otimes\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ℍ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo></mover><mrow><msup><mi>ℍ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{H}^{*}(X_{*},K_{A})\otimes\mathbb{Q}\xrightarrow{\sim}\mathbb{H}^{*}(X_{*},K_{A}\otimes\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math>,
<br class="ltx_break">ii) A filtered complex
<math id="S7.SS1.SSS9.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(K_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}},W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(K_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}},W)</annotation></semantics></math> of filtered sheaves of <math id="S7.SS1.SSS9.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\otimes\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\otimes\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> modules on <math id="S7.SS1.SSS9.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{*}</annotation></semantics></math> with an increasing filtration <math id="S7.SS1.SSS9.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> and an
isomorphism
<math id="S7.SS1.SSS9.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}}\simeq K_{A}\otimes\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}}\simeq K_{A}\otimes\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> in the derived category on
<math id="S7.SS1.SSS9.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{*}</annotation></semantics></math>,
<br class="ltx_break">iii) A bi-filitered complex <math id="S7.SS1.SSS9.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(K_{\mathbb{C}},W,F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(K_{\mathbb{C}},W,F)</annotation></semantics></math> of sheaves of
complex vector spaces on <math id="S7.SS1.SSS9.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{*}</annotation></semantics></math> with an increasing
(resp. decreasing) filtration <math id="S7.SS1.SSS9.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> (resp. <math id="S7.SS1.SSS9.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> ) and an isomorphism
<math id="S7.SS1.SSS9.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha:(K_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}},W)\otimes\mathbb{C}\xrightarrow{\sim}(K_{\mathbb{C}},W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha:(K_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}},W)\otimes\mathbb{C}\xrightarrow{\sim}(K_{\mathbb{C}},W)</annotation></semantics></math>
in the derived category on <math id="S7.SS1.SSS9.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{*}</annotation></semantics></math>.
<br class="ltx_break">Moreover, the following axiom is satisfied 
<br class="ltx_break">(CMHC) The restriction of <math id="S7.SS1.SSS9.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> to each <math id="S7.SS1.SSS9.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is an <math id="S7.SS1.SSS9.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>-Cohomological
Mixed Hodge Complex .</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS1.SSS10" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">7.1.10. </span></h4>

<div id="S7.SS1.SSS10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS10.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S7.SS1.SSS10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{*}</annotation></semantics></math> be a simplicial complex compact smooth
algebraic variety with <math id="S7.SS1.SSS10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{*}</annotation></semantics></math> a Normal Crossing Divisor in <math id="S7.SS1.SSS10.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{*}</annotation></semantics></math>
such that <math id="S7.SS1.SSS10.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j:U_{*}=(X_{*}-Y_{*})\to X_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j:U_{*}=(X_{*}-Y_{*})\to X_{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is an open simplicial
embedding, then</p>
<table id="S7.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Rj_{*}\mathbb{Z},(Rj_{*}\mathbb{Q},\tau_{\leq}),(\Omega^{*}_{X_{*}}(LogY_{*}),W,F))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mo>≤</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Rj_{*}\mathbb{Z},(Rj_{*}\mathbb{Q},\tau_{\leq}),(\Omega^{*}_{X_{*}}(LogY_{*}),W,F))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS10.p1.2" class="ltx_p">is a Cohomological Mixed Hodge Complex on <math id="S7.SS1.SSS10.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{*}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS1.SSS11" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">7.1.11. </span></h4>

<div id="S7.SS1.SSS11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS11.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If we apply the global section functor to
an <math id="S7.SS1.SSS11.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>-Cohomological Mixed Hodge Complex <math id="S7.SS1.SSS11.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S7.SS1.SSS11.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, we get an
<math id="S7.SS1.SSS11.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>-cosimplicial Mixed Hodge
Complex defined as follows:
<br class="ltx_break">1) A cosimplicial complex <math id="S7.SS1.SSS11.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\Gamma^{*}K_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\Gamma^{*}K_{A}</annotation></semantics></math> in the derived category of cosimplicial
<math id="S7.SS1.SSS11.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A-</annotation></semantics></math>modules,
<br class="ltx_break">2 ) A filtered cosimplicial complex <math id="S7.SS1.SSS11.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\Gamma^{*}(K_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}},W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\Gamma^{*}(K_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}},W)</annotation></semantics></math> in the derived category of filtered cosimplicial vector
spaces, and an isomorphism <math id="S7.SS1.SSS11.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(R\Gamma^{*}K_{A})\otimes\mathbb{Q}\simeq R\Gamma^{*}(K_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(R\Gamma^{*}K_{A})\otimes\mathbb{Q}\simeq R\Gamma^{*}(K_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math>. 
<br class="ltx_break">3) A bi-filtered cosimplicial complex <math id="S7.SS1.SSS11.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\Gamma^{*}(K_{\mathbb{C}},W,F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\Gamma^{*}(K_{\mathbb{C}},W,F)</annotation></semantics></math>
in the derived category of bi-filtered cosimplicial
vector spaces,
<br class="ltx_break">4) An isomorphism <math id="S7.SS1.SSS11.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\Gamma^{*}(K_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}},W)\otimes\mathbb{C}\simeq R\Gamma^{*}(K_{\mathbb{C}},W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\Gamma^{*}(K_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}},W)\otimes\mathbb{C}\simeq R\Gamma^{*}(K_{\mathbb{C}},W)</annotation></semantics></math> in the
derived category of filtered cosimplicial vector spaces.
<br class="ltx_break">The hypercohomology
of a cosimplicial <math id="S7.SS1.SSS11.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>-cohomological mixed Hodge complex on <math id="S7.SS1.SSS11.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is
such a complex.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS1.SSS12" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">7.1.12. </span>Diagonal filtration</h4>

<div id="S7.SS1.SSS12.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS12.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To a cosimplicial mixed
Hodge complex <math id="S7.SS1.SSS12.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>, we associate here a differential graded complex
which is viewed as a double complex whose associated simple
complex is denoted <math id="S7.SS1.SSS12.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="sK" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">sK</annotation></semantics></math>. We put on <math id="S7.SS1.SSS12.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="sK" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">sK</annotation></semantics></math> a weight filtration by a
diagonal process.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 7.9</span></span><span id="S7.Thmtheorem9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Differential
graded <math id="S7.Thmtheorem9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>-MHC)<span id="S7.Thmtheorem9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A differential graded <math id="S7.Thmtheorem9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="DG^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">DG^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> ( or a complex of
graded objects) is a bounded below complex with two degrees, the
first defined by the complex and the second by the gradings. It
can be viewed as a double
complex.
<br class="ltx_break">A differential graded <math id="S7.Thmtheorem9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>-Mixed Hodge Complex is defined by a system of
<math id="S7.Thmtheorem9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="DG^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">DG^{+}</annotation></semantics></math>-complex
(resp. filtered, bi-filtered):</p>
<table id="S7.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{A},(K_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}},W),K_{A}\otimes\mathbb{Q}\simeq K_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}},(K_{\mathbb{C}},W,F),(K_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}},W)\otimes\mathbb{C}\simeq(K_{\mathbb{C}},W)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><mo>≃</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{A},(K_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}},W),K_{A}\otimes\mathbb{Q}\simeq K_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}},(K_{\mathbb{C}},W,F),(K_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}},W)\otimes\mathbb{C}\simeq(K_{\mathbb{C}},W)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem9.p1.2" class="ltx_p">such that for each degree
<math id="S7.Thmtheorem9.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> of the grading, the component <math id="S7.Thmtheorem9.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(K_{\mathbb{C}}^{*,n},W,F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mi>ℂ</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(K_{\mathbb{C}}^{*,n},W,F)</annotation></semantics></math> underlies
an <math id="S7.Thmtheorem9.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>-Mixed Hodge Complex.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS1.SSS12.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS12.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A cosimplicial Mixed
Hodge Complex <math id="S7.SS1.SSS12.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(K,W,F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(K,W,F)</annotation></semantics></math> defines a <math id="S7.SS1.SSS12.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="DG^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">DG^{+}</annotation></semantics></math>-<math id="S7.SS1.SSS12.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>-Mixed Hodge Complex</p>
<table id="S7.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="sK_{A},(sK_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}},W),sK_{A}\otimes\mathbb{Q}\simeq sK_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}},(sK_{\mathbb{C}},W,F),(sK_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}},W)\otimes\mathbb{C}\simeq(sK_{\mathbb{C}},W)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">sK_{A},(sK_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}},W),sK_{A}\otimes\mathbb{Q}\simeq sK_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}},(sK_{\mathbb{C}},W,F),(sK_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}},W)\otimes\mathbb{C}\simeq(sK_{\mathbb{C}},W)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS12.p2.2" class="ltx_p">the degree of the grading is the cosimplicial degree.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 7.10</span></span><span id="S7.Thmtheorem10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Diagonal filtration)<span id="S7.Thmtheorem10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem10.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The diagonal filtration
<math id="S7.Thmtheorem10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta(W,L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta(W,L)</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S7.Thmtheorem10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.Thmtheorem10.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S7.Thmtheorem10.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="sK" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">sK</annotation></semantics></math> is defined by:</p>
<table id="S7.Ex19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\delta(W,L)_{n}(sK)^{i}:=\oplus_{p+q=i}W_{n+q}K^{p,q}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup><mo rspace="0em">:=</mo><msub><mo lspace="0em">⊕</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta(W,L)_{n}(sK)^{i}:=\oplus_{p+q=i}W_{n+q}K^{p,q}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem10.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S7.Thmtheorem10.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{r}(sK)=s(K^{p,q})_{q\geq r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{r}(sK)=s(K^{p,q})_{q\geq r}</annotation></semantics></math>. For a bi-filtered
complex <math id="S7.Thmtheorem10.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(K,W,F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(K,W,F)</annotation></semantics></math> with a decreasing <math id="S7.Thmtheorem10.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>, the sum over <math id="S7.Thmtheorem10.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> is
natural (not diagonal).</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS1.SSS13" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">7.1.13. </span>Properties</h4>

<div id="S7.SS1.SSS13.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS13.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We have:</p>
<table id="S7.Ex20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="Gr^{\delta(W,L)}_{n}(sK)\simeq\oplus_{p}Gr^{W}_{n+p}K^{*,p}[-p]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">≃</mo><msub><mo lspace="0em">⊕</mo><mi>p</mi></msub><mi>G</mi><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr^{\delta(W,L)}_{n}(sK)\simeq\oplus_{p}Gr^{W}_{n+p}K^{*,p}[-p]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS13.p1.2" class="ltx_p">In the case of a <math id="S7.SS1.SSS13.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="DG^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">DG^{+}</annotation></semantics></math>-complex
defined as the hypercohomology of a complex <math id="S7.SS1.SSS13.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(K,W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(K,W)</annotation></semantics></math> on a
simplicial space <math id="S7.SS1.SSS13.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have:</p>
<table id="S7.Ex21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="Gr^{\delta(W,L)}_{n}R\Gamma K\simeq\oplus_{p}R\Gamma(X_{p},Gr^{W}_{n+p}K^{*,p})[-p]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>K</mi><mo rspace="0em">≃</mo><msub><mo lspace="0em">⊕</mo><mi>p</mi></msub><mi>R</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>G</mi><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr^{\delta(W,L)}_{n}R\Gamma K\simeq\oplus_{p}R\Gamma(X_{p},Gr^{W}_{n+p}K^{*,p})[-p].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS13.p1.3" class="ltx_p">and for a bi-filtered complex with a decreasing <math id="S7.SS1.SSS13.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S7.Ex22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="Gr^{\delta(W,L)}_{n}R(\Gamma K,F)\simeq\oplus_{p}R\Gamma(X_{p},(Gr^{W}_{n+p}K,F))[-p]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">≃</mo><msub><mo lspace="0em">⊕</mo><mi>p</mi></msub><mi>R</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr^{\delta(W,L)}_{n}R(\Gamma K,F)\simeq\oplus_{p}R\Gamma(X_{p},(Gr^{W}_{n+p}K,F))[-p].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS13.p1.4" class="ltx_p">Next we remark:</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem11" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem11.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 7.11</span></span><span id="S7.Thmtheorem11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem11.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem11.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="S7.Thmtheorem11.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H=(H_{A},W,F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H=(H_{A},W,F)</annotation></semantics></math> is an <math id="S7.Thmtheorem11.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>-Mixed Hodge Structure, a filtration <math id="S7.Thmtheorem11.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>
of <math id="S7.Thmtheorem11.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{A}</annotation></semantics></math> is a filtration of Mixed Hodge Structure, if and only
if, for all <math id="S7.Thmtheorem11.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Gr^{n}_{L}H_{A},Gr^{n}_{L}(W),Gr^{n}_{L}(F))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>L</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>L</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>L</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Gr^{n}_{L}H_{A},Gr^{n}_{L}(W),Gr^{n}_{L}(F))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem11.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem11.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">is an
<math id="S7.Thmtheorem11.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>-mixed Hodge structure.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem12" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem12.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 7.12</span></span><span id="S7.Thmtheorem12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Deligne (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> thm. 8.1.15))<span id="S7.Thmtheorem12.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem12.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem12.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem12.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S7.Thmtheorem12.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> be a graded differential
<math id="S7.Thmtheorem12.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>-Mixed Hodge Complex (for example,
defined by a cosimplicial <math id="S7.Thmtheorem12.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>-mixed Hodge complex).
<br class="ltx_break">i) Then, <math id="S7.Thmtheorem12.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(sK,\delta(W,L),F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(sK,\delta(W,L),F)</annotation></semantics></math> is an <math id="S7.Thmtheorem12.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>-Mixed Hodge Complex. 
<br class="ltx_break">The first terms of the
weight spectral sequence:</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex24.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\delta(W,L)}E_{1}^{pq}(sK\otimes\mathbb{Q})=\oplus_{n}H^{q-n}(Gr^{W}_{n}K^{*,p+n})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><msub><mo lspace="0em">⊕</mo><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\delta(W,L)}E_{1}^{pq}(sK\otimes\mathbb{Q})=\oplus_{n}H^{q-n}(Gr^{W}_{n}K^{*,p+n})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem12.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem12.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">form the simple complex <math id="S7.Thmtheorem12.p1.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(_{\delta(W,L)}E_{1}^{pq},d_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(_{\delta(W,L)}E_{1}^{pq},d_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> of
<math id="S7.Thmtheorem12.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\otimes\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\otimes\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-Hodge structures of
weight <math id="S7.Thmtheorem12.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>
associated to the double complex where <math id="S7.Thmtheorem12.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=n+p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=n+p</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.Thmtheorem12.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{1}^{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{1}^{pq}</annotation></semantics></math>
is represented by the sum of the terms on the diagonal :</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{array}[]{ccccc}H^{q-(n+1)}(Gr^{W}_{n+1}K^{*,m+1})&amp;\xrightarrow{\partial}&amp;H^{q-n}(Gr^{W}_{n}K^{*,m+1})&amp;\xrightarrow{\partial}&amp;H^{q-(n-1)}(Gr^{W}_{n-1}K^{*,m+1})\\
d^{\prime\prime}\uparrow&amp;&amp;d^{\prime\prime}\uparrow&amp;&amp;d^{\prime\prime}\uparrow\\
H^{q-(n+1)}(Gr^{W}_{n+1}K^{*,m})&amp;\xrightarrow{\partial}&amp;H^{q-n}(Gr^{W}_{n}K^{*,m})&amp;\xrightarrow{\partial}&amp;H^{q-(n-1)}(Gr^{W}_{n-1}K^{*,m})\end{array}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">∂</mo></mover></mtd><mtd><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">∂</mo></mover></mtd><mtd><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">∂</mo></mover></mtd><mtd><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">∂</mo></mover></mtd><mtd><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{array}[]{ccccc}H^{q-(n+1)}(Gr^{W}_{n+1}K^{*,m+1})&amp;\xrightarrow{\partial}&amp;H^{q-n}(Gr^{W}_{n}K^{*,m+1})&amp;\xrightarrow{\partial}&amp;H^{q-(n-1)}(Gr^{W}_{n-1}K^{*,m+1})\\
d^{\prime\prime}\uparrow&amp;&amp;d^{\prime\prime}\uparrow&amp;&amp;d^{\prime\prime}\uparrow\\
H^{q-(n+1)}(Gr^{W}_{n+1}K^{*,m})&amp;\xrightarrow{\partial}&amp;H^{q-n}(Gr^{W}_{n}K^{*,m})&amp;\xrightarrow{\partial}&amp;H^{q-(n-1)}(Gr^{W}_{n-1}K^{*,m})\end{array}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem12.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem12.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="S7.Thmtheorem12.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∂</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial</annotation></semantics></math> is a connecting morphism and <math id="S7.Thmtheorem12.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is
simplicial.
<br class="ltx_break">ii) The terms <math id="S7.Thmtheorem12.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}_{L}E_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mmultiscripts><mi>E</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mi>r</mi><mrow></mrow><mi>L</mi><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{L}E_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S7.Thmtheorem12.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, of the spectral sequence defined by <math id="S7.Thmtheorem12.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(sK_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}},L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(sK_{A\otimes\mathbb{Q}},L)</annotation></semantics></math>
are endowed with a natural <math id="S7.Thmtheorem12.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>-Mixed Hodge Structure, with
differentials <math id="S7.Thmtheorem12.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>
compatible with such structures. 
<br class="ltx_break">iii) The filtration <math id="S7.Thmtheorem12.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S7.Thmtheorem12.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}(sK)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}(sK)</annotation></semantics></math> is a filtration in the
category of Mixed Hodge Structures and:</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex26" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr^{p}_{L}(H^{p+q}((sK),\delta(W,L)[p+q],F)=({{}_{L}E}_{\infty}^{pq},W,F)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>L</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>E</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mi>L</mi><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr^{p}_{L}(H^{p+q}((sK),\delta(W,L)[p+q],F)=({{}_{L}E}_{\infty}^{pq},W,F).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS1.SSS14" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">7.1.14. </span></h4>

<div id="S7.SS1.SSS14.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS14.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the case of a smooth simplicial variety complement of
a normal crossing divisor at infinity,
the cohomology groups <math id="S7.SS1.SSS14.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{n}(U_{*},\mathbb{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{n}(U_{*},\mathbb{Z})</annotation></semantics></math> are
endowed with the Mixed Hodge Structure defined
by the following Mixed Hodge Complex:</p>
<table id="S7.Ex27" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex27.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="R\Gamma(U_{*},\mathbb{Z}),\,\,R\Gamma(U_{*},\mathbb{Q}),\delta(W,L)),R\Gamma(U_{*},\Omega^{*}_{X_{*}}(LogY_{*})),\delta(W,L)),F)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.507em">,</mo><mi>R</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mi>o</mi><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>Y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\Gamma(U_{*},\mathbb{Z}),\,\,R\Gamma(U_{*},\mathbb{Q}),\delta(W,L)),R\Gamma(U_{*},\Omega^{*}_{X_{*}}(LogY_{*})),\delta(W,L)),F)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS14.p1.2" class="ltx_p">with natural compatibility isomorphisms,
satisfying:</p>
<table id="S7.Ex28" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex28.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="Gr^{\delta(W,L)}_{n}R\Gamma(U_{*},\mathbb{Q})\simeq\oplus_{m}Gr^{W}_{n+m}R\Gamma(U_{m},,\mathbb{Q}))[-m]\simeq\oplus_{m}R\Gamma(Y^{n+m}_{m},\mathbb{Q})[-n-2m]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">≃</mo><msub><mo lspace="0em">⊕</mo><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>G</mi><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mo rspace="0em">≃</mo><msub><mo lspace="0em">⊕</mo><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>R</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr^{\delta(W,L)}_{n}R\Gamma(U_{*},\mathbb{Q})\simeq\oplus_{m}Gr^{W}_{n+m}R\Gamma(U_{m},,\mathbb{Q}))[-m]\simeq\oplus_{m}R\Gamma(Y^{n+m}_{m},\mathbb{Q})[-n-2m]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS14.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where the first
isomorphism corresponds to the diagonal filtration and the second
to the logarithmic complex for the open set <math id="S7.SS1.SSS14.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>; recall that
<math id="S7.SS1.SSS14.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y^{n+m}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y^{n+m}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the disjoint union of intersections of <math id="S7.SS1.SSS14.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n+m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+m</annotation></semantics></math>
components of the normal crossing divisor <math id="S7.SS1.SSS14.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> of simplicial
degree <math id="S7.SS1.SSS14.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover:</p>
<table id="S7.Ex29" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex29.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\delta(W,L)}E_{1}^{p,q}=\oplus_{n}H^{q-2n}(Y^{n}_{n+p},Q)\Rightarrow H^{p+q}(U_{*},\mathbb{Q})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><msub><mo lspace="0em">⊕</mo><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⇒</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\delta(W,L)}E_{1}^{p,q}=\oplus_{n}H^{q-2n}(Y^{n}_{n+p},Q)\Rightarrow H^{p+q}(U_{*},\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS14.p1.4" class="ltx_p">The filtration <math id="S7.SS1.SSS14.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>
induces on <math id="S7.SS1.SSS14.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}_{\delta(W,L)}E_{1}^{p,q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mmultiscripts><mi>E</mi><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mn>1</mn><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\delta(W,L)}E_{1}^{p,q}</annotation></semantics></math> a Hodge Structure of weight
<math id="S7.SS1.SSS14.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> and the differentials <math id="S7.SS1.SSS14.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> are compatible with the Hodge
Structures. The term <math id="S7.SS1.SSS14.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is the simple complex associated to the
double complex of Hodge Structure of weight <math id="S7.SS1.SSS14.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S7.SS1.SSS14.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is an
alternating Gysin map:</p>
<table id="S7.Ex30" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{array}[]{ccccc}H^{q-(2n+2)}(Y^{n+1}_{p+n+1},\mathbb{Q})&amp;\xrightarrow{G}&amp;H^{q-2n}({Y}^{n}_{p+n+1},\mathbb{Q})&amp;\xrightarrow{G}&amp;H^{q-2(n-2)}({Y}^{n-1}_{p+n+1},\mathbb{Q})\\
\sum_{i}(-1)^{i}\delta_{i}\uparrow&amp;&amp;\sum_{i}(-1)^{i}\delta_{i}\uparrow&amp;&amp;\sum_{i}(-1)^{i}\delta_{i}\uparrow\\
H^{q-(2n+2)}(Y^{n+1}_{p+n},\mathbb{Q})&amp;\xrightarrow{G}&amp;H^{q-2n}({Y}^{n}_{p+n},\mathbb{Q})&amp;\xrightarrow{G}&amp;H^{q-2(n-2)}({Y}^{n-1}_{p+n},\mathbb{Q})\end{array}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝐺</mo></mover></mtd><mtd><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝐺</mo></mover></mtd><mtd><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub></mstyle><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub></mstyle><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub></mstyle><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝐺</mo></mover></mtd><mtd><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝐺</mo></mover></mtd><mtd><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{array}[]{ccccc}H^{q-(2n+2)}(Y^{n+1}_{p+n+1},\mathbb{Q})&amp;\xrightarrow{G}&amp;H^{q-2n}({Y}^{n}_{p+n+1},\mathbb{Q})&amp;\xrightarrow{G}&amp;H^{q-2(n-2)}({Y}^{n-1}_{p+n+1},\mathbb{Q})\\
\sum_{i}(-1)^{i}\delta_{i}\uparrow&amp;&amp;\sum_{i}(-1)^{i}\delta_{i}\uparrow&amp;&amp;\sum_{i}(-1)^{i}\delta_{i}\uparrow\\
H^{q-(2n+2)}(Y^{n+1}_{p+n},\mathbb{Q})&amp;\xrightarrow{G}&amp;H^{q-2n}({Y}^{n}_{p+n},\mathbb{Q})&amp;\xrightarrow{G}&amp;H^{q-2(n-2)}({Y}^{n-1}_{p+n},\mathbb{Q})\end{array}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS14.p1.5" class="ltx_p">where the Hodge Structure on the columns are twisted respectively
by <math id="S7.SS1.SSS14.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(-n-1),(-n),(-n+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(-n-1),(-n),(-n+1)</annotation></semantics></math>, the lines are defined by the
logarithmic complex, while the vertical differentials are
simplicial. We deduce from the general theory:</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem13" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem13.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 7.13</span></span><span id="S7.Thmtheorem13.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem13.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem13.p1.1" class="ltx_p">i) The Mixed Hodge Structure on <math id="S7.Thmtheorem13.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{n}(U_{*},\mathbb{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{n}(U_{*},\mathbb{Z})</annotation></semantics></math> is defined by the
graded differential mixed Hodge complex associated to the
simplicial Mixed Hodge Complex defined by the logarithmic complex
on each term of <math id="S7.Thmtheorem13.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{*}</annotation></semantics></math> and it is functorial in the
couple <math id="S7.Thmtheorem13.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(U_{*},X_{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(U_{*},X_{*})</annotation></semantics></math>,
<br class="ltx_break">ii) The rational weight spectral sequence degenerates at rank <math id="S7.Thmtheorem13.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>
and the Hodge Structure on <math id="S7.Thmtheorem13.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> induced by <math id="S7.Thmtheorem13.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is
isomorphic to the Hodge Structure on <math id="S7.Thmtheorem13.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr_{W}H^{n}(U_{*},\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr_{W}H^{n}(U_{*},\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math>.
<br class="ltx_break">iii)The Hodge numbers <math id="S7.Thmtheorem13.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{pq}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S7.Thmtheorem13.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{n}(U_{*},\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{n}(U_{*},\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> vanish for <math id="S7.Thmtheorem13.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\notin[0,n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∉</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\notin[0,n]</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S7.Thmtheorem13.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\notin[0,n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∉</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\notin[0,n]</annotation></semantics></math>.
<br class="ltx_break">iv) For <math id="S7.Thmtheorem13.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{*}=\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{*}=\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>, the Hodge numbers <math id="S7.Thmtheorem13.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{pq}</annotation></semantics></math> of
<math id="S7.Thmtheorem13.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{n}(X_{*},\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{n}(X_{*},\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> vanish for <math id="S7.Thmtheorem13.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\notin[0,n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∉</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\notin[0,n]</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S7.Thmtheorem13.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\notin[0,n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∉</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\notin[0,n]</annotation></semantics></math> or
<math id="S7.Thmtheorem13.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p+q&gt;n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p+q&gt;n</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem14" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem14.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 7.14</span></span><span id="S7.Thmtheorem14.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem14.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem14.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The Mixed Hodge Structure on the cohomology
of a complex algebraic variety
<math id="S7.Thmtheorem14.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{n}(X,\mathbb{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{n}(X,\mathbb{Z})</annotation></semantics></math> is defined by any logarithmic simplicial resolution of
<math id="S7.Thmtheorem14.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> via the isomorphism
with <math id="S7.Thmtheorem14.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{n}(U_{*},\mathbb{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{n}(U_{*},\mathbb{Z})</annotation></semantics></math> defined by the augmentation <math id="S7.Thmtheorem14.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a:U.\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a:U.\to X</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS1.SSS14.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS14.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The Mixed Hodge Structure just defined does not depend on the
resolution and is functorial in <math id="S7.SS1.SSS14.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> since we can reduce to the
case where a morphism <math id="S7.SS1.SSS14.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:X\to Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:X\to Z</annotation></semantics></math> is covered by a morphism of
hypercoverings.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS1.SSS15" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">7.1.15. </span>Problems</h4>

<div id="S7.SS1.SSS15.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS15.p1.1" class="ltx_p">1) Let <math id="S7.SS1.SSS15.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i:Y\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i:Y\to X</annotation></semantics></math> be a closed subvariety
of <math id="S7.SS1.SSS15.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.SS1.SSS15.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j:U:=X-Y\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j:U:=X-Y\to X</annotation></semantics></math> the embedding of the complement.
Then the two long exact sequences of cohomology:</p>
<table id="S7.Ex31" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}&amp;\ldots\to H^{i}(X,X-Y,\mathbb{Z})\to H^{i}(X,\mathbb{Z})\to H^{i}(Y,\mathbb{Z})\to H^{i+1}_{Y}(X,\mathbb{Z})\to\ldots\\
&amp;\ldots\to H^{i}_{Y}(X,\mathbb{Z})\to H^{i}(X,\mathbb{Z})\to H^{i}(X-Y,\mathbb{Z})\to H^{i+1}_{Y}(X,\mathbb{Z})\to\ldots\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}&amp;\ldots\to H^{i}(X,X-Y,\mathbb{Z})\to H^{i}(X,\mathbb{Z})\to H^{i}(Y,\mathbb{Z})\to H^{i+1}_{Y}(X,\mathbb{Z})\to\ldots\\
&amp;\ldots\to H^{i}_{Y}(X,\mathbb{Z})\to H^{i}(X,\mathbb{Z})\to H^{i}(X-Y,\mathbb{Z})\to H^{i+1}_{Y}(X,\mathbb{Z})\to\ldots\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS15.p1.2" class="ltx_p">underly exact sequences of Mixed Hodge Structure.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS1.SSS15.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS15.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The idea is to use a simplicial hypercovering of the morphism <math id="S7.SS1.SSS15.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>
in order to define two Mixed Hodge Complexes: <math id="S7.SS1.SSS15.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K(Y)</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S7.SS1.SSS15.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S7.SS1.SSS15.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K(X)</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S7.SS1.SSS15.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> with a well defined morphism on the level of
complexes <math id="S7.SS1.SSS15.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i^{*}:K(X)\to K(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i^{*}:K(X)\to K(Y)</annotation></semantics></math> (resp. <math id="S7.SS1.SSS15.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j^{*}:K(X)\to K(X-Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j^{*}:K(X)\to K(X-Y)</annotation></semantics></math>),
then the long exact sequence is associated to the mixed cone
<math id="S7.SS1.SSS15.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{M}(i^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{M}(i^{*})</annotation></semantics></math>(resp. <math id="S7.SS1.SSS15.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{M}(j^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{M}(j^{*})</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS1.SSS15.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS15.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In particular, one deduce associated long exact sequences by
taking the graded spaces with respect to the filtrations <math id="S7.SS1.SSS15.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S7.SS1.SSS15.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS1.SSS15.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS15.p4.1" class="ltx_p">2) <span id="S7.SS1.SSS15.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Künneth formula</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite>. Let <math id="S7.SS1.SSS15.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.SS1.SSS15.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> be two
algebraic
varieties, then the isomorphisms of cohomology vector spaces:</p>
<table id="S7.Ex32" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex32.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="H^{i}(X\times Y,\mathbb{C})\simeq\oplus_{r+s=i}H^{r}(X,\mathbb{C})\otimes H^{s}(Y,\mathbb{C})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">≃</mo><msub><mo lspace="0em">⊕</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{i}(X\times Y,\mathbb{C})\simeq\oplus_{r+s=i}H^{r}(X,\mathbb{C})\otimes H^{s}(Y,\mathbb{C})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS15.p4.2" class="ltx_p">underly isomorphisms of <math id="S7.SS1.SSS15.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-mixed Hodge structure.
The answer is in two steps:
<br class="ltx_break">i) Consider the tensor product of two mixed Hodge complex defining
the mixed Hodge structure of <math id="S7.SS1.SSS15.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S7.SS1.SSS15.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> and deduce the right term, direct sum of tensor product
of mixed Hodge structures.
<br class="ltx_break">ii) Construct a quasi-isomorphism of the tensor product with a
mixed Hodge complex defining the mixed Hodge structure of <math id="S7.SS1.SSS15.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\times Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\times Y</annotation></semantics></math>.
<br class="ltx_break">iii) Deduce that the cup product on the cohomology of an algebraic
variety is compatible with mixed Hodge structure.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7.2. </span>MHS on the
cohomology of a complete embedded algebraic variety</h3>

<div id="S7.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For embedded varieties into smooth varieties,
the mixed Hodge structure on cohomology can be deduced by a simple
method using
exact sequences, once the mixed Hodge structure for normal
crossing divisor has been constructed, which should easily
convince of the natural aspect of this theory. The technical
ingredients consist of Poincaré duality and its dual the trace
(or Gysin ) morphism.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S7.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p:X^{\prime}\rightarrow X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p:X^{\prime}\rightarrow X</annotation></semantics></math> be a proper morphism of complex smooth
varieties of same dimension, <math id="S7.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> a closed subvariety of <math id="S7.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S7.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> = <math id="S7.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{-1}(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{-1}(Y)</annotation></semantics></math>. We suppose that <math id="S7.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is a Normal Crossing
Divisor in <math id="S7.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and the restriction of <math id="S7.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> induces an isomorphism
<math id="S7.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{/X^{\prime}-Y^{\prime}}:X^{\prime}-Y^{\prime}\xrightarrow{\sim}X-Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>/</mo><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo></mover><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{/X^{\prime}-Y^{\prime}}:X^{\prime}-Y^{\prime}\xrightarrow{\sim}X-Y</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S7.Ex33" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{array}[]{ccccc}Y^{\prime}&amp;\xrightarrow{i^{\prime}}&amp;X^{\prime}&amp;\xleftarrow{j^{\prime}}&amp;X^{\prime}-Y^{\prime}\\
\quad\downarrow{p_{Y}}&amp;&amp;\quad\downarrow{p}&amp;&amp;\quad\downarrow{p_{X^{\prime}-Y^{\prime}}}\\
Y&amp;\xrightarrow{i}&amp;X&amp;\xleftarrow{j}&amp;X-Y\end{array}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mtd><mtd><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.700em">i</mi><mo mathsize="0.710em">′</mo></msup></mover></mtd><mtd><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mtd><mtd><mover accent="true"><mo>←</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.700em">j</mi><mo mathsize="0.710em">′</mo></msup></mover></mtd><mtd><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="1.278em" stretchy="false">↓</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="1.278em" stretchy="false">↓</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="1.278em" stretchy="false">↓</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>Y</mi></mtd><mtd><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑖</mo></mover></mtd><mtd><mi>X</mi></mtd><mtd><mover accent="true"><mo>←</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑗</mo></mover></mtd><mtd><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{array}[]{ccccc}Y^{\prime}&amp;\xrightarrow{i^{\prime}}&amp;X^{\prime}&amp;\xleftarrow{j^{\prime}}&amp;X^{\prime}-Y^{\prime}\\
\quad\downarrow{p_{Y}}&amp;&amp;\quad\downarrow{p}&amp;&amp;\quad\downarrow{p_{X^{\prime}-Y^{\prime}}}\\
Y&amp;\xrightarrow{i}&amp;X&amp;\xleftarrow{j}&amp;X-Y\end{array}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">The trace morphism <math id="S7.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Trp" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Trp</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as Poincaré dual to the
inverse image <math id="S7.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> on cohomology, hence the <math id="S7.SS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Trp" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Trp</annotation></semantics></math> is compatible
with the Hodge Structures. It can be defined at the level of sheaf
resolutions of <math id="S7.SS2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}_{X^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}_{X^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.SS2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> as constructed by Verdier
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite>, that is in derived category <math id="S7.SS2.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Tr\,p\colon Rp_{*}\mathbb{Z}_{X^{\prime}}\rightarrow\mathbb{Z}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Tr\,p\colon Rp_{*}\mathbb{Z}_{X^{\prime}}\rightarrow\mathbb{Z}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>
hence we deduce
by restriction morphisms depending on
the embeddings of <math id="S7.SS2.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.SS2.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S7.SS2.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.
<br class="ltx_break"><math id="S7.SS2.p2.m19" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(Tr\,p)/_{Y}:Rp_{*}\mathbb{Z}_{Y^{\prime}}\rightarrow\mathbb{Z}_{Y},\,(Tr\,p)/_{Y}:H^{i}(Y^{\prime},\mathbb{Z})\rightarrow H^{i}(Y,\mathbb{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mo rspace="0em">/</mo><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>R</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mo rspace="0em">/</mo><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Tr\,p)/_{Y}:Rp_{*}\mathbb{Z}_{Y^{\prime}}\rightarrow\mathbb{Z}_{Y},\,(Tr\,p)/_{Y}:H^{i}(Y^{\prime},\mathbb{Z})\rightarrow H^{i}(Y,\mathbb{Z})</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S7.SS2.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Trp:H_{c}^{i}(Y^{\prime},\mathbb{Z})\rightarrow H_{c}^{i}(Y,\mathbb{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Trp:H_{c}^{i}(Y^{\prime},\mathbb{Z})\rightarrow H_{c}^{i}(Y,\mathbb{Z})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem15" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem15.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 7.15</span></span><span id="S7.Thmtheorem15.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem15.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem15.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S7.Thmtheorem15.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> be a neighbourhood of <math id="S7.Thmtheorem15.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S7.Thmtheorem15.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, retract by deformation
onto <math id="S7.Thmtheorem15.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S7.Thmtheorem15.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{\prime}=p^{-1}(U)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>U</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{\prime}=p^{-1}(U)</annotation></semantics></math> is a retract by deformation onto
<math id="S7.Thmtheorem15.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>; this is the case if <math id="S7.Thmtheorem15.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> is a sub-variety of <math id="S7.Thmtheorem15.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the
morphism <math id="S7.Thmtheorem15.p1.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(Tr\,p)/_{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mo>/</mo><mi>Y</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Tr\,p)/_{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> is deduced from <math id="S7.Thmtheorem15.p1.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="Tr(p/_{U})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mo rspace="0em">/</mo><mi>U</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Tr(p/_{U})</annotation></semantics></math> in the diagram:</p>
<table id="S7.Ex34" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{array}[]{ccc}H^{i}(Y^{\prime},\mathbb{Z})&amp;\xleftarrow{\sim}&amp;H^{i}(U^{\prime},\mathbb{Z})\\
\quad\downarrow{(Tr\,p)/_{Y}}&amp;&amp;\quad\downarrow{Tr(p/_{U})}\\
H^{i}(Y,\mathbb{Z})&amp;\xleftarrow{\sim}&amp;H^{i}(U,\mathbb{Z})\end{array}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd><mover accent="true"><mo>←</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo></mover></mtd><mtd><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>U</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>/</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>Y</mi></msub></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd><mrow><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><mi>T</mi><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo rspace="0em">/</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>U</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd><mover accent="true"><mo>←</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo></mover></mtd><mtd><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{array}[]{ccc}H^{i}(Y^{\prime},\mathbb{Z})&amp;\xleftarrow{\sim}&amp;H^{i}(U^{\prime},\mathbb{Z})\\
\quad\downarrow{(Tr\,p)/_{Y}}&amp;&amp;\quad\downarrow{Tr(p/_{U})}\\
H^{i}(Y,\mathbb{Z})&amp;\xleftarrow{\sim}&amp;H^{i}(U,\mathbb{Z})\end{array}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Consider now the diagram:</p>
<table id="S7.Ex35" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex35.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{array}[]{ccccc}R\Gamma_{c}(X^{\prime}-Y^{\prime},\mathbb{Z})&amp;\xrightarrow{j^{\prime}_{*}}&amp;R\Gamma(X^{\prime},\mathbb{Z})&amp;\xrightarrow{{i^{\prime}}^{*}}&amp;R\Gamma(Y^{\prime},\mathbb{Z})\\
Trp\downarrow&amp;&amp;\downarrow{}Trp&amp;&amp;\downarrow{}(Trp)_{|Y}\\
R\Gamma_{c}(X-Y,\mathbb{Z})&amp;\xrightarrow{j_{*}}&amp;R\Gamma(X,\mathbb{Z})&amp;\xrightarrow{i^{*}}&amp;R\Gamma(Y,\mathbb{Z})\end{array}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.700em">j</mi><mo mathsize="0.710em">∗</mo><mo mathsize="0.710em">′</mo></msubsup></mover></mtd><mtd><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mmultiscripts><mi mathsize="0.700em">i</mi><mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.710em">′</mo><mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.710em">∗</mo></mmultiscripts></mover></mtd><mtd><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd><mrow><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">j</mi><mo mathsize="0.710em">∗</mo></msub></mover></mtd><mtd><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.700em">i</mi><mo mathsize="0.710em">∗</mo></msup></mover></mtd><mtd><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{array}[]{ccccc}R\Gamma_{c}(X^{\prime}-Y^{\prime},\mathbb{Z})&amp;\xrightarrow{j^{\prime}_{*}}&amp;R\Gamma(X^{\prime},\mathbb{Z})&amp;\xrightarrow{{i^{\prime}}^{*}}&amp;R\Gamma(Y^{\prime},\mathbb{Z})\\
Trp\downarrow&amp;&amp;\downarrow{}Trp&amp;&amp;\downarrow{}(Trp)_{|Y}\\
R\Gamma_{c}(X-Y,\mathbb{Z})&amp;\xrightarrow{j_{*}}&amp;R\Gamma(X,\mathbb{Z})&amp;\xrightarrow{i^{*}}&amp;R\Gamma(Y,\mathbb{Z})\end{array}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem16" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem16.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 7.16</span></span><span id="S7.Thmtheorem16.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem16.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem16.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> i) The morphism <math id="S7.Thmtheorem16.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{Y}^{*}\colon H^{i}(Y,\mathbb{Z})\rightarrow H^{i}(Y^{\prime},\mathbb{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>Y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{Y}^{*}\colon H^{i}(Y,\mathbb{Z})\rightarrow H^{i}(Y^{\prime},\mathbb{Z})</annotation></semantics></math> is injective with retraction <math id="S7.Thmtheorem16.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Tr\,p)_{/Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>/</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Tr\,p)_{/Y}</annotation></semantics></math>.
<br class="ltx_break">ii) We have a quasi-isomorphism of
<math id="S7.Thmtheorem16.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{*}\mathbb{Z}_{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{*}\mathbb{Z}_{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> with the cone <math id="S7.Thmtheorem16.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C({i^{\prime}}^{*}-Tr\,p)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>i</mi><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></mmultiscripts><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C({i^{\prime}}^{*}-Tr\,p)</annotation></semantics></math> of the morphism
<math id="S7.Thmtheorem16.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{i^{\prime}}^{*}-Tr\,p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>i</mi><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></mmultiscripts><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{i^{\prime}}^{*}-Tr\,p</annotation></semantics></math>. The long exact sequence associated to the
cone splits into short exact sequences:</p>
<table id="S7.Ex36" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex36.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\rightarrow H^{i}(X^{\prime},\mathbb{Z})\xrightarrow{{i^{\prime}}^{*}-Tr\,p}H^{i}(Y^{\prime},\mathbb{Z})\oplus H^{i}(X,\mathbb{Z})\xrightarrow{(Tr\,p)_{/Y}+i^{*}}H^{i}(Y,\mathbb{Z})\rightarrow 0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi mathsize="0.700em">i</mi><mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.710em">′</mo><mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.710em">∗</mo></mmultiscripts><mo mathsize="0.700em">−</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">r</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">p</mi></mrow></mrow></mover><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">r</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">p</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo maxsize="0.710em" minsize="0.710em" stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo><mi mathsize="0.710em">Y</mi></mrow></msub><mo mathsize="0.700em">+</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.700em">i</mi><mo mathsize="0.710em">∗</mo></msup></mrow></mover><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\rightarrow H^{i}(X^{\prime},\mathbb{Z})\xrightarrow{{i^{\prime}}^{*}-Tr\,p}H^{i}(Y^{\prime},\mathbb{Z})\oplus H^{i}(X,\mathbb{Z})\xrightarrow{(Tr\,p)_{/Y}+i^{*}}H^{i}(Y,\mathbb{Z})\rightarrow 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem16.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Moreover <math id="S7.Thmtheorem16.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{i^{\prime}}^{*}-Tr\,p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>i</mi><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></mmultiscripts><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{i^{\prime}}^{*}-Tr\,p</annotation></semantics></math> is a morphism of mixed Hodge structures.
In particular,
the weight of <math id="S7.Thmtheorem16.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{i}(Y,\mathbb{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{i}(Y,\mathbb{C})</annotation></semantics></math> vary in the interval
[<math id="S7.Thmtheorem16.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0,i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0,i</annotation></semantics></math>] since this is true for <math id="S7.Thmtheorem16.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.Thmtheorem16.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem17" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem17.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 7.17</span></span><span id="S7.Thmtheorem17.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem17.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem17.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The mixed Hodge structure of <math id="S7.Thmtheorem17.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as cokernel of
<math id="S7.Thmtheorem17.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{i^{\prime}}^{*}-Tr\,p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>i</mi><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></mmultiscripts><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{i^{\prime}}^{*}-Tr\,p</annotation></semantics></math> via its isomorphism with <math id="S7.Thmtheorem17.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{i}(Y,\mathbb{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{i}(Y,\mathbb{Z})</annotation></semantics></math>, induced by
<math id="S7.Thmtheorem17.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Tr\,p)_{/Y}+i^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>/</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Tr\,p)_{/Y}+i^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>. It coincides with Deligne’s mixed Hodge
structure.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">This result
shows the uniqueness of the theory of mixed Hodge structure, once
the Mixed Hodge Structure of the normal crossing divisor <math id="S7.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> has
been constructed. The above technique consists in the
realization of the Mixed Hodge Structure on the cohomology of <math id="S7.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>
as relative cohomology with Mixed Hodge Structures on <math id="S7.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S7.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S7.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> all smooth proper or normal crossing divisor. Notice that
the Mixed Hodge Structure on <math id="S7.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{i}(Y,\mathbb{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{i}(Y,\mathbb{Z})</annotation></semantics></math> is realized as a
quotient and not as an extension.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem18" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem18.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 7.18</span></span><span id="S7.Thmtheorem18.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem18.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem18.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S7.Thmtheorem18.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p:X^{\prime}\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p:X^{\prime}\to X</annotation></semantics></math> be a desingularization of a
complete variety <math id="S7.Thmtheorem18.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, then for all integers <math id="S7.Thmtheorem18.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</p>
<table id="S7.Ex37" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex37.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{i-1}H^{i}(X,\mathbb{Q})=Ker\,(H^{i}(X,\mathbb{Q})\xrightarrow{p^{*}}H^{i}(X^{\prime},\mathbb{Q})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.700em">p</mi><mo mathsize="0.710em">∗</mo></msup></mover><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{i-1}H^{i}(X,\mathbb{Q})=Ker\,(H^{i}(X,\mathbb{Q})\xrightarrow{p^{*}}H^{i}(X^{\prime},\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S7.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i:Y\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i:Y\to X</annotation></semantics></math> be the subvariety of singular points in <math id="S7.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and
let <math id="S7.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y^{\prime}:=p^{-1}(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y^{\prime}:=p^{-1}(Y)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S7.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i^{\prime}:Y^{\prime}\to X^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i^{\prime}:Y^{\prime}\to X^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, then we have a long
exact sequence:</p>
<table id="S7.Ex38" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex38.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{i-1}(Y^{\prime},\mathbb{Q})\to H^{i}(X,\mathbb{Q})\xrightarrow{(p^{*},-i^{*})}H^{i}(X^{\prime},\mathbb{Q})\oplus H^{i}(Y,\mathbb{Q})\xrightarrow{i^{\prime*}+p_{|Y^{\prime}}^{*}}H^{i}(Y^{\prime},\mathbb{Q})\ldots" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.700em">p</mi><mo mathsize="0.710em">∗</mo></msup><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="0.700em">−</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.700em">i</mi><mo mathsize="0.710em">∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mover><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">i</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo mathsize="0.710em">′</mo><mo lspace="0.222em">⁣</mo><mo mathsize="0.710em">∗</mo></mrow></msup><mo mathsize="0.700em">+</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo fence="false" maxsize="0.710em" minsize="0.710em" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mi mathsize="0.710em">Y</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.710em">∗</mo></msup></mrow></mover><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{i-1}(Y^{\prime},\mathbb{Q})\to H^{i}(X,\mathbb{Q})\xrightarrow{(p^{*},-i^{*})}H^{i}(X^{\prime},\mathbb{Q})\oplus H^{i}(Y,\mathbb{Q})\xrightarrow{i^{\prime*}+p_{|Y^{\prime}}^{*}}H^{i}(Y^{\prime},\mathbb{Q})\ldots</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS2.p5.2" class="ltx_p">Since the weight of <math id="S7.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{i-1}(Y^{\prime},\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{i-1}(Y^{\prime},\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S7.SS2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq i-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq i-1</annotation></semantics></math>, we deduce an
injective morphism <math id="S7.SS2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr^{W}_{i}H^{i}(X,\mathbb{Q})\xrightarrow{(p^{*},-i^{*})}Gr^{W}_{i}H^{i}(X^{\prime},\mathbb{Q})\oplus Gr^{W}_{i}H^{i}(Y,\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.700em">p</mi><mo mathsize="0.710em">∗</mo></msup><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="0.700em">−</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.700em">i</mi><mo mathsize="0.710em">∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mover><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr^{W}_{i}H^{i}(X,\mathbb{Q})\xrightarrow{(p^{*},-i^{*})}Gr^{W}_{i}H^{i}(X^{\prime},\mathbb{Q})\oplus Gr^{W}_{i}H^{i}(Y,\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math>. It is enough to
prove for any element <math id="S7.SS2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in Gr^{W}_{i}H^{i}(X,\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in Gr^{W}_{i}H^{i}(X,\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S7.SS2.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr^{W}_{i}(p^{*})(a)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr^{W}_{i}(p^{*})(a)=0</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S7.SS2.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr^{W}_{i}(i^{*})(a)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr^{W}_{i}(i^{*})(a)=0</annotation></semantics></math>; which follows from <math id="S7.SS2.p5.m11" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="Gr^{W}_{i}(p_{|Y^{\prime}}^{*}\circ i^{*})(a))=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>Y</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr^{W}_{i}(p_{|Y^{\prime}}^{*}\circ i^{*})(a))=0</annotation></semantics></math> if we prove <math id="S7.SS2.p5.m12" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="Gr^{W}_{i}(p_{|Y^{\prime}}^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>Y</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr^{W}_{i}(p_{|Y^{\prime}}^{*})</annotation></semantics></math> is injective. By induction on dim.<math id="S7.SS2.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>, we may assume
the injectivity for a resolution <math id="S7.SS2.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{Y}\to Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{Y}\to Y</annotation></semantics></math>. There exists a
subvariety <math id="S7.SS2.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\subset Y^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\subset Y^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>generically covering <math id="S7.SS2.p5.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>, then a
desingularization <math id="S7.SS2.p5.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>Z</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S7.SS2.p5.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{\prime}\times_{Y}\tilde{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Z</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>Y</mi></msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{\prime}\times_{Y}\tilde{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> is a
ramified covering of <math id="S7.SS2.p5.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>, hence <math id="S7.SS2.p5.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{i}(\tilde{Y},\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{i}(\tilde{Y},\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> injects
into <math id="S7.SS2.p5.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{i}(\tilde{Z},\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>Z</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{i}(\tilde{Z},\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math>. We deduce that <math id="S7.SS2.p5.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{i}(Y,\mathbb{Q})\to H^{i}(\tilde{Z},\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>Z</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{i}(Y,\mathbb{Q})\to H^{i}(\tilde{Z},\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> is injective and in particular the factor
<math id="S7.SS2.p5.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{i}(Y,\mathbb{Q})\to H^{i}(Y^{\prime},\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{i}(Y,\mathbb{Q})\to H^{i}(Y^{\prime},\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> is also injective.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem19" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem19.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 7.19</span></span><span id="S7.Thmtheorem19.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> </span>(Mixed Hodge Structure on the cohomology of an embedded algebraic
variety)<span id="S7.Thmtheorem19.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem19.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem19.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The construction still apply for non proper varieties if
we construct
the Mixed Hodge Structure of an open normal crossing divisor.
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem19.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Hypothesis</span>. Let <math id="S7.Thmtheorem19.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{Z}:Z\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{Z}:Z\to X</annotation></semantics></math> a closed embedding and <math id="S7.Thmtheorem19.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{X}:X\to P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{X}:X\to P</annotation></semantics></math>
a closed embedding in a projective space (or any proper smooth complex
algebraic variety). By Hironaka desingularization we
construct a diagram:</p>
<table id="S7.Ex39" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex39.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{array}[]{ccccc}Z^{\prime\prime}&amp;\rightarrow&amp;X^{\prime\prime}&amp;\rightarrow&amp;P^{\prime\prime}\\
\downarrow&amp;&amp;\downarrow&amp;&amp;\downarrow\\
Z^{\prime}&amp;\rightarrow&amp;X^{\prime}&amp;\rightarrow&amp;P^{\prime}\\
\downarrow&amp;&amp;\downarrow&amp;&amp;\downarrow\\
Z&amp;\rightarrow&amp;X&amp;\rightarrow&amp;P\\
\end{array}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msup><mi>Z</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></mtd><mtd><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mtd><mtd><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></mtd><mtd><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mtd><mtd><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msup><mi>Z</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mtd><mtd><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mtd><mtd><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mtd><mtd><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mtd><mtd><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>Z</mi></mtd><mtd><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mtd><mtd><mi>X</mi></mtd><mtd><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mtd><mtd><mi>P</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{array}[]{ccccc}Z^{\prime\prime}&amp;\rightarrow&amp;X^{\prime\prime}&amp;\rightarrow&amp;P^{\prime\prime}\\
\downarrow&amp;&amp;\downarrow&amp;&amp;\downarrow\\
Z^{\prime}&amp;\rightarrow&amp;X^{\prime}&amp;\rightarrow&amp;P^{\prime}\\
\downarrow&amp;&amp;\downarrow&amp;&amp;\downarrow\\
Z&amp;\rightarrow&amp;X&amp;\rightarrow&amp;P\\
\end{array}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem19.p1.2" class="ltx_p">first by blowing up centers over <math id="S7.Thmtheorem19.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> so to obtain a smooth space
<math id="S7.Thmtheorem19.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p:P^{\prime}\to P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p:P^{\prime}\to P</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S7.Thmtheorem19.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{\prime}:=p^{-1}(Z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Z</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{\prime}:=p^{-1}(Z)</annotation></semantics></math> is a normal crossing
divisor; set <math id="S7.Thmtheorem19.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\prime}:=p^{-1}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\prime}:=p^{-1}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>, then:</p>
<table id="S7.Ex40" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex40.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p|:X^{\prime}-Z^{\prime}\xrightarrow{\sim}X-Z,\quad p|:P^{\prime}-Z^{\prime}\xrightarrow{\sim}P-Z" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>X</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>Z</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo></mover><mi>X</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>P</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>Z</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo></mover><mi>P</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p|:X^{\prime}-Z^{\prime}\xrightarrow{\sim}X-Z,\quad p|:P^{\prime}-Z^{\prime}\xrightarrow{\sim}P-Z</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem19.p1.3" class="ltx_p">are isomorphisms since the modifications are all over <math id="S7.Thmtheorem19.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>.
Next, by blowing up centers over <math id="S7.Thmtheorem19.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> we obtain a smooth space
<math id="S7.Thmtheorem19.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q:P^{\prime\prime}\to P^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q:P^{\prime\prime}\to P^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S7.Thmtheorem19.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\prime\prime}:=q^{-1}(X^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>q</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\prime\prime}:=q^{-1}(X^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.Thmtheorem19.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{\prime\prime}:=q^{-1}(Z^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Z</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>q</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>Z</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{\prime\prime}:=q^{-1}(Z^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>
are normal crossing divisor, and <math id="S7.Thmtheorem19.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q|:P^{\prime\prime}-X^{\prime\prime}\xrightarrow{\sim}P^{\prime}-X^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo></mover><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q|:P^{\prime\prime}-X^{\prime\prime}\xrightarrow{\sim}P^{\prime}-X^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, we deduce the diagram:</p>
<table id="S7.Ex41" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex41.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{array}[]{ccccc}X^{\prime\prime}-Z^{\prime\prime}&amp;\xrightarrow{i^{\prime\prime}_{X}}&amp;P^{\prime\prime}-Z^{\prime\prime}&amp;\xleftarrow{j^{\prime\prime}}&amp;P^{\prime\prime}-X^{\prime\prime}\\
q_{X}\downarrow&amp;&amp;q\downarrow&amp;&amp;q\downarrow\!\wr\\
X^{\prime}-Z^{\prime}&amp;\xrightarrow{i^{\prime}_{X}}&amp;P^{\prime}-Z^{\prime}&amp;\xleftarrow{j^{\prime}}&amp;P^{\prime}-X^{\prime}\\
\end{array}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>Z</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></mrow></mtd><mtd><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.700em">i</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">X</mi><mo mathsize="0.710em">′′</mo></msubsup></mover></mtd><mtd><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>Z</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></mrow></mtd><mtd><mover accent="true"><mo>←</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.700em">j</mi><mo mathsize="0.710em">′′</mo></msup></mover></mtd><mtd><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd><mrow><mi>q</mi><mpadded style="width:0.330em;" width="0.330em"><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo></mpadded><mo>≀</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>Z</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mtd><mtd><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.700em">i</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">X</mi><mo mathsize="0.710em">′</mo></msubsup></mover></mtd><mtd><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>Z</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mtd><mtd><mover accent="true"><mo>←</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.700em">j</mi><mo mathsize="0.710em">′</mo></msup></mover></mtd><mtd><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{array}[]{ccccc}X^{\prime\prime}-Z^{\prime\prime}&amp;\xrightarrow{i^{\prime\prime}_{X}}&amp;P^{\prime\prime}-Z^{\prime\prime}&amp;\xleftarrow{j^{\prime\prime}}&amp;P^{\prime\prime}-X^{\prime\prime}\\
q_{X}\downarrow&amp;&amp;q\downarrow&amp;&amp;q\downarrow\!\wr\\
X^{\prime}-Z^{\prime}&amp;\xrightarrow{i^{\prime}_{X}}&amp;P^{\prime}-Z^{\prime}&amp;\xleftarrow{j^{\prime}}&amp;P^{\prime}-X^{\prime}\\
\end{array}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem19.p1.4" class="ltx_p">Since all modification are above <math id="S7.Thmtheorem19.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, we still have an
isomorphism induced by <math id="S7.Thmtheorem19.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> at right. For dim.<math id="S7.Thmtheorem19.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P=d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P=d</annotation></semantics></math> and all
integers <math id="S7.Thmtheorem19.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, the morphism <math id="S7.Thmtheorem19.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q^{*}:H^{2d-i}_{c}(P^{\prime\prime}-Z^{\prime\prime},\mathbb{Q})\to H^{2d-i}_{c}(P^{\prime}-Z^{\prime},\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>Z</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>Z</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q^{*}:H^{2d-i}_{c}(P^{\prime\prime}-Z^{\prime\prime},\mathbb{Q})\to H^{2d-i}_{c}(P^{\prime}-Z^{\prime},\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> is well defined on cohomology with
compact support since <math id="S7.Thmtheorem19.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> is proper; its Poincaré dual is called
the trace morphism <math id="S7.Thmtheorem19.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Trq:H^{i}(P^{\prime\prime}-Z^{\prime\prime},\mathbb{Q})\to H^{i}(P^{\prime}-Z^{\prime},\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>Z</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>Z</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Trq:H^{i}(P^{\prime\prime}-Z^{\prime\prime},\mathbb{Q})\to H^{i}(P^{\prime}-Z^{\prime},\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> and satisfy the relation <math id="S7.Thmtheorem19.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Trq\,\circ\,q^{*}\,=\,Id" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.392em" rspace="0.392em">∘</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.448em">=</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Trq\,\circ\,q^{*}\,=\,Id</annotation></semantics></math>.
Moreover, the trace morphism is defined as a morphism of sheaves
<math id="S7.Thmtheorem19.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{*}\mathbb{Z}_{P^{\prime\prime}-Z^{\prime\prime}}\to\mathbb{Z}_{P^{\prime}-Z^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>Z</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>Z</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{*}\mathbb{Z}_{P^{\prime\prime}-Z^{\prime\prime}}\to\mathbb{Z}_{P^{\prime}-Z^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite>, hence an induced trace morphism
<math id="S7.Thmtheorem19.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Trq)|(X^{\prime\prime}-Z^{\prime\prime}):H^{i}(X^{\prime\prime}-Z^{\prime\prime},\mathbb{Q})\to H^{i}(X^{\prime}-Z^{\prime},\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>Z</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>Z</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>Z</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Trq)|(X^{\prime\prime}-Z^{\prime\prime}):H^{i}(X^{\prime\prime}-Z^{\prime\prime},\mathbb{Q})\to H^{i}(X^{\prime}-Z^{\prime},\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> is well
defined.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem20" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem20.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 7.20</span></span><span id="S7.Thmtheorem20.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem20.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem20.p1.1" class="ltx_p">With the notations of the above diagram, we have
short exact sequences:</p>
<table id="S7.Ex42" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex42.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}0\to H^{i}(P^{\prime\prime}-Z^{\prime\prime},\mathbb{Q})\xrightarrow{(i^{\prime\prime}_{X})^{*}-Trq}&amp;H^{i}(X^{\prime\prime}-Z^{\prime\prime},\mathbb{Q})\oplus H^{i}(P^{\prime}-Z^{\prime},\mathbb{Q})\\
&amp;\xrightarrow{(i^{\prime}_{X})^{*}-(Trq)|(X^{\prime\prime}-Z^{\prime\prime})}H^{i}(X^{\prime}-Z^{\prime},\mathbb{Q})\to 0\\
\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>Z</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.700em">i</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">X</mi><mo mathsize="0.710em">′′</mo></msubsup><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow><mo mathsize="0.710em">∗</mo></msup><mo mathsize="0.700em">−</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">q</mi></mrow></mrow></mover><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>Z</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>Z</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.700em">i</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">X</mi><mo mathsize="0.710em">′</mo></msubsup><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow><mo mathsize="0.710em">∗</mo></msup><mo mathsize="0.700em">−</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">q</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathsize="0.700em">X</mi><mo mathsize="0.710em">′′</mo></msup><mo mathsize="0.700em">−</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.700em">Z</mi><mo mathsize="0.710em">′′</mo></msup></mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mover><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>Z</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}0\to H^{i}(P^{\prime\prime}-Z^{\prime\prime},\mathbb{Q})\xrightarrow{(i^{\prime\prime}_{X})^{*}-Trq}&amp;H^{i}(X^{\prime\prime}-Z^{\prime\prime},\mathbb{Q})\oplus H^{i}(P^{\prime}-Z^{\prime},\mathbb{Q})\\
&amp;\xrightarrow{(i^{\prime}_{X})^{*}-(Trq)|(X^{\prime\prime}-Z^{\prime\prime})}H^{i}(X^{\prime}-Z^{\prime},\mathbb{Q})\to 0\\
\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem19.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem19.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Since we have a vertical isomorphism <math id="S7.Thmtheorem19.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> at right of the above
diagram, we deduce a long exact sequence of cohomology spaces
containing the sequences of the proposition; the injectivity of
<math id="S7.Thmtheorem19.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i^{\prime\prime}_{X})^{*}-Trq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>i</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i^{\prime\prime}_{X})^{*}-Trq</annotation></semantics></math> and the surjectivity of <math id="S7.Thmtheorem19.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i^{\prime}_{X})^{*}-(Trq)|X^{\prime\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>i</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i^{\prime}_{X})^{*}-(Trq)|X^{\prime\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> are deduced from <math id="S7.Thmtheorem19.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Trq\circ q^{*}=Id" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Trq\circ q^{*}=Id</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.Thmtheorem19.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Trq)|(X^{\prime\prime}-Z^{\prime\prime})\circ q^{*}|(X^{\prime}-Z^{\prime})=Id" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>Z</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>Z</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Trq)|(X^{\prime\prime}-Z^{\prime\prime})\circ q^{*}|(X^{\prime}-Z^{\prime})=Id</annotation></semantics></math>, hence the long exact
sequence splits into short exact sequences.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem21" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem21.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 7.21</span></span><span id="S7.Thmtheorem21.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem21.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem21.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The cohomology <math id="S7.Thmtheorem21.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{i}(X-Z,\mathbb{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{i}(X-Z,\mathbb{Z})</annotation></semantics></math> is isomorphic to <math id="S7.Thmtheorem21.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{i}(X^{\prime}-Z^{\prime},\mathbb{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>Z</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{i}(X^{\prime}-Z^{\prime},\mathbb{Z})</annotation></semantics></math> since <math id="S7.Thmtheorem21.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X-Z\simeq X^{\prime}-Z^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>Z</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X-Z\simeq X^{\prime}-Z^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and then carries the
Mixed Hodge Structure isomorphic to the cokernel of
<math id="S7.Thmtheorem21.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i^{\prime\prime}_{X})^{*}-Trq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>i</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i^{\prime\prime}_{X})^{*}-Trq</annotation></semantics></math> acting as a morphism of Mixed Hodge Structures.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem19.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem19.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The left term carry a Mixed Hodge Structure as the special case
of the complementary of the normal crossing divisor: <math id="S7.Thmtheorem19.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Z</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> into
the smooth proper variety <math id="S7.Thmtheorem19.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> , while the middle term is the
complementary of the normal crossing divisor: <math id="S7.Thmtheorem19.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Z</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> into the
normal crossing divisor: <math id="S7.Thmtheorem19.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. Both cases can be treated by the
above special cases without the general theory of simplicial
varieties. This shows that the Mixed Hodge Structure is uniquely
defined by the construction on an open normal crossing divisor and
the logarithmic case for a smooth variety.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
